window.ACE_FULL_MOCKS = {"version":"2026-10-01","rules":"five choices; key never the longest; even key letters per section; no item from The Climb or the Prometric mocks; every item blind re-solved","mocks":[{"id":"FULL_1","name":"Full-Length Test 1","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"bio:BIO_p077","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Cholecystokinin is released by duodenal cells when fat and protein enter the small intestine. Which response does it produce?","opts":["Release of intrinsic factor from the stomach lining","Insulin","Acid release","Bile release + pancreatic enzyme secretion","Stomach contraction"],"correct":3,"why":"Match each gut hormone to the meal component that triggers it. Fat and protein in the duodenum release cholecystokinin, which contracts the gallbladder to send bile for emulsifying fat and signals the pancreas to release its digestive enzymes, while acid in the duodenum releases secretin for bicarbonate instead. The choice pairing bile release with pancreatic enzyme secretion is the cholecystokinin package, which is exactly what a fatty meal calls for.","traps":["Intrinsic factor is released by the stomach's parietal cells alongside acid, and it is not under cholecystokinin control.","Insulin comes from the pancreatic beta cells in response to rising blood glucose, which is the endocrine pancreas rather than the digestive one.","Gastrin is the hormone that drives acid secretion, and cholecystokinin actually slows the stomach down while the intestine works.",null,"Cholecystokinin reduces gastric motility rather than increasing it, giving the duodenum time to handle a fatty meal."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g079","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"During which stage of cell division does the synaptonemal complex form?","opts":["Metaphase I, as the pairs line up on the metaphase plate","Prophase I (holds homologs together)","Metaphase II","Mitosis prophase","Anaphase II"],"correct":1,"why":"The synaptonemal complex is the protein zipper that runs between paired homologs, so it can only exist where synapsis happens, which is prophase I. Its job is to hold the partners in register long enough for crossing over to take place. If you file synapsis and crossing over in the same neighborhood, this stage answers itself.","traps":["The pairs are already synapsed by the time they reach the plate, since the complex assembles earlier in prophase I and is taken apart before the pairs separate.",null,"Metaphase II lines up the sister chromatids of single chromosomes rather than homologous partners.","Mitotic prophase never pairs homologs, so there is nothing for the zipper to hold together.","By anaphase II the homologs are in different cells, long past any pairing."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which cells of the alveolus produce pulmonary surfactant?","opts":["Type II alveolar cells","Goblet cells","Capillary endothelial cells in the alveolar wall","Macrophages","Type I alveolar cells"],"correct":0,"why":"Surfactant is the soapy film that lowers surface tension inside the alveolus so it does not collapse as it empties, and it comes from the type II alveolar cells. Keep the division of labor straight: type I cells are thin and flat and cover most of the surface for gas exchange, while type II cells are cuboidal, fewer in number, and do the secreting. Premature infants breathe with difficulty partly because their type II cells have not made enough surfactant yet.","traps":[null,"Goblet cells secrete mucus in the airways above the alveoli, a different secretion in a different place.","Capillary endothelium forms the blood side of the barrier, and surfactant is made on the air side.","Alveolar macrophages clean up dust and microbes inside the alveolus instead of making surfactant.","Type I cells line most of the alveolar surface and are built thin for gas exchange rather than for secretion."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1qjf2kq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Which enzyme sets the pace of the Krebs cycle, and what turns it down?","opts":["Citrate synthase, slowed by oxygen","Aconitase, slowed by acetyl-CoA","Malate dehydrogenase, slowed by oxaloacetate","Isocitrate dehydrogenase, slowed by NADH and ATP","Succinate dehydrogenase, slowed by a shortage of FAD"],"correct":3,"why":"The cycle is regulated the same way glycolysis is, by the products that signal a full tank. Isocitrate dehydrogenase is the rate-limiting enzyme, and rising NADH and ATP slow it while ADP speeds it back up. If a question tells you a cell is energy-rich and asks what the Krebs cycle does, expect the pathway to throttle at this step.","traps":["Citrate synthase is regulated, but oxygen is not its signal, since the cycle never uses oxygen directly.","Aconitase only rearranges citrate into isocitrate, and acetyl-CoA feeds the cycle rather than slowing it.","Oxaloacetate is the product of that step and citrate synthase consumes it immediately, so it does not build up as a brake.",null,"Succinate dehydrogenase needs FAD as its electron acceptor, so FAD drives that step rather than blocking it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-dmpc6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"The first half of glycolysis costs the cell two ATP. Which pair of enzymes spends them?","opts":["Aldolase and triose phosphate isomerase","Phosphoglucose isomerase and enolase","Hexokinase and phosphofructokinase","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase and phosphoglycerate kinase","Hexokinase and pyruvate kinase"],"correct":2,"why":"Two phosphorylations open glycolysis and both are paid for with ATP: glucose to glucose-6-phosphate, then fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate. Notice that both reactions add a phosphate onto the sugar, and that is the tell. Enzymes that spend ATP are the ones putting phosphates on, enzymes that make ATP are the ones taking phosphates off.","traps":["Aldolase cuts the six-carbon sugar in two and the isomerase reshuffles one half, and neither reaction touches ATP.","These two rearrange the molecule, and the enolase step removes water rather than spending a phosphate.",null,"These are the two payoff-phase steps: one makes NADH and the other makes ATP.","Hexokinase does spend ATP, but pyruvate kinase is a payoff-phase enzyme that makes ATP instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-11djtx4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A hormone activates phospholipase C in a smooth muscle cell. Which pair of second messengers is produced, and what does the water-soluble one do?","opts":["cAMP and cGMP, and cAMP activates protein kinase A in the cytoplasm of the cell","DAG and calcium, and DAG travels to the nucleus to bind DNA","IP3 and DAG, and IP3 stays in the membrane to activate protein kinase C","cAMP and calcium, and cAMP opens calcium channels in the nucleus","IP3 and DAG, and IP3 releases calcium from the smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],"correct":4,"why":"Phospholipase C splits the membrane lipid PIP2 into two pieces with different fates. IP3 is water soluble, so it leaves the membrane, travels to the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, and opens calcium channels there, while DAG is lipid soluble and stays in the membrane where it helps switch on protein kinase C. Sort the two by solubility and their destinations follow.","traps":["Those are the products of the two cyclases, not of phospholipase C.","DAG is not a transcription factor, and it does not leave the membrane.","The roles are swapped, DAG is the piece that remains in the membrane for protein kinase C.","Phospholipase C does not make cAMP, and the calcium store being tapped is the smooth endoplasmic reticulum.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1cnibqa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cell holds at the boundary between metaphase and anaphase and will not proceed. What is the checkpoint at that boundary actually monitoring?","opts":["That the DNA has finished replicating and carries no unrepaired damage","That the cell has grown large enough and has the nutrients to divide","That every kinetochore is attached to microtubules from opposite poles","That the cleavage furrow has already begun pinching the cytoplasm inward","That the nuclear envelope has completely reassembled around both sets of chromosomes"],"correct":2,"why":"Match each checkpoint to the disaster it prevents. The spindle checkpoint exists to keep a daughter cell from getting the wrong number of chromosomes, so the only thing it can be watching is attachment. Any kinetochore still unattached keeps sending a wait signal, and anaphase begins only when the last one is captured with pull coming from both poles. G1 asks about resources, G2 asks about the copy, and this one asks about the grip.","traps":["That is what the G2 checkpoint asks, and by metaphase the replication question has long since been settled.","Size and nutrients are the G1 checkpoint's business, because that is where the cell decides whether to commit to a whole division.",null,"Cytokinesis follows chromosome separation rather than permitting it, and a furrow forming early would cut the cell before the chromosomes were sorted.","The nuclear envelope reassembles during telophase, which comes after anaphase, so it cannot be a condition for starting anaphase."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p021","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Bile is produced by which organ?","opts":["Pancreas","Liver","Gallbladder","Small intestine","Spleen"],"correct":1,"why":"Two organs share the bile story and the exam checks which does which. The liver's hepatocytes make bile continuously, then the gallbladder stores and concentrates it and squeezes it out when a fatty meal arrives. Made in the liver, kept in the gallbladder, used in the small intestine.","traps":["The pancreas sends digestive enzymes and bicarbonate into the same stretch of intestine, which is easy to blur with bile, but it does not make bile.",null,"The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile between meals, and storing is not the same as producing.","The small intestine is where bile does its work on fat, not where it is made.","The spleen recycles worn out red blood cells and releases bilirubin, which the liver then takes up and puts into bile."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p091","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In a long bone such as the femur, the elongated cylindrical shaft between the two ends is called which of the following?","opts":["Periosteum","Metaphysis","Endosteum","Diaphysis","Epiphysis"],"correct":3,"why":"Long bone terms map onto one picture: a tube with a knob at each end. The diaphysis is that tube, a cylinder of compact bone wrapped around the marrow cavity and built to resist bending, while each epiphysis is a knob of spongy bone under articular cartilage and the metaphysis joins the two. The choice naming the diaphysis is the shaft, and holding that picture keeps the other terms in place.","traps":["The periosteum is the fibrous sheath wrapping the outside of the bone rather than a region of the bone itself.","The metaphysis is the transition zone between shaft and end, and it contains the growth plate in a growing bone.","The endosteum is the thin membrane lining the marrow cavity inside the shaft, so it is a lining rather than the shaft.",null,"The epiphysis is the expanded end of the bone that forms the joint surface and holds mostly spongy bone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e040","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Human third molars, commonly called wisdom teeth, are frequently impacted, extracted, or never formed at all. In evolutionary terms they are best described as which of the following?","opts":["Adaptive now","An atavism, a trait that reappears after being absent for many generations","Vestigial - remnants of larger ancestral jaw","Functional","New innovation"],"correct":2,"why":"A vestigial structure is a reduced remnant of something that was fully functional in ancestors. Earlier hominins had larger jaws and a coarse diet that wore teeth down heavily, so a third set of molars earned its place; modern human jaws are shorter, and the third molars often have nowhere to erupt. Reduced, unreliable, and sometimes absent altogether is the pattern the word vestigial is pointing at.","traps":["Calling them adaptive means they raise fitness today, and a jaw too short to hold them points the other way.","An atavism is a trait that reappears after generations of absence, while third molars have been continuously present and merely reduced in usefulness.",null,"Third molars can chew perfectly well when they erupt properly, but a structure that is so often impacted, removed, or never formed does not fit a trait being maintained for its function.","Third molars are old and shared with other primates; what changed recently in humans is the shortened jaw around them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-8da5ad8639","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels. What is the direct effect on a neuron exposed to it?","opts":["Neurotransmitter release increases at the terminal","Repolarization fails, so the membrane stays positive","The resting potential immediately rises to 0 mV","The neuron fires action potentials continuously","The neuron can no longer fire action potentials"],"correct":4,"why":"No Na+ entry means no upstroke. The cell still has a normal resting potential, but it can never reach the rising phase, so no signal travels. That is why this toxin causes numbness and paralysis.","traps":["With no action potential reaching the terminal, release drops.","Repolarization is the K+ channel job, and it never gets the chance because the spike never starts.","Resting potential depends mainly on the K+ leak and the pump, which the toxin leaves alone.","Continuous firing would need Na+ channels that stay open. The toxin BLOCKS them.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-3-a1ldow","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Nitric oxide released by an endothelial cell reaches the smooth muscle of a blood vessel and relaxes it. Which feature of this signal is most unusual compared with a typical hormone?","opts":["It is stored in secretory vesicles and released slowly by exocytosis over several hours","It travels through the blood to reach distant target organs","It binds a G-protein-coupled receptor on the muscle cell surface","It is a gas that crosses membranes directly and acts on an enzyme inside the cell","It acts as a transcription factor once it reaches the nucleus"],"correct":3,"why":"Nitric oxide is a small uncharged gas, so it needs no surface receptor and no transporter: it diffuses straight through the membrane. Inside the muscle cell it activates guanylyl cyclase, cGMP rises, and the muscle relaxes so the vessel widens. It also breaks down within seconds, which is why its effect stays local.","traps":["Nitric oxide is made on demand and breaks down in seconds rather than being stored.","It is far too short lived for long-distance travel, its action is local.","Its receptor is the intracellular enzyme guanylyl cyclase, not a receptor at the surface.",null,"Nitric oxide works through cGMP rather than by binding DNA."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-nd28q3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A compound makes the thylakoid membrane freely permeable to protons. What is the immediate effect on an illuminated chloroplast?","opts":["The Calvin cycle speeds up because more protons reach the stroma","Both electron transport and ATP synthesis speed up together","Water splitting stops while NADPH production continues","Electron transport halts while ATP synthesis continues","Electron transport continues while ATP synthesis collapses"],"correct":4,"why":"Separate the two halves of chemiosmosis: the chain that builds the gradient and the turbine that spends it. Poking holes in the membrane lets protons slip back without turning ATP synthase, so the pumping continues and the payoff disappears. The same logic applies to any uncoupler in any membrane, including the mitochondrion.","traps":["Protons that leak past ATP synthase do no work, and the Calvin cycle still needs the ATP it is no longer getting.","Electron transport may run slightly faster with no gradient pushing back, but ATP synthesis cannot rise without one.","Water splitting keeps feeding electrons into photosystem II, and NADPH production continues right along with it.","The carriers keep working as long as light is hitting them, because the leak destroys the gradient rather than the chain.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g016","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following best describes the consequence of a frameshift mutation in the coding region of a gene?","opts":["Creates a stop codon","Shifts reading frame, often catastrophic","Removes exactly one codon, deleting a single amino acid","Has no effect","Changes one amino acid"],"correct":1,"why":"A frameshift comes from adding or losing a number of bases that is not a multiple of three. The ribosome keeps reading three bases at a time from the original start point, so every codon after the change is misread, usually producing a stretch of wrong amino acids and often an early stop. The quick test to apply on any insertion or deletion question is whether the number of bases is divisible by three; if it is not, the frame moves.","traps":["A frameshift often does run into a premature stop, but naming a new stop codon directly describes a nonsense mutation, which does not move the frame.",null,"Losing three bases keeps the frame intact and simply removes one amino acid, which is why in-frame deletions are usually far less damaging.","Having no effect describes a silent mutation, whereas a shifted frame changes everything from that point onward.","Changing a single amino acid is what a missense substitution does, and it leaves the reading frame intact."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev019","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"What role does the yolk sac play in a mammalian embryo?","opts":["Has nutrition role only","No function","Secretion of the hormone that maintains the corpus luteum","Becomes placenta","First site of hematopoiesis + germ cell origin"],"correct":4,"why":"This question tests why an ancient structure sticks around after its original job is gone. A mammalian embryo gets its food across the placenta, so the yolk sac carries no meaningful yolk, yet it is still where the first blood cells and blood vessels appear and where the primordial germ cells arise before migrating to the developing gonads. The lesson generalizes: evolution reuses old structures for new work, which is why the same four extraembryonic membranes show up in mammals with rewritten job descriptions.","traps":["In mammals the yolk sac holds essentially no yolk, since nutrition comes across the placenta instead, so a purely nutritional role does not fit.","It is kept precisely because it took on other jobs, so a structure that looks vestigial turns out to be essential early on.","Human chorionic gonadotropin, which keeps the corpus luteum producing progesterone, is made by the trophoblast and chorion rather than the yolk sac.","The fetal side of the placenta comes from the chorion and its villi, not from the yolk sac.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p027","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following correctly states a way that spermatogenesis in the male differs from oogenesis in the female?","opts":["Continuous vs finite from birth","Both produce 4 viable gametes","Same number of gametes per cycle","Spermatogenesis pauses at metaphase II and finishes only if fertilization occurs","Both arrest in prophase I"],"correct":0,"why":"This is testing the timeline of gamete production rather than the mechanics of meiosis. A male makes new sperm continuously from puberty onward, because spermatogonia keep dividing and renewing the supply for decades. A female is born with her whole set of primary oocytes already formed and arrested, and that pool only shrinks from then on. Anchor it on supply: one is a factory that keeps running, the other is a fixed inventory.","traps":[null,"Only sperm come out as four usable cells, since in the female the three polar bodies are discarded.","One round of meiosis in the male yields four sperm while one round in the female yields a single egg, so the counts are not equal.","It is the oocyte, not the sperm, that waits at metaphase II and completes meiosis only when a sperm arrives.","The long pause in prophase I belongs to oocytes; developing sperm run through meiosis without waiting there."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g040","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A trait is determined by a gene located on the Y chromosome. How is this trait transmitted through a family?","opts":["Father to all sons (only)","From a father to his sons, but only if the mother also carries the allele","Mother to son","Both sexes","Father to daughter"],"correct":0,"why":"The Y chromosome has exactly one route through a family. A father gives it to every son and to no daughter, because a daughter receives his X instead. So a Y-linked trait shows up in an unbroken male line, generation after generation, with no skips and no affected females anywhere. If a pedigree ever shows an affected female or a skipped generation, the gene is not on the Y.","traps":[null,"The Y travels on its own from father to son, and the mother's genotype has no bearing on it whatsoever.","Mothers have no Y chromosome to give, so nothing Y-linked can ever arrive from the mother.","Females have no Y chromosome at all, so they can neither carry nor show a Y-linked trait.","A daughter receives her father's X chromosome, never his Y."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e021","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following describes hybrid breakdown?","opts":["F1 immediately dies","Each successive hybrid generation is more vigorous than the one before it","No hybrid forms","F1 viable + fertile, F2 weak/sterile","Mules sterile"],"correct":3,"why":"Hybrid breakdown is the latest acting postzygotic barrier of the set. The first generation hybrid is healthy and can reproduce, but when those hybrids breed with each other or back to a parent, the second generation is weak or sterile, because gene combinations that worked together in each parent species get shuffled apart. Keep the ladder in order: inviability kills the hybrid, sterility stops it reproducing, and breakdown waits one more generation before the trouble shows.","traps":["A hybrid that dies right away is showing hybrid inviability, which acts a full step earlier than breakdown.","Hybrids becoming stronger in later generations is hybrid vigor, the opposite outcome from breakdown.","If no hybrid forms at all, the barrier was prezygotic and acted before a zygote ever existed.",null,"The mule is the standard case of hybrid sterility, where the first generation itself cannot reproduce, so there is never a second generation to break down."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p098","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A cytotoxic T cell carries the CD8 co-receptor. What does its T cell receptor recognize?","opts":["Free antigen","Surface antibodies","MHC II","MHC I (presents endogenous antigens)","Peptides displayed only by professional antigen presenting cells"],"correct":3,"why":"The question is testing the CD8 with class I, CD4 with class II pairing and the reason it exists. MHC class I sits on essentially every nucleated cell and displays peptides made inside that cell, so it is a window into what the cell is manufacturing, which is how a cytotoxic T cell spots a virus infected or abnormal cell and kills it. A memory hook that holds up: the numbers multiply to eight, so CD8 goes with class I and CD4 goes with class II.","traps":["A T cell receptor only recognizes peptide already loaded into an MHC groove, so antigen floating free in solution is invisible to it.","Membrane bound antibody is the B cell receptor, which binds antigen in its native shape, and T cells cannot see antigen that way.","MHC class II is the helper T cell's partner, since CD4 binds class II, and class II displays material the cell took in from outside.",null,"Every nucleated cell in the body displays MHC class I, which is exactly why a cytotoxic T cell can inspect an ordinary infected cell and not just a dedicated antigen presenting cell."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p047","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"What is the approximate resting membrane potential of a typical neuron?","opts":["-70 mV","0 mV","-55 mV","+70 mV","+30 mV"],"correct":0,"why":"At rest the inside of a neuron is negative relative to the outside, landing near -70 millivolts. Two things put it there: potassium leak channels let potassium drift out down its gradient and leave negatively charged proteins behind, and the sodium-potassium pump exports three sodium for every two potassium it imports. Lock in the sign first, negative inside, then the size.","traps":[null,"Zero would mean no charge separation across the membrane, leaving the neuron with no gradient to work with.","Around -55 millivolts is the threshold a neuron must reach in order to fire, which sits above the resting value rather than at it.","Positive 70 millivolts has about the right magnitude but the wrong sign, and a resting neuron is negative on the inside.","Positive 30 millivolts is roughly the peak reached at the top of an action potential, not the resting state."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e045","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the equation for logistic population growth?","opts":["dN/dt = rN(K-N)/K","dN/dt = rN(N-K)/K","N = K","dN/dt = rN","N = N₀ × e^(rt)"],"correct":0,"why":"Logistic growth is exponential growth with a brake bolted on. The rN piece is the same engine that drives exponential growth, and the (K minus N) over K term is a fraction between 0 and 1 measuring how much room is left. When N is small that fraction sits near 1 and growth looks exponential; as N approaches K it shrinks toward 0 and growth stops, producing the S-shaped curve. Read the brake term that way and you never have to memorize the formula blind.","traps":[null,"This reverses the sign, which would make the population shrink whenever it sits below carrying capacity and grow once it passes it.","This states that the population equals carrying capacity, which is the endpoint rather than an equation for growth.","This is exponential growth, with no ceiling at all, so the population would never level off.","This is the integrated form of exponential growth, giving population size over time rather than a logistic rate of change."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g063","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A woman who is a carrier for hemophilia, an X-linked recessive condition, has children with a man who has normal clotting. What is expected among their sons?","opts":["25% affected","50% affected, 50% normal","All affected","All normal","All sons are unaffected carriers"],"correct":1,"why":"Sons receive their single X chromosome from their mother, so her two X chromosomes set the odds entirely on their own. She has a normal allele on one X and the hemophilia allele on the other, and each son receives one or the other at random, giving roughly half affected and half unaffected. The father contributes a Y to every son, so his clotting status never enters the calculation.","traps":["One quarter is the fraction of all the children who are affected sons, not the fraction of the sons who are affected.",null,"Every son would be affected only if the mother carried the hemophilia allele on both of her X chromosomes.","A carrier does have one hemophilia allele available to pass on, so her sons are genuinely at risk.","Males have a single X chromosome and so have no carrier state; a son either receives the allele and has the condition, or does not."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d010","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"The wing of a bird and the wing of an insect are best described as which of the following?","opts":["Vestigial","Synapomorphies","Homologous structures","Divergent evolution from a common winged ancestor","Analogous structures (convergent)"],"correct":4,"why":"The test here is same job versus same blueprint. Analogous structures do the same work but are built from different ancestral parts, which is convergent evolution, and a bird wing, a modified forelimb with bones, next to an insect wing, an outgrowth of the body wall, is the classic pair. Homologous structures are the reverse: the same underlying blueprint, sometimes doing very different jobs. Ask what a structure is made of before you ask what it does, and these two never blur.","traps":["Vestigial means a reduced remnant that no longer does its original job, such as the wing of a flightless bird; both of these wings work.","A synapomorphy is a derived trait shared because it came from a common ancestor, which would group birds with insects, and it does not.","Homologous structures share ancestry and internal architecture, the way a bat wing, a whale flipper and your arm share the same bone series; these two wings share neither.","Divergent evolution starts from one ancestral structure and pulls it in different directions; here the two wings began from different tissue and converged on the same job.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_xp008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"In a community with three trophic levels (producers, primary consumers, secondary consumers), the introduction of a top predator (a tertiary consumer) typically results in:","opts":["Trophic cascade; secondary-consumer numbers decline, primary consumers rebound, producer biomass decreases","Trophic cascade; secondary-consumer numbers decline, primary consumers also decline, producer biomass INCREASES","No effect; the new predator only eats producers","Loss of primary consumers across the food web","Linear decline in all populations"],"correct":0,"why":"Adding a tertiary predator triggers a top-down trophic cascade: the new predator suppresses secondary consumers, which releases primary consumers from predation pressure (primary consumers rebound), and the increased primary-consumer grazing then reduces producer biomass. Option A describes this classic four-level cascade correctly.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1f9fdnq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"In linear electron flow, each electron is boosted once at photosystem II and again at photosystem I. What is the minimum number of photons absorbed per molecule of oxygen released?","opts":["Two","Six","Eight","Twelve","Four"],"correct":2,"why":"Build the number up from the electrons. One oxygen molecule comes from two waters, which release four electrons between them, and each of those four gets lifted twice, so four times two is eight. Any photon counting question on the light reactions starts by counting electrons first.","traps":["Two is the number of water molecules split, not the number of photons absorbed.","Six does not match any step in the count of electrons or of boosts.",null,"Twelve overshoots, because each electron takes two uphill jumps rather than three.","Four counts the electrons, or one boost each, and forgets the second excitation at photosystem I."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev025","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Hox genes are a family of transcription factors expressed in a characteristic order along a developing embryo. What do they specify?","opts":["Sex","Dorsal-ventral polarity of the early embryo","Symmetry","Tissue color","Anterior-posterior body axis identity"],"correct":4,"why":"This is testing what Hox genes do once the body axes already exist: they assign identity to each region along the head to tail axis, telling a segment what structure to become. Mutations in them usually do not delete a part, they swap one part for another, such as a leg growing where an antenna belongs, and that swap is the fingerprint of an identity gene. The order of the genes along the chromosome matches the order of the regions they control, which is a useful way to hold them.","traps":["Sex determination runs through its own genes and chromosomes, while these transcription factors act after the axes are laid down to name each region.","Back and belly are patterned by a different set of signaling molecules; this family assigns identity along the head to tail axis instead.","Whether an animal is radially or bilaterally symmetric is settled well before these genes act; their job is regional identity along an axis that already exists.","Pigment is set by separate pathways; these genes assign what a body region becomes, not what color it ends up.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-yiltle","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"The outer mitochondrial membrane is the gate for the intrinsic pathway, and two opposing families of proteins compete over it. A tumor cell is found to make far more Bcl-2 protein than normal. What is the effect on that cell?","opts":["It punches pores in the mitochondrial membrane, so the cell dies more readily than usual","It forces the cell into the extrinsic pathway, so death receptors take the decision over","It arrests the cell in G1, so the tumor ends up growing more slowly over time","It activates caspase-8 through the death receptor, which speeds apoptosis up","It blocks cytochrome c release, so the cell survives damage that should kill it"],"correct":4,"why":"Two families, one gate. Bax and Bak open the outer mitochondrial membrane and let cytochrome c out, and Bcl-2 is the antiapoptotic partner that holds them in check. Too much Bcl-2 means the gate never opens, so a cell carrying damage that should have ended it survives and keeps dividing, which is why this protein is overproduced in certain cancers. Sort the family by the first letters: Bax breaks the gate open, Bcl-2 bolts it closed.","traps":["Pore formation is the job of the opposing family, Bax and Bak, and Bcl-2 exists specifically to keep those proteins in check.","Blocking one pathway does not redirect a cell into the other, and the extrinsic pathway needs a ligand from outside to start it.","Bcl-2 acts on cell death rather than on the cell cycle, and a cell that resists dying accumulates rather than slows down.","Caspase-8 belongs to the extrinsic pathway at the cell surface, and Bcl-2 acts at the mitochondrion instead.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p105","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"When an action potential reaches the axon terminal, neurotransmitter is released into the synaptic cleft by which mechanism?","opts":["Passive flow through voltage gated sodium channels in the terminal membrane","Diffusion through membrane","Active transport","Ca²⁺-triggered exocytosis (SNARE proteins)","Phagocytosis"],"correct":3,"why":"Follow the signal to the end of the axon. The arriving action potential opens voltage gated calcium channels, calcium floods into the terminal, and that calcium drives SNARE proteins to fuse the vesicle membrane with the cell membrane so the vesicle dumps its contents into the cleft. Calcium is the trigger worth remembering here, because without calcium entry there is no release even when the action potential arrives.","traps":["Ion channels pass small ions such as sodium and calcium, and a neurotransmitter molecule is far too large to fit through one.","Neurotransmitters sit packaged inside vesicles and most are water soluble, so they cannot drift straight across the lipid membrane.","Active transport moves molecules across a membrane one at a time, and it is used to load neurotransmitter into vesicles rather than to release it.",null,"Phagocytosis brings large material into a cell, which is the wrong direction for getting neurotransmitter out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In the nitrogen cycle, the process of nitrogen fixation converts:","opts":["NO₃⁻ to N₂","N₂ to NH₃","Organic to inorganic","NH₄⁺ to amino acids in plant tissue","NH₃ to NO₃⁻"],"correct":1,"why":"Nitrogen gas makes up most of the air, but the triple bond in N₂ puts it out of reach for nearly every organism, so the cycle has to start by breaking that bond. Fixation is that step: bacteria convert N₂ into ammonia, a form plants can actually use. Keep the order straight by following the atom: fixation brings nitrogen in from the air, nitrification oxidizes ammonia to nitrite and then nitrate, assimilation builds it into tissue, ammonification releases it from dead material, and denitrification returns it to the atmosphere as N₂.","traps":["This is denitrification, which sends nitrogen back to the atmosphere instead of bringing it in.",null,"This describes decomposition and ammonification, which recycle nitrogen that is already inside living things.","This is assimilation, the step where a plant builds already fixed nitrogen into its own molecules.","This is nitrification, carried out by soil bacteria after fixation has already delivered the nitrogen."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g068","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a bacterial cloning experiment that uses a plasmid with a cloning site inside the lacZ gene, blue/white screening lets a researcher distinguish which of the following?","opts":["Plasmids that have insert (white) vs intact (blue)","Antibiotic resistance","Colonies carrying the insert in the correct orientation","Mutations","GC content"],"correct":0,"why":"The trick works by breaking a gene on purpose. The cloning site sits inside lacZ, whose enzyme product turns a colorless substrate in the plate blue. If a fragment gets inserted there, lacZ is disrupted and the colony stays white; if the plasmid simply closed back up empty, lacZ still works and the colony turns blue. White means something went in, so white colonies are the ones worth picking.","traps":[null,"Resistance is handled by a separate marker on the plasmid, which selects for cells that took up any plasmid at all before color is ever considered.","The color tells you that an insert interrupted the gene, but a fragment can go in either direction and still give a white colony.","This screen reports only whether the cloning site was interrupted, and says nothing about whether any sequence carries a mutation.","Base composition is measured by sequencing or by melting behavior, not by a color reaction on an agar plate."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-hd8ex8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A sprinting muscle cell is fermenting. What does it make from pyruvate, which enzyme does it, and is any gas released?","opts":["Lactate, by pyruvate decarboxylase, with CO2 released","Lactate, by lactate dehydrogenase, with no gas released","Acetyl-CoA, by pyruvate dehydrogenase, with CO2 released","Acetaldehyde, by pyruvate decarboxylase, with CO2 released","Ethanol, by alcohol dehydrogenase, with CO2 released"],"correct":1,"why":"Human muscle runs lactic acid fermentation: NADH hands its electrons straight to pyruvate, lactate dehydrogenase catalyzes the transfer, and NAD+ comes back ready for glycolysis. Nothing is decarboxylated, so no carbon dioxide comes off and lactate keeps all three of pyruvate's carbons. The carbon count is the cleanest way to tell the two fermentations apart: three carbons in and three carbons out means lactate.","traps":["The enzyme and the gas both belong to the alcoholic route, and making lactate removes no carbon at all.",null,"This is aerobic pyruvate oxidation in the matrix, the pathway that shuts down when oxygen runs short.","Acetaldehyde is the intermediate of alcoholic fermentation, again a yeast and plant pathway.","Ethanol is the yeast and plant route, and animal cells have no pyruvate decarboxylase to start it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-d8063fa866","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"An organism has a diploid number of 8. How many chromatids are present in one cell at metaphase I and in one cell at metaphase II?","opts":["16 at metaphase I and 8 at metaphase II","16 at metaphase I and 4 at metaphase II","8 at metaphase I and 4 at metaphase II","16 at metaphase I and 16 at metaphase II","32 at metaphase I and 16 at metaphase II"],"correct":0,"why":"Start with 8 chromosomes. After replication each has two chromatids, so metaphase I has 8 chromosomes and 16 chromatids. Meiosis I halves the chromosome number, so a metaphase II cell has 4 chromosomes, each still with two chromatids, for 8 chromatids.","traps":[null,"At metaphase II the sister chromatids are still joined, so 4 chromosomes means 8 chromatids.","You counted chromosomes. Each chromosome is replicated here, so double it to count chromatids.","Meiosis I already split the cell, so a metaphase II cell holds half as much.","DNA is replicated once, so 8 chromosomes become 16 chromatids, not 32."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Monocots and dicots differ in a set of paired traits. Which of the following is a monocot trait?","opts":["Vascular bundles arranged in a ring around the stem","2 cotyledons","Parallel leaf veins, flower parts in 3s","Taproot","Net leaf venation"],"correct":2,"why":"Monocot traits travel together as a package built around the counting rule in the name, one cotyledon. Picture a blade of grass or a lily: parallel veins running the length of the leaf, flower parts in threes or multiples of three, vascular bundles scattered through the stem, fibrous roots. Picture a bean or an oak for the dicot column: netted veins, parts in fours or fives, a ring of bundles, a taproot. The choice pairing parallel veins with parts in threes is the monocot side of that table.","traps":["A ring of vascular bundles is the dicot stem; monocot bundles are scattered through the stem with no ring.","Two seed leaves is the dicot pattern, and the name monocot tells you there is one.",null,"A single deep taproot is the dicot habit; monocots spread a fibrous root system, which is why grass sod lifts as a mat.","Netted, branching venation belongs to dicot leaves such as a maple; the monocot picture is a grass blade with parallel lines."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-i1qexy","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A researcher raises the outside glucose concentration step by step and measures how fast glucose enters a muscle cell. Uptake rises at first, then levels off at a maximum rate. What does that plateau tell you?","opts":["The cell has begun pumping glucose back out at an equal rate","Glucose crossing has switched over to active transport","The membrane became impermeable to glucose at high concentration","The carrier proteins are saturated, so transport has a ceiling","Glucose is entering by simple diffusion straight through the lipid bilayer"],"correct":3,"why":"A plateau is the fingerprint of a finite number of protein binding sites. Simple diffusion climbs in a straight line with the gradient because there is nothing to fill up, while a carrier has a limited number of seats, and once every seat is occupied, adding more glucose cannot speed anything up. Curve flattens means protein involved, whether the transport is facilitated or active.","traps":["Nothing in the data shows efflux, and growing efflux would bend the curve downward rather than flatten it.","Glucose entry down its gradient needs no energy input, and changing modes would not by itself create a ceiling.","Membranes do not switch off their permeability when solute concentration rises.",null,"Simple diffusion gives a straight line with no ceiling, since there is no protein to saturate."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-104j1lu","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"In an illuminated chloroplast, which way do protons move through ATP synthase, and where does the ATP appear?","opts":["From the stroma into the lumen, with the ATP appearing in the lumen","From the cytosol into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the stroma","From the lumen into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the stroma","From the lumen into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the lumen","From the stroma into the lumen, with the ATP appearing in the stroma"],"correct":2,"why":"Pumps push uphill and turbines run downhill. The electron carriers load protons into the lumen, so the lumen becomes the crowded acidic side, and ATP synthase lets that crowd stream back out into the stroma. The ATP shows up exactly where the Calvin cycle needs it, which makes a handy sanity check.","traps":["That is the direction the electron carriers pump protons, which is the uphill direction rather than the direction through the enzyme.","The cytosol sits outside the chloroplast entirely and takes no part in this gradient.",null,"The direction is right, but the catalytic head of the enzyme faces the stroma, so the ATP is released there.","Protons move down their gradient through the enzyme, which means out of the lumen, not into it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d014","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"With respect to germ layers, cnidarians such as jellyfish and hydra are:","opts":["Acoelomate bilaterians","Built from ectoderm and mesoderm, with no endoderm","Diploblastic (only ecto + endo)","Triploblastic","Deuterostomes"],"correct":2,"why":"Count the layers by picturing a cross section of the body wall of a hydra. There is an outer epidermis from ectoderm, an inner gastrodermis from endoderm lining the gastrovascular cavity, and between them a jelly-like mesoglea rather than a true cellular middle layer. Two germ layers is diploblastic, which is the choice to take. Mesoderm, and with it true muscle, organs and any body cavity, arrives with the bilaterians.","traps":["Acoelomate bilaterians are the flatworms, which do have mesoderm and a distinct head end; cnidarians are radial and have neither.","Endoderm is the layer lining the gastrovascular cavity of a cnidarian, so it is mesoderm that is missing, not endoderm.",null,"Three germ layers begins with the bilaterians; a cnidarian has two, with mesoglea in between rather than a mesoderm layer.","Deuterostome and protostome are categories for bilaterians with three germ layers, and cnidarians sit outside that split."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g057","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-6","topic":"The central dogma","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"What does the term anticipation mean when it is used to describe an inherited disorder?","opts":["Carrier expectancy","Earlier onset / more severe in successive generations","Genetic counseling","The chance that a carrier couple's next pregnancy will be affected","Pre-natal screening"],"correct":1,"why":"This is testing a pattern seen in the trinucleotide repeat disorders, such as Huntington disease and myotonic dystrophy. The repeat tends to expand as it passes through the germ line, so a child inherits a longer repeat than the parent carried, and a longer repeat brings the disease on earlier and more severely. The word names a pattern visible in the family tree, not a clinical service offered to families. If a pedigree shows the disease striking younger with each generation, look for an expanding repeat.","traps":["This blends carrier status with the idea of expecting something, but the term is about onset shifting earlier, not about who carries the allele.",null,"Counseling is the conversation about risk, and this pattern is one of the facts that conversation would have to account for.","That is recurrence risk, a probability attached to a single pregnancy, while this term describes how the disease shifts earlier from one generation to the next.","Screening is a test performed during a pregnancy, while this term describes a pattern observed across generations of a family."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p060","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A urinalysis shows glucose present in the urine. This finding most commonly indicates which of the following?","opts":["Filtration defect","Normal","Too little antidiuretic hormone reaching the collecting duct","Kidney failure","Plasma glucose above the reabsorption capacity"],"correct":4,"why":"Glucose reabsorption runs on sodium glucose cotransporters, and transporters saturate. Below the transport maximum, around 180 to 200 milligrams per deciliter of plasma glucose, every filtered glucose molecule is recovered in the proximal tubule; above it the carriers are full and the excess spills into the urine and drags water osmotically with it. The choice describing plasma glucose passing the reabsorption limit is the right read, and it also explains the frequent urination that goes with it.","traps":["A damaged filtration barrier leaks large molecules such as albumin and whole blood cells; glucose is small and is filtered freely even in a healthy kidney.","Healthy nephrons recover essentially all filtered glucose in the proximal tubule, so urine glucose is normally undetectable.","That describes diabetes insipidus, a water problem, where the urine comes out in large dilute volumes but carries no sugar.","Failing kidneys usually show a falling filtration rate with rising creatinine and urea, while losing glucose specifically points at transporters being overwhelmed.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_xp003","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-9","topic":"Mendelian genetics","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"In a dihybrid cross involving two unlinked genes, parents AaBb × AaBb produce 800 offspring. Phenotype counts are: 460 A_B_, 152 A_bb, 158 aaB_, 30 aabb. What does the deviation from the expected 9:3:3:1 ratio suggest?","opts":["Partial dominance; the AB combination shows incomplete penetrance","Linkage between the two genes; the 9:3:3:1 ratio is distorted because parental combinations are over-represented","Lethal allele; aabb individuals die","The ratio matches 9:3:3:1; no deviation is meaningful","Epistasis; the bb genotype is suppressed"],"correct":2,"why":"Expected 9:3:3:1 of 800 = 450:150:150:50. Observed 460/152/158/30. The aabb class is 30 (vs expected 50) - significantly fewer. This suggests homozygous-recessive (aabb) is partially lethal, while other classes match expectations.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1q9mvxk","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"Along one stretch of lagging-strand template 6,000 nucleotides long, Okazaki fragments average 200 nucleotides each, and every fragment begins with its own 10-nucleotide RNA primer. How many primers are laid down along that stretch, and once the primers have been replaced with DNA, how many nicks must ligase seal between neighboring fragments?","opts":["29 primers and 29 nicks","30 primers and 29 nicks","600 primers and 599 nicks","30 primers and 30 nicks","60 primers and 59 nicks"],"correct":1,"why":"Two separate counts here, and they are deliberately not the same number. Fragments equal 6,000 divided by 200, which is 30, and each fragment gets exactly one primer, so 30 primers. Junctions are always one fewer than the pieces they join, so 30 fragments meet at 29 nicks. Whenever a question hands you fragment length and asks how many seals, do the division, then subtract one.","traps":["This undercounts the primers, since all 30 fragments need one each even though only 29 junctions sit between them.",null,"This divides 6,000 by the 10-nucleotide primer length instead of the 200-nucleotide fragment length, counting a primer for every stretch the size of a primer.","The fragment count is right, but 30 fragments laid end to end meet at only 29 junctions, so one of those nicks does not exist.","Doubling both numbers is what happens if you count fragments on both new strands, and the leading strand across this stretch is one continuous piece."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"For the gas-phase reaction H2(g) + Cl2(g) -&gt; 2 HCl(g), use bond energies (kJ/mol): D(H - H) = 436, D(Cl - Cl) = 243, D(H - Cl) = 431. The bond-energy approximation gives ΔH = sum(bonds broken) − sum(bonds formed). What is ΔH for this reaction?","opts":["−183 kJ","+679 kJ","−92 kJ","+92 kJ","+183 kJ"],"correct":0,"why":"Bonds broken: 436 + 243 = 679 kJ. Bonds formed: 2 x 431 = 862 kJ. ΔH = 679 − 862 = −183 kJ. Forgetting to multiply H - Cl by 2 gives +248; reversing the formula gives +183.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:x7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A 0.10 M weak acid HA has Ka = 1×10⁻⁴. % dissociation?","opts":["0.032%","0.32%","3.2%","10%","32%"],"correct":2,"why":"x = √(Ka·C) = √(10⁻⁵) = 3.16×10⁻³. %diss = (3.16×10⁻³ / 0.10)×100 = 3.16% ≈ 3.2%.","traps":["That is the fraction dissociated written as though it were already a percent, so the factor of one hundred never got applied.","That comes from dividing the 3.2×10⁻³ by 1.0 M instead of by the 0.10 M actually given.",null,"Ten percent comes from taking the square root of Ka alone and leaving the concentration out of the root.","Those digits are ten times too large, which is the root of 10⁻³ rather than 10⁻⁵."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":3,"q":"Hydrogen cyanide HCN is a linear, sp-hybridized molecule with structure H-C≡N. The C≡N triple bond consists of one σ + two π bonds, while the H-C bond is a single σ bond. Count and identify the total number of σ and π bonds in HCN.","opts":["1 σ + 3 π","2 σ + 2 π","3 σ + 1 π","2 σ + 1 π","3 σ + 2 π"],"correct":1,"why":"H-C is a single bond (1 σ); C≡N is a triple bond (1 σ + 2 π). Totals: σ = 1 + 1 = 2; π = 0 + 2 = 2. Hence HCN has 2 σ bonds and 2 π bonds. Both C and N are sp hybridized in this linear molecule.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A reaction has ΔH = +40.0 kJ/mol and ΔS = +100. J/(mol·K), with both values approximately temperature-independent. Setting ΔG = ΔH − T·ΔS = 0 to find the crossover temperature above which the reaction becomes spontaneous, what is T_cross?","opts":["1000 K","400 K","300 K","500 K","200 K"],"correct":1,"why":"Set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS = 40.0 kJ / 0.100 kJ/K = 400 K. Above 400 K the reaction is spontaneous. Forgetting J-&gt;kJ conversion (using 100 directly) gives 0.4 K.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_p-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Consider the iso-electronic series N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, and Na⁺ - each species has exactly 10 electrons (Ne configuration). However, the proton counts differ: 7, 8, 9, and 11 respectively. With more protons attracting the same 10 electrons, the radius shrinks. Which species is the LARGEST?","opts":["F⁻ (Z = 9)","Na⁺ (Z = 11)","O²⁻ (Z = 8)","All four are the same size","N³⁻ (Z = 7)"],"correct":4,"why":"In an isoelectronic series, ionic radius decreases as nuclear charge (Z) increases because more protons pull the 10 e⁻ cloud tighter. N³⁻ has the fewest protons (Z = 7) and therefore the weakest pull, giving it the largest radius. Order: N³⁻ &gt; O²⁻ &gt; F⁻ &gt; Na⁺.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-dilution#1twugoq","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"How many mL of water must be ADDED to 50 mL of 3 M HCl to make a 0.6 M solution?","opts":["250 mL","200 mL","201 mL","300 mL","10 mL"],"correct":1,"why":"M1V1 = M2V2, so V2 = (3 × 50) / 0.6 = 250 mL total. Water added = 250 − 50 = 200 mL.","traps":["That is the FINAL volume. The question asks how much water you add, so subtract the volume you started with.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You added the starting volume on top of the final volume. M1V1 = M2V2 already gives the total.","You divided the volume. Diluting makes the volume bigger, not smaller."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A laboratory report adds three independently measured volumes: 1.234 mL (4 sig figs), 5.6 mL (2 sig figs), and 12.34 mL (4 sig figs). The calculator returns 19.174. Apply the addition rule (smallest decimal places) to round the sum to its proper precision.","opts":["19","19.17","19.174","19.2","19.20"],"correct":3,"why":"Decimal places: 1.234 (3), 5.6 (1), 12.34 (2). The sum keeps the smallest = 1 decimal place. 19.174 rounds to 19.2.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:nr3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A concentration cell uses Cu electrodes in [Cu²⁺] = 0.01 M (left) and 1.0 M (right). E_cell?","opts":["0.030 V","0.118 V","0.059 V","0","−0.059 V"],"correct":2,"why":"Concentration cell → E° = 0. The 1.0 M side becomes the cathode (Cu²⁺ wants to deposit), the 0.01 M side becomes the anode. E = (0.0592/n)·log([Cu²⁺]_cathode/[Cu²⁺]_anode) = (0.0592/2)·log(1.0/0.01) = 0.0296 × 2 = 0.0592 V ≈ 0.059 V.","traps":["0.030 V uses log(10) instead of log(100); the ratio here is 100, so the log is 2.","0.118 V leaves out the division by n: with 2 electrons the 0.0592 is halved before it multiplies log Q.",null,"Concentration cells DO produce voltage.","−0.059 V makes the dilute side the cathode; copper ions plate out on the concentrated side, so the cell voltage comes out positive."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_rl-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"A first-order decomposition has rate constant k = 0.0100 s^-1 at 25°C. A reactor begins with [A]0 = 1.00 M; we want to know how long until [A] drops to 0.125 M (1/8 of initial). Use ln([A]0/[A]) = kt or recognize three half-lives, then solve for t.","opts":["140 s","210 s","100 s","70 s","280 s"],"correct":1,"why":"1/8 = (1/2)^3 means three half-lives have passed. t1/2 = ln2/k = 0.693/0.010 = 69.3 s, so t = 3 x 69.3 = 208 s ~ 210 s. Equivalently ln(1/0.125)/0.01 = 208 s.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_qn7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A 350 nm UV photon strikes a sodium photocathode whose work function is phi = 2.28 eV. Using the photoelectric relation KE = E_photon - phi and the convenient formula E_photon (eV) ~ 1240/lambda(nm), calculate the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected photoelectron.","opts":["1.26 eV","0.50 eV","5.82 eV","3.54 eV","0 eV (photon below threshold)"],"correct":0,"why":"E_photon = 1240/350 = 3.54 eV. KE = 3.54 - 2.28 = 1.26 eV. Because E_photon &gt; phi, ejection occurs; the leftover energy becomes kinetic energy of the photoelectron.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#151b5a5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"What is the pH of a 1.0 × 10⁻² M solution of NaOH at 25 °C?","opts":["2","7","11","12","16"],"correct":3,"why":"Strong base: [OH⁻] = 10⁻² M, pOH = 2, pH = 14 − 2 = 12.","traps":["That is the pOH. NaOH gives you [OH⁻], so finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","You assumed neutral. A strong base is well above 7.","Exponent slip. For 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ the pOH is exactly n.",null,"You added instead of subtracting from 14. A pH that high is a signal to recheck."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_cal-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A bomb calorimeter with a calorimeter constant of C_cal = 7.5 kJ/°C burns a 0.500 g sample of an unknown sugar, raising the bomb's temperature by 4.00°C. Using q = C_cal · ΔT (mass cancels), calculate the energy released per gram of sample.","opts":["60 kJ/g","120 kJ/g","7.5 kJ/g","15 kJ/g","30 kJ/g"],"correct":0,"why":"Total q = 7.5 x 4.00 = 30.0 kJ. Per gram = 30.0/0.500 = 60 kJ/g. Bomb calorimeters use a known C_cal, not m·c, because the system has multiple heat-absorbing components.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:qn5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A photon ejects an electron from hydrogen (IE = 13.6 eV) with kinetic energy of 5.0 eV. Photon energy?","opts":["18.6 eV","5.0 eV","13.6 eV","15.2 eV","8.6 eV"],"correct":0,"why":"Photoelectric effect: E_photon = work function (IE) + KE_ejected = 13.6 + 5.0 = 18.6 eV.","traps":[null,"This is the kinetic energy on its own, before adding the 13.6 eV the photon spent freeing the electron.","This pays only the ionization energy and leaves nothing for the 5.0 eV the electron carries away.","This uses 10.2 eV, the cost of lifting the electron only to n=2, when setting it free costs the full 13.6 eV.","This subtracts the kinetic energy instead of adding it, and the photon pays the binding energy first with the leftover becoming speed."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-e16","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Ethane combusts in air according to the balanced equation 2 C₂H₆(g) + 7 O₂(g) → 4 CO₂(g) + 6 H₂O(g). Starting with exactly 1.00 mol of C₂H₆, use the mole ratio of O₂ : C₂H₆ from the balanced equation to determine how many moles of O₂ are required.","opts":["1.0","2.0","3.0","3.5","7.0"],"correct":3,"why":"Stoichiometric ratio = 7 mol O₂ / 2 mol C₂H₆ = 3.5. So 1.00 mol C₂H₆ needs 1.00 × 3.5 = 3.5 mol O₂. Forgetting to halve the ratio gives the 7.0 trap; reading 7:2 inverted gives 3.0 or 2.0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_b8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Molecular nitrogen (N2, MW 28, bond length 1.10 A, bond energy 945 kJ/mol) has 10 valence electrons. Filling the MO diagram (sigma_2s, sigma*_2s, pi_2p_x, pi_2p_y, sigma_2p_z) gives 8 bonding and 2 antibonding electrons. Compute its bond order using BO = (n_bonding - n_antibonding)/2.","opts":["1","1.5","2","3","4"],"correct":3,"why":"Bond order = (8 - 2)/2 = 3, consistent with the Lewis structure :N#N: showing a triple bond. The very high bond energy (945 kJ/mol) and short length (1.10 A) corroborate BO = 3.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ph-e11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"At 25 °C, 50.0 mL of 0.10 M HCl is mixed with 50.0 mL of 0.10 M NaOH in a beaker, producing 100.0 mL of solution containing only NaCl and water. Considering that HCl + NaOH -&gt; NaCl + H2O fully neutralizes (equimolar strong acid + strong base), what is the final pH?","opts":["1.0","3.0","7.0","10","13"],"correct":2,"why":"Equal moles of strong acid and strong base produce a salt of a strong acid and strong base (NaCl), which is pH-neutral. The resulting solution is pure NaCl(aq) at pH = 7.0 at 25 °C.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ek-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"For the gas-phase reaction A(g) ⇌ B(g) at 298 K, the equilibrium constant is K_1 = 5.0. The chemist now multiplies all stoichiometric coefficients by 2 to write 2A(g) ⇌ 2B(g) at the same temperature. What is the new equilibrium constant K_2?","opts":["2K_1 = 10","K_1/2 = 2.5","1/K_1 = 0.20","K_1 = 5.0","K_1^2 = 25"],"correct":4,"why":"Multiplying coefficients by n raises K to the n-th power: K_new = K_old^n. Here n = 2, so K_2 = (5.0)^2 = 25. Doubling K (giving 10) is the most common error.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A magnesium ribbon contains 6.02 × 10²² atoms of Mg. Atomic mass of Mg = 24.3 g/mol. First convert to moles using n = atoms/N_A (with N_A = 6.022 × 10²³), then to mass via m = n × MW.","opts":["2.43 g","0.0243 g","243 g","24.3 g","0.243 g"],"correct":0,"why":"n = 6.02 × 10²² / 6.022 × 10²³ = 0.100 mol. m = 0.100 × 24.3 = 2.43 g. The atom count is exactly 1/10 of Avogadro's number, so the answer is 1/10 of one molar mass.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-rate-order#7n26bk","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"For A + B, so products, the initial rates were measured:\n\nTrial 1: [A] = 0.10 M, [B] = 0.10 M, rate = 5 × 10⁻⁵ M/s\nTrial 2: [A] = 0.20 M, [B] = 0.10 M, rate = 20 × 10⁻⁵ M/s\nTrial 3: [A] = 0.20 M, [B] = 0.20 M, rate = 80 × 10⁻⁵ M/s\n\nWhat is the OVERALL reaction order?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":4,"why":"Trials 1 to 2 double [A] only: rate ×4, so order in A = 2. Trials 2 to 3 double [B] only: rate ×4, so order in B = 2. Overall = 2 + 2 = 4.","traps":["Compare two trials where only ONE concentration changes. Rate ×2 means first order, ×4 means second order, no change means zero order. Then add the orders.","Compare two trials where only ONE concentration changes. Rate ×2 means first order, ×4 means second order, no change means zero order. Then add the orders.","That is the order in A only. The overall order adds the order in B.","Compare two trials where only ONE concentration changes. Rate ×2 means first order, ×4 means second order, no change means zero order. Then add the orders.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-gibbs#148vz11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a reaction, ΔH = -80 kJ and ΔS = 200 J/K. What is ΔG at 400 K?","opts":["-80 kJ","-160 kJ","-80100 kJ","-80.2 kJ","0 kJ"],"correct":1,"why":"TΔS = 400 × 200 J/K = 80 kJ. ΔG = -80 − (80) = -160 kJ, so the reaction is spontaneous at this temperature.","traps":["You dropped ΔH. Both terms matter.",null,"Unit trap. ΔS is in J/K but ΔH is in kJ. Divide TΔS by 1000 before subtracting.","You forgot to multiply ΔS by the temperature.","Sign slip. ΔG = ΔH MINUS TΔS."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#1u8ztlk","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻³ M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["2","2.3","11","11.7","12"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻³ = 1.0 × 10⁻² M. pOH = 2, pH = 14 − 2 = 12.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#vr8i0f","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 0.5 m aqueous solution of KBr? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-0.512 °C","1.86 °C","-2.79 °C","-0.93 °C","-1.86 °C"],"correct":4,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (2)(1.86)(0.5) = 1.86 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 1.86 = -1.86 °C.","traps":["You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C.","You counted one ion too many. Write the dissociation out and count the particles.","You forgot the van't Hoff factor. KBr splits into 2 ions, so i = 2.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Acetylene (H−C≡C−H) is the linear hydrocarbon used in welding torches that produces flames near 3100°C in oxygen. Counting the C≡C triple bond and both C−H bonds, how many π bonds are present in one molecule of acetylene?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"why":"Each triple bond consists of one σ + two π bonds, while each single bond is purely σ. Acetylene therefore has 3 σ bonds (2 C-H + 1 C-C σ) and 2 π bonds, both within the C≡C unit. The carbons are sp hybridized, leaving two perpendicular 2p orbitals to overlap into the two π bonds.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A 0.20 m aqueous KCl solution (i = 2 for full dissociation) has Kb = 0.52°C·kg/mol. Pure water boils at 100.00°C at 1 atm. Using ΔTb = i·Kb·m, what is the boiling point of this solution?","opts":["100.10°C","100.52°C","100.21°C","100.00°C","101.04°C"],"correct":2,"why":"ΔTb = (2)(0.52)(0.20) = 0.208°C ≈ 0.21°C. New Tb = 100.00 + 0.21 = 100.21°C. Ignoring i = 2 gives 100.10; using i = 3 gives 100.31; the 1 m result (100.52) is the wrong-molality distractor.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_xp009","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"10.0 g of N₂ and 4.00 g of H₂ are placed in a sealed reactor and heated until the reaction N₂(g) + 3 H₂(g) → 2 NH₃(g) goes to completion. Identify the limiting reagent and calculate the maximum mass of NH₃ formed.","opts":["N₂ limits; 12.1 g NH₃","H₂ limits; 22.4 g NH₃","Both consumed simultaneously; 14.0 g NH₃","N₂ limits; 17.0 g NH₃","H₂ limits; 11.3 g NH₃"],"correct":0,"why":"mol N₂ = 10/28 = 0.357. mol H₂ = 4/2 = 2.00. Stoichiometric ratio H₂:N₂ = 3:1, so H₂ needed for all N₂ = 3 × 0.357 = 1.07 mol &lt; 2.00 available. N₂ limits, so mol NH₃ = 2 × 0.357 = 0.714, so mass = 0.714 × 17 = 12.1 g.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lf005","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A 5.00 L flask contains 0.250 mol N2 and 0.500 mol O2 at 27°C. Assuming ideal gas behavior, what is the partial pressure of O2 in atm?","opts":["3.69 atm","12.3 atm","2.46 atm","4.92 atm","1.23 atm"],"correct":2,"why":"P(O2) = n(O2)·R·T / V = (0.500)(0.08206)(300) / 5.00 = 2.46 atm. Or use mole fraction: x(O2)·P_total = (2/3)(3.69) ≈ 2.46.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A physiology buffer contains 0.10 M HA and 0.20 M A- in 1.0 L of solution (pKa = 4.74). The buffer capacity for added strong acid is limited by the moles of A- available before all conjugate base is converted back to HA. Approximately how many mol/L of strong acid (H+) can be added before the buffer is exhausted?","opts":["~1.0 mol/L","~0.10 mol/L","~0.05 mol/L","~0.30 mol/L","~0.20 mol/L"],"correct":4,"why":"Each mole of added H+ converts one mole of A- to HA. Buffering ends when [A-] reaches zero, so the maximum H+ that can be neutralized equals the initial moles of A-, ~0.20 mol/L. Beyond that, pH crashes.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1lxw3py","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 2 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 127 °C and 1 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["98.5 L","32.8 L","6650 L","65.7 L","20.9 L"],"correct":3,"why":"T = 127 + 273 = 400 K. V = nRT / P = (2)(0.0821)(400) / 1 = 65.7 L.","traps":["Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You dropped the moles. n belongs on top with R and T.","You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.",null,"You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nr-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"A copper electrode is dipped into 1.00 x 10^-3 M Cu^2+ at 25°C and connected to a standard reference. For the half-reaction Cu^2+ + 2 e- -&gt; Cu(s), E° = +0.34 V and n = 2. Apply E = E° - (0.0592/n)·log(1/[Cu^2+]) to compute the actual electrode potential E.","opts":["+0.43 V","0 V","+0.34 V","+0.25 V","+0.16 V"],"correct":3,"why":"Q = 1/[Cu^2+] = 1/0.001 = 1000, log Q = 3. E = 0.34 - (0.0592/2)(3) = 0.34 - 0.0888 = 0.2512 ~ 0.25 V. Lower [Cu^2+] makes reduction less favorable, so E &lt; E°.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-molecular-formula#13jl0t8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A compound has the empirical formula CH₂O and a molar mass of 60 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?","opts":["CH₂O","C₂H₄O₂","C₃H₆O₃","C₄H₈O₄","C₅H₁₀O₅"],"correct":1,"why":"Empirical mass = 30. 60 / 30 = 2, so multiply every subscript by 2: C₂H₄O₂.","traps":["That is the empirical formula again. Divide the molar mass by the empirical mass to get the multiplier.",null,"Multiplier is one too high. Molar mass divided by empirical mass must come out to a whole number.","You doubled the multiplier. Check the empirical formula mass.","Wrong multiplier. Molar mass divided by empirical mass gives it exactly."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-1lfvsj4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Salicylaldehyde (shown) has an intramolecular hydrogen bond between its phenol O-H and the adjacent aldehyde oxygen. Compared with the IR of a simple phenol, what is the expected effect of this intramolecular hydrogen bonding on the O-H stretch?","opts":["The O-H stretch becomes a sharp doublet, much like the two N-H bands of a primary aromatic amine","The O-H stretch sharpens and shifts to HIGHER wavenumber (above 3600 cm-1)","The O-H stretch disappears entirely from the spectrum once the internal hydrogen bond forms","The O-H band moves to ~2250 cm-1, overlapping the nitrile region","The O-H stretch is broadened and shifted to LOWER wavenumber (below ~3200 cm-1)"],"correct":4,"why":"Hydrogen bonding weakens the O-H bond, lowering its frequency and broadening the band. The intramolecular H-bond in salicylaldehyde persists even when dilute, so O-H is broad and shifted to lower wavenumber vs a free O-H (~3600).","traps":null,"qs":"O=Cc1ccccc1O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-10sxo6i","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Pentan-2-yltrimethylammonium hydroxide (drawn) is heated to give Hofmann elimination. Considering both regiochemistry (Hofmann orientation) and the favored geometry, which is the MAJOR alkene product?","opts":["CC=C(C)C","CC/C=C/C","CCCCC","CC/C=C\\C","C=CCCC"],"correct":4,"why":"Hofmann elimination favors the LESS substituted (terminal) alkene because of the bulky NMe3+ leaving group and the more accessible/acidic terminal protons. Deprotonating the C1 methyl gives 1-pentene (C=CCCC), the major product, rather than the more substituted internal 2-pentene that Zaitsev would predict.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C.[OH-]","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1og4kez","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A brominated compound (C3H7Br) shows TWO 1H NMR signals: a doublet integrating for 6H near 1.7 ppm and a septet integrating for 1H near 4.2 ppm. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","opts":["CCCBr","CCCCBr","CC(C)Br","CCBr","CC(C)CBr"],"correct":2,"why":"Two signals from C3H7Br with a 6H doublet and a 1H septet = 2-bromopropane: two equivalent CH3 coupled to the single CHBr (doublet), and the CHBr split by 6 neighbors into a septet, deshielded to ~4.2 by Br. 1-Bromopropane gives THREE signals (triplet/sextet/triplet); 1-bromobutane and isobutyl bromide are C4H9Br; bromoethane is C2H5Br.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-11r58ig","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"2,2-Dimethylpropan-1-ol (neopentyl alcohol, shown) is treated with concentrated HBr. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)=CC","CC(C)(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CBr"],"correct":3,"why":"Neopentyl alcohol with HBr cannot easily do SN2 (hindered) or form a primary cation, so a 1,2-methyl shift gives a stable tertiary cation; bromide traps it to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (option A). option B is the non-rearranged neopentyl bromide, option C a different rearranged skeleton, option D an alkene, option E the chloride. Neopentyl rearrangement is a classic textbook example.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)CO","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-pnq96k","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"trans-3-Hexene is subjected to ozonolysis with an OXIDATIVE workup (O3, then H2O2). What are the organic products?","opts":["CCC(=O)CC","CCC(=O)O","CCC=O","CC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"Cleavage of the symmetric internal alkene gives two identical 3-carbon CH fragments; oxidative (H2O2) workup carries each aldehyde on to a carboxylic acid, giving two equivalents of propanoic acid. Propanal is the reductive-workup answer; the E geometry is irrelevant since the sp2 carbons are destroyed.","traps":null,"qs":"CC/C=C/CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17kba84","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"3-Hexyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2, which adds anti across the triple bond. Which structure is the major product, including geometry?","opts":["CCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)CC","CCCC(Br)(Br)CC","CCC(Br)C(Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(\\Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(/Br)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"Anti Br2 addition places the two bromines trans. On each internal carbon Br&gt;ethyl, so trans-Br =&gt; E. Others are over-brominated or saturated wrong skeletons. (E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene is the answer.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC#CCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-11po0a5","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"(E)-Pent-3-en-2-one is treated with KCN (a soft nucleophile) and then aqueous acid. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CCCC(C)=O","CCC(C#N)CC=O","CC(C#N)CC(C)=O","CC(=O)C(C)CC#N","C/C=C/C(C)(O)C#N"],"correct":2,"why":"Cyanide is a soft nucleophile and adds 1,4. CN bonds to the beta carbon (C4, bearing the terminal CH3) giving CH3-CO-CH2-CH(CN)-CH3 = option 0 (verified: carbonyl-&gt;nitrile bond distance = 3, i.e. CN on beta; formula C6H9NO = stem + HCN). Option 1 is the 1,2-cyanohydrin (disfavored for soft CN, retains alkene). Option 2 is a wrong skeleton (CN at gamma/alpha-side). Option 3 is a rearranged aldehyde. Option 4 is simple reduction with no nitrile.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/C(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1s6azf9","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"m-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene, drawn) undergoes Friedel-Crafts acylation with CH3COCl/AlCl3. Both methyls direct ortho/para, and the position between them is activated by both. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["Cc1ccc(C(C)O)c(C)c1","Cc1ccc(C(C)=O)c(C)c1","Cc1cccc(C)c1C(C)=O","Cc1cccc(C)c1CC(C)=O","Cc1cc(C(C)=O)cc(C)c1"],"correct":1,"why":"option B is the blocked between-methyls product, option C is meta to both, option E has a CH2-inserted skeleton, and option 5 is an alcohol not a ketone.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-xzfj6l","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with (1) Hg(OAc)2, H2O then (2) NaBH4. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["OCC(C)(C)CC","CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(O)(C)C(C)C","OCC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Oxymercuration-demercuration is Markovnikov with NO rearrangement because the bridged mercurinium ion blocks a free cation. OH adds to the more-substituted alkene carbon, leaving the t-butyl group intact, giving 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol (CC(O)C(C)(C)C). option B is the rearranged product you would only get under acid (the trap); option C is anti-Markovnikov; option D is a diol; option E is the wrong skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"own-organic:ace-0010","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2 in dichloromethane. Where do the two bromines end up?","opts":["On opposite faces only if the flask is heated","On the same face of the ring","On opposite faces of the ring","One bromine adds and the other stays in solution","Both attached to the same carbon"],"correct":2,"why":"The first bromine closes into a three-membered bridge across one face of the ring, so the only face still open when the second bromine arrives is the other one. Anti addition is not a rule to memorize, it is what the bridge leaves room for.","traps":null,"qs":"C1=CCCC1","os":false,"src":"own-organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1rhxjv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3CH2Br; (3) H2, Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the final major product?","opts":["C/C=C\\CCCCC","CCC#CCCCC","CC/C=C\\CCCC","CC/C=C/CCCC","CCCCCCCC"],"correct":2,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the C6 acetylide with ethyl (C2) to give 3-octyne; step 3 (Lindlar) does syn reduction to cis (Z)-3-octene. Trans needs Na/NH3; octane is over-reduced; [3] is the alkyne (skipped Lindlar); [4] is cis-2-octene (wrong position).","traps":null,"qs":"C#CCCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-0a1ca31176","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Which of these acids has the lowest pKa?","opts":["2-chlorobutanoic acid","They are all equally strong","4-chlorobutanoic acid","Butanoic acid","3-chlorobutanoic acid"],"correct":0,"why":"The inductive effect works through sigma bonds and drops off with every bond it has to cross. Put the chlorine on the carbon next to the carboxyl group and it stabilizes the carboxylate the most.","traps":[null,"The inductive effect depends on distance, so position matters.","The inductive effect fades quickly with distance. This chlorine is too far away to help much.","With no electron-withdrawing group this is the weakest of the four acids.","The chlorine is one carbon further from the carboxyl group, so its pull is weaker."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-1cv6f2u","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: (E)-1-phenylpropene (trans-beta-methylstyrene) is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O. Which set of products forms?","opts":["CC(=O)O.O=C(O)c1ccccc1","C=O.CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","C=O.O=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the internal C=C. The phenyl-bearing carbon becomes benzaldehyde and the methyl-bearing carbon becomes acetaldehyde.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-18i1svf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"1-Cyclobutylethan-1-ol is treated with acid. A ring-expansion (alkyl) shift relieves ring strain before elimination. Which is the major alkene product?","opts":["CC1(O)CCCC1","CC=C1CCC1","C=CC1CCC1","C1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"The initial secondary cation on the exocyclic carbon triggers a ring-bond migration that expands cyclobutane to a 1-methylcyclopentyl (tertiary) cation, relieving strain. Zaitsev E1 gives 1-methylcyclopentene (option A). option B/option C are unexpanded cyclobutane alkenes, option D is a substitution (cyclopentanol) product, option E (cyclohexene) has the wrong ring size. Single correct answer.","traps":null,"qs":"OC(C)C1CCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-sm043k","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (drawn), then NaOH/H2O, then H3O+/heat. What is the major product of this intramolecular dialkylation?","opts":["OC(=O)C1(C(=O)O)CCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCC1","OC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Both alpha-C-H positions are alkylated by the two ends of the dibromide, closing a ring; malonate carbon plus four CH2 units form a five-membered ring; after hydrolysis/decarboxylation one COOH remains = cyclopentanecarboxylic acid. opt[0] matches. Distractors: cyclobutane (miscount), cyclohexane (extra carbon), gem-diacid (no decarboxylation), open-chain adipic acid (no cyclization).","traps":null,"qs":"BrCCCCBr","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-j76r4f","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"3-chloro-3-methyl-1-butene (drawn, a tertiary allylic chloride) is solvolyzed in water. Besides the unrearranged tertiary alcohol, a rearranged constitutional isomer also forms. Which structure is that rearranged product?","opts":["C=CC(C)(C)O","C=CC(C)(C)Cl","C=C(C)CCO","OCC=C(C)C","OCC=CC(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Ionization gives an allyl cation delocalized over tertiary C3 and primary C1. Water capture at C1 gives the rearranged 3-methyl-2-buten-1-ol (prenol) = option A (formula C5H10O, matches substrate-derived product). option B is the unrearranged tertiary alcohol (named in stem), option C a non-arising isomer, option E the starting chloride.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)(C)Cl","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-75v5a0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanoyl chloride (drawn) is treated with methanol. The bond to the stereocenter is never broken. What is the major product?","opts":["COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Nucleophilic acyl substitution occurs only at the carbonyl carbon; the alpha stereocenter is a spectator, so configuration is retained. Recognition-level stereo, in DAT scope (no mechanism drawn).","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(Cl)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1vj81jk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in the first step of an acetoacetic ester synthesis. Which species correctly represents the stabilized nucleophile generated, and why is that site deprotonated?","opts":["CCOC(=O)CC([CH2-])=O","[CH2-]COC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","CC[O-]","CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Ethoxide removes the most acidic proton on the central methylene flanked by both carbonyls (pKa ~11), giving a doubly resonance-stabilized enolate, option A (CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O). The terminal acetyl carbanion (B) is stabilized by only one carbonyl; ethoxide (C) is the base not the formed nucleophile; the OEt carbon (D) is not acidic; E shows no reaction.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gnnsax","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The molecule drawn is the cyclopropenyl (cyclopropenium) cation. Apply Huckel's rule: count the pi electrons and classify the ring.","opts":["2 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=0); aromatic","6 pi electrons; aromatic","2 pi electrons; antiaromatic","4 pi electrons (4n with n=1); antiaromatic","0 pi electrons; non-aromatic"],"correct":0,"why":"One C=C provides 2 pi electrons and the cationic carbon is an empty p orbital completing cyclic overlap. 2 pi electrons = 4n+2 with n=0 -&gt; aromatic, the textbook reason this tiny cation is unusually stable. Option 0 is correct; the 4-, 0-, and 6-electron counts are wrong, and 2 pi electrons cannot be antiaromatic.","traps":null,"qs":"[CH+]1C=C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-129frs5","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Succinic anhydride (the cyclic anhydride shown) is treated with exactly 1 equivalent of methanol. Which structure is the product?","opts":["O=C(O)CCC(=O)O","O=C1CCC(=O)O1","COC(=O)CCC(=O)O","COC(=O)CC(=O)O","COC(=O)CCC(=O)OC"],"correct":2,"why":"The diester (2 eq), succinic diacid (water not methanol), unopened ring, and the shorter malonate monoester (wrong chain length) are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CCC(=O)O1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-13b7fabf1b","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"Which base is strong enough to deprotonate a terminal alkyne (pKa about 25) essentially completely?","opts":["Sodium amide, NaNH2 (the pKa of ammonia is about 38)","Sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO3 (the pKa of carbonic acid is about 6)","Sodium hydroxide, NaOH (the pKa of water is about 16)","Sodium ethoxide, NaOEt (the pKa of ethanol is about 16)","Sodium acetate (the pKa of acetic acid is about 5)"],"correct":0,"why":"Equilibrium favors the side with the WEAKER acid, the one with the higher pKa. Cross out the metal to find the real base. Amide ion gives ammonia at pKa 38, which is weaker than the alkyne at 25, so the reaction goes forward. Hydroxide would give water at 16, which is stronger than the alkyne, so it does not.","traps":[null,"Bicarbonate is a very weak base. It is nowhere near strong enough.","Water is a STRONGER acid than the alkyne, so the equilibrium would favor the alkyne and hydroxide.","Ethanol is a stronger acid than the alkyne, so ethoxide cannot pull that proton off to any useful extent.","Acetate is a very weak base."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-14tudr3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"The substrate shown is 1,5-hexadien-3-ol. Heating its alkoxide (anionic oxy-Cope) gives, after tautomerization, an unsaturated carbonyl compound. Which reaction class and product feature apply?","opts":["Electrocyclic ring closure giving a cyclohexenol with the remaining alkene inside the new ring","[3,3]-sigmatropic (oxy-Cope) giving a delta,epsilon-unsaturated aldehyde/ketone (5-hexenal)","Claisen rearrangement giving an allyl phenol","Retro-aldol giving two carbonyl fragments","[1,5]-H shift across the pi system, giving a fully conjugated dienol that keeps its oxygen as an enol"],"correct":1,"why":"This 1,5-diene with OH at C3 is the oxy-Cope substrate. The [3,3]-sigmatropic Cope shift forms a C1-C6 bond, breaks C3-C4, and generates an enol that tautomerizes to 5-hexenal (OHC-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH=CH2), confirmed isomeric to the substrate (C6H10O). The carbonyl is C1 and the alkene C5=C6 sits delta,epsilon to it, matching the keyed option. The OH-bearing terminus drives the irreversible enol-to-carbonyl step. No ring forms and no aromatic ether is present, ruling out electrocyclic and Claisen.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCC(O)C=C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1k3iorm","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"On a silica-gel TLC plate developed in a nonpolar solvent, four compounds are spotted: octanoic acid (carboxylic acid), octylamine (the amine drawn), 1-octanol (alcohol), and octane (alkane). Which compound is expected to travel the FARTHEST up the plate (highest Rf)?","opts":["All four travel equally","Octanoic acid (the carboxylic acid)","Octane (the alkane)","1-Octanol (the alcohol)","Octylamine (the amine)"],"correct":2,"why":"On polar silica, the least polar compound interacts least with the stationary phase and moves with the nonpolar mobile phase, giving the highest Rf; nonpolar octane wins over the polar acid, amine, and alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCCCCCN","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-19rdwte","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"2,3-dichlorobutane is drawn below. This molecule has two stereocenters, but one of its stereoisomers is a meso compound. Taking the meso coincidence into account, how many DISTINCT (different) stereoisomers actually exist for 2,3-dichlorobutane?","opts":["1 (only the meso form exists)","3 (a pair of enantiomers plus one meso compound)","2 (a single pair of enantiomers, because the meso form is one member of that pair)","4 (the full 2^n with no overlap)","6 (2^n for the two stereocenters, then doubled again for cis and trans forms)"],"correct":1,"why":"Two stereocenters give a 2^n upper bound of 4, but because both stereocenters carry identical substituent sets the (R,S) and (S,R) forms are the same achiral meso compound. That overlap removes one isomer, leaving 3 distinct stereoisomers: the (R,R)/(S,S) enantiomeric pair plus the single meso (R,S). Classic 2^n meso exception.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ym5nrn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Propene is treated with HBr in the presence of organic peroxides (ROOR). What is the major product?","opts":["CCCBr","CC(Br)CBr","CCCO","C=CCBr","CC(Br)C"],"correct":0,"why":"HBr/peroxides is a radical chain (anti-Markovnikov) addition. Br radical adds to the less substituted carbon (forms the more stable secondary radical), so Br ends on the terminal carbon: 1-bromopropane (opt[0]). opt[1] is the Markovnikov product (ionic HBr), opt[2] is the Br2 dibromide, opt[3] is allyl bromide (allylic substitution), opt[4] is an alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-19ll1h0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"The chiral epoxide (R)-2-methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is opened by sodium azide (NaN3, basic conditions). Considering BOTH the regiochemistry of basic opening AND the stereochemistry of the SN2 step, which statement is correct about the carbon that the azide attacks?","opts":["Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon under basic conditions, and backside attack at that carbon inverts the configuration of the original stereocenter","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon with retention of configuration","Azide attacks the less-substituted CH2 carbon, and the backside displacement inverts the configuration at the neighboring stereocenter as the ring opens","Azide attacks the less-substituted CH2 carbon, which is not a stereocenter, so the original stereocenter is untouched and retains its configuration","Azide attacks both carbons, racemizing the product"],"correct":3,"why":"Under basic conditions azide does SN2 at the less-substituted terminal CH2. That carbon is not a stereocenter, so SN2 inversion there has no stereochemical consequence; the methyl-bearing stereocenter is never bonded to the nucleophile and retains its configuration. Layers basic-condition regioselectivity with the recognition that inversion only matters at the carbon actually attacked. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CO1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-zlk3b3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione is treated with NaOH and warmed (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1=CC(=O)CCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC(=O)C1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Intramolecular aldol closing the favored six-membered ring then dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (C7H10O). Keyed option A is the conjugated enone (verified identical to O=C1CCCC(C)=C1). Distractors: a non-conjugated methylcyclohexenone regioisomer, plain cyclohexanone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and a 5-membered ring. All valid, unique.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-j7pele","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"cis-1-Bromo-4-tert-butylcyclohexane reacts with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CCC=CC1","C=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCCC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CCOC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"tBu locks equatorial; in the cis isomer Br is forced axial, the geometry E2 needs (anti-periplanar axial beta-H on C2/C6). Strong base on a secondary axial halide gives fast E2 to 4-tert-butylcyclohexene (option A). Alcohol/ether are minor substitution; exocyclic methylene cannot form; alkane is reduction.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](Br)CC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1vz42k1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"5-Hydroxypentanal (drawn) is dissolved in dilute acid, where it equilibrates with its cyclic form. Predict the structure of the major cyclic product.","opts":["O=CCCCCO","OC1CCCCO1","O=C1CCCCO1","OC1CCCO1","OCCCCCO"],"correct":1,"why":"The hydroxyl oxygen adds intramolecularly to the aldehyde to form a cyclic hemiacetal (lactol). The chain length gives the favored six-membered ring; the five-membered ring is the wrong ring size; the lactone is an oxidation product, the diol is a reduction product, and the open-chain hydroxy aldehyde is the starting material.","traps":null,"qs":"O=CCCCCO","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-mnw0d4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic). The two new oxygens add to the same face of the ring. Which structure is the product?","opts":["O=C1CCCC1O","OC1CCCC1","C1CC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O"],"correct":4,"why":"Cold dilute KMnO4 performs syn-dihydroxylation; same-face addition on the ring gives the cis-1,2-diol (meso cis-cyclopentane-1,2-diol), opt 0. C is the epoxide (peracid product), D is a mono-ol, E is an over-oxidized alpha-hydroxy ketone.","traps":null,"qs":"C1=CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#rqbqco","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the SUM of the solutions of x² + x − 20 = 0?","opts":["−1","−20","9","1","20"],"correct":0,"why":"It factors as (x − 4)(x + 5), so the roots are 4 and −5. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = −1, product = c/a = −20.","traps":[null,"That is the PRODUCT of the roots (c/a). The sum is −b/a.","That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their sum.","Sign slip. The sum of the roots is the OPPOSITE of the x coefficient: −b/a.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1502","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"The sum of a number and 2 times the number is 6. Find the number.","opts":["1","2","3","4","6"],"correct":1,"why":"A number plus 2 times the number is 3 of them, because the number itself counts as one. That gives 3x = 6 and x = 2. Count the pieces first, then divide.","traps":["One plus twice 1 comes to only 3, half of the 6 the stem asks for.",null,"Divided by 2 instead of 3; the number itself is one of the three pieces.","That is 2 times the number, the second piece of the sum, rather than the number.","That is the total from the stem, copied down before the 6 was split three ways."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1ntn2bl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"Two sides of a triangle measure 5 and 7, and the angle between them is 60 degrees. How long is the third side?","opts":["√39, about 6.2","12","√109, about 10.4","2","√74, about 8.6"],"correct":0,"why":"The Law of Cosines gives the third side squared as 25 plus 49 minus 2 times 5 times 7 times cos 60. Cos 60 is 1/2, so the subtracted piece is 70 times 1/2, which is 35, leaving 74 minus 35, or 39, and the side is √39, about 6.2. A 60 degree angle is narrower than a right angle, so the third side has to come out shorter than the √74 a right angle would give, which checks the sign of that last term for you.","traps":[null,"This adds the two given sides, a length the third side can never reach.","This adds the 35 rather than subtracting it, which is what a 120 degree angle would produce.","This multiplies by 1 instead of by cos 60, leaving 74 minus 70.","This drops the cosine term entirely and treats the triangle as a right triangle."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp006","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A solution that is 25% acid by volume is mixed with a solution that is 60% acid. To produce 35 liters of a final solution that is 40% acid, how many liters of the 25% solution must be used?","opts":["15 L","25 L","10 L","12 L","20 L"],"correct":4,"why":"Let x = L of 25%, then 35 − x = L of 60%. 0.25x + 0.60(35 − x) = 0.40(35). 0.25x + 21 − 0.60x = 14. −0.35x = −7. x = 20 L.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1392","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One assistant can stock a supply room in 5 hours and another can stock it in 8 hours. Working together, how many hours does the job take?","opts":["3.08","5","6.15","6.5","13"],"correct":0,"why":"1/T = 1/5 + 1/8 = 13/40. T = 40/13 = 3.08 hr.","traps":[null,"Reporting the faster worker's solo time; the help pulls it lower still.","Using 2ab/(a+b), the average speed shortcut, where shared work calls for ab/(a+b).","Averaging the two times instead of combining the rates.","Adding the two times; together they finish sooner than either one alone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1736","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"Which values of x satisfy |x − 1| = 1?","opts":["1 or -1","0 or -2","0 or 0","2","2 or 0"],"correct":4,"why":"|x − 1| = 1 means x − 1 = ±1, so x = 2 or 0.","traps":["You set x equal to plus or minus 1 and never undid the minus 1 inside the bars.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","That keeps the branch x - 1 = -1 twice and drops the branch that gives 2.","You solved x - 1 = 1 and stopped, and the branch x - 1 = -1 gives 0 as well.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1479","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A rinse cycle runs for 3.4 minutes. How many seconds is that?","opts":["0.06","20.4","63.4","184","204"],"correct":4,"why":"1 min = 60 sec, so 3.4 min × 60 = 204 sec.","traps":["You divided the 3.4 by 60 instead of multiplying, which sends the count the wrong way.","You multiplied by 6 instead of 60, dropping a zero.","You added 60 to the 3.4 instead of multiplying the 3.4 by it.","You read 3.4 minutes as 3 minutes and 4 seconds; the 0.4 is four tenths of a minute, which is 24 seconds.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#4jxc6","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 72 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["20 m/s","0.02 m/s","259 m/s","72000 m/s","1200 m/s"],"correct":0,"why":"72 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 20 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":[null,"That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.","That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1286","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"A first aid cabinet holds 3 bandage rolls and 8 sterile pads. How many items are in 2 identical cabinets?","opts":["11","14","20","22","24"],"correct":3,"why":"Distribute: 2·3 + 2·8 = 6 + 16 = 22.","traps":["That is just the 3 + 8 inside the parentheses, so the 2 outside never got multiplied in.","Distributing the 2 only to the 3 gives 6 + 8 = 14, leaving the 8 unmultiplied.","That is 2 times 10, so the sum inside came up one short of 3 + 8 = 11.",null,"That is 2 times 12, so the sum inside was read one too high."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1698","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A line passes through (12,-7) and (13,-2). What is its slope?","opts":["-9","-5","0.2","5","6"],"correct":3,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = 5/1 = 5.","traps":["-9 comes from writing -2 minus 7 for the rise instead of -2 minus -7, over a run of 1.","-5 has the right size and the wrong sign, since y rises from -7 to -2.","0.2 divides the run by the rise, 1 over 5, which is the slope upside down.",null,"6 adds the change in y and the change in x instead of dividing them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:men1231","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A box holds 32 masks, and a clinic opens 3 boxes. How many masks is that?","opts":["86","90","96","106","196"],"correct":2,"why":"(30 + 2) × 3 = 90 + 6","traps":["That is ten light, since 90 plus 6 is 96.","That is 30 times 3, which leaves the 2 times 3 still to add.",null,"That is ten over, since 30 times 3 is 90 and 2 times 3 is 6.","An extra hundred appeared in the carry, since 90 plus 6 is 96."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1598","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"How many real roots does 2x² = 0 have?","opts":["one real and one imaginary root","one real root","two real roots","no real roots","two imaginary roots"],"correct":1,"why":"Δ = b² − 4ac = 0 − 4·2·0 = 0. = 0, so one.","traps":["Complex roots arrive as a pair, so a single imaginary root cannot appear.",null,"2x² = 0 collapses to the single point x = 0, so the two roots sit on top of each other.","x = 0 does satisfy 2x² = 0, so there is a real root here.","Imaginary roots need a negative discriminant, and this one is zero."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1378","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A boat runs at 30 miles per hour for 3.5 hours. How many miles does it travel?","opts":["8.57","75","90","105","135"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 30 × 3.5 = 105 mi.","traps":["Dividing 30 by 3.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 2.5 hours instead of 3.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 3 hours flat.",null,"Using 4.5 hours instead of the 3.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:sk5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-14","topic":"Test-day execution","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"You're 30 min in, 25 of 40 questions done, no skips yet. Pace check?","opts":["On track - about 1.2 min/q","On track - about 0.83 min/q","Behind - should be at 28","Ahead - slow down","Too many skipped"],"correct":0,"why":"30 min / 25 questions = 1.2 min/q. DAT QR pace is roughly 1.13 min/q (45 min / 40). Slightly slow but within margin if remaining problems are easier.","traps":[null,"This flips the division: 25 questions over 30 minutes is 0.83 questions per minute, not minutes per question.","25 in 30 min IS the right zone for sustainable pace.","Slowing down from here would leave the last 15 questions with less time than they need.","Zero skips isn't the worry - pace is."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#af82js","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (2 × 10¹)(6 × 10⁵) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["1.2 × 10⁵","1.2 × 10⁷","12 × 10⁶","8 × 10⁷","1.2 × 10⁶"],"correct":1,"why":"2 × 6 = 12 and 10¹ × 10⁵ = 10⁶. 12 × 10⁶ is not proper form, so write 1.2 × 10⁷. Answer: 1.2 × 10⁷.","traps":["The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.",null,"Right size, wrong form. In proper scientific notation the front number sits between 1 and 10.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1414","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A technician combines 300 mL of a 20% alcohol solution with 100 mL of a 5% alcohol solution. What is the percent concentration of the mixture?","opts":["12.5","16.25","21.67","50","65"],"correct":1,"why":"Solute total = 60 + 5 = 65 mL in 400 mL → 16.25%.","traps":["Averaged the two percents without weighting them by how much of each solution went in.",null,"Divided the 65 mL of solute by the original 300 mL instead of by the new 400 mL total.","Took 12.5 percent, the plain average of the two strengths, of the 400 mL total, which gives a volume rather than a concentration.","That is the total milliliters of solute, not the percent of the mixture."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1026","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A storeroom holds 6/15 of a pallet of gloves and 2/5 of a pallet of masks. How many pallets of stock is that?","opts":["8/15","12/75","4/5","8/20","1"],"correct":2,"why":"6/15 reduces to 2/5, so this is two fifths plus two fifths, which is 4/5. Spotting that the two fractions are the same size saves the whole conversion.","traps":["You put 2/5 over 15 but left the 2 on top; two fifths is 6/15.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.",null,"You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 8 over 20.","You reduced 6/15 to 3/5 instead of 2/5, which pushes the total up to a whole."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pe3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is the value of 10 − 3 × 2² + 1?","opts":["−1","5","11","17","−2"],"correct":0,"why":"2² = 4. 3 × 4 = 12. 10 − 12 + 1 = −1.","traps":[null,"You used 3 times 2 = 6 instead of squaring the 2 first.","Taking 12 away from 10 has to cross zero, so a value above ten means that product never got subtracted.","You read the minus in front of the 3 as a plus and skipped the square as well: 10 + 6 + 1.","You did the hard part right and stopped one step short of adding the final 1."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:le7","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies 5(2x − 4) = 30?","opts":["x = 2","x = 3","x = 7","x = 1","x = 5"],"correct":4,"why":"Distribute: 10x − 20 = 30. Add 20: 10x = 50. Divide: x = 5. (Or divide both sides by 5 first: 2x − 4 = 6 → 2x = 10, so x = 5.)","traps":["Substituting 2 gives 5(4 − 4) = 0, not 30.","Dividing by 5 first gives 2x − 4 = 6, and stopping at 2x = 6 skips adding the 4 back.","Both terms got multiplied by 10, but the 4 only takes the 5 out front, giving 10x − 20 = 30.","Distributing gave 10x + 20, but 5 times −4 is −20.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#znh491","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 9 runners, in how many ways can 1st through 3rd place be awarded?","opts":["6","27","84","504","729"],"correct":3,"why":"Order matters: 9P3 = 9 × 8 × 7 = 504.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 3 people. You still have to choose them from 9.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.","You used a combination. Places are different prizes, so ORDER matters: use a permutation.",null,"You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:di5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A 3-column table shows GPA × MCAT × Hours-of-study for 4 students. Student 1: 3.8/520/40. Student 2: 3.6/510/30. Student 3: 3.9/515/45. Student 4: 3.5/505/35. Which student has the highest GPA per hour of study?","opts":["Student 2","Student 3","Student 1","Student 4","Cannot be determined"],"correct":0,"why":"Compute GPA/hour for each. Student 1: 3.8/40 = 0.095. Student 2: 3.6/30 = 0.120. Student 3: 3.9/45 = 0.0867. Student 4: 3.5/35 = 0.100. Student 2 highest at 0.12. The trap: highest absolute GPA (Student 3) does NOT give the highest efficiency.","traps":[null,"Student 3 has the highest GPA but spent 45 hours. Efficiency = GPA/hour, where 3.9/45 = 0.087 - actually the LOWEST of the four.","Student 1 has 3.8/40 = 0.095. Not highest.","Student 4 = 3.5/35 = 0.100 - second highest but not max.","Choosing this reads the table as missing something, and both numbers you need, GPA and hours, sit right there for every student."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#1rqxl1i","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A car travels at 75 miles per hour. What is its speed in feet per second? (1 mile = 5280 ft)","opts":["0.0208 ft/s","110 ft/s","51.1 ft/s","3.96 × 10⁵ ft/s","6600 ft/s"],"correct":1,"why":"75 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi ÷ 3600 s/h = 110 ft/s. Handy anchor: 60 mph is 88 ft/s.","traps":["That is miles per second. Multiply by 5280 to get feet.",null,"Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so miles and hours cancel.","That is feet per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:cou1579","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Ten runners finish a race and the first three places are recorded in order. How many different orderings of the top three are possible?","opts":["13","30","120","720","1000"],"correct":3,"why":"nPr = 10!/(10-3)! = 720.","traps":["Added 10 and 3.","Multiplied 10 by 3.","That counts the groups with order ignored; each trio can be arranged 6 ways.",null,"Treated it as 10 choices for each of three slots, which lets an item repeat."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#39vs01","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 5 red and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["25/121","5/11","2/5","20/121","2/11"],"correct":4,"why":"First red: 5/11. Second red: 4/10. Multiply: 2/11.","traps":["That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.","That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too.","You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp008","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An item is marked up 20% from its cost, then discounted 25% from the marked-up price. The final price is $90. What was the ORIGINAL cost?","opts":["$72","$90","$100","$108","$120"],"correct":2,"why":"Cost C, so markup 1.20C, so discount 0.75 × 1.20C = 0.90C. 0.90C = 90 → C = 100.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1659","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Three tent poles measure 17, 14, and 3 units. Can they be joined end to end to form a triangle?","opts":["Only if right triangle","No","Cannot determine","Only if it is obtuse","Yes"],"correct":1,"why":"|17−14| = 3 and 17+14 = 31. 3 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["There is no angle to open up: the two shorter sides lie flat along the 17, tying it rather than beating it.",null,"The three lengths settle it: 14 plus 3 equals 17, and the sum has to be greater than the longest side, not equal to it.","There is no corner to open: 14 and 3 lie flat along the 17 rather than rising above it.","14 and 3 add to exactly 17, so they lie flat along the longest side instead of rising above it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#6668h","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (3 × 10¹)(7 × 10¹) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["2.1 × 10³","2.1 × 10¹","2.1 × 10²","21 × 10²","1 × 10³"],"correct":0,"why":"3 × 7 = 21 and 10¹ × 10¹ = 10². 21 × 10² is not proper form, so write 2.1 × 10³. Answer: 2.1 × 10³.","traps":[null,"The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","Right size, wrong form. In proper scientific notation the front number sits between 1 and 10.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1308","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A delivery contains 8 loose vials plus 7 trays holding 4 vials each. How many vials is that in total?","opts":["19","28","36","39","60"],"correct":2,"why":"Multiply before adding: 7 × 4 = 28, then 8 + 28 = 36.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:g2dm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A circle's radius triples. Area changes by what factor?","opts":["×3","×9","×6","×81","×27"],"correct":1,"why":"Area scales as r². Tripling r gives 3² = 9× the area.","traps":["Linear scaling, but area is squared.",null,"Multiplied the factor of 3 by the 2 in the exponent instead of squaring it.","Squared the scale factor twice over, once more than an area calls for.","That's cubed, for volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1161","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An $89 pair of shoes is discounted 22 percent. What is the sale price?","opts":["19.58","67","69.42","108.58","111"],"correct":2,"why":"New = 89 × (1 - 22/100) = 89 × 0.78 = 69.42.","traps":["That is 22 percent of 89, the size of the discount rather than the price left after it.","You subtracted 22 dollars instead of 22 percent, and 22 percent of 89 is 19.58.",null,"That puts 22 percent on instead of taking it off.","You added 22 dollars, and the problem takes 22 percent off."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#skajfl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the SUM of the solutions of x² − 5x + 4 = 0?","opts":["−5","3","4","−4","5"],"correct":4,"why":"It factors as (x − 1)(x − 4), so the roots are 1 and 4. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = 5, product = c/a = 4.","traps":["Sign slip. The sum of the roots is the OPPOSITE of the x coefficient: −b/a.","That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their sum.","That is the PRODUCT of the roots (c/a). The sum is −b/a.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1179","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A wholesaler drops the $480 price of a handpiece part by 10% and then raises the new price 25%. What is the price in dollars after both changes?","opts":["324","432","540","552","600"],"correct":2,"why":"Multiply factors: 480 × 0.9 × 1.25 = 540. Naïvely adding gives 552, which is wrong.","traps":["That cuts the second step by 25 percent instead of raising by it.","That is the price after the 10 percent cut, one step short of the answer.",null,"You added the two changes into a net 15 percent rise, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That applies only the 25 percent rise and leaves the cut out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1372","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A family drives at 50 miles per hour for 5.25 hours. How many miles do they cover?","opts":["9.52","212.5","250","262.5","312.5"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 50 × 5.25 = 262.5 mi.","traps":["Dividing 50 by 5.25 instead of multiplying.","Using 4.25 hours instead of 5.25.","Dropping the quarter hour and using 5 hours flat.",null,"Using 6.25 hours instead of the 5.25 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1419","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A research assistant mixes 100 mL of a 25% sugar solution with 100 mL of a 10% sugar solution. What is the percent concentration of the mixture?","opts":["8.75","12.5","15","17.5","35"],"correct":3,"why":"Solute total = 25 + 10 = 35 mL in 200 mL → 17.5%.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#c35opm","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (5 × 10⁻²)(3 × 10⁻⁶) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["8 × 10⁻⁷","1.5 × 10¹²","1.5 × 10⁻⁸","1.5 × 10⁻⁹","1.5 × 10⁻⁷"],"correct":4,"why":"5 × 3 = 15 and 10⁻² × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻⁸. 15 × 10⁻⁸ is not proper form, so write 1.5 × 10⁻⁷. Answer: 1.5 × 10⁻⁷.","traps":["You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pom5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A solution is 12% salt. 250g of solution contains how much salt?","opts":["12g","238g","220g","120g","30g"],"correct":4,"why":"10% of 250 = 25. 2% of 250 = 5. 12% = 30g.","traps":["That is the percent number itself, not 12 percent of the 250 grams.","That is 250 minus 12, subtracting the percent as though it were grams.","That is everything in the solution that is not salt, the other 88 percent.","You scaled the 12 by ten instead of taking 12 percent of the 250 grams.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1261","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What value of x satisfies 9x + 3 = x - 8?","opts":["-2.38","-1.38","-0.63","-0.38","1.38"],"correct":1,"why":"Move the x terms together and the numbers together. Subtract 1x from both sides for 8x + 3 = -8, then subtract 3 for 8x = -11. Divide by 8 and x is -11/8, which is -1.375 and rounds to -1.38. Keep the fraction exact through the work and round only at the finish, so the rounding never leaks into an earlier step.","traps":["That is -19 divided by 8, but the constants combine to -11, not -19.",null,"The 3 was added to the -8 instead of subtracted, leaving 8x = -5.","That is -3 divided by 8, so the -8 on the right never made it across.","Dividing 11 by 8 gives 1.38, but 8x = -11 makes x negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pco1560","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Disease prevalence 1%, test sensitivity 95%, specificity 95%. P(disease | positive test)?","opts":["0.0095","0.01","0.16","0.84","0.95"],"correct":2,"why":"Bayes: P(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+) = 0.95·0.01 / 0.059 = 0.161.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-missing-value-mean#521vni","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A student scored 79, 71, 88 on 3 quizzes. What score is needed on the next quiz to bring the average to exactly 81?","opts":["5","81","83","86","90"],"correct":3,"why":"Needed total = 81 × 4 = 324. Current total = 238. Next score = 324 − 238 = 86.","traps":["You used 3 quizzes. After the next one there are 4.","Scoring the target only works if you were already AT the target. You have to make up the gap on every earlier quiz too.","You averaged the old average with the new score. The old average counts for several quizzes, not one.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Required total = target × number of quizzes; subtract what you already have."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1458","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A museum floor is mapped on a grid, with one exhibit at (-8, 7) and another at (0, 12). In a straight line, about how far apart are they?","opts":["3.61","8","9.43","13","89"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((8)² + (5)²) = √89 = 9.43.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261001"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s15"}},{"id":"FULL_2","name":"Full-Length Test 2","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"bio:BIO_p069","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following best describes slow twitch (type I) skeletal muscle fibers?","opts":["Smooth muscle","Glycolytic, white, fatigue fast","Recruited only during maximal effort, after the fast twitch fibers fire","Oxidative, red (myoglobin-rich), fatigue resistant","Fast and powerful"],"correct":3,"why":"Fiber type questions come down to which fuel system the fiber is built around. Type I fibers are packed with mitochondria, capillaries, and myoglobin, which makes them look red and lets them make ATP aerobically for long stretches without fatiguing, the fibers behind posture and distance running. The choice pairing oxidative metabolism, red color from myoglobin, and fatigue resistance is the type I description.","traps":["Type I and type II are both skeletal muscle fiber types, while smooth muscle is a separate tissue with no striations and involuntary control.","That profile belongs to the fast twitch type II fibers, which trade endurance for quick, powerful contractions.","The size principle recruits the small slow motor units first, which is why type I fibers carry posture and easy walking all day long.",null,"Speed and peak force are type II strengths; type I fibers contract more slowly and produce less force, but they keep going."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Whales have small pelvic bones embedded in the body wall that are not attached to any hind limb. The presence of such vestigial structures is evidence for which of the following?","opts":["Random mutation","Convergent evolution","Common ancestry","Ongoing adaptation","Structures shrink through disuse during an animal's lifetime and the reduction is passed to its offspring"],"correct":2,"why":"A vestigial structure is a reduced version of something that was fully functional in an ancestor, and the only reason it is still present is that it was inherited. Whale pelvic bones make sense if whales descend from four limbed land mammals, a conclusion that fossils such as Ambulocetus and shared skeletal anatomy support independently. Whenever a structure exists in a form that does not match any current job, think inheritance from an ancestor in which it did.","traps":["Mutation supplies the raw variation evolution works with, but on its own it does not explain why a whale carries a reduced version of a land mammal's hip.","Convergent evolution explains similar structures in unrelated lineages doing the same job, and a reduced bone with no locomotor job is a different situation entirely.",null,"Adaptation describes a trait being shaped for a role it plays now, while a vestigial structure is a reduced remnant of an ancestral one.","Changes an animal acquires during its own lifetime are not written back into its DNA, so disuse in one generation cannot be inherited by the next."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1kxv9cz","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A drug completely blocks triose phosphate isomerase, so dihydroxyacetone phosphate can no longer be turned into glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate. What happens to the net ATP yield of glycolysis per glucose?","opts":["It falls from 2 to 0","It falls from 4 to 2","It stays at 2","It falls from 2 to 1","It rises above 2"],"correct":0,"why":"Work the two halves of the pathway separately. The cell still pays 2 ATP to split the whole six-carbon sugar, but now only one of the two three-carbon pieces continues, so the payoff phase returns 2 ATP and 1 NADH instead of 4 and 2. Two spent against two returned leaves a net of zero, which is exactly why that isomerase step exists.","traps":[null,"The 4 is the gross figure, and the question asks for net, which starts at 2.","Only half the carbon now reaches the payoff phase, so the output cannot be unchanged.","Halving feels right, but the investment is not halved: the cell still spends 2 ATP up front on the whole sugar.","Blocking a step in the middle of the pathway does not undo what was already spent at the start."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-158brxy","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Two turns of the Krebs cycle, the amount one glucose supports, produce 6 NADH and 2 FADH2. Using the modern estimates of about 2.5 ATP per NADH and about 1.5 ATP per FADH2, how much ATP does oxidative phosphorylation get from those carriers alone?","opts":["12","15","18","20","24"],"correct":2,"why":"Run the two carriers on separate lines, then add. Six NADH at 2.5 each is 15, two FADH2 at 1.5 each is 3, and 15 plus 3 is 18. Keeping NADH and FADH2 on separate lines is what protects you from the most common slip here, which is quietly giving them the same value.","traps":["This gives every carrier the FADH2 value of 1.5, so all eight are counted at the lower rate.","This counts the 6 NADH correctly but leaves the 2 FADH2 out of the total entirely.",null,"This uses the older textbook values of 3 ATP per NADH and 1 ATP per FADH2.","This applies 3 ATP per NADH and then credits FADH2 with that same higher value."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In a developing bird embryo, which extraembryonic structure collects and stores nitrogenous waste?","opts":["Allantois","Yolk sac","Chorion","The albumen surrounding the embryo","Amnion"],"correct":0,"why":"A bird embryo is sealed inside a shell, so its waste has nowhere to go, and this question asks how it copes. Birds and reptiles excrete uric acid, which is nearly insoluble and low in toxicity, and they pack it into the allantois, a sac growing out of the gut that serves as a built in waste bin until hatching. Four membranes, four jobs: the amnion cushions, the chorion exchanges gas, the yolk sac feeds, and the allantois stores waste.","traps":[null,"The yolk sac supplies food, delivering yolk nutrients to the growing embryo, which is traffic running in the opposite direction.","The chorion lines the inside of the shell and handles gas exchange with the outside air, working alongside the allantois but storing nothing.","Albumen is the egg white, a water and protein reserve supplied by the mother, not an embryonic structure that collects waste.","The amnion is the fluid filled sac that cushions the embryo and keeps it from drying out, so it holds water rather than waste."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-q3fw7z","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Compared with a peptide hormone acting on the same tissue, a steroid hormone usually produces a response that:","opts":["Begins more slowly and lasts longer","Begins faster and lasts longer","Begins faster and fades faster","Begins more slowly and fades faster","Is identical in timing, since both work through second messengers"],"correct":0,"why":"Timing follows mechanism. A peptide hormone flips switches that are already built, so cAMP rises and kinases fire within seconds, and the effect dies as soon as the messenger is broken down. A steroid changes which genes are transcribed, so new protein has to be made before anything happens, and that protein keeps working for hours after the hormone is gone. Gene transcription means slow on and slow off.","traps":[null,"Steroid effects are not fast, transcription and translation both take time.","That is the peptide profile, since second messenger cascades rise quickly and quit quickly.","Steroid effects persist, because the newly made protein lingers after the signal ends.","Steroids do not use second messengers, they act as transcription factors."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d018","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"An animal described as triploblastic possesses which of the following?","opts":["Three complete sets of chromosomes in every cell","3 germ layers (ecto + meso + endo)","No tissues","4 layers","2 germ layers"],"correct":1,"why":"Break the word apart: triplo for three, blast for the embryonic layers laid down at gastrulation. The three are ectoderm on the outside, giving skin and nervous system, endoderm on the inside, giving the gut lining and its glands, and mesoderm in between, giving muscle, bone, blood and the lining of a true coelom. Mesoderm is the layer that matters most for the exam, because everything with organs and a body cavity has it, and that begins with the bilaterians.","traps":["Triploblastic counts germ layers in the embryo, not chromosome sets; triploid is the word for three sets of chromosomes.",null,"Having no true tissues describes a sponge, which never forms germ layers at all.","There is no fourth germ layer in animal development; the set is ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm.","Two layers is diploblastic, the cnidarian grade with only ectoderm and endoderm."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p108","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule pulls glucose and amino acids out of the filtrate against their concentration gradients. What does this transport depend on most directly?","opts":["ATP hydrolysis at apical membrane","Pinocytosis","Diffusion","Na+ gradient set by basolateral Na/K pump","Aldosterone driven sodium channels in the proximal tubule"],"correct":3,"why":"This question is about the difference between primary and secondary active transport. The sodium potassium ATPase on the basolateral membrane burns ATP to push sodium out into the blood, which keeps sodium low inside the cell, and that steep inward gradient is the energy source apical cotransporters use to drag glucose and amino acids in alongside sodium. Whenever you see a cotransporter on the filtrate side of a cell, trace the energy back to a pump on the blood side, because that is where the ATP is actually spent.","traps":["ATP is spent, but not at the apical surface, since the apical carriers are cotransporters riding the sodium gradient while the pump that costs ATP sits on the basolateral side facing the blood.","Pinocytosis moves small amounts of protein and fluid in bulk, a minor pathway that cannot account for the steady reabsorption of nearly all filtered glucose and amino acids.","Plain diffusion can only move a solute down its gradient, so it cannot pull glucose out of filtrate that is already more dilute than the inside of the cell.",null,"Aldosterone acts on the far end of the nephron, the late distal tubule and the collecting duct, so it is not what powers proximal tubule reabsorption."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1rvt47w","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A cell with an internal osmolarity of 300 mOsm is placed in a 0.15 M solution of NaCl, which dissociates completely. What happens to the cell volume?","opts":["It swells, because 150 mOsm outside is well below the 300 mOsm inside","It shrinks, because 0.15 M NaCl comes to 600 mOsm","It swells, because sodium enters and water follows it in","It shrinks, because 300 mOsm of NaCl outdraws the cytoplasm","It stays the same, because the outside is also 300 mOsm"],"correct":4,"why":"The whole question turns on counting particles rather than molecules. Each NaCl unit splits into two particles in solution, so 0.15 M times 2 gives 0.30 osmol per liter, which is 300 mOsm and matches the cell exactly. Equal osmolarity on both sides means no net water movement and no volume change. Multiply molarity by the number of ions a salt releases before comparing anything.","traps":["This forgets that NaCl splits into two particles, so it uses 150 mOsm instead of 300 mOsm.","This multiplies by four, 0.15 M NaCl yields 0.30 osmol per liter.","Sodium does not freely leak in here, and water moves by osmosis rather than chasing a solute that stayed outside.","The outside is 300 mOsm, which equals the inside rather than exceeding it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p016","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Parathyroid hormone (PTH) and calcitonin have what functional relationship?","opts":["Synergistic","Same target","Independent","Permissive, since calcitonin must be present before PTH can act","Antagonistic"],"correct":4,"why":"Put both hormones on the same axis, blood calcium, and ask which way each one pushes. PTH raises blood calcium by pulling it out of bone, reclaiming it in the kidney, and activating vitamin D so the gut absorbs more, while calcitonin lowers it by tucking calcium back into bone. Opposite directions on one variable is the definition of antagonistic, the same relationship insulin and glucagon have for glucose.","traps":["Synergistic would mean the two push the same direction together, but here one raises blood calcium while the other lowers it.","They do share bone as a target, but a shared target is not a relationship term; the question is asking which way each one pushes.","They are not independent, since they regulate the very same variable, which is what puts them in opposition.","Permissive describes one hormone making a tissue able to respond to another, and neither of these is required for the other to work.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g014","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A cell with a diploid number of 2n = 8 undergoes meiosis. How many chromosomes does each resulting gamete contain?","opts":["It cannot be determined without knowing how many crossovers occurred","4","8","16","2"],"correct":1,"why":"Meiosis runs one round of DNA replication followed by two divisions, which halves the chromosome number exactly once. If 2n equals 8, then n equals 4, so every normal gamete carries 4 chromosomes. When a stem hands you 2n, write n beside it before you look at the choices and the arithmetic stops being a trap.","traps":["Crossing over swaps segments between homologs and changes which alleles travel together, but it never changes how many chromosomes a gamete receives.",null,"This is the parent cell's diploid count, which is what you get if the halving step is left out.","This doubles instead of halving; replication in S phase makes sister chromatids, not extra chromosomes.","This divides by four, probably because meiosis yields four cells, but four products does not mean four halvings of the chromosome number."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p001","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Oxygenated blood returning from the lungs enters which part of the heart first?","opts":["The coronary sinus","Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle","Left ventricle"],"correct":2,"why":"Trace one drop of blood. It leaves the lungs loaded with oxygen in the pulmonary veins, and those veins empty into the left atrium, which passes it through the mitral valve into the left ventricle for the trip out to the body. The right side of the heart handles blood coming back from the body and sends it to the lungs, so anything arriving from the lungs lands on the left.","traps":["The coronary sinus drains used blood from the heart's own muscle into the right atrium, not oxygenated blood from the lungs.","The right atrium collects blood returning from the body through the venae cavae, blood that has already given up its oxygen.",null,"The right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs, so it handles blood on the way there rather than on the way back.","The left ventricle does receive that oxygenated blood, but only after the left atrium has taken it in and passed it through the mitral valve."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev020","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In mammals, the chorion contributes to which structure?","opts":["The maternal portion of the placenta","Fetal portion of placenta","Yolk","Amniotic fluid","Umbilical cord vessels"],"correct":1,"why":"Sort the four membranes by which side of the placenta each one builds. The chorion is the outermost membrane around the embryo, and it sends chorionic villi burrowing into the uterine lining, so it becomes the fetal half of the placenta and secretes human chorionic gonadotropin early in pregnancy. The mother supplies the other half, the decidua, and the two interlock so the two blood supplies run close together without ever mixing.","traps":["The maternal side of the placenta is the decidua, the mother's own transformed endometrium, while the chorion is fetal tissue.",null,"Mammalian eggs carry essentially no yolk, and the small yolk sac that forms has separate jobs in early blood formation and germ cell origin.","Amniotic fluid sits inside the amnion, the inner membrane wrapped directly around the fetus, while the chorion is the outer membrane.","The umbilical vessels develop in association with the allantois and the connecting stalk, which is a different membrane."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-9webrs","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A human somatic cell has 46 chromosomes while it sits in G1. Consider that same cell at three moments: the end of G1, the end of G2, and the instant after anaphase has separated the sister chromatids but before the cytoplasm divides. How many chromosomes are present at each of those three moments?","opts":["46, then 92, then 92","23, then 46, then 92","46, then 92, then 46","92, then 92, then 46","46, then 46, then 92"],"correct":4,"why":"The trap is counting chromatid arms and calling them chromosomes. Count centromeres instead: after S phase the cell holds twice the DNA but still has 46 centromeres, so still 46 chromosomes, each now with two chromatids. The moment anaphase pulls those centromeres apart, every chromatid becomes a chromosome in its own right, so one undivided cell briefly holds 92. Cytokinesis then hands 46 to each daughter.","traps":["This counts chromatids at the end of G2, but replication leaves the two copies joined at a single centromere, so they still count as one chromosome.","23 is the haploid number that belongs to a gamete after meiosis, not to a somatic cell at any point in the ordinary cell cycle.","This makes the same G2 error and then halves too soon, since the count per cell only returns to 46 once cytokinesis splits the cytoplasm.","This treats G1 as already replicated, and a G1 cell carries 46 chromosomes of one chromatid each.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p080","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which statement explains the functional significance of the antibody class IgM?","opts":["It is the most abundant antibody class in blood and carries the secondary response","Crosses placenta","Mediates allergies","First antibody secreted in primary immune response (pentamer)","Smallest"],"correct":3,"why":"Each antibody class earns its identity from its shape and its timing. IgM is the first class a plasma cell secretes in a primary response, assembled as a pentamer with ten antigen binding sites, which makes it very good at clumping pathogens and activating complement even though each individual site binds weakly. Finding IgM in a patient suggests a current or recent first exposure, so the choice naming the first secreted antibody and the pentamer structure is the one.","traps":["IgG is the most abundant antibody in serum and dominates the secondary response, while IgM peaks early and then falls away.","Crossing the placenta is IgG's role, and its small monomer size is exactly what lets it pass, while a pentamer is far too large.","IgE is the class that drives allergic reactions by arming mast cells and basophils.",null,"IgM is the largest antibody, five monomers joined into a pentamer, so size is the opposite of its distinguishing feature."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d038","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A mycorrhiza is best described as which of the following?","opts":["Bacteria","Specialized root hairs the plant grows on its own to increase absorptive surface","Disease-causing","Lichens","Mutualistic fungus + plant root associations"],"correct":4,"why":"The word itself is the hint: myco for fungus, rhiza for root, so look for the choice that puts a fungus and a plant root together as partners. The fungal threads reach far past the root into soil the root cannot touch and hand over phosphorus and other minerals, and the plant pays in sugar, which makes this mutualism rather than disease. Most land plants carry this partnership, so treat it as the normal condition of a root, not a rare event.","traps":["Bacteria form their own root partnership in legume nodules where they fix nitrogen, and that is a separate arrangement from a fungal one.","Root hairs are extensions of the plant's own epidermal cells, so this choice leaves out the fungus that gives the structure its name.","Some fungi are plant pathogens, but this partnership feeds the plant instead of damaging it, so disease is the wrong category.","A lichen is also a fungal mutualism, but it pairs the fungus with an alga or a cyanobacterium rather than with a plant root.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1hhdtel","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Two cells from the same organism are examined. One is partway through mitosis and the other is partway through meiosis I. Which statement distinguishes the two correctly?","opts":["In mitosis the daughter cells differ genetically, while in meiosis I they are identical","In mitosis sister chromatids separate, while in meiosis I homologous chromosomes separate","In mitosis a spindle forms, while in meiosis I no spindle is required at all","In mitosis homologous chromosomes pair up and cross over, while in meiosis I they stay apart","In mitosis the chromosome number is halved, while in meiosis I it stays the same"],"correct":1,"why":"One sentence sorts almost every mitosis versus meiosis question: mitosis splits sisters, meiosis I splits homologs. That single difference explains the rest, because pulling homologs apart is what halves the number and what requires them to pair up beforehand. Picture anaphase in each: in mitosis a chromosome comes apart at its centromere, while in meiosis I the centromeres stay intact and whole chromosomes travel opposite ways. Meiosis II then looks like mitosis running on a haploid cell.","traps":["Mitosis produces genetically identical daughters, and meiosis is where crossing over and independent assortment create the variation.",null,"Both divisions use a spindle, because any cell that moves chromosomes needs microtubules to do the moving.","This is backward, because pairing and crossing over are the signature of prophase I of meiosis and homologs behave independently in mitosis.","Halving is the meiotic outcome, and mitosis preserves the chromosome number exactly, which is what lets it repeat indefinitely."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-t3v9co","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cell with DNA damage it cannot repair commits to the intrinsic pathway of apoptosis. Which molecule leaves the mitochondrion to set the cascade going, and where does it go once it is out?","opts":["Cytochrome c, into the cytosol, where it helps assemble the apoptosome","Fas ligand, into the extracellular space, where it binds the neighboring cell","Calcium, into the nucleus, where it condenses the chromatin all by itself","ATP synthase, into the cytosol, where it goes on to activate death receptors","Bcl-2, into the cytosol, where it directly cleaves the executioner caspases"],"correct":0,"why":"The elegant part of the intrinsic pathway is that it borrows a respiratory protein. Cytochrome c normally shuttles electrons in the intermembrane space, and when the outer membrane is permeabilized it spills into the cytosol, binds Apaf-1, and builds the apoptosome, which activates caspase-9. Finding cytochrome c in the cytosol is the classic sign that a cell has committed. Remember it by its address: inside the mitochondrion it means respiration, outside it means death.","traps":[null,"Fas ligand is made by another cell entirely and belongs to the extrinsic pathway, not to anything released from a mitochondrion.","Calcium is stored mainly in the endoplasmic reticulum rather than released as the apoptotic trigger, and chromatin condensation happens downstream of the caspases.","ATP synthase is anchored in the inner mitochondrial membrane and stays there, and death receptors are activated by ligands arriving from outside the cell.","Bcl-2 sits on the mitochondrial membrane holding this release shut, so it is the brake rather than the signal, and it is not a protease."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-5547ba43c3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A person drinks alcohol, which inhibits the release of antidiuretic hormone. What is the expected result?","opts":["Less glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule","A higher filtration rate at the glomerulus with no change in urine concentration","More aquaporins in the collecting duct, so less urine is produced","Fewer aquaporins in the collecting duct, so more dilute urine is produced","More Na+ reabsorption in the distal tubule, so blood pressure rises"],"correct":3,"why":"ADH is the water saver. It puts aquaporin water channels into the collecting duct so water can leave the filtrate and return to the blood. Take ADH away and the water stays in the tubule, so urine is plentiful and dilute.","traps":["ADH does not control glucose handling.","The main ADH effect is on water permeability of the collecting duct, which changes urine concentration.","That is what ADH does when it IS present. Blocking ADH gives the opposite.",null,"That is the aldosterone effect, not the ADH effect."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p019","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which cells of the stomach lining secrete hydrochloric acid?","opts":["G cells","Goblet cells","Mucous neck cells","Parietal cells","Chief cells"],"correct":3,"why":"Sort the stomach lining by what each cell hands over. Parietal cells pump out hydrochloric acid and also make intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption, while chief cells release pepsinogen, the inactive form of the protein digesting enzyme. The acid from the parietal cells is what converts pepsinogen into active pepsin, so pair them as parietal for acid and chief for enzyme.","traps":["G cells release the hormone gastrin, which tells the parietal cells to make acid rather than making it themselves.","Goblet cells secrete protective mucus and are found mainly in the intestine and the airways.","Mucous neck cells produce the mucus layer that shields the stomach wall from its own acid.",null,"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the enzyme precursor that the acid then activates."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"An autosomal recessive disorder affects 1 in 10,000 individuals in a population that is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. What is the frequency of the recessive allele (q)?","opts":["0.0001","0.001","0.01","0.1","0.99"],"correct":2,"why":"This is a Hardy-Weinberg question wearing a sentence as a disguise. For a recessive condition, the word affected describes homozygous recessive individuals, so the number you are handed is q squared, not q. One in 10,000 is 0.0001, and the square root of that is 0.01. Any time a stem gives you the frequency of affected recessive individuals, take the square root first, then build p and the carrier frequency off that value.","traps":["This is q squared, the frequency of affected individuals, and the square root step still has to happen.","Moving the decimal three places is not the same operation as taking a square root, so this lands between the two real values.",null,"This is the square root of 1/100, so it would fit a disorder affecting 1 in 100 people rather than 1 in 10,000.","This is p, the frequency of the dominant allele, which is what you get after subtracting q from 1."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g015","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A single base pair in the coding region of a gene is changed, yet the resulting polypeptide has exactly the same amino acid sequence as before. This type of mutation is called:","opts":["Transversion","Frameshift","Missense","Nonsense","Silent"],"correct":4,"why":"This tests whether you can carry a DNA change through the genetic code and out to the protein. The code is redundant, so several different codons spell the same amino acid, and a base swap that lands on one of those synonyms leaves the protein untouched. Sort the names by what the protein ends up doing: same protein is silent, one amino acid swapped is missense, an early stop is nonsense, and a shifted reading frame is a frameshift.","traps":["Transversion describes the chemistry of the swap, a purine traded for a pyrimidine, and says nothing about whether the amino acid stayed the same.","A frameshift comes from inserting or deleting bases in a number not divisible by three, which rewrites every codon downstream.","A missense mutation swaps one amino acid for another, so the protein sequence does change.","A nonsense mutation turns a codon into a stop signal and cuts the protein short, so the product is definitely altered.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-4tojjq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A leaf running linear flow starts building up NADPH faster than the Calvin cycle can spend it. Why does routing some electrons into cyclic flow help?","opts":["It converts the surplus NADPH back to NADP+ by handing its electrons straight to carbon dioxide in the stroma","The Calvin cycle needs more ATP than NADPH, so extra ATP with no extra NADPH balances the books","It lets the leaf fix carbon in the dark using electrons stored during the day","It raises oxygen output, which the leaf needs for its own respiration at night","It rebuilds water molecules from the protons in the lumen, so the leaf loses less water"],"correct":1,"why":"This is a stoichiometry question wearing a physiology costume. Three turns of the Calvin cycle burn nine ATP but only six NADPH, so a plant running linear flow alone slowly falls short on ATP. Cyclic flow is the top up valve: pure ATP, no NADPH, called for whenever that ratio drifts.","traps":["Carbon dioxide is reduced by Calvin cycle enzymes in the stroma, not by a photosystem handing electrons straight to it.",null,"Cyclic flow still needs light striking photosystem I, so it is not a pathway for darkness.","Cyclic flow bypasses photosystem II completely, so it produces no oxygen at all.","Nothing in the light reactions puts water back together, and the protons in the lumen are spent driving ATP synthase."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p071","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Rigor mortis, the stiffening of skeletal muscle after death, occurs for which of the following reasons?","opts":["No ATP to detach myosin from actin","Cellular swelling","Permanent shortening of the actin and myosin filaments themselves","Acidification","Calcium release stops"],"correct":0,"why":"Cross bridge cycling needs ATP twice, and the second use is what this question hangs on. ATP binding to the myosin head is what makes the head release actin, and hydrolysis then recocks it, so with no ATP the heads bind and stay bound. After death the supply runs out while calcium leaks into the cytoplasm and keeps the binding sites exposed, which locks the muscle until the proteins themselves break down, so the choice about no ATP to detach myosin is the mechanism.","traps":[null,"Cells do swell as membrane pumps fail, and that softens tissue over time rather than locking it rigid.","Filaments never change length in a living or a dead muscle; they slide past one another, and the stiffness comes from cross bridges stuck in place.","Lactic acid does build up after death and contributes to later tissue breakdown, but the stiffness itself comes from cross bridges that cannot let go.","Calcium actually leaks into the cytoplasm after death, because the pumps that clear it need ATP, so the binding sites stay open."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1hvash6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"A chloroplast shunts electrons from ferredoxin back to the cytochrome complex instead of passing them on to NADP+. What does that cyclic route produce?","opts":["Glucose, assembled directly inside the thylakoid","NADPH only","ATP only","ATP and NADPH in roughly equal amounts","Oxygen and NADPH, but no ATP"],"correct":2,"why":"This is a question about what a proton pump alone can do. Electrons circling from photosystem I back through the cytochrome complex still drive protons into the lumen, so ATP keeps coming, but nothing reaches NADP+ and no water is split. Picture a hamster wheel: plenty of motion and plenty of ATP, nothing new delivered at the far end.","traps":["No photosystem builds sugar, and nothing is assembled inside the thylakoid.","NADPH is exactly what this route skips, since the electrons never reach NADP+.",null,"Both together is the output of linear flow, not of electrons looping back to the cytochrome complex.","Oxygen comes from splitting water at photosystem II, which the cyclic route never involves."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e027","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A symbiotic relationship in which one organism benefits while the other is neither helped nor harmed is called:","opts":["Mutualism","Parasitism","Amensalism (one harmed, one unaffected)","Commensalism","Competition"],"correct":3,"why":"Score each partner with a plus, a minus, or a zero and these names sort themselves out. Mutualism is plus plus, parasitism and predation are plus minus, competition is minus minus, and commensalism is plus zero. Barnacles riding on a whale are the standard picture: the barnacle gets transport and a feeding current while the whale carries on unaffected. Truly neutral partners are rare in nature, so exam items keep it clean by telling you outright that the second organism is unaffected.","traps":["Mutualism has both partners gaining, the way plant roots and mycorrhizal fungi trade sugar for minerals.","Parasitism has one partner gaining at the other's expense, so the second organism is harmed rather than untouched.","Amensalism is the mirror image of this relationship: one organism is harmed while the other is unaffected.",null,"Competition costs both organisms, since each reduces what the other can obtain."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-11y9km5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Mature human red blood cells have no mitochondria at all. What does that mean for how they make ATP?","opts":["They run the Krebs cycle in the cytosol instead","They use the oxygen they carry to run an electron transport chain in the cytosol","They rely on glycolysis and convert pyruvate to lactate to keep NAD+ coming","They import ATP from neighboring cells through gap junctions","They make no ATP and live on stored reserves until they are cleared"],"correct":2,"why":"No mitochondria means no pyruvate oxidation, no Krebs cycle, and no electron transport chain, so glycolysis is the whole energy budget. To keep glycolysis turning, the cell has to regenerate NAD+, and lactate fermentation is how it does that, which is why red blood cells put out lactate even in a well-oxygenated person. It is a useful case to keep on hand, because it cleanly separates carrying oxygen from using it.","traps":["Krebs enzymes live in the mitochondrial matrix, so a cell without mitochondria has nowhere to run them.","The oxygen a red blood cell carries is cargo bound to hemoglobin, and the chain needs membrane complexes this cell does not have.",null,"Red blood cells do not maintain gap junctions with other cells, and ATP is not traded around that way.","These cells work for roughly 120 days, which no fixed ATP store would cover."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d003","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Bryophytes such as mosses are unique among land plants in being which of the following?","opts":["Sporophyte-dominant","Able to fertilize without free water, because their sperm are carried by wind","Seed-producing","Vascular","Gametophyte-dominant"],"correct":4,"why":"The question is testing which generation you actually see when you look at a moss. The green carpet underfoot is the haploid gametophyte, and the little stalk with a capsule on top is the sporophyte growing out of it and depending on it for nutrition. Every other land plant flips that arrangement, with a large dominant sporophyte and a tiny gametophyte. So the choice naming gametophyte dominance is the one that sets bryophytes apart.","traps":["A dominant sporophyte is the pattern in ferns, gymnosperms and flowering plants, where the large green body is the diploid generation.","Moss sperm still swim through a film of water to reach the egg, so free water is required rather than optional.","Bryophytes release spores, not seeds; seeds arrive later, with gymnosperms and angiosperms.","Mosses have no xylem or phloem, which is why they stay low and close to moisture; the vascular plants are everything else.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-wo30xs","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"The reaction center of photosystem II is called P680 and the one in photosystem I is called P700. What do those numbers tell you?","opts":["The number of chlorophyll molecules packed into each antenna complex","The number of protons each center pumps per electron passed","The order in which the two photosystems were discovered","The wavelength in nanometers each reaction center absorbs best","The energy an electron carries as it leaves each center"],"correct":3,"why":"The P stands for pigment and the number is a wavelength in nanometers, both sitting in the red end of the spectrum. Shorter wavelength means higher energy photons, so P680 works with slightly more energetic light than P700 does. Keeping those two numbers straight is also a reliable way to keep the two photosystems straight.","traps":["Antenna complexes hold hundreds of pigment molecules, and the name does not record that count.","Neither reaction center is itself a proton pump, since that work happens at the cytochrome complex.","The Roman numerals in photosystem I and II record discovery order, while the P numbers are about absorbed light.",null,"The names track the light being absorbed, not an energy value carried by an electron."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A pedigree shows that affected individuals are predominantly male, and that no affected father ever passes the condition to his sons. Which inheritance pattern best fits this pedigree?","opts":["X-linked dominant","Mitochondrial","Y-linked","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive"],"correct":4,"why":"Two clues do all the work here. No father to son transmission tells you the gene is not on the Y and is not being carried through males on an autosome, because a father gives his son a Y chromosome rather than an X. A strong male excess tells you one copy is enough to show the trait, which is what happens when the gene sits on the X and males have only one X to begin with. Put those together and you land on X-linked recessive every time.","traps":["X-linked dominant also blocks father to son transmission, but it usually produces more affected females than males rather than a male excess.","Mitochondrial conditions come only from the mother and affect sons and daughters alike, with no male bias.","A Y-linked trait would pass from every affected father to every one of his sons, which is exactly what this pedigree never shows.","Autosomal recessive conditions strike males and females at about the same rate, and affected fathers can pass the allele to sons.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p055","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune disease directed against which target?","opts":["Myelin sheath (CNS)","Neurons themselves","The myelin made by Schwann cells in peripheral nerves","Muscle","Synapses"],"correct":0,"why":"Multiple sclerosis is a myelin disease of the central nervous system: the immune system strips the sheath that oligodendrocytes wrap around brain and spinal cord axons. Losing that insulation slows or blocks saltatory conduction, which is why the symptoms are scattered and variable, touching vision, sensation, and coordination. The axon is still there, so the problem is conduction speed rather than a missing wire.","traps":[null,"The axons themselves are relatively spared early on, and it is the insulation around them that is attacked.","Peripheral myelin from Schwann cells is the target in Guillain-Barre syndrome, while multiple sclerosis stays confined to central myelin.","Muscle tissue is not the target, although weakness appears because the signals arriving at muscle are degraded.","Synaptic transmission stays intact, since the failure happens along the axon before the signal ever reaches a synapse."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-ndmaar","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A few thousand epinephrine molecules reaching a liver cell can release hundreds of millions of glucose molecules. The main reason is that:","opts":["The hormone enters the cell and binds DNA directly","Each step in the cascade is an enzyme that activates many copies of the next","Each hormone molecule is used over and over again by a single receptor protein","The receptor is a channel that admits glucose from outside","The hormone is stored and then released again by the target cell"],"correct":1,"why":"Amplification is the payoff of using enzymes as relay runners. One active G protein switches on an adenylyl cyclase that makes many cAMP molecules, each protein kinase A phosphorylates many targets, and the numbers multiply at every stage. When a question marvels at how small the input was, the answer is almost always the enzyme cascade.","traps":["Epinephrine is not lipid soluble and works through a surface receptor, and direct DNA binding would not amplify this way.",null,"Even repeated binding by one molecule gives nothing like a millionfold gain, the multiplication happens downstream.","The glucose released comes from the cell's own glycogen stores, not from outside.","Storing and rereleasing a hormone does not multiply the size of the response."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g031","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-9","topic":"Mendelian genetics","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Polygenic inheritance produces traits with which pattern of variation?","opts":["One gene with many different alleles in the population, as in the ABO blood system","Continuous variation (e.g., height, skin color)","Only 2 phenotypes","Sex-linkage only","Discrete categories"],"correct":1,"why":"When many genes each add a small amount to one trait, the possible totals fill in a smooth range instead of a few buckets, and the population distribution ends up bell shaped. Height and skin color are the standard examples. If a stem describes a trait measured on a scale rather than sorted into categories, think polygenic.","traps":["Multiple alleles at one locus is a different idea, since the ABO system still sorts people into a few discrete types rather than a continuous range.",null,"Two phenotypes is what one gene with complete dominance gives, not a trait built from many contributing genes.","Polygenic traits can involve genes on any chromosome, and sex linkage is a separate question about location.","Discrete categories are the signature of single gene Mendelian traits, which is the opposite of what many small contributions produce."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g051","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-6","topic":"The central dogma","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"When lactose is absent from the medium, where on the DNA does the lac repressor protein bind?","opts":["Operator (between promoter and structural genes)","The structural genes lacZ, lacY, and lacA themselves","CAP site","Terminator","Promoter"],"correct":0,"why":"This is testing the geography of an operon. The operator is a short control sequence sitting just downstream of the promoter and in front of the structural genes, and the repressor parks there so RNA polymerase, even when bound at the promoter, cannot move forward. When allolactose appears it changes the repressor's shape and the repressor lets go. Picture the promoter as the parking spot and the repressor as a car blocking the driveway out of it.","traps":[null,"The repressor blocks access at a single short control sequence; it does not coat the enzyme-coding genes themselves.","That site is where the activator binds when cyclic AMP is high, so it belongs to positive control rather than to the repressor.","The terminator sits at the far end and ends a transcript that has already been made, which would be far too late to prevent expression.","The promoter is where RNA polymerase binds; the repressor sits just past it and blocks the polymerase from advancing."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev012","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Why do the cells of an early embryo become smaller with each division during cleavage?","opts":["Each division gives the daughter cells half the number of chromosomes","Cells lose cytoplasm","Polyploidy","Cells die","Mitosis without growth (S + M only, no G1/G2)"],"correct":4,"why":"The reason is entirely about which parts of the cell cycle the embryo runs. Cleavage cycles alternate S phase and M phase with the G1 and G2 growth gaps left out, so a cell copies its DNA and divides without ever rebuilding its bulk, and each round halves the cytoplasm it started with. The useful picture is cutting one large cake into more and more pieces: same cake, smaller slices, and here every slice still carries a complete copy of the recipe.","traps":["Halving the chromosome number is what meiosis does when gametes are made, while cleavage is mitosis, so every cell keeps a full diploid set.","Nothing is discarded to the outside, because the cytoplasm of the zygote is divided among the daughters, so the total stays put while each share gets smaller.","Polyploidy would mean extra sets of chromosomes and would not shrink a cell, and cleavage keeps every cell diploid.","No cells are being lost here, since the cell count climbs at every division, which is how the embryo goes from one cell to many.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g060","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"An individual with a 47,XXX karyotype (triple X) most often shows which phenotype?","opts":["Severe disability","Sterile male","Short stature with a webbed neck and non-functional ovaries","Often unremarkable phenotype, taller, mild learning issues","Always sterile"],"correct":3,"why":"Extra X chromosomes are tolerated far better than extra autosomes, because all but one X is inactivated in every cell. That is why triple X is usually mild: female, often taller than average, frequently with no obvious findings, sometimes with mild learning or language difficulties. When a karyotype question hands you extra X chromosomes, expect a mild picture rather than a severe one.","traps":["Severe disability fits an autosomal trisomy such as 13 or 18, not an extra X that is largely silenced.","A 47,XXX individual is female, since there is no Y chromosome here to direct male development.","That description is Turner syndrome, 45,X, which is the opposite situation of one X too few rather than one too many.",null,"Fertility is usually preserved in triple X, which is part of why the condition often goes unnoticed."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g005","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Crossing a true-breeding red-flowered snapdragon with a true-breeding white-flowered snapdragon produces offspring with pink flowers. This result demonstrates:","opts":["Epistasis between two different genes","Sex-linkage","Incomplete dominance","Linkage","Codominance"],"correct":2,"why":"Look at the heterozygote and ask whether you are seeing both colors or an average of them. One working pigment allele makes only about half the usual amount of red pigment, so the petal lands between the two parents as a uniform pink rather than showing red and white separately. The proof that the alleles themselves stay intact is the next generation, which comes back as one red to two pink to one white.","traps":["Epistasis requires a second gene masking the first, but one pigment gene accounts for all three phenotypes on its own.","Sex-linkage would make the result depend on which parent contributed which allele, and it does not here.",null,"Linkage describes two genes riding together on one chromosome, and only a single flower color gene is involved here.","Codominance would show both colors at full strength at the same time, such as red and white streaks on one petal, rather than an even in-between shade."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e012","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Newly hatched goslings that first saw Konrad Lorenz rather than their mother followed him as if he were the parent, and continued to do so afterward. This behavior is an example of:","opts":["A fixed action pattern triggered by a sign stimulus","Imprinting (critical period)","Insight learning","Classical conditioning","Operant conditioning"],"correct":1,"why":"The tell for imprinting is a narrow window early in life during which an attachment forms and then holds. Ask two questions: was there a critical period, and did a single brief exposure lock the behavior in. Here the goslings attached to the first moving object they encountered after hatching, with nothing paired to a reward or to an existing reflex. Imprinting sits between innate and learned: the tendency to imprint is built in, but what the animal imprints on is learned.","traps":["A fixed action pattern is a complete innate motor sequence released by a specific stimulus, while what the goslings followed depended entirely on what they happened to see.",null,"Insight learning is solving a new problem in one stroke without trial and error, a far more flexible ability than a hatchling's attachment.","Classical conditioning requires pairing a neutral stimulus with one that already draws out a response, and no such pairing happened here.","Operant conditioning is learning from the consequences of an action, and the goslings earned nothing by following."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g061","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a three-point testcross, two of the three genes lie 25 centimorgans apart on the chromosome. What recombination frequency is expected between that pair of genes?","opts":["5%","12.5%","25%","50%","75%"],"correct":2,"why":"A centimorgan is defined as one percent recombination, so the conversion is direct and 25 centimorgans corresponds to about 25 percent recombinant offspring. The word approximate matters at larger distances, because a double crossover between two markers restores the parental arrangement and makes the observed frequency read slightly low. That is exactly why a three-point cross, with a marker sitting in the middle, gives better distances than a two-point cross.","traps":["Five percent would correspond to genes only about 5 centimorgans apart, a far tighter linkage than stated.","This halves the map distance, but centimorgans are already expressed as percent recombinants.",null,"Fifty percent is the value for genes that assort independently, whether unlinked or very far apart on the same chromosome.","Recombination frequency cannot exceed 50 percent, so any value above that is impossible."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following organisms shows a Type I survivorship curve?","opts":["Humans, large mammals","Most insects and other invertebrates","Annual plants","Hydra","Oysters"],"correct":0,"why":"A survivorship curve is a picture of when in life the deaths happen. Type I organisms produce few offspring and care for them, so most survive through early and middle life and the curve stays high before dropping late, which is the human and large mammal pattern. Type II is a straight diagonal, a roughly constant chance of dying at any age. Type III falls off a cliff at the start, the shape for organisms that release enormous numbers of eggs or seeds and invest nothing afterward.","traps":[null,"Most insects produce many offspring with very heavy early mortality, so they sit on the Type III curve.","Annual plants make many seeds and lose most of them as seedlings, another Type III pattern.","Hydra face roughly the same chance of dying at any age, which gives the straight Type II line.","Oysters release huge numbers of larvae and lose almost all of them early, which is the Type III shape."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-a:sw-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which acid solution gives the lowest pH at the SAME molarity (0.1 M)?","opts":["HCl","HCN","CH₃COOH","HCOOH","HF"],"correct":0,"why":"At equal molarity, the acid that ionizes most completely gives the most hydrogen ion and therefore the lowest pH. Hydrochloric acid is strong, so the full 0.1 M shows up as H⁺ and the pH is 1.0. The weak acids in this list hold onto most of their protons, which puts their pH values one to four units higher. Rank by Ka, and remember that a strong acid outruns every weak one at the same concentration.","traps":[null,"Hydrocyanic acid has a Ka near 10⁻¹⁰, making it the weakest of this group and the one with the highest pH.","Acetic acid ionizes about 1 percent at this concentration, so its hydrogen ion concentration stays far below 0.1 M.","Formic acid is about ten times stronger than acetic acid and still weak, so it never reaches full ionization.","HF has the largest Ka of the weak acids here, but it still ionizes only a few percent, so it cannot match a fully ionized strong acid."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ent-e4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Consider the gas-phase reaction 2 NO2(g) + 0.5 O2(g) -&gt; N2O5(g) at 298 K and 1 atm. The number of moles of gas decreases substantially as the reactants combine into a single gaseous product. Based on Δn(gas), what is the sign of ΔS_system for this reaction?","opts":["Negative","Zero","Cannot be determined without S° values","Depends only on temperature","Positive"],"correct":0,"why":"Δn(g) = 1 − (2 + 0.5) = −1.5. Fewer gas-phase particles means fewer microstates, so ΔS_system &lt; 0 (negative). Δn(gas) is the dominant factor when phase counts change.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ti-e4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"At 25 °C, 25.0 mL of 0.20 M acetic acid (Ka = 1.8x10^(-5)) is titrated with 25.0 mL of 0.20 M NaOH, exactly reaching the equivalence point. The product is 0.10 M sodium acetate (CH3COO-) in 50.0 mL solution. Using Kb = Kw/Ka and [OH-] ≈ sqrt(Kb·[A-]), what is the equivalence-point pH?","opts":["7.00","8.00","8.72","9.50","10.50"],"correct":2,"why":"Kb = 1x10^(-14)/1.8x10^(-5) = 5.56x10^(-10). [OH-] ≈ sqrt(5.56x10^(-10) x 0.10) ≈ 7.45x10^(-6) M. pOH ≈ 5.13, so pH ≈ 8.87 (closest answer: 8.72; the slightly different number reflects rounding). At a WA + SB equivalence point, pH &gt; 7.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":3,"q":"Xenon tetrafluoride (XeF₄) is a square planar molecule with F-Xe-F angles of 90°. The central xenon has four Xe-F σ bonds and two lone pairs that occupy axial positions to minimize lone-pair/lone-pair repulsion. What hybridization is assigned to the xenon?","opts":["sp³d","sp³d³","sp³d²","dsp²","sp³"],"correct":2,"why":"Steric number at Xe = 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs = 6, requiring sp³d² hybridization (octahedral electron geometry). The two lone pairs sit trans to each other (axial), leaving the four fluorines in a square plane with 90° bond angles.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lf006","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"For the combustion of methane: CH4(g) + 2 O2(g) → CO2(g) + 2 H2O(l), ΔH° = −890 kJ/mol and ΔS° = −243 J/(mol·K). At what temperature (approximate) does the spontaneity of the reaction reverse? (Assume ΔH° and ΔS° are temperature-independent.)","opts":["About 3,660 K - far above any practical temperature","The reaction is never spontaneous at any temperature","About 27 K - only at cryogenic temperatures","About 273 K (0°C)","The reaction is always spontaneous; spontaneity never reverses"],"correct":0,"why":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = 0 when T = ΔH/ΔS = 890,000 / 243 ≈ 3663 K. Below this T, ΔG &lt; 0 (spontaneous); above, ΔG &gt; 0. In practice, methane combustion is always spontaneous at attainable temperatures.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_e4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Combustion of 1 mol CH₄ has ΔH_rxn = −100 kJ/mol (a hypothetical exothermic reaction). If you reverse the reaction (the products become reactants and vice versa), the magnitude of ΔH stays the same but the sign flips. What is ΔH for the reverse reaction?","opts":["−200 kJ/mol","+100 kJ/mol","−100 kJ/mol","0 kJ/mol","+200 kJ/mol"],"correct":1,"why":"Reversing a reaction reverses the sign of ΔH (Hess's law). ΔH_reverse = −ΔH_forward = −(−100) = +100 kJ/mol. The reverse of an exothermic reaction is endothermic with the same magnitude.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#qrgh47","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.0231 min⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["43.3 min","86.6 min","30 min","0.016 min","0.0333 min"],"correct":2,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.0231 = 30 min. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":["That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.","You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693.",null,"You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath.","Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:sr5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"You mix aqueous solutions and want to confirm sulfate (SO₄²⁻) is present in an unknown. Which cation should you add to make sulfate precipitate cleanly?","opts":["Na⁺","Ba²⁺","Mg²⁺","NH₄⁺","K⁺"],"correct":1,"why":"The classic sulfate confirmation: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s), a white precipitate. Ba²⁺ is one of the sulfate exceptions to the \"sulfates are soluble\" rule. Na⁺, NH₄⁺, and K⁺ all form fully soluble sulfates (Group 1 + ammonium), so they cannot precipitate sulfate.","traps":["Na₂SO₄ is soluble - Group 1 sulfates are always soluble. No precipitate.",null,"MgSO₄ is soluble, which is why Epsom salts dissolve in a bath; a 2+ charge by itself does not precipitate sulfate.","(NH₄)₂SO₄ is soluble - ammonium sulfates are always soluble. No precipitate.","K₂SO₄ is soluble - same Group 1 rule. To precipitate sulfate you need Ba²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Sr²⁺."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:col-q12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"You compute osmotic pressure with π = i·M·R·T. For the answer to come out in atm with M in mol/L and T in K, R must have units:","opts":["J/(mol·K)","mmHg·L/(mol·K)","cal/(mol·K)","atm·mol/(L·K)","L·atm/(mol·K)"],"correct":4,"why":"π [atm] = i · M [mol/L] · R · T [K]. Solving for R's units: atm / (mol/L · K) = atm·L/(mol·K) = L·atm/(mol·K). That is the gas constant 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K). Picking R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) gives π in J/L (not atm).","traps":["R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) is the SI value, but it does not give atm directly. Use 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K) when the answer must be in atm.","The structure is right but the pressure unit is not; R = 62.4 mmHg·L/(mol·K) returns the answer in mmHg, and this question asks for atm.","Calorie units are obsolete for this and would produce the wrong magnitude - π in cal/L is not standard.","Units do not balance: this would give π in (atm·mol/(L·K)) × (mol/L) × K = atm·mol²/L² - not a pressure unit.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-molecular-formula#14njfl4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A compound has the empirical formula CH and a molar mass of 65 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?","opts":["CH","C₆H₆","C₄H₄","C₅H₅","C₁₀H₁₀"],"correct":3,"why":"Empirical mass = 13. 65 / 13 = 5, so multiply every subscript by 5: C₅H₅.","traps":["That is the empirical formula again. Divide the molar mass by the empirical mass to get the multiplier.","Multiplier is one too high. Molar mass divided by empirical mass must come out to a whole number.","Multiplier is one too low. Compute the empirical formula mass carefully, then divide.",null,"You doubled the multiplier. Check the empirical formula mass."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:bf3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Buffer 0.10 M HA, 0.10 M A⁻ (pKa 5). Add 0.01 mol H⁺/L. pH?","opts":["4.5","4.91","4.95","5.00","5.09"],"correct":1,"why":"Adding strong acid converts A⁻ → HA. New [A⁻] = 0.09, new [HA] = 0.11. pH = 5 + log(0.09/0.11) = 5 + log(0.82) = 5 − 0.087 = 4.91. (The buffer absorbed most of the added H+ - pH barely moved.)","traps":["4.5 would be the pH if there were no buffer present. The buffer keeps the change small (5.00 → 4.91), not large.",null,"That drops the base form to 0.09 M but leaves the acid form at 0.10 M, when it climbs to 0.11 M.","5.00 would mean adding strong acid didn't change pH at all. Buffers RESIST change but don't eliminate it; pH does drop slightly.","5.09 means pH went UP after adding acid - wrong direction. Adding H+ should lower pH, never raise it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-cell-potential#tl00x0","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Standard reduction potentials:\n\nZn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn E° = −0.76 V\nNi²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni E° = −0.25 V\n\nWhat is E°cell for the galvanic cell built from these two half-cells?","opts":["−0.51 V","+1.02 V","+0.77 V","+0.51 V","−1.01 V"],"correct":3,"why":"Cathode = higher reduction potential (Ni). Anode = Zn. E°cell = (-0.25) − (-0.76) = +0.51 V. Red cat, an ox: reduction at the cathode, oxidation at the anode.","traps":["A galvanic cell runs by itself, so E°cell must be POSITIVE. The higher reduction potential is the cathode.","You multiplied E° by the electron coefficients. Potential is intensive: never scale it when you balance.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.",null,"You added the two table values. E°cell = E°cathode MINUS E°anode, both read as reductions."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Phosphorus pentachloride PCl5 in the gas phase has a trigonal-bipyramidal geometry with three equatorial P-Cl bonds at 120° and two axial bonds at 90°. The central P forms 5 sigma bonds with no lone pairs (steric number = 5). Which hybridization scheme correctly accommodates these 5 bonding orbitals?","opts":["sp^2","sp^3 d^2","sp","sp^3","sp^3 d"],"correct":4,"why":"Steric number 5 demands 5 hybrid orbitals: one s + three p + one d = sp^3 d. (sp^3 d^2 would be 6 hybrids for octahedral SF6.) The mixing of 3d orbitals with s and p enables the expanded octet around P.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lr4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Continuing the Haber reaction N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ from earlier (28.0 g N₂ + 9.0 g H₂, with N₂ limiting at 1.00 mol), how many grams of NH₃ (molar mass 17.0 g/mol) are theoretically produced?","opts":["8.5 g","51.0 g","68.0 g","34.0 g","17.0 g"],"correct":3,"why":"From 1 mol N₂, the 1:2 ratio gives 2 mol NH₃. Mass = 2.00 mol × 17.0 g/mol = 34.0 g. Using a 1:1 ratio mistakenly gives 17 g; tripling N₂'s mass gives 51 g.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Nitrogen (Z = 7) has the ground-state configuration 1s² 2s² 2p³, with the three 2p electrons singly occupying the p_x, p_y, and p_z orbitals (Hund's rule, parallel spins). Using m_l = -1, 0, +1 for the 2p orbitals filled in order, what set (n, l, m_l, m_s) describes the LAST (highest-energy) electron added?","opts":["(2, 1, +1, −½)","(2, 0, 0, −½)","(2, 1, 0, +½)","(2, 1, +1, +½)","(2, 1, −1, +½)"],"correct":3,"why":"Hund's rule fills the 2p orbitals in order m_l = -1, 0, +1, all with the same spin (+½ by convention). The 7th electron is the third 2p electron, occupying m_l = +1 with m_s = +½. n = 2 (second shell), l = 1 (p subshell).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#yt5pvw","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 48 g of CH₄? (molar mass 16 g/mol)","opts":["2.89 × 10²⁵","12","1.81 × 10²⁴","4.52 × 10²³","7.22 × 10²⁴"],"correct":4,"why":"48 g / 16 g/mol = 3 mol CH₄. Times 4 hydrogen per molecule = 12 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 7.22 × 10²⁴.","traps":["You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.","That is the number of MOLECULES. Each CH₄ holds 4 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 4.","You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1lnwi0z","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 2 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 127 °C and 0.5 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["41.7 L","32.8 L","131 L","13300 L","65.7 L"],"correct":2,"why":"T = 127 + 273 = 400 K. V = nRT / P = (2)(0.0821)(400) / 0.5 = 131 L.","traps":["You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.","You multiplied by pressure. V = nRT / P, so pressure goes underneath.",null,"You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.","You dropped the moles. n belongs on top with R and T."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lr-e12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"For Mg(s) + 2 HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g), a strip of magnesium (24.0 g, MW = 24 → 1.00 mol Mg) is added to 36.5 g HCl (MW = 36.5 → 1.00 mol HCl). Compute mole/coefficient ratios to identify the limiting reactant.","opts":["HCl is limiting","Both simultaneously","Mg is limiting","H₂ is limiting","Cannot determine"],"correct":0,"why":"Ratios: Mg → 1.0/1 = 1.0; HCl → 1.0/2 = 0.5. Smaller ratio = HCl limits. With HCl limiting, only 0.5 mol Mg reacts (consuming 0.5 mol Mg), leaving 0.5 mol Mg unreacted in excess.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lw-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Chlorine trifluoride (ClF₃) is a powerful fluorinating agent with a T-shaped molecular geometry and F-Cl-F angles near 87°. The central Cl has 7 valence electrons, forms 3 Cl-F σ bonds, and has remaining electrons distributed as lone pairs in equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid. How many lone pairs are on Cl?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"why":"Cl contributes 7 e⁻; 3 are used in the three Cl-F σ bonds, leaving 4 e⁻ = 2 lone pairs. Steric number = 3 + 2 = 5 (sp³d, trigonal bipyramidal electron geometry). The two LPs go equatorial, producing the T-shaped molecular geometry.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Copper (Cu, Z = 29) is one of the well-known anomalies in the Aufbau filling order. Rather than [Ar] 4s² 3d⁹, Cu's ground-state configuration is [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰ because a fully filled 3d¹⁰ subshell is exceptionally stable. How many electrons are in the 3d subshell of neutral Cu?","opts":["8","9","10","11","12"],"correct":2,"why":"Cu's actual ground-state configuration is [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰, with a fully-filled d-subshell. Verify: 18 (Ar) + 1 + 10 = 29 . The full d¹⁰ shell gives extra exchange-energy stability that beats the predicted 4s² 3d⁹.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-gibbs#12ayr68","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a reaction, ΔH = -120 kJ and ΔS = 200 J/K. What is ΔG at 400 K?","opts":["-200 kJ","-40 kJ","-80 kJ","-120 kJ","-80100 kJ"],"correct":0,"why":"TΔS = 400 × 200 J/K = 80 kJ. ΔG = -120 − (80) = -200 kJ, so the reaction is spontaneous at this temperature.","traps":[null,"Sign slip. ΔG = ΔH MINUS TΔS.","You dropped ΔH. Both terms matter.","You forgot to multiply ΔS by the temperature.","Unit trap. ΔS is in J/K but ΔH is in kJ. Divide TΔS by 1000 before subtracting."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_xp006","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A 2.50 L container holds N₂ at 1.00 atm and 25 °C. The container is sealed and the temperature is increased to 100 °C. Simultaneously, an additional 0.020 mol of N₂ is added (no leaks). What is the new pressure?","opts":["0.75 atm","5.50 atm","2.49 atm","1.50 atm","4.00 atm"],"correct":3,"why":"Initial: PV=nRT, so n = PV/RT = (1.00)(2.50)/(0.0821 × 298) = 0.1023 mol. Add 0.020 mol, so n_new = 0.1223 mol. New T = 373 K. P = nRT/V = (0.1223)(0.0821)(373)/2.50 ≈ 1.50 atm. (The +0.020 mol contributes substantially despite the higher T.) Re-check: actually 0.1223 × 0.0821 × 373 / 2.5 = 1.498 atm - answer is 1.50 atm.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ek-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"For the gas-phase equilibrium 2 NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g) at 298 K, the equilibrium constant in terms of concentrations is K_c = 4.7 M⁻¹. Using K_p = K_c·(RT)^Δn with R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K and Δn = (1 − 2) = -1, calculate K_p at 298 K.","opts":["0.0085","0.19","4.7","41","115"],"correct":1,"why":"Δn = mol gas products - mol gas reactants = 1 - 2 = -1. K_p = K_c·(RT)^Δn = 4.7 × (0.0821 × 298)^(-1) = 4.7 / 24.5 ≈ 0.19. Negative Δn means K_p &lt; K_c whenever RT &gt; 1, as it always is at room temperature.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lf001","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"The reaction A + 2B → C was studied at 25°C with the following initial rate data:Trial[A] (M)[B] (M)Initial rate (M/s)10.100.102.0×10−420.200.104.0×10−430.100.208.0×10−4What is the rate law for this reaction?","opts":["rate = k[A][B]","rate = k[A]²[B]","rate = k[A][B]³","rate = k[A]²[B]²","rate = k[A][B]²"],"correct":4,"why":"Doubling [A] (T1→T2) doubles rate → 1st order in A. Doubling [B] (T1→T3) quadruples rate → 2nd order in B. rate = k[A][B]².","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A 1.00 m aqueous solution of KCl (which dissociates completely into K⁺ and Cl⁻, so van't Hoff factor i = 2) is prepared. Pure water freezes at 0.00°C with Kf = 1.86°C·kg/mol. Using ΔTf = i·Kf·m, what is the freezing point of this solution?","opts":["−3.72°C","0.00°C","−7.44°C","−1.86°C","−5.58°C"],"correct":0,"why":"ΔTf = (2)(1.86)(1.00) = 3.72°C. New Tf = 0.00 − 3.72 = −3.72°C. Forgetting i = 2 (treating KCl as a nonelectrolyte) gives −1.86°C; using i = 3 gives −5.58.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gv-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"The Daniell cell Zn|Zn²⁺(1 M)‖Cu²⁺(1 M)|Cu has a standard cell potential E°cell = +1.10 V at 25°C, with n = 2 electrons transferred per mole of reaction. Using ΔG° = -n·F·E°cell with F = 96,485 C/mol, calculate ΔG° in kJ/mol.","opts":["−424 kJ/mol","−212 kJ/mol","−54 kJ/mol","+424 kJ/mol","+212 kJ/mol"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔG° = -n·F·E°cell = -(2)(96,485 C/mol)(1.10 V) = -212,267 J/mol ≈ -212 kJ/mol. The negative sign confirms spontaneity, consistent with E°cell &gt; 0. Sign errors and forgetting the factor of n are the main error modes.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"When ADDING measured values, the result must be rounded to the same DECIMAL PLACE as the input with the fewest decimals (not fewest sig figs). Compute 25.34 + 1.2 + 0.123 and report with correct precision.","opts":["26.66","26.663","26.6630","26.7","27"],"correct":3,"why":"Sum = 26.663. The least precise addend is 1.2 (one decimal place), so the sum must be rounded to one decimal place: 26.663 → 26.7. Addition uses decimal places (not sig figs); multiplication uses sig figs.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lc4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"An equilibrium mixture of N2(g) + 3 H2(g) ⇌ 2 NH3(g) sits in a sealed 5.00 L vessel at 500 K. A chemist injects 1.0 mol of inert argon while keeping the volume constant at 5.00 L. Considering that K_p depends only on partial pressures of N2, H2, and NH3 (not on Ar), which way does the equilibrium shift?","opts":["Shifts forward, then reverse","Shifts forward (toward NH3)","Shifts reverse (toward N2 + H2)","Equilibrium dissociates","No shift"],"correct":4,"why":"Adding inert gas at constant V does not change the partial pressures or molar concentrations of N2, H2, or NH3, so Q is unchanged and Q = K still holds. Le Chatelier's principle predicts no shift. (At constant pressure, dilution would shift toward more moles of gas.)","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 100. mL buffer at 25 °C contains 0.010 mol HA and 0.010 mol A- (pKa = 4.74). 0.005 mol HCl is added (no volume change assumed). HCl converts A- -&gt; HA: new mol HA = 0.015, new mol A- = 0.005. Using pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]), what is the new pH?","opts":["3.74","4.26","4.74","4.92","5.74"],"correct":1,"why":"log(0.005/0.015) = log(1/3) ≈ -0.477. pH = 4.74 + (-0.48) = 4.26. Forgetting the sign of log gives 5.22; ignoring the shift gives 4.74.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_p5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"An isoelectronic series Na⁺, Mg²⁺, F⁻, and O²⁻ all share 10 electrons (the Ne configuration) but differ in nuclear charge Z. Which species has the LARGEST ionic radius?","opts":["Na⁺ (Z=11)","F⁻ (Z=9)","All four have the same radius because they have the same electron count","Mg²⁺ (Z=12)","O²⁻ (Z=8)"],"correct":4,"why":"In an isoelectronic series, the ion with the SMALLEST nuclear charge (Z) holds the 10 electrons most loosely, giving the largest radius. O²⁻ (Z=8) &lt; F⁻ (Z=9) &lt; Na⁺ (Z=11) &lt; Mg²⁺ (Z=12), so O²⁻ is largest.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-1lvugyo","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione (drawn) is treated with aqueous NaOH and warmed. The major product is 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one. This transformation is best classified as which kind of reaction?","opts":["Claisen condensation","Intermolecular crossed aldol condensation","Cannizzaro reaction","Robinson annulation","Intramolecular aldol condensation"],"correct":4,"why":"A terminal methyl enolate attacks the distal carbonyl carbon, closing a six-membered ring beta-hydroxy ketone that dehydrates to the conjugated enone (3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, C7H10O, loss of H2O verified). Both reacting centers are in the same molecule, so it is an intramolecular aldol condensation. Claisen needs an ester; Robinson needs a Michael donor/enone; Cannizzaro needs a non-enolizable aldehyde.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-c74e4a1474","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"Which substituent in the para position makes a phenol the most acidic?","opts":["A nitro group (NO2)","A hydrogen, meaning unsubstituted phenol","An amino group (NH2)","A methyl group (CH3)","A methoxy group (OCH3)"],"correct":0,"why":"Ask what the substituent does to the phenoxide. A para nitro group pulls the negative charge all the way onto its own oxygens by resonance, so the anion is far more stable and the phenol is more acidic. Groups that push electron density in do the opposite.","traps":[null,"An electron-withdrawing group beats no substituent.","An amino group is a strong electron donor by resonance, which makes the phenol less acidic.","An alkyl group donates electron density, which makes the phenol slightly LESS acidic.","A methoxy group DONATES electron density by resonance, which makes the phenoxide less stable."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-1hta81k","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is treated with a strong base under E2 conditions. E2 requires the C-H and C-Br bonds to be anti-periplanar (both axial). Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","opts":["CC1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O"],"correct":2,"why":"In the trans isomer, when Br is axial the C2 methyl is equatorial, so the C2 hydrogen is equatorial and cannot be anti-periplanar to Br. The Zaitsev 1-methylcyclohexene is geometrically blocked; methylenecyclohexane and the unreacted/substitution products are wrong. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CCCCC1Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1njmhoj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A chemist wants to reduce the ketone of a keto-aldehyde to an alcohol while leaving the aldehyde untouched. They first convert the aldehyde to a 1,3-dioxolane with ethylene glycol/TsOH, run the reduction, then treat with aqueous acid. Why is the acetal an effective protecting group for this sequence?","opts":["Acetals are inert to acid, base and hydride alike, so the masked aldehyde can only be freed by oxidative cleavage with periodic acid","Acetals are stable to acid and to nucleophiles but are cleaved by hydroxide, so a basic workup after the reduction restores the carbonyl","Acetals are reduced by hydride, regenerating the aldehyde directly","Acetals spontaneously revert to the carbonyl on standing, requiring no deprotection step","Acetals are stable to base and nucleophiles/hydride but are hydrolyzed back to the carbonyl by aqueous acid"],"correct":4,"why":"Acetal formation/hydrolysis are acid-catalyzed equilibria. With no carbonyl present, acetals are inert to hydride, base, and nucleophiles (the protection), and are removed by aqueous acid that reverses the equilibrium to carbonyl plus diol. They are not base-cleaved or hydride-reduced.","traps":null,"qs":"O=Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1evfkjo","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"What single reagent/condition is required to convert allyl vinyl ether (shown) into 4-pentenal?","opts":["Simply heat (thermal, no added reagent)","mCPBA","O3 at low temperature, then a reductive workup with Zn/H2O","Aqueous NaOH at reflux, followed by an H3O+ workup","H2 / Pd-C"],"correct":0,"why":"The Claisen rearrangement is a thermal, concerted pericyclic reaction needing only heat; no acid, base, oxidant, or catalyst. Ozonolysis, hydrogenation, and epoxidation would destroy the alkene rather than perform the [3,3] shift.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCOC=C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-146gkua","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene oxide (drawn) is treated with sodium azide (NaN3) in DMF. Which structure correctly shows the major product, including its stereochemistry?","opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","[N-]=[N+]=NC1CCCC1","OC1CCC(O)C1"],"correct":1,"why":"Azide opens the epoxide by anti (backside) SN2, placing azide and OH trans: trans-2-azidocyclopentanol. C is a diol (no nitrogen), D has no hydroxyl (ring not opened to an alcohol), E is a six-membered ring (wrong ring size).","traps":null,"qs":"C1CC2OC2C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1op9odj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"In a Gabriel synthesis using potassium phthalimide (shown) followed by hydrazinolysis, which alkyl halide will give the highest yield of the desired primary amine?","opts":["2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)","1-bromobutane (a primary alkyl halide)","2-chloro-2-methylbutane (a tertiary halide)","bromobenzene (an aryl halide)","vinyl bromide (a vinyl halide)"],"correct":1,"why":"The phthalimide anion alkylates via SN2, so it needs an unhindered substrate; 1-bromobutane (primary) gives the best yield. Tertiary halides undergo E2 elimination, and aryl/vinyl halides are unreactive toward SN2.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1c2ccccc2C(=O)[N-]1.[K+]","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1u7uugo","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"An unknown shows a molecular-ion cluster at m/z 120 and 122 in an intensity ratio of about 1:1, and combustion/formula analysis indicates exactly one degree of unsaturation. Which structure is consistent with BOTH observations?","opts":["BrCCCBr","CC(C)Br","C=CCBr","CCCCBr","CCCBr"],"correct":2,"why":"The 1:1 M/M+2 ratio means exactly one bromine (rules out the dibromide, which would be 1:2:1). One degree of unsaturation means one ring or pi bond. Allyl bromide (C3H5Br) has one bromine and one C=C (DoU = 1). 1-bromopropane and 2-bromopropane (C3H7Br) and 1-bromobutane (C4H9Br) are saturated (DoU = 0), and the dibromide has two Br.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCBr","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1f5q9m8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A student attempts a Friedel-Crafts acylation on the molecule shown (aniline) using CH3COCl and AlCl3 and obtains essentially no ring-acylated product. What is the best explanation?","opts":["The basic -NH2 nitrogen complexes with the Lewis acid AlCl3, forming a strongly deactivating -NH2-AlCl3 group that shuts down EAS","The reaction gives only the meta product, which the student discarded","Acetyl chloride is too unreactive to form an acylium ion","The amino group withdraws so much electron density from the ring that the aromatic system becomes too electron-poor for any electrophile to attack","Aniline is aromatic, and an aromatic ring cannot undergo electrophilic substitution without permanently losing its aromatic stabilization"],"correct":0,"why":"FC reactions fail on aniline because the Lewis-basic amino N coordinates to AlCl3; the resulting positively charged -NH2-AlCl3 nitrogen strongly deactivates the ring (and consumes catalyst). The free -NH2 is normally a strong activator, so the ring is not inherently electron-poor.","traps":null,"qs":"Nc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-10b6fdu","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"Phenylacetylene (drawn) is treated with (1) disiamylborane (Sia2BH) then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","opts":["OCCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC=Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal CH carbon, and oxidation/tautomerization gives the aldehyde phenylacetaldehyde. Acetophenone is the Markovnikov product; 2-phenylethanol is alkene-hydration thinking; the enol is the intermediate; 1-phenylethanol has the OH on the wrong carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"C#Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1boz2p7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Two keto acids are considered for thermal decarboxylation: the gamma-keto acid shown (4-oxopentanoic / levulinic acid) and 3-oxo-3-phenylpropanoic acid (a beta-keto acid). Which statement is correct?","opts":["Both decarboxylate equally well because both contain a ketone","Only the beta-keto acid decarboxylates readily on heating; the gamma-keto acid does not, because its carbonyl is too far for a six-membered cyclic transition state","The gamma-keto acid decarboxylates faster, because the extra distance between its carbonyl and its carboxyl relieves strain in the cyclic transition state that delivers the proton","Neither decarboxylates; a ketone cannot assist loss of CO2","Only the gamma-keto acid decarboxylates, because only its carbonyl and carboxyl are spaced to close a ring, and the product is a cyclic lactone rather than a simple ketone"],"correct":1,"why":"Facile thermal decarboxylation requires the second carbonyl to be beta to the carboxyl so a six-membered cyclic transition state can form. The beta-keto acid satisfies this; in the gamma-keto acid the ketone is one carbon too far, so no low-energy cyclic TS exists.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC(=O)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8kbyva","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Acetophenone is shown (C8H8O, M+ = 120). Its base peak appears at m/z 105 (loss of 15). Identify the fragmentation that produces m/z 105 and the structure of that cation.","opts":["Loss of a methyl radical followed by ring expansion to the seven-membered aromatic tropylium cation at m/z 91","Loss of water to give a styrene radical cation","Loss of CO to give the phenyl cation","Alpha-cleavage losing a methyl radical to give the resonance-stabilized benzoyl (C6H5CO+) acylium ion","McLafferty rearrangement through a six-membered transition state, expelling neutral ethylene to give the ion at m/z 105"],"correct":3,"why":"Alpha-cleavage of the bond between the carbonyl carbon and the methyl group ejects a methyl radical (15 u), giving the benzoyl acylium cation C6H5C=O+ at m/z 105 (resonance-stabilized by the ring and the O lone pair). Acetophenone has no gamma-H, so McLafferty is not available.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-jfeiyo","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"You must convert 1-nitro-4-propylbenzene (shown) into 4-aminobenzoic acid. Which sequence of reagents accomplishes BOTH the side-chain and the nitro-group transformations in a workable order?","opts":["(1) Sn/HCl only (it also oxidizes the propyl chain to COOH)","(1) hot KMnO4 only (it also reduces the nitro group)","(1) hot KMnO4, H3O+ to make the COOH; then (2) Sn/HCl, then base to reduce NO2 to NH2","(1) Sn/HCl, then base, to make the NH2 group first; then (2) hot KMnO4 with an H3O+ workup on the propyl chain","(1) H2/Pd to reduce NO2 to NH2; then (2) HNO3/H2SO4 to turn the propyl chain into the carboxylic acid"],"correct":2,"why":"Two changes: propyl (has benzylic H) is oxidized to COOH by hot KMnO4, and nitro is reduced to NH2 by Sn/HCl. Oxidize first, since hot KMnO4 would destroy/oxidize an amine; then reduce the surviving nitro. No single reagent does both.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8neusk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. Which structure is the major product? (Consider carbocation stability carefully.)","opts":["BrCC(C)C(C)C","BrCCC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)CBr","CC(C)(Br)C(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2 next to the quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives a more stable tertiary cation that Br- traps, yielding 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (keyed option A, canonical CC(C)C(C)(C)Br). The classic trap (option B) is the unrearranged secondary bromide.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-xtm96d","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"You need to convert the internal alkyne shown into the corresponding cis (Z) alkene WITHOUT over-reducing it to the alkane. Which reagent should you choose?","opts":["H2 with Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb and quinoline)","Excess H2 with Raney nickel (a highly active sponge-nickel catalyst)","H2 with PtO2 (Adams' catalyst, a finely divided platinum oxide surface)","Na metal in liquid NH3 (dissolving-metal reduction through a vinyl radical anion)","H2 with Pd/C at one atmosphere in ethanol"],"correct":0,"why":"Lindlar's poisoned Pd catalyst stops at the alkene and delivers both H syn, giving the cis (Z) alkene. Pd/C, PtO2, and Raney Ni over-reduce to the alkane; Na/NH3 dissolving-metal gives the trans alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"CC#CC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-16yhmrn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (drawn) is placed in warm methanol containing no added strong base. Classify the competing reactions and identify which substitution mechanism is operative.","opts":["Only SN2 occurs, with inversion of configuration at the tertiary carbon","SN2 and E2 compete (bimolecular), because methanol is a strong enough base and nucleophile to attack the substrate directly in one concerted step","Only E2 occurs, giving exclusively the Hofmann alkene","SN1 and E1 compete (unimolecular), because the tertiary substrate ionizes to a carbocation in a polar protic solvent with a weak nucleophile","SN1 and E2 compete, pairing unimolecular substitution with a bimolecular elimination in which methanol removes a beta-hydrogen as bromide leaves in the same step"],"correct":3,"why":"Tertiary halide in warm methanol (polar protic, weak nucleophile, no strong base) = solvolysis: SN1 and E1 compete; substitution via SN1. SN2 blocked (tertiary), E2 needs strong base (absent). Correct=0.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-11y5yyy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl oxalate (drawn) is treated with ethyl acetate and sodium ethoxide, followed by acidic workup. What is the major crossed-Claisen product?","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)C(=O)OCC","O=CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC"],"correct":3,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha H and acts only as the electrophilic acceptor. Distractors: self-Claisen product, aldehyde variant, beta-hydroxy diester, double-addition over-condensation. All valid, exactly one correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-yuv7z3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Styrene is shown below. Without any spectra, how many degrees of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) does its molecular formula C8H8 correspond to?","opts":["3","4","5","6","8"],"correct":2,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (16 + 2 - 8)/2 = 5. This accounts for the benzene ring (3 pi bonds + 1 ring = 4) plus the vinyl C=C (1), totaling 5.","traps":null,"qs":"C=Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1xqx7n5","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Which reagent oxidizes benzyl alcohol (shown) selectively to the aldehyde (benzaldehyde) without over-oxidizing to the acid?","opts":["H2/Pd","LiAlH4","CrO3 in aqueous H2SO4 (Jones reagent), a mild oxidant that halts at the aldehyde","Hot KMnO4 with aqueous workup, which delivers the aldehyde and goes no further","PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2"],"correct":4,"why":"PCC is the mild, anhydrous oxidant that stops a primary alcohol at the aldehyde. Hot KMnO4 and Jones over-oxidize to the acid; LiAlH4 and H2/Pd are reducing conditions.","traps":null,"qs":"OCc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-18wvvu9","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"The ester below (ethyl acetate) can be hydrolyzed under acidic OR basic conditions. Which statement correctly contrasts the two pathways?","opts":["Acidic hydrolysis is irreversible because water is present in large excess; saponification is a reversible equilibrium that can be driven back toward the ester","Acidic hydrolysis is reversible (it is the reverse of Fischer esterification); basic hydrolysis (saponification) is effectively irreversible","Acidic hydrolysis requires a stoichiometric amount of acid that is consumed","Saponification delivers the neutral carboxylic acid directly in the basic reaction medium, so no acidification step is needed at the workup stage","Both pathways are reversible equilibria with similar position"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid hydrolysis is the microscopic reverse of Fischer esterification (reversible, acid is catalytic). Saponification is driven irreversibly because hydroxide deprotonates the acid to the stable carboxylate. Options 2-5 each misstate reversibility/catalysis/workup.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)OCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-9wwa4n","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Reaction of the cyclic alkene shown with O3 then Zn/AcOH gives a single dicarbonyl product. Classify the two carbonyl groups in that product.","opts":["One ketone and one aldehyde","Two aldehydes (a dialdehyde)","One ketone and one carboxylic acid","Two ketones (a diketone)","Two carboxylic acids (a diacid)"],"correct":3,"why":"1,2-dimethylcyclopentene is tetrasubstituted at the C=C (each sp2 carbon carries a methyl and a ring carbon, no H). Ring-opening cleavage places a ketone at both former alkene carbons, giving the open-chain diketone 2,6-heptanedione (C7H12O2). With no vinylic C-H, no aldehyde or acid forms.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=C(C)CCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-tgbsqx","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"Which set of reagents/conditions would best convert the tertiary alkyl bromide shown into the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene as the major product?","opts":["Concentrated H2SO4, heat (E1 conditions that favor the Zaitsev alkene)","Potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu) in tert-butanol, heat","Dilute aqueous NaOH at room temperature","Sodium iodide (NaI) in acetone","Sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol, heat (a small, strong, unhindered base)"],"correct":1,"why":"Bulky KOtBu gives the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene; NaOEt and H2SO4/E1 favor Zaitsev, aqueous NaOH favors substitution, NaI/acetone is SN2 Finkelstein.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCC(C)(C)Br","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-19um632","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a malonic ester synthesis, the enolate of diethyl malonate (shown) is alkylated at its central carbon to create a new stereocenter (for example, sequential methylation and ethylation followed by decarboxylation gives 2-methylbutanoic acid). What is the stereochemical outcome at that newly formed stereocenter, and why?","opts":["An optically active single enantiomer forms, because the chiral base sets the configuration","Only the R enantiomer forms, because alkylation always proceeds with retention of configuration","A racemic (50:50) mixture of R and S forms, because the planar sp2 enolate is attacked by the alkyl halide from both faces with equal probability","A single meso compound forms, because the two ester groups sit on either side of the new center and make the molecule internally symmetric, so it cannot be optically active","Only the S enantiomer forms, because the alkyl halide is steered to the face anti to the larger ester group and approaches from that side exclusively"],"correct":2,"why":"The nucleophilic enolate carbon is sp2/planar, so alkyl halide attack on each face is equally likely, generating the new stereocenter as a 50:50 racemate (optically inactive). No chiral influence is present, so single-enantiomer or retention options are wrong; a meso form is impossible with one stereocenter.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-cc3354","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Allylbenzene (3-phenyl-1-propene) is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. A student worries the benzene ring will redirect where bromine adds. Which structure is actually the major product?","opts":["CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","BrCC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccccc1Br","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The terminal C=C is separated from the ring by a CH2, so it is NOT conjugated and gains no benzylic stabilization. Anti-Markovnikov addition simply puts Br on the terminal CH2: (3-bromopropyl)benzene. The trap options assume conjugation and place Br on an internal or benzylic carbon; others are a dibromide or ring-brominated arene.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1o3v4s0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexyne (shown) is treated with NaNH2, then with 1-bromopropane, and finally with Na in liquid NH3. Which statement correctly describes the final product?","opts":["trans-4-nonene, an internal E alkene","cis-4-nonene, an internal Z alkene","trans-2-nonene, an internal E alkene","1-nonyne, a terminal alkyne","4-nonyne, an internal alkyne (no reduction occurs)"],"correct":0,"why":"Acetylide alkylation of 1-hexyne with 1-bromopropane gives 4-nonyne; Na/NH3 then reduces that internal alkyne by anti addition to trans-4-nonene. Numbering: propyl(C3)-C#C-butyl(C4) places the bond at C4.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CCCCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-vqku6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"The molecule drawn is cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane. Which single statement BEST classifies this molecule with respect to chirality and optical activity?","opts":["It is a meso compound: it has stereocenters but an internal mirror plane, so it is achiral and optically inactive","It is chiral and optically active, existing as a pair of non-superimposable enantiomers that each rotate plane-polarized light in opposite directions","It is a constitutional isomer of 1,4-dichlorocyclohexane and is optically active","It exists only as a single non-superimposable mirror image, so it has no achiral stereoisomer at all","It has no stereocenters at all, so chirality is not possible"],"correct":0,"why":"So cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane is meso, achiral, optically inactive - option A correct. option C wrongly denies stereocenters; option D confuses constitutional vs stereoisomerism.","traps":null,"qs":"C1C[C@H](Cl)C[C@H](Cl)C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-a2e657aa1b","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"The hydrogens on the carbon next to a ketone carbonyl have a pKa near 20, far lower than the pKa of an ordinary alkane. Why?","opts":["The carbonyl carbon is sp3 hybridized, which strains the alpha C-H bonds and lets them break easily","Alpha hydrogens form hydrogen bonds to the carbonyl oxygen, which holds them loosely enough for a base to remove one","The C-H bond is shorter next to a carbonyl","The conjugate base is an enolate, and its negative charge is shared with the carbonyl oxygen by resonance","The carbonyl oxygen donates its lone pairs into the alpha C-H bond and weakens it enough to release the proton"],"correct":3,"why":"Pull off an alpha hydrogen and the lone pair left on carbon sits right next to the C=O pi bond. Resonance moves the negative charge onto the oxygen, which is happy to hold it. That stabilized anion, the enolate, is the starting point for aldol and Claisen chemistry.","traps":["The carbonyl carbon is sp2. Hybridization of that carbon is not the reason.","C-H hydrogens do not take part in hydrogen bonding in a meaningful way.","The driving force is the stability of the conjugate base.",null,"The carbonyl group WITHDRAWS electron density, which is what stabilizes the anion."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-1h9d318","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Methyl hexanoate is treated with one equivalent of diisobutylaluminum hydride (DIBAL-H) at -78 degrees C, followed by aqueous workup. What is the major product?","opts":["CCCCCC(=O)O","CCCCC=O","CCCCCC(=O)OC","CCCCCCO","CCCCCC=O"],"correct":4,"why":"DIBAL-H (1 equiv, -78 C) partially reduces an ester to the aldehyde: the tetrahedral aluminum hemiacetal is stable at low T and collapses to hexanal only on workup, stopping before the alcohol. Excess LiAlH4 would give the alcohol. DIBAL is not an oxidant, and the 6-carbon chain is retained (pentanal would lose a carbon).","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCCC(=O)OC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1bw83st","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv), limited to monobromination. What is the major product?","opts":["BrCC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccc(Br)cc1","CC(C)(Br)c1ccc(Br)cc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1Br"],"correct":1,"why":"NBS/hv abstracts the most stabilized H. Cumene's single benzylic C-H is on the central carbon; the resulting radical is both tertiary AND benzylic (doubly stabilized), far weaker than the primary methyl C-H. Br installs there: (2-bromopropan-2-yl)benzene (opt[0]). opt[1] brominates a primary methyl (non-benzylic, minor), opt[2]/opt[3] are ring (EAS) products, opt[4] is a dibrominated over-reaction.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-dp6bvf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Hexane-2,5-dione is treated with aqueous NaOH (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1C","CC1=CCC(=O)C1","CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1CCC(=O)C1=O","O=C1CCCC1=O"],"correct":2,"why":"An enolate at a terminal methyl attacks the other carbonyl intramolecularly to close a favorable five-membered ring; dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one (C6H8O). Keyed structure correct. Distractors: cyclopentane-1,2-dione (no aldol), a regioisomeric enone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and an extra-methyl ring. All valid, unique.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"qr-a:exp1720","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"What is the value of 5^1 × 5^6?","opts":["0","15625","15630","78125","6103515625"],"correct":3,"why":"Same base × adds exponents: 5^(1+6) = 5^7 = 78125.","traps":["Two positive powers multiplied together can never give zero.","You multiplied the exponents, so you landed back on 5^6.","You added the two values, 15625 and 5, instead of multiplying them.",null,"You multiplied the bases as well as adding the exponents, which gives 25^7."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pa3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Two fair coins are flipped. What is the probability that at least one of them lands heads?","opts":["3/4","1","1/4","1/3","1/2"],"correct":0,"why":"OR: P(H1) + P(H2) − P(both). 1/2 + 1/2 − 1/4 = 3/4. Or flip-to-none: 1 − 1/4 = 3/4.","traps":[null,"Treated at least one head as a sure thing; both coins land tails once in every four tries.","That is the chance that both land heads, not the chance of at least one.","Counted two heads, two tails and one of each as equally likely; one of each happens twice as often as either other.","That is the chance for one coin on its own; two coins give four equally likely outcomes, three of which hold a head."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1457","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Two weather stations sit at (-12, 2) and (-6, 8) on a survey grid. Measured in a straight line, about how many units apart are they?","opts":["3.46","6","8.49","12","72"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (6)²) = √72 = 8.49.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1183","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $250 nightly room rate falls 10% in the off season and then rises 25% for the holidays. What is the holiday rate in dollars?","opts":["168.75","225","281.25","287.5","312.5"],"correct":2,"why":"Multiply factors: 250 × 0.9 × 1.25 = 281.25. Naïvely adding gives 287.5, which is wrong.","traps":["That cuts the second step by 25 percent instead of raising by it.","That is the price after the 10 percent cut, one step short of the answer.",null,"You added the two changes into a net 15 percent rise, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That applies only the 25 percent rise and leaves the cut out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1494","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A number is multiplied by 3, then 5 is subtracted, leaving 13. What is the number?","opts":["5","6","7","9","18"],"correct":1,"why":"'Five less than 3 times a number' is 3x minus 5, not 5 minus 3x, and it equals 13. Add the 5 back to get 3x = 18, then divide by 3 for 6. The phrase 'less than' flips the order of what you write, so translate it slowly.","traps":["That is the 5 lifted straight out of the sentence rather than the number solved for.",null,"Three 7s minus 5 lands at 16, so this one runs a step high; 3x has to hit exactly 18.","Split the 18 by 2 instead of by 3, so the number came out half again too big.","Stopped one step short at 3x = 18 without splitting that 18 three ways."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1284","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"Each row of a waiting area has 4 padded chairs and 7 plain chairs. How many chairs are in 5 identical rows?","opts":["11","27","50","55","60"],"correct":3,"why":"Distribute: 5·4 + 5·7 = 20 + 35 = 55.","traps":["That is just the 4 + 7 inside the parentheses, so the 5 outside never got multiplied in.","Distributing the 5 only to the 4 gives 20 + 7 = 27, leaving the 7 unmultiplied.","That is 5 times 10, so the sum inside came up one short of 11.",null,"That is 5 times 12, so the sum inside was read as 12 instead of 11."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#b96vmp","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (5 × 10⁻⁶)(7 × 10⁵) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["3.5 × 10⁰","3.5 × 10⁻²","3.5 × 10⁻¹","1.2 × 10⁰","3.5 × 10⁻³⁰"],"correct":0,"why":"5 × 7 = 35 and 10⁻⁶ × 10⁵ = 10⁻¹. 35 × 10⁻¹ is not proper form, so write 3.5 × 10⁰. Answer: 3.5 × 10⁰.","traps":[null,"The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1740","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What values of x satisfy |x − 3| = 7?","opts":["4 or -10","10 or -4","10","-4 or -10","4 or -4"],"correct":1,"why":"|x − 3| = 7 means x − 3 = ±7, so x = 10 or -4.","traps":["You subtracted the 3 instead of adding it back on both branches.",null,"You solved x - 3 = 7 and stopped, and the branch x - 3 = -7 gives -4 as well.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","That pairs 4 with its negative, and the branches give 3 plus 7 and 3 minus 7, which are 10 and -4."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1053","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A crew used 2/4 of a can of stain to cover 3/7 of a deck. What fraction of the deck would a full can cover?","opts":["5/28","6/11","6/28","7/6","6/7"],"correct":4,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 3/7 times 4/2 gives 12/14. Both numbers are even, so in lowest terms that is 6/7. A quick sanity check is that dividing by something smaller than 1 has to make the answer bigger, and 6/7 is indeed above 3/7.","traps":["You added the tops (3 + 2) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.","You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (7 + 4).","You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first.","That flips the first fraction instead of the second. The one being divided by is the one that turns over.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp003","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A bag contains 5 red marbles and 7 blue marbles. Three marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that exactly 2 are red and 1 is blue?","opts":["21/44","7/22","5/12","7/12","15/44"],"correct":1,"why":"Use combinations: P = C(5,2) · C(7,1) / C(12,3) = (10)(7) / 220 = 70/220 = 7/22.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#ejj15","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 108 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["30 m/s","389 m/s","0.03 m/s","1.08 × 10⁵ m/s","1800 m/s"],"correct":0,"why":"108 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 30 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":[null,"Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.","That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.","That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#3jvdp0","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 5 red and 3 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["5/14","4/7","25/64","5/8","5/16"],"correct":0,"why":"First red: 5/8. Second red: 4/7. Multiply: 5/14.","traps":[null,"That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too.","That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.","That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:est1209","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A mailing list has 3,182 subscribers and gains 30 more. How many subscribers does it have now?","opts":["3152","3180","3212","3230","3280"],"correct":2,"why":"Work it out: 3182 + 30 = 3212. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 3182 minus 30, so the plus got read as a minus.","That is 3182 rounded down, with the 30 never added.",null,"That is 3200 plus 30, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 3,212.","That adds about a hundred rather than the 30 on the page."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rt5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"You drive 60 mph for 2 hr, then 40 mph for 3 hr. Average speed?","opts":["50 mph","45 mph","100 mph","52 mph","48 mph"],"correct":4,"why":"Average speed is never the average of the speeds; it is total distance over total time. The legs cover 60 times 2, or 120 miles, and 40 times 3, or another 120 miles, so 240 miles in 5 hours gives 48 mph. The tell is that the times differ, so the 3 hours at 40 mph drag the average below the halfway point of 50.","traps":["Averaging 60 and 40 straight across, which ignores the extra hour spent at 40.","Estimating between the two speeds instead of dividing 240 miles by 5 hours.","Adding the two speeds together.","Pairing each speed with the other leg's time, 60 for 3 hours and 40 for 2.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1397","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"One student can enter a stack of survey forms in 5 hours and another can enter the same stack in 4 hours. Working together, how many hours does the stack take?","opts":["0.45","2.22","4","4.5","9"],"correct":1,"why":"1/T = 1/5 + 1/4 = 9/20. T = 20/9 = 2.22 hr.","traps":["Stopping at the combined rate of 9/20 of the job per hour instead of flipping it to get the hours.",null,"Reporting the faster worker's solo time; the help pulls it lower still.","Averaging the two times instead of combining the rates.","Adding the two times; together they finish sooner than either one alone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1109","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A vat already holds 40 liters of rinse solution, and another 28.9 liters is poured in. How many liters does the vat hold now?","opts":["6.89","11.1","68.9","69","689"],"correct":2,"why":"Write 40 as 40.0 so the columns line up, then add: 40.0 plus 28.9 is 68.9. Padding a whole number with a decimal zero stops the 9 from drifting into the wrong column.","traps":["You placed the point one spot too far left, shrinking the total tenfold.","You subtracted instead of adding.",null,"You rounded 28.9 up to 29 before adding; this one is exact at 68.9.","You dropped the decimal point, which makes the total ten times too large."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:g2d5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A square has side 4. If you double the side, the area becomes…","opts":["×4","×2","×16","×64","×8"],"correct":0,"why":"Area scales as length squared. Double the side, so area × 2² = ×4.","traps":[null,"Linear scaling, but area is squared.","Raised the factor of 2 to the fourth power, scaling the figure twice over.","That is the new area in square units, not the factor the area grew by.","That's cubed, for volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1blwuhr","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$10000 is invested at 10% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 3 years?","opts":["$3310.00","$12100.00","$13000.00","$13310.00","$14641.00"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 10000 × (1.1)³ = $13310.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1nphbtr","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"In triangle ABC, angle A = 40°, side a = 6, and side b = 8. Using sin 40° = 0.643, what is sin B?","opts":["0.482","0.643","0.766","0.802","0.857"],"correct":4,"why":"The Law of Sines rearranges to sin B equals b times sin A divided by a, so it is 8 times 0.643 divided by 6, which is about 0.857. Because side b is longer than side a, angle B has to be the larger angle, so sin B should land above sin 40 rather than below it. That size check catches the most common version of this slip immediately.","traps":["This puts the sides in the wrong spots, computing 6 times sin 40 divided by 8.","This reuses sin 40 without scaling by the ratio of the sides.","This is cos 40 rather than any sine.","This scales the angle itself by 8 over 6 and then takes the sine, but the Law of Sines scales the sines, not the angles.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1477","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A race course is 7.8 kilometers long. What is that distance in meters?","opts":["0.01","78","780","1007.8","7800"],"correct":4,"why":"1 km = 1000 m, so 7.8 km × 1000 = 7800 m.","traps":["You divided by 1000 instead of multiplying; the smaller unit always gives the bigger count.","You moved the decimal one place instead of three, multiplying by 10 rather than 1000.","You multiplied by 100 instead of 1000; kilometers to meters moves three places.","You added 1000 to the 7.8 instead of multiplying the 7.8 by it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1204","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A pallet holds 543 bricks, and a mason uses 57 of them. How many bricks are left on the pallet?","opts":["390","440","486","490","600"],"correct":2,"why":"Work it out: 543 - 57 = 486. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That takes about 150 off 543, far more than the 57 the problem gives.","That is 500 minus 60, rounding that overshoots the subtraction by 46.",null,"That is 540 minus 50, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 486.","That is 543 plus 57, so the minus got read as a plus."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#ta7kr","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 100 and standard deviation 5. About what percent of scores fall between 100 and 105?","opts":["16%","34%","47.5%","50%","68%"],"correct":1,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 34%.","traps":["That is the tail beyond one SD, not the slice inside it.",null,"That is the slice out to TWO SD.","That is everything above the mean. The slice stops at one SD.","That is one SD on BOTH sides. Only the upper side is asked for."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:int1439","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A deposit of $1000 earns 7.5% interest compounded annually for 5 years. To the nearest cent, what is the total amount in dollars after 5 years?","opts":["375","1000.38","1075","1375","1435.63"],"correct":4,"why":"A = P(1+r)^t = 1000·(1.075)^5 = 1435.63.","traps":["That is the simple interest for five years, not the ending amount.","Added rt as about thirty-eight cents rather than as 37.5 percent of the 1000.","Grew the balance for one year instead of five.","Used simple interest, so the later years never earned interest on the earlier interest.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1453","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A trail map marks a trailhead at (-1, -3) and a lookout at (5, 1). In a straight line, about how many units apart are they?","opts":["3.16","6","7.21","10","52"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (4)²) = √52 = 7.21.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1700","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"On a mapping grid, a straight cable runs between (-9,-12) and (-16,-14). What is its slope, to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["-9","-0.29","0.08","0.29","3.5"],"correct":3,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = -2/-7 = 0.29.","traps":["-9 adds the change in y and the change in x instead of dividing them.","-0.29 keeps one negative and drops the other, and two negatives divided give a positive.","0.08 comes from writing -16 minus 9 for the run instead of -16 minus -9, so the run lands at -25.",null,"3.5 divides the run by the rise, -7 over -2, which is the slope upside down."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ps5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Rent on a storage unit is $200 a month and rises 25 percent of its current amount in each of the next two years. What is the monthly rent after both increases?","opts":["$250","$256","$275.00","$300","$312.50"],"correct":4,"why":"×1.25 × 1.25 = ×1.5625. 200 × 1.5625 = $312.50.","traps":["That is one 25 percent step, and the second rise still has to be applied.","Nothing in the two steps lands at 256: 1.25 times 1.25 is 1.5625.","You added 25 dollars on the second step instead of 25 percent, which on 250 is 62.50.","You added the two rises into a single 50 percent, and sequential percents multiply instead.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1154","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $245 airline seat rises 67 percent over a holiday week. What is the new price?","opts":["80.85","164.15","178","312","409.15"],"correct":4,"why":"New = 245 × (1 + 67/100) = 245 × 1.67 = 409.15.","traps":["You took the 67% off the price instead of putting it on.","You stopped on the increase itself, 164.15, instead of adding it to the 245.","You subtracted 67 dollars when the price is going up.","You added 67 dollars instead of adding 67 percent of the 245.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-missing-value-mean#1xxivpg","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A student scored 92, 71, 87, 72 on 4 quizzes. What score is needed on the next quiz to bring the average to exactly 83?","opts":["10","83","85","93","98"],"correct":3,"why":"Needed total = 83 × 5 = 415. Current total = 322. Next score = 415 − 322 = 93.","traps":["You used 4 quizzes. After the next one there are 5.","Scoring the target only works if you were already AT the target. You have to make up the gap on every earlier quiz too.","You averaged the old average with the new score. The old average counts for several quizzes, not one.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Required total = target × number of quizzes; subtract what you already have."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1285","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"Each of 4 clinics received 5 crates of supplies and returned 2 of them. How many crates did the clinics keep in total?","opts":["3","8","12","16","18"],"correct":2,"why":"Distribute: 4·5 - 4·2 = 20 - 8 = 12.","traps":["That is just the 5 - 2 inside the parentheses, so the 4 outside never got multiplied in.","That is 4 times 2 on its own, so the 4 times 5 piece got dropped.",null,"Only a single 4 came off the 20 instead of 4 times 2, which leaves 16.","Distributing the 4 only to the 5 gives 20 - 2 = 18, leaving the 2 unmultiplied."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fam5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A delivery route runs 2/3 of a mile, then 5/6 of a mile further, then doubles back 1/2 of a mile. How many miles is the driver from the start?","opts":["1","3/4","2","7/6","5/6"],"correct":0,"why":"LCD = 6. 4/6 + 5/6 − 3/6 = 6/6 = 1.","traps":[null,"You drifted into fourths, but 3, 6 and 2 all fit inside sixths.","You added all three instead of subtracting the last one, 4/6 plus 5/6 plus 3/6.","You rescaled 1/2 as 2/6 instead of 3/6, leaving one sixth too much.","You rescaled 1/2 as 4/6 instead of 3/6, taking away one sixth too many."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1102","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"To the nearest tenth, what is 0.7 divided by 7.4?","opts":["0.01","0.1","0.2","0.95","10.6"],"correct":1,"why":"Move both decimal points one place and divide: 7 into 74, which is about 0.095. The nearest value on the list is 0.1. A size check helps first: 7.4 goes into 0.7 far less than once, so the answer has to be a small piece of 1.","traps":["You pushed the point two places too far left, making the answer a hundred times too small.",null,"You rounded 7.4 down to about 4 before dividing, which nearly doubles the size of the answer.","You moved the point one place too far right; 0.7 divided by 7.4 is about 0.095, not ten times that.","You divided 7.4 by 0.7 instead of the other way round."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:cou1580","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"From 6 items, how many different ordered arrangements of 2 can be formed?","opts":["8","12","15","30","36"],"correct":3,"why":"nPr = 6!/(6-2)! = 30.","traps":["Added 6 and 2.","Multiplied 6 by 2 instead of 6 by 5.","That counts unordered pairs; when order matters each pair counts twice.",null,"Treated it as 6 choices for both slots, which allows the same item twice."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#1xmzrot","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the SUM of the solutions of x² − 6x − 16 = 0?","opts":["6","−16","−6","16","10"],"correct":0,"why":"It factors as (x − 8)(x + 2), so the roots are 8 and −2. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = 6, product = c/a = −16.","traps":[null,"That is the PRODUCT of the roots (c/a). The sum is −b/a.","Sign slip. The sum of the roots is the OPPOSITE of the x coefficient: −b/a.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.","That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their sum."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:unt1761","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-14","topic":"Test-day execution","nights":[14],"d":1,"q":"You finish 30 untimed QR problems with 22 correct. What's your next step?","opts":["Move to timed practice immediately","Take a full mock","Redo the full set of 30 before reviewing anything","Skip QR for a week","Drill the 8 missed problems and re-test untimed"],"correct":4,"why":"22/30 = 73% - below 80% knowledge gate. Re-drill before timed.","traps":["Adding the clock before the untimed set is solid just runs the same misses faster.","A full mock measures everything at once, so it will not show what made these eight go wrong.","Rerunning all 30 spends the session on the 22 that already landed instead of the 8 that teach.","Stepping away leaves the eight misses unexamined, and they hold the whole lesson here.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1737","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A tank is meant to hold 6 liters, and its actual level of x liters is off by exactly 5 liters. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["5 or -5","11 or 1","11","1 or -11","-1 or 1"],"correct":1,"why":"|x − 6| = 5 means x − 6 = ±5, so x = 11 or 1.","traps":["You set x equal to plus or minus 5 and never undid the minus 6 inside the bars.",null,"You solved x - 6 = 5 and stopped, and the branch x - 6 = -5 gives 1 as well.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","That pairs 1 with its negative, and the other branch is 6 plus 5, which is 11."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1160","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A $37 water bill rises 33 percent. What is the new bill?","opts":["4","12.21","38.22","49.21","70"],"correct":3,"why":"New = 37 × (1 + 33/100) = 37 × 1.33 = 49.21.","traps":["You subtracted 33 dollars from 37, and this price is rising rather than falling.","That is 33 percent of 37 on its own, the increase before it joins the price.","That multiplies by 1.033, a 3.3 percent rise; 33 percent means multiplying by 1.33.",null,"You added 33 dollars instead of 33 percent."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1353","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A team of 22 volunteers is assigned to two sites in a ratio of 4 to 7. How many volunteers are at the smaller site?","opts":["2","4","8","11","14"],"correct":2,"why":"Total parts = 11; one part = 22/11 = 2. Smaller = 4·2 = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1029","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"What is the sum of 8/14 and 8/12?","opts":["37/28","26/21","16/26","64/168","6/21"],"correct":1,"why":"Reduce first: 8/14 is 4/7 and 8/12 is 2/3. Sevenths and thirds meet at 21, so 12/21 plus 14/21 is 26/21. Reducing before you pick a denominator keeps you out of the big numbers.","traps":["You forced both into twenty-eighths and turned 2/3 into 21/28, which is three quarters.",null,"You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 16 over 26.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You put the reduced tops, 4 and 2, over 21 without rescaling either one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rt3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Two cars start 200 miles apart, driving toward each other. One at 50 mph, the other 30 mph. When do they meet?","opts":["2.5 hr","2 hr","6.67 hr","5 hr","4 hr"],"correct":0,"why":"They close the gap at COMBINED speed: 50 + 30 = 80 mph. Time = 200 / 80 = 2.5 hours.","traps":[null,"You used 200/100 instead of 200/80.","Using only the slower car's 30 mph; both cars are eating into the gap.","Averaging the two speeds to 40 instead of adding them to 80.","You used one car's speed only."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1715","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A rack holds 3^2 trays and each tray holds 3^1 samples. How many samples fit in the rack?","opts":["3","9","12","27","729"],"correct":3,"why":"Same base × adds exponents: 3^(2+1) = 3^3 = 27.","traps":["You subtracted the exponents, which is what division does.","You multiplied the exponents, so you stayed at 3^2.","You added the two values, 9 and 3, instead of multiplying them.",null,"You multiplied the bases as well as adding the exponents, which gives 9^3."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261002"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s14"}},{"id":"FULL_3","name":"Full-Length Test 3","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"written:bio-5-4nakyd","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Both C4 and CAM plants keep carbon dioxide concentrated around RuBisCO. What separates the two strategies?","opts":["C4 skips RuBisCO entirely, while CAM still relies on it","C4 plants close their stomata at night, CAM plants close theirs at noon","C4 separates them by time of day, CAM separates them by place in the leaf","C4 separates the steps by place, CAM separates them by time","C4 plants use PEP carboxylase, CAM plants use RuBisCO alone"],"correct":3,"why":"Anchor each one to a picture. Corn is C4: two cell types side by side, so the separation is spatial. A cactus is CAM: one cell doing two jobs at different hours, so the separation is temporal, and cactus and clock both starting with C is a workable hook.","traps":["Both pathways rely on RuBisCO for the actual Calvin cycle, and the extra enzyme only concentrates carbon first.","C4 plants open their stomata by day like most plants, and CAM plants keep theirs shut through the heat of the day.","This is the reversal to watch for, since the cactus is the one working on a clock and the corn is the one working in two different cells.",null,"Both pathways use PEP carboxylase for that first capture."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d046","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"In the life cycle of a moss, which generation is dominant?","opts":["Spore","Gametophyte (n)","Both equally","Sporophyte","Neither, because mosses have no alternation of generations"],"correct":1,"why":"The dominant generation is the one you actually see as the green carpet, and in mosses that is the haploid gametophyte. The sporophyte grows up out of it as a slender stalk with a capsule and stays attached and nutritionally dependent on the green tissue below. Ferns and seed plants flip this, so keep the pair together: mosses are gametophyte plants, and everything from ferns onward is a sporophyte plant.","traps":["A spore is a single cell that starts the gametophyte, so it is a stage in the cycle rather than a multicellular generation.",null,"The two generations are strikingly unequal in mosses, with the green haploid plant doing nearly all of the living.","The moss sporophyte is the small stalk and capsule perched on the green plant and fed by it, so it is not the dominant stage.","Mosses do alternate generations, and they show it more visibly than most plants, since both stages sit in view at once."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1yx1bd4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Two membranes are identical except that one is built largely from phospholipids with unsaturated fatty acid tails. At a cool temperature, that membrane will be:","opts":["More fluid, because the kinks keep the tails from packing tightly","Less fluid, because the double bonds lock neighboring tails tightly together","Solid, because unsaturated tails crystallize first","Identical in fluidity, since only cholesterol matters","Completely impermeable to dissolved gases"],"correct":0,"why":"Picture the tails. A double bond puts a permanent kink in the chain, and kinked tails cannot stack neatly, so they keep sliding past one another as the temperature drops. Saturated tails are straight, pack tight, and solidify first. Kink means loose, straight means tight.","traps":[null,"A double bond kinks the chain and prevents tight packing rather than locking chains together.","Saturated tails solidify first, unsaturated tails stay mobile longer as temperature falls.","Tail saturation is a major determinant of fluidity right alongside cholesterol.","Small nonpolar gases cross any bilayer readily, whatever the tails look like."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g067","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a DNA cloning experiment, the term vector refers to which of the following?","opts":["Plasmid/phage carrying foreign DNA","Polymerase","The host bacterial cell that takes up the recombinant DNA","Insert DNA","Restriction enzyme"],"correct":0,"why":"Think of the vector as the vehicle. It is a piece of DNA that can replicate on its own inside a host cell, usually a bacterial plasmid or a bacteriophage, and the gene you care about is spliced into it so that the host copies your gene along with it. A useful vector also carries an origin of replication and a selectable marker, so you can pick out the cells that actually took one up.","traps":[null,"Polymerase copies DNA strands and is central to techniques such as PCR, but it carries nothing into a cell.","The host is the cellular factory that replicates the vector, and the two are separate components of the experiment.","The insert is the passenger gene being carried, not the vehicle that carries it.","Restriction enzymes cut the DNA so that ends match, but they are tools used on the vector rather than the vector itself."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-jthhks","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"A cactus opens its stomata only after dark. How does it still fix carbon in daylight?","opts":["It stores carbon dioxide as a gas in large vacuoles until morning","It takes up carbon dioxide through its roots while the stomata are shut","It ships the night's carbon to neighboring bundle sheath cells for daytime use","It locks the carbon into organic acids overnight and releases it by day","It runs the light reactions at night and the Calvin cycle during the day"],"correct":3,"why":"CAM is a night shift for gas exchange. Stomata open in the cool dark, carbon dioxide gets locked into malic acid and parked in the vacuole, then by day the acid hands that carbon back to RuBisCO while the stomata stay shut. Same trick as C4, concentrate carbon dioxide near the enzyme, but staged in time instead of in space.","traps":["Gas cannot be warehoused that way, since the carbon has to be locked into a molecule first.","Roots take up water and minerals, not the carbon dioxide used in photosynthesis.","Handing carbon to bundle sheath cells is the C4 solution, which separates the steps by place rather than by time.",null,"The light reactions need light, so they cannot run in the dark no matter how the carbon is stored."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p041","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"During quiet breathing at rest, the term tidal volume refers to which of the following?","opts":["The extra air that can be drawn in after a normal inhalation","Air moved per normal breath (~500 mL)","Residual volume","Vital capacity","Total lung capacity"],"correct":1,"why":"Lung volume questions reward one habit: anchor every term to a quiet breath and ask how far from that breath the term sits. Tidal volume is the quiet breath itself, close to 500 mL moving in and 500 mL moving out at rest, with the inspiratory and expiratory reserves stacked above and below it and residual volume at the bottom. The choice describing the air moved in one normal breath is the match.","traps":["That is inspiratory reserve volume, the extra room stacked above a resting breath, and it is counted separately from the resting breath itself.",null,"Residual volume is the air that stays behind after the hardest possible exhale, so it never moves during quiet breathing.","Vital capacity is the largest breath a person can move, from maximal inhalation to maximal exhalation, which is many times a resting breath.","Total lung capacity is everything the lungs hold at full inflation, which is the sum of all four volumes rather than a single resting breath."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p075","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Pepsin, the protein digesting enzyme of the stomach, requires which of the following in order to work?","opts":["Activation by enterokinase from the duodenum","Bile","Calcium","Acidic pH (stomach)","Alkaline pH"],"correct":3,"why":"Enzymes are shaped for the compartment they work in, and pepsin is built for the stomach. Hydrochloric acid from the parietal cells drops stomach pH to about 2, which both clips inactive pepsinogen into active pepsin and holds pepsin in its working shape, with each new pepsin activating more pepsinogen. When that mixture reaches the duodenum, bicarbonate raises the pH and pepsin shuts down, so the choice naming an acidic pH is the requirement.","traps":["Enterokinase is the duodenal enzyme that switches on trypsinogen; pepsinogen is switched on by stomach acid and then by pepsin itself.","Bile emulsifies fats downstream in the small intestine and plays no part in protein digestion in the stomach.","Calcium is a cofactor for several enzymes, including some clotting factors, but pepsin's requirement is a low pH.",null,"Alkaline conditions are what the pancreatic enzymes such as trypsin prefer in the duodenum, and that pH shuts pepsin down."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is characteristic of an r-selected species?","opts":["Late maturity","A Type I survivorship curve, with most deaths late in life","Many small offspring, no parental care","Few large offspring, parental care","Slow growth"],"correct":2,"why":"The r versus K split comes down to one question: is the environment unpredictable and open, or crowded and stable. An r-selected species treats reproduction like buying a stack of cheap lottery tickets, so it matures early, reproduces fast, produces many small offspring, and gives little or no parental care, because most of those young will die regardless. A K-selected species lives near carrying capacity where competition decides everything, so it invests heavily in a few well cared for young. Read the environment in the stem first and the traits follow.","traps":["Late maturity is a K-selected trait, while r-selected species mature early so they can reproduce before the habitat shifts.","Type I means high survival until old age, the K-selected pattern; r-selected species show Type III, with heavy loss of the young.",null,"This is the K-selected strategy, pouring investment into a few offspring in a stable, crowded environment.","Slow growth belongs to K-selected species; an r-selected species grows fast to exploit an opening before conditions change."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-d4b5kr","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A cell enters mitosis carrying a mutation that keeps cohesin from being cleaved at the centromere. Everything else works: chromosomes condense, the envelope breaks down, and the spindle attaches normally from both poles. What is the most likely result?","opts":["Replication restarts, because the cell reads its chromosomes as unduplicated","The chromatids stay joined and fail to segregate, so anaphase cannot complete","Anaphase runs on schedule, but each daughter cell receives only half the usual DNA","The cell skips metaphase entirely and moves straight into cytokinesis","The chromosomes never condense, so mitosis cannot get started at all"],"correct":1,"why":"Trace the one job the missing step was doing. Cohesin is the glue holding sister chromatids together, and cleaving it is the trigger that lets the spindle pull them apart. Attachment is fine here, so the cell reaches metaphase and tries to go on, but the chromatids are still glued and cannot be separated. Any question that removes cohesin cleavage is asking about segregation failure, not about entry into mitosis.","traps":["Replication is licensed once per cycle during S phase, and cohesin has no role in telling the cell whether its DNA has been copied.",null,"If the chromatids never come apart there is nothing to distribute, so anaphase cannot run normally in the first place.","Metaphase depends on spindle attachment, which the question tells you is working, so the cell has no reason to skip it.","Condensation is driven by a separate set of proteins and happens well before cohesin cleavage would ever matter."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p073","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Lactose from milk is broken down into glucose and galactose by which of the following?","opts":["Salivary amylase","Bile","Lactase (intestinal brush border)","Pepsin","Sucrase (also a brush border enzyme)"],"correct":2,"why":"Disaccharides get finished off right where they are absorbed. Lactase sits in the brush border of the small intestine epithelium and splits lactose into glucose and galactose, working alongside sucrase and maltase doing the same for their own sugars. Losing that enzyme with age leaves undigested lactose for colonic bacteria to ferment, which is the lactose intolerance picture, so the choice naming the brush border lactase is the one.","traps":["Salivary amylase starts on starch, breaking long glucose chains, and has no action on a disaccharide.","Bile is not an enzyme at all; it emulsifies fat into small droplets so that lipase can reach them.",null,"Pepsin is the stomach's protein enzyme, so it cuts peptide bonds rather than the bond joining two sugars.","Sucrase is lactase's neighbor on the same brush border, but each of these enzymes is specific and sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g028","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"If nondisjunction occurs during meiosis I, what is the outcome for the four gametes produced by that cell?","opts":["4 normal gametes","2 normal + 2 abnormal gametes","3 abnormal gametes and 1 normal gamete","No effect","4 abnormal gametes (all)"],"correct":4,"why":"The real question is when the error happens relative to the divisions that follow. In meiosis I the homologs fail to separate before either daughter cell exists, so both daughters are already wrong and each splits into two faulty gametes: two carry an extra chromosome and two are missing one. An error in meiosis II happens after the cells have split, so only one daughter is affected and half the gametes come out intact.","traps":["Nondisjunction means chromosomes did not separate properly, so some gametes must carry the wrong number.","This is the meiosis II picture, where the error happens after the first division, leaving one daughter cell untouched.","No pattern of nondisjunction leaves an odd count like this, because the error propagates to both products of whichever cell divided incorrectly, so affected gametes come in pairs.","An unbalanced chromosome count is exactly the kind of error that shows up as aneuploidy in the offspring.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_cm157","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-6","topic":"The central dogma","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"In a eukaryotic protein-coding gene, which sequence in the transcript marks the point where RNA polymerase II is brought to a stop?","opts":["The GU and AG sequences at the ends of introns","TATA","AAA","Stop codon UAA","AAUAAA polyadenylation signal"],"correct":4,"why":"This is testing whether you keep the signals for transcription and translation separate. The polymerase runs past a short hexamer in the transcript, and that hexamer recruits the machinery that cleaves the RNA and adds the poly-A tail, after which the polymerase lets go. Translation is what stops at a stop codon, read by the ribosome much later and in the cytoplasm. When a question names a sequence, ask first which machine reads it: polymerase or ribosome.","traps":["Those short sequences mark intron boundaries for the spliceosome, a processing step separate from where the polymerase stops.","The TATA box sits upstream in the promoter and helps transcription start, so it marks the beginning rather than the end.","A run of adenines is what gets added to the tail after cleavage, not the signal sequence that directs the cleavage.","A stop codon ends translation at the ribosome, and transcription continues well past it before the polymerase releases.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g041","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A large population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium for a gene with two alleles, A and a. If the frequency of the A allele (p) is 0.7, what is the expected frequency of heterozygous (Aa) individuals?","opts":["0.09","0.21","0.30","0.42","0.49"],"correct":3,"why":"Hardy-Weinberg hands you two equations, and the whole game is knowing which one a question wants. Allele frequencies add to one, so p of 0.7 makes q 0.3. Genotype frequencies also add to one, split as p squared for AA, 2pq for Aa, and q squared for aa, so heterozygotes are 2 times 0.7 times 0.3. That factor of 2 is the piece most often dropped, and it is there because a heterozygote can receive the A from either parent.","traps":["This is q squared, the frequency of homozygous recessive individuals.","This is p times q without the factor of 2, which leaves out the second way a heterozygote can be assembled.","This is q, the frequency of the a allele, and an allele frequency is not a genotype frequency.",null,"This is p squared, the frequency of homozygous dominant individuals."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d025","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"In which way do crustaceans differ from insects?","opts":["No molting","Smaller","Have biramous (branched) appendages, more body segments","No exoskeleton","They carry only one pair of antennae, while insects carry two pairs"],"correct":2,"why":"Both groups are arthropods, so anything they share, an exoskeleton of chitin, jointed appendages, molting to grow, cannot be the difference. What separates them is appendage design and body segmentation: crustacean limbs are often biramous, branching into two parts from a common base, and the body carries many more segments with appendages on the abdomen as well as the thorax. Insect limbs are uniramous, a single unbranched series, on a strictly three-part body. Antenna count is the other quick tell, with two pairs on a crustacean and one on an insect.","traps":["Every arthropod grows by molting its old exoskeleton, and a soft-shell crab is a crustacean caught right after doing exactly that.","Size is not a class trait, since crustaceans run from microscopic copepods to lobsters far larger than any insect.",null,"Crustaceans have a hard exoskeleton, often stiffened with calcium carbonate, which is why a crab shell is so rigid.","This has the numbers backwards: crustaceans carry two pairs of antennae and insects carry one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g075","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-9","topic":"Mendelian genetics","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"The penetrance of an allele refers to what?","opts":["How many offspring","How reliably the allele is passed on to the next generation","% of carriers showing phenotype","Expressivity","Severity"],"correct":2,"why":"Penetrance is a head count: out of everyone carrying the genotype, what fraction shows any sign of the trait at all. It is a yes or no question asked across a group. Keep it separate from expressivity, which asks how strongly the trait shows in the people who do have it.","traps":["Number of offspring is a measure of fertility, and penetrance says nothing about how many children a carrier has.","Transmission follows the ordinary rules of inheritance, while penetrance is about whether a carrier shows the trait.",null,"Expressivity is the variability in how strongly the trait appears, which is a different measurement from the fraction of carriers who show it at all.","Severity in an affected individual is expressivity, the companion term rather than this one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-1392213531","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which pairing of myelin-producing cell and location is correct?","opts":["Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system and oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system","Microglia in both systems","Oligodendrocytes in the peripheral nervous system and Schwann cells in the central nervous system","Astrocytes in the central nervous system and Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system","Ependymal cells in the central nervous system and satellite cells in the peripheral nervous system"],"correct":0,"why":"One Schwann cell wraps one segment of one peripheral axon. One oligodendrocyte reaches out and wraps segments on many central axons. Same job, different neighborhood.","traps":[null,"Microglia are the immune cleanup crew of the central nervous system. They do not make myelin.","Right cells, swapped locations. Schwann cells wrap PERIPHERAL axons.","Astrocytes support neurons and help form the blood brain barrier. They do not make myelin.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles and move cerebrospinal fluid. Neither of these makes myelin."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e030","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is a defining characteristic of the tundra biome?","opts":["Tall trees","Permafrost (permanently frozen subsoil)","High biodiversity","Dense coniferous forest with deep, well-drained soil","Tropical climate"],"correct":1,"why":"Tundra is defined by cold that never entirely lets go: a layer of permanently frozen subsoil called permafrost sits beneath a thin surface layer that thaws each summer. That frozen layer blocks deep roots and blocks drainage at the same time, which is why the landscape is treeless, boggy in summer, and low in species richness. The neighbor to keep it separate from is the taiga, which is also cold and has a short season but does thaw.","traps":["Frozen subsoil and the short season prevent deep roots, so the vegetation stays low: mosses, lichens, sedges, and dwarf shrubs.",null,"Harsh cold and a very short season keep tundra species richness low compared with warmer biomes.","This describes the taiga, the boreal conifer forest that lies just south of the tundra.","Tundra is the coldest biome, with a growing season measured in weeks rather than months."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p079","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Natural killer (NK) cells patrol the body and destroy virus infected and tumor cells. They are best classified as part of which of the following?","opts":["Innate immunity","T cell line","The myeloid cell line","Adaptive immunity","B cell line"],"correct":0,"why":"Sort immune cells with one question: does this cell have to learn its target first. Natural killer cells do not; they watch for cells that have lost their class I major histocompatibility complex display, which is exactly what many viruses and tumors do to hide from cytotoxic T cells, and they kill on contact with perforin and granzymes. That missing self trigger, plus the absence of memory, places them in innate immunity even though they develop along the lymphoid line.","traps":[null,"Cytotoxic T cells also kill infected cells, but they need antigen displayed on class I major histocompatibility complex, which is nearly the opposite of what triggers a natural killer cell.","Natural killer cells develop along the lymphoid line with B and T cells, even though they behave like an innate first responder.","Adaptive cells need prior exposure and build memory, while natural killer cells attack on first contact using general danger signals.","B cells make antibodies and carry antigen specific receptors, while natural killer cells kill without any antigen specific receptor at all."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d036","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is classified in which fungal phylum?","opts":["Basidiomycota","Chytridiomycota","Ascomycota","Glomeromycota","Zygomycota"],"correct":2,"why":"Yeasts are ascomycetes, the sac fungi, named for the ascus, the small sac in which their sexual spores form. The familiar members are the practical hooks: baker's and brewer's yeast, Penicillium, morels and truffles. Yeast is unusual for a fungus in being single celled and reproducing mostly by budding, so do not let the missing mushroom shape push you toward another group. Pair ascus with sac and basidium with club, and the two large phyla stay separate.","traps":["Basidiomycetes are the club fungi, the mushrooms, puffballs, shelf fungi and rusts, which make spores on basidia.","Chytrids are mostly aquatic fungi with flagellated spores, the only fungi that swim.",null,"Glomeromycetes are the arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi that grow inside plant roots and do not live as free single cells.","Zygomycetes are the bread molds such as Rhizopus, which form a thick-walled zygospore when two mating types fuse."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g020","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"How many Barr bodies would you expect in a somatic cell from a person with Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)?","opts":["3","2","0","The number varies from cell to cell, because X inactivation is random","1"],"correct":4,"why":"A Barr body is an inactivated X chromosome, and a cell keeps exactly one X active no matter how many it carries. So the count is always the number of X chromosomes minus one, which for XXY is one. Run that subtraction on any karyotype you are given and this whole family of questions becomes a single step.","traps":["Three Barr bodies would require four X chromosomes, which is not what this karyotype describes.","Two Barr bodies would require three X chromosomes, as in a 47,XXX karyotype.","Zero Barr bodies fits a typical 46,XY male or a 45,X female, where there is only one X to begin with and nothing to inactivate.","Which X gets silenced is random, and that is why females are mosaics, but how many get silenced is fixed at all but one.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p026","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Human chorionic gonadotropin, the hormone a pregnancy test detects, is secreted by the tissue of the implanting embryo. What does it maintain?","opts":["Endometrium directly","Corpus luteum (which makes progesterone)","Placenta","Pituitary","Corpus albicans (which then secretes estrogen)"],"correct":1,"why":"This is testing why a pregnancy does not end in a period. Normally the corpus luteum breaks down after about two weeks, progesterone falls, and the uterine lining sheds. The implanting embryo secretes human chorionic gonadotropin, which acts on the corpus luteum much as luteinizing hormone does and keeps it alive and making progesterone, so the lining stays put. Trace it as a chain: the embryo's hormone keeps the corpus luteum, the corpus luteum keeps progesterone, progesterone keeps the lining.","traps":["The lining is indeed held in place, but by progesterone; this hormone works one step upstream, on the structure that makes that progesterone.",null,"The developing placenta is where this hormone comes from, so this reverses the direction: it is the placenta's message, not the placenta's support.","This hormone comes from the embryo and acts on the ovary, and pituitary gonadotropin output actually falls during pregnancy rather than being propped up.","The corpus albicans is the scar left behind after the corpus luteum breaks down, and it no longer secretes hormones for anything to sustain."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-zfom9x","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"One Krebs cycle enzyme is not floating in the matrix. It is built into the inner mitochondrial membrane and doubles as a complex of the electron transport chain. Which reaction does it catalyze?","opts":["Succinate to fumarate","Malate to oxaloacetate","Succinyl-CoA to succinate","Citrate to isocitrate","Isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate"],"correct":0,"why":"The enzyme is succinate dehydrogenase, which is also complex II of the electron transport chain. Because it is wedged into the membrane, the electrons it strips off go onto FAD and straight into the chain rather than onto NAD+. That membrane address is the reason the cycle's only FADH2 comes from this one step.","traps":[null,"This last oxidation reduces NAD+ in the matrix and regenerates oxaloacetate for the next turn.","This is the substrate-level phosphorylation that makes GTP, and it takes place in the matrix.","This isomerization belongs to aconitase, a soluble matrix enzyme that just relocates a hydroxyl group.","This is the rate-limiting decarboxylation, run by a matrix enzyme that reduces NAD+."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e016","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the clearest example of directional selection?","opts":["A snail population in which both very pale and very dark shells survive better than intermediate ones","Antibiotic resistance, peppered moth darkening","Human birth weight","Sickle-cell maintenance","Cheetah genetics"],"correct":1,"why":"Directional selection favors one end of the range, so the whole distribution slides that way over generations and the average shifts. Bacteria under antibiotic pressure and peppered moths on soot darkened trees show the same shape of change: one extreme wins and the mean moves toward it. The question to ask yourself is which part of the curve is being favored, and if the answer is one end only, the selection is directional.","traps":["Favoring both extremes over the middle splits the population toward two ends at once, which is disruptive rather than directional.",null,"Birth weight sits near the middle with both extremes doing worse, which holds the average in place rather than shifting it.","The sickle cell pattern keeps two alleles in the population because the heterozygote is fittest, so nothing moves in a single direction.","The cheetah's low diversity comes from a crash in population size and drift, not from selection favoring one extreme of a trait."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e035","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In operant conditioning, negative reinforcement is:","opts":["Add aversive to decrease","Punishment","Removal of a desirable reward to decrease a behavior","Removal of aversive stimulus to increase a behavior","Add reward to increase"],"correct":3,"why":"Split the term in two and the confusion clears. Reinforcement versus punishment tells you the direction: reinforcement makes a behavior more likely, punishment makes it less likely. Positive versus negative tells you whether something is added or taken away, not whether it feels pleasant. Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant so the behavior increases, which is why buckling a seatbelt to stop the alarm is the textbook picture.","traps":["This is positive punishment, adding something unpleasant in order to decrease a behavior.","Punishment decreases a behavior, while reinforcement of either kind increases it, so these are not interchangeable.","This is negative punishment, taking away something desirable so the behavior becomes less likely.",null,"This is positive reinforcement, adding something pleasant rather than removing something unpleasant."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_cm155","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"In skeletal muscle and fat cells, glucose enters through the GLUT4 transporter. What does insulin do to this system?","opts":["Promotes GLUT4 insertion into PM","Binds GLUT4 directly and opens the transporters already in the membrane","Phosphorylates glucose","Activates SGLT","Inhibits GLUT4"],"correct":0,"why":"This is testing how a cell changes its glucose uptake: not by making one transporter work harder, but by changing how many transporters sit on the surface. Muscle and fat cells keep GLUT4 parked in internal vesicles, and insulin binding its receptor triggers those vesicles to fuse with the plasma membrane, so more doors appear and glucose flows in down its gradient. This is still facilitated diffusion, which is why no ATP is spent at the transporter itself. When a transport question mentions insulin, ask where the transporter is rather than how fast it turns over.","traps":[null,"Insulin binds its own receptor at the cell surface, not the transporter, and it acts by delivering more transporters to the membrane rather than opening ones already there.","Trapping glucose inside the cell by phosphorylating it is the work of hexokinase; insulin is a signaling hormone, not an enzyme acting on the sugar.","The sodium-linked glucose transporters work in the intestine and kidney to pull glucose against its gradient, a separate system from the insulin-responsive one.","Insulin is the signal that blood glucose is high, so it has to increase uptake into muscle and fat rather than shut it down."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g085","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A Western blot separates a sample by gel electrophoresis, transfers the separated material onto a membrane, and then probes that membrane with a labeled reagent. Which class of molecule does this technique detect?","opts":["Intact cells, sorted one at a time by the markers on their surface","RNA","DNA","Proteins (using antibodies)","Lipids"],"correct":3,"why":"The blot family is named by what is being probed: Southern for DNA, Northern for RNA, Western for protein. In a Western blot the proteins are separated by size, transferred to a membrane, and then found with antibodies raised against the protein of interest, so a band tells you the protein is present and roughly how large it is. The memory hook is that antibodies bind proteins, so the assay built on antibodies reports protein.","traps":["Sorting whole cells by surface markers describes flow cytometry, while a Western blot begins by breaking the cells open, so no intact cell survives the first step.","Probing RNA on a membrane is a Northern blot, again with a nucleic acid probe rather than an antibody.","Probing DNA on a membrane is a Southern blot, which uses a complementary nucleic acid probe rather than an antibody.",null,"Lipids are analyzed by chromatography or mass spectrometry; they do not resolve into antibody labeled bands in this kind of assay."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1v7ag0l","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Apoptosis runs on a family of enzymes that sit in the cell at all times as inactive precursors and are switched on in a cascade once the decision to die is made. What type of enzyme are they, and how does the cascade work?","opts":["Nucleases, activated by a calcium surge, which cut the plasma membrane into blebs","Ligases, activated by ATP binding, which crosslink the cytoskeleton into a rigid mesh","Kinases, activated by cyclin binding, which phosphorylate the nuclear lamins","Phosphatases, activated by cytochrome c, which strip phosphate off the mitotic spindle","Proteases, switched on by cleavage, initiator caspases activating executioner ones"],"correct":4,"why":"Caspases are proteases, and the name is worth unpacking: they carry a cysteine in the active site and cut their targets after aspartate residues. They wait in the cytosol as inactive procaspases, which lets a cell commit to dying in seconds without having to make anything new. Initiator caspases are activated first and then cleave executioner caspases, which go on to cut hundreds of cell proteins. Cleaving is both how they get switched on and how they do their work.","traps":["Nucleases do act later in apoptosis to chop up the DNA, but they are downstream, and a nuclease cannot cut a membrane.","Ligases join molecules together, and apoptosis works by taking cell structures apart rather than stiffening them.","Lamin phosphorylation is how a cell takes its nuclear envelope apart for mitosis, which is a different process run by a different enzyme.","Cytochrome c does belong to apoptosis, but what it triggers is a protease cascade, and stripping phosphates off the spindle is not part of cell death.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-3vie62","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Under the microscope, a dividing cell shows a single tight line of chromosomes across the middle of the cell, each one gripped from both sides. Which stage is this, and what holds the chromosomes in that line?","opts":["Anaphase, held in place by the contractile ring forming around the equator","Metaphase, held by balanced pull from microtubules reaching to each pole","Prophase, held together by a nuclear envelope that has not yet broken down","Interphase, held by the cell plate that is forming across the equator","Telophase, held by the two nuclear envelopes that are reassembling nearby"],"correct":1,"why":"Metaphase is defined by the balance, not only by the position. Each chromosome is attached on one face to microtubules from one pole and on the other face to microtubules from the other pole, and the plate is exactly where those two pulls come out even. That balance is also what the spindle checkpoint is measuring. When you see one tidy line with attachments on both sides, that is metaphase every time.","traps":["In anaphase the chromatids have already left the middle and are on their way to the poles, so you would see two groups rather than one line.",null,"In prophase the chromosomes are condensed but scattered inside an intact nucleus, with nothing yet gripping them.","Interphase chromosomes are decondensed and invisible as separate bodies, and a cell plate belongs to plant cytokinesis after mitosis is over.","In telophase the chromosomes are clustered at the two poles and unwinding, not lined up across the middle."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p056","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Filtration of plasma from the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule is driven mainly by which of the following?","opts":["Active transport","Colloid osmotic pressure of the plasma proteins","Aquaporin gradient","Diffusion only","Hydrostatic pressure"],"correct":4,"why":"Filtration here is a plumbing question rather than a transport question. Blood enters the glomerulus through a wide afferent arteriole and leaves through a narrower efferent one, which holds capillary hydrostatic pressure high and pushes water and small solutes through the filtration barrier in bulk. Net filtration is that pushing pressure minus the opposing plasma colloid osmotic pressure and capsular pressure, so the choice naming hydrostatic pressure is the driving force.","traps":["Filtration at the glomerulus costs the kidney no ATP directly; active transport shows up later, during reabsorption along the tubule.","Plasma proteins stay behind in the capillary and their osmotic pull works against filtration, holding fluid in rather than pushing it out.","Aquaporins move water in the collecting duct under antidiuretic hormone control, well downstream of the filtration step.","Diffusion moves individual solutes down their own gradients, while filtration pushes water and small solutes together through the filtration slits.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g033","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Roan cattle have coats containing both fully red hairs and fully white hairs intermixed, and they result from crossing red cattle with white cattle. This coat pattern is an example of:","opts":["Codominance","Random X-inactivation, as in calico cats","Incomplete dominance","Epistasis","Polygenic"],"correct":0,"why":"The test for codominance is whether the heterozygote displays both parental products at full strength rather than an average of them. Look closely at a roan coat and it is not pink: it is red hairs and white hairs side by side, each individual hair committed to one allele's product. Compare that with a pink snapdragon, where every cell makes an in-between amount of a single pigment, and the difference between codominance and incomplete dominance stops being slippery.","traps":[null,"That mechanism creates the patchwork coat of calico cats and applies to X-linked genes in females, while roan appears in bulls and cows alike.","Incomplete dominance would give one uniform intermediate shade across the whole coat, not two distinct hair colors on the same animal.","Epistasis requires a second gene masking the first, and a single coat color gene accounts for this cross.","Polygenic inheritance produces a smooth range of shades across many offspring, rather than two discrete hair colors within one animal."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The lens of the eye develops from which embryonic tissue?","opts":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Mesoderm and neural crest together","Endoderm","Neural crest"],"correct":0,"why":"This is the payoff of the classic induction experiment. The optic vesicle grows out from the forebrain and presses against the surface ectoderm of the head, and that contact instructs the surface ectoderm to thicken into a lens placode, fold inward, and pinch off as the lens. Hold the geometry in mind: the retina comes from the brain side, and the lens comes from the outer covering that the brain reached out and touched.","traps":[null,"Mesoderm in the head forms the muscles that move the eye and the vessels that supply it, but not the clear lens itself.","Both of those tissues contribute to the eye, supplying muscles and corneal layers among other parts, but neither one gives rise to the lens.","Endoderm never reaches the surface of the head, since it is committed to the lining of the gut tube and the organs that bud from it.","Neural crest does build parts of the eye, including much of the cornea and the stroma of the iris, which makes this tempting, but the lens comes from the surface layer instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-a90f3aa0fb","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which statement about cortisol is correct?","opts":["It is a steroid from the adrenal cortex that lowers blood glucose","It is a steroid from the adrenal cortex that raises blood glucose","It strengthens the immune response during stress","It is an amino acid derivative from the adrenal medulla that raises heart rate within seconds","It is released in response to TSH from the anterior pituitary"],"correct":1,"why":"Cortisol is the long-haul stress hormone. ACTH from the anterior pituitary tells the adrenal cortex to release it. It keeps fuel available by raising blood glucose and it quiets the immune system.","traps":["Cortisol RAISES blood glucose by promoting new glucose production.",null,"Cortisol suppresses inflammation and immunity, which is why related drugs are used as anti-inflammatories.","That describes epinephrine. Cortisol is a CORTEX steroid and acts over hours.","The signal for cortisol is ACTH, not TSH."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1xq2vux","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A liver cell is flush with ATP and has very little AMP. What happens to glycolysis, and through which enzyme?","opts":["It speeds up, because ATP activates phosphofructokinase","It is unaffected, because glycolysis is controlled only by how much glucose is available","It speeds up, because ATP is the substrate of the investment phase","It slows down, because ATP inhibits pyruvate kinase, the committed step","It slows down, because ATP inhibits phosphofructokinase"],"correct":4,"why":"This is feedback inhibition asked in a cell-biology costume. Phosphofructokinase catalyzes the committed step, so it is the valve the cell turns: ATP and citrate close it while AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate open it. Any time a question hands you the energy state of a cell and asks about glycolysis, go straight to that enzyme.","traps":["ATP does bind phosphofructokinase at a regulatory site, but it turns the enzyme down rather than up.","Substrate supply matters, but the pathway also reads the cell's energy state through feedback inhibition.","ATP is consumed in the investment phase, but being a substrate there does not set the pathway's rate.","Right logic, wrong enzyme: the committed, rate-limiting step of glycolysis is the one phosphofructokinase runs.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p033","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"At the beginning of ventricular systole, which valves close?","opts":["Semilunar (aortic + pulm)","The mitral valve only, since the left side carries the higher pressure","Both at same time","No valves","AV (tricuspid + mitral)"],"correct":4,"why":"Valves are pushed by pressure differences, so track the pressure. As the ventricles begin to contract, pressure inside them rises above atrial pressure and slams the atrioventricular valves shut, which makes the first heart sound, while the semilunar valves stay shut until ventricular pressure climbs past aortic and pulmonary pressure and forces them open. Only when the ventricles relax at the end of systole do the semilunar valves close, giving the second sound.","traps":["The semilunar valves open during systole so blood can leave, and they close as the ventricles relax at the end of it.","Both sides contract together, so the tricuspid on the right shuts at essentially the same moment as the mitral on the left.","If both pairs were shut together through systole, blood could neither enter nor leave the ventricle.","The first heart sound exists precisely because valves close as systole begins.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-f98995ebcf","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which hormone is released from the posterior pituitary but is actually made in the hypothalamus?","opts":["Prolactin","Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)","Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)","Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)","Growth hormone (GH)"],"correct":2,"why":"The posterior pituitary is a storage closet, not a factory. Hypothalamic neurons make ADH and oxytocin, ship them down their axons, and store them in the posterior lobe until release. The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones.","traps":["Prolactin is made and released by the anterior pituitary.","TSH is an anterior pituitary hormone.",null,"ACTH is an anterior pituitary hormone.","GH is made AND released by the anterior pituitary."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1lljp1j","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Assuming every salt dissociates completely, which of these solutions has the highest osmolarity?","opts":["0.15 M KCl","0.1 M glucose","0.1 M CaCl2","0.3 M glucose","0.2 M NaCl"],"correct":4,"why":"Convert each solution to particles per liter, then compare. Glucose stays as one particle, so 0.3 M glucose is 300 mOsm, calcium chloride releases three particles so 0.1 M is 300 mOsm, potassium chloride releases two so 0.15 M is 300 mOsm, and sodium chloride releases two so 0.2 M is 400 mOsm. Spotting that three of them tie at 300 is the fastest way through.","traps":["Potassium chloride releases two particles, so 0.15 times 2 gives 300 mOsm.","This is only 100 mOsm, the lowest of the group.","Calcium chloride releases three particles, so 0.1 times 3 gives 300 mOsm.","Glucose does not dissociate, so this one is 300 mOsm.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-a4a4xb","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A brightly lit leaf that is actively fixing carbon is suddenly moved into the dark, with carbon dioxide still available. In the first seconds, what happens to its RuBP and 3-PGA levels?","opts":["Neither changes until the leaf runs out of carbon dioxide","Both fall immediately","RuBP rises and 3-PGA falls","RuBP falls and 3-PGA rises","Both rise together"],"correct":3,"why":"Ask which step stalls first. Darkness cuts off ATP and NADPH, so the reduction step stops, while RuBisCO keeps attaching carbon dioxide to whatever RuBP is left. The molecule just before a blocked step piles up and the one just after it drains, and that rule reads any blocked pathway on any section of the exam.","traps":["The Calvin cycle depends on ATP and NADPH from the light reactions, so darkness shows up within seconds.","Fixation keeps running for a few seconds in the dark, so 3-PGA is produced rather than lost.","That is the pattern for cutting off carbon dioxide instead of light, and it is the reversal students land on most often.",null,"3-PGA does rise, but RuBP cannot rise while it is still being consumed and no longer being rebuilt."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-oggnpm","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Exactly one reaction in glycolysis reduces NAD+ to NADH. Which one?","opts":["Glucose to glucose-6-phosphate","1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate split into two three-carbon sugars","Phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate"],"correct":2,"why":"You are being asked to find the oxidation step. Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate is oxidized as an inorganic phosphate is attached, and the electrons pulled off land on NAD+. Look for the aldehyde turning into an acyl phosphate: that is where the electrons leave, and because one glucose gives two of these molecules, one glucose makes two NADH here.","traps":["This is the opening phosphorylation, which spends ATP and moves no electrons.","This step hands a phosphate to ADP, so what it makes is ATP rather than NADH.",null,"This is the cut made by aldolase, a rearrangement with no oxidation involved.","This is the final step of the pathway, and it also makes ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g086","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes aneuploidy from polyploidy?","opts":["Aneuploidy is the gain of entire extra chromosome sets, while polyploidy is the gain or loss of a single chromosome","Aneuploidy = duplication","Polyploidy is point mutation","Aneuploidy = wrong # of one chromosome; polyploidy = entire extra sets","Same"],"correct":3,"why":"Count what actually changed. Aneuploidy means the number of one particular chromosome is off, as in trisomy 21, where the total comes to 47 rather than 46. Polyploidy means the whole set was duplicated, as in a triploid cell with 69 chromosomes, which is common in plants and not survivable in humans. If the count is a clean multiple of the haploid number, it is polyploid; if one chromosome is off on its own, it is aneuploid.","traps":["The two definitions have been swapped here, which is the commonest way these terms get mixed up.","Duplication of a segment is a structural change within one chromosome, while aneuploidy is about how many whole chromosomes are present.","A point mutation changes a single base inside a gene, a scale far below whole chromosomes, so it cannot define polyploidy.",null,"These are two different kinds of error, one affecting a single chromosome and the other affecting every chromosome at once."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev022","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Protostomes and deuterostomes differ in how early the fate of each cell in the embryo is fixed. Which statement correctly contrasts determinate cleavage with indeterminate cleavage?","opts":["Indeterminate cleavage is the protostome pattern and determinate cleavage is the deuterostome pattern, so protostomes are the group that can form identical twins","Same","Determinate (proto): fates fixed early. Indeterminate (deutero): cells totipotent, so identical twins are possible","Determinate is faster","Both can give twins"],"correct":2,"why":"This is testing whether you can attach each cleavage label to the right group and then to a real consequence. Determinate means every blastomere's fate is locked in early, the protostome pattern, so an isolated early cell builds only the piece it was assigned. Indeterminate means the early blastomeres can each still build a whole animal, the deuterostome pattern, which is why one embryo can split and give identical twins. Recognize it by the consequence: if separating early cells still yields two whole embryos, cleavage was indeterminate.","traps":["This has the two groups swapped: protostomes are the determinate group, and identical twins come from the indeterminate cleavage of deuterostomes.","Treating the two words as synonyms misses that they name opposite outcomes for an isolated blastomere: one can still build a whole embryo and one cannot.",null,"Speed is not what these terms describe; they describe how early each cell's developmental fate is locked in, not how fast the divisions run.","This assumes any early embryo can split into two complete animals, but once fates are determined an isolated blastomere makes only its assigned part."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"}],"gc":[{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#xp7b40","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 51 g of NH₃? (molar mass 17 g/mol)","opts":["6.02 × 10²³","1.81 × 10²⁴","9","5.42 × 10²⁴","3.07 × 10²⁵"],"correct":3,"why":"51 g / 17 g/mol = 3 mol NH₃. Times 3 hydrogen per molecule = 9 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 5.42 × 10²⁴.","traps":["You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","That is the number of MOLECULES. Each NH₃ holds 3 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 3.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.",null,"You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Use Hess's law to combine: (1) C(s) + ½O₂(g) → CO(g), ΔH₁ = −110 kJ/mol; (2) CO(g) + ½O₂(g) → CO₂(g), ΔH₂ = −283 kJ/mol. Adding (1) and (2) gives C(s) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g). What is ΔH for this overall reaction?","opts":["−393 kJ/mol","+393 kJ/mol","−283 kJ/mol","−173 kJ/mol","−110 kJ/mol"],"correct":0,"why":"Hess's law: ΔH_overall = ΔH₁ + ΔH₂ = (−110) + (−283) = −393 kJ/mol. Subtracting instead of adding gives −173; reversing one reaction's sign gives wrong sign. The two-step path equals the direct path because enthalpy is a state function.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-limiting#5e30zy","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"N₂ + 3 H₂ → 2 NH₃\n\nIf 1.5 mol of N₂ reacts with 12 mol of H₂, how many moles of NH₃ can form?","opts":["4 mol","13.5 mol","1.5 mol","3 mol","8 mol"],"correct":3,"why":"N₂: 1.5 / 1 = 1.5. H₂: 12 / 3 = 4. The smaller one limits. Product = 1.5 × 2 = 3 mol.","traps":["Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You added the reactants. Moles are not conserved; use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","You compared raw moles. Divide each by its coefficient first, then pick the smaller.",null,"You built the answer from the reagent in EXCESS. The smaller moles-per-coefficient runs out first and sets the yield."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A reaction has ΔH = -120 kJ/mol and ΔS = -150 J/(K·mol). Spontaneity requires ΔG = ΔH - T·ΔS &lt; 0. Set ΔG = 0 to find the crossover temperature, then state the temperature range over which the reaction is spontaneous.","opts":["Above 800 K only","Spontaneous at all T","Below 800 K","Below 80 K","Below 120 K"],"correct":2,"why":"Set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS = (-120,000 J)/(-150 J/K) = 800 K. Below 800 K, the favorable enthalpy dominates the unfavorable entropy. Above 800 K, T·|ΔS| exceeds |ΔH| and the reaction becomes nonspontaneous.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:lc6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"For 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), ΔH &lt; 0. To MAXIMIZE SO₃ yield, you should:","opts":["↓T, ↑P","↑T, ↑P","↑T, ↓P","Add argon at constant volume","↓T, ↓P"],"correct":0,"why":"Exo (heat = product), so low T pushes forward. 3 mol gas → 2 mol gas, so high P pushes forward (toward fewer mol). Industrial constraint: too low T = too slow, so compromise temperature is used.","traps":[null,"High pressure is the right half, but heating an exothermic reaction pushes it back toward SO₂ and O₂.","Both halves work against the yield: heating reverses an exothermic reaction and low pressure favors the three-mole side.","Argon at fixed volume raises the total pressure while leaving every partial pressure alone, so the yield does not move.","Low temperature is the right half, but dropping the pressure lets the system drift toward the three-mole side."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lf009","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A 5.00 g sample of an unknown hydrocarbon is combusted completely in excess oxygen. Analysis of the products yields 15.4 g of CO2 and 6.30 g of H2O. The molar mass of the compound is determined separately to be 86 g/mol. What is the molecular formula?","opts":["C6H10 (cyclohexene)","C5H10 (cyclopentane)","C6H14 (hexane)","C7H10","C5H12 (pentane)"],"correct":2,"why":"mol C = 15.4/44 = 0.350; mol H = 2(6.30/18) = 0.700. C:H = 0.350:0.700 = 1:2, so empirical CH2 (mass 14). MW 86 → 86/14 = 6.14 ≈ 6 → C6H14 (hexane), but 6×14 = 84, not 86 - using 86/MW(CH2) = 86/14.03 ≈ 6.13 close to 6; the molecular formula is C6H14 (M = 86.18).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lr-e11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"An organic chemist calculates the theoretical yield of an aldol condensation as 50.0 g and isolates 45.0 g of pure aldol product after column chromatography. Use % yield = (actual / theoretical) × 100 to compute the percent yield.","opts":["75%","80%","85%","90%","95%"],"correct":3,"why":"% yield = (45.0 / 50.0) × 100 = 90.0%. Most published organic syntheses report yields between 50% and 95%; &gt;100% yields suggest residual solvent or impurities.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ph5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A blood plasma sample at 37°C has pH = 4.50 (an unusually acidic test sample). Using the inverse relation [H⁺] = 10^(−pH), what is the hydrogen-ion concentration in mol/L?","opts":["3.2×10⁻⁴ M","1.0×10⁻⁴ M","1.0×10⁻⁵ M","4.5 M","3.2×10⁻⁵ M"],"correct":4,"why":"[H⁺] = 10^(−4.50) = 10^(−5) × 10^(0.50) = 1.0×10⁻⁵ × 3.16 ≈ 3.2×10⁻⁵ M. Rounding the half-decade incorrectly toward 10⁻⁴ or 10⁻⁵ alone is the common error; pH 4.50 lies BETWEEN these orders of magnitude.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:p-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Which has the LARGEST 2nd ionization energy: Na, Mg, Al, K?","opts":["Na","All four are about equal","Mg","K","Al"],"correct":0,"why":"IE2 = energy to remove the SECOND electron (after IE1 has already pulled one off). The rule: IE jumps when the next electron must come from a noble-gas core. Na is [Ne]3s¹ → Na⁺ is already [Ne]. Pulling another electron off Na⁺ means cracking the full octet - huge jump. Mg, Al still have valence electrons after IE1. K is [Ar]4s¹ → K⁺ is also [Ar], so K shows a similar jump, but Na⁺ is smaller and has higher effective nuclear charge, so its IE2 is the biggest.","traps":[null,"They are far apart: about 1451 kJ/mol for magnesium against about 4562 for sodium, because only sodium and potassium have to break into a noble gas core.","Magnesium's second removal takes 3s¹ down to the neon core, still a valence electron, so at about 1451 kJ/mol its IE2 is the gentlest of the four.","K is a great candidate - K⁺ is [Ar], so its IE2 also jumps. But Na⁺ is smaller and more tightly held than K⁺, so Na has the higher IE2. (Across a period IE rises; down a group IE falls.)","Aluminium goes from Al⁺ ([Ne]3s²) to Al²⁺ ([Ne]3s¹), still inside the 3s subshell, so no core is being broken."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 1.00 L buffer of 0.10 M HA and 0.10 M A- (pKa 5.00) receives a 0.05 mol bolus of HCl, which converts 0.05 mol of A- into HA. New concentrations: [HA] = 0.15 M, [A-] = 0.05 M. Using Henderson-Hasselbalch, find the resulting pH.","opts":["4.00","4.52","4.74","5.00","5.48"],"correct":1,"why":"pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]) = 5.00 + log(0.05/0.15) = 5.00 + log(0.333) = 5.00 - 0.477 = 4.52. The buffer absorbed the proton and pH dropped only ~0.5 unit instead of crashing to ~1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A reaction stoichiometry problem requires the product 4.56 x 1.4 (concentration in M times path length in cm). The calculator displays 6.384, but proper sig-fig rules for multiplication state the answer keeps the smallest number of significant figures among the factors. What is the correctly reported result?","opts":["6","6.38","6.384","6.4","6.40"],"correct":3,"why":"Multiplication retains the smallest sig-fig count of the factors. 4.56 has 3 sig figs and 1.4 has 2, so the product keeps 2. 6.384 rounds to 6.4.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:at-e9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Average atomic mass of Cl if isotopes are ³⁵Cl (75%) and ³⁷Cl (25%)?","opts":["35.0","35.5","36.0","36.5","37.0"],"correct":1,"why":"Avg = 0.75(35) + 0.25(37) = 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.5.","traps":["35.0 takes the most abundant isotope on its own and skips the weighting.",null,"36.0 averages the two masses evenly, which would need a 50-50 split rather than 75 and 25.","Swapping the percentages, 25 percent of 35 plus 75 percent of 37, gives 36.5 and tilts the average toward the rarer isotope.","37.0 is the heavier isotope alone, and it makes up only a quarter of the sample."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_rp-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"At 25 °C, the cell reaction is 2 Ag+(aq) + Cu(s) -&gt; 2 Ag(s) + Cu^(2+)(aq), with E°(Ag+/Ag) = +0.80 V and E°(Cu^(2+)/Cu) = +0.34 V. Using E°_cell = E°_cathode - E°_anode (potentials are intensive, NOT multiplied by stoichiometric coefficients), what is E°_cell?","opts":["+1.60 V","+1.14 V","+0.46 V","+0.92 V","-0.46 V"],"correct":2,"why":"Cathode (reduction): Ag+ + e- -&gt; Ag, E° = +0.80 V. Anode (oxidation): Cu -&gt; Cu^(2+), E°_red = +0.34 V. E°_cell = 0.80 - 0.34 = +0.46 V. NOT multiplied by 2, since potentials are intensive.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Argon (Z = 18) has the noble-gas configuration [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶ with 18 electrons. Among the following common ions, identify the one that is BOTH isoelectronic with Ar AND carries a +2 charge.","opts":["Ca²⁺ (Z = 20)","K⁺ (Z = 19)","Cl⁻ (Z = 17)","S²⁻ (Z = 16)","Sc³⁺ (Z = 21)"],"correct":0,"why":"Isoelectronic with Ar means 18 electrons. Ca has Z = 20 and protons = 20; Ca²⁺ has lost 2 electrons → 18 e⁻, matching Ar. K⁺ and S²⁻ are also 18-electron species but carry +1 and -2 charges respectively, failing the +2 requirement.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A 5.0% (w/w) NaCl solution has a measured density of 1.04 g/mL at 20 °C, and the molar mass of NaCl is 58.4 g/mol. Assuming the solution is at the same temperature and well mixed, what is the molarity of NaCl in this solution?","opts":["5.0 M","1.04 M","0.45 M","1.78 M","0.89 M"],"correct":4,"why":"Per 1.00 L: mass = 1040 g, mass NaCl = 0.050 x 1040 = 52.0 g, mol = 52.0/58.4 = 0.890 mol. Therefore M = 0.89 mol/L. Skipping the density factor gives ~0.86, while doubling gives 1.78.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gl-e11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A fixed sample of ideal gas initially has P1 = 2.0 atm, V1 = 10. L, and T1 = 300. K. The gas is then warmed and allowed to expand to a final volume V2 = 20. L at a final temperature T2 = 600. K. Using the combined gas law, what is the final pressure P2?","opts":["0.50 atm","2.0 atm","3.0 atm","1.0 atm","4.0 atm"],"correct":1,"why":"Combined gas law: P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2, so P2 = P1 x (V1/V2) x (T2/T1) = 2.0 x (10/20) x (600/300) = 2.0 atm. The volume doubles (halving P) but T doubles (doubling P), so P stays the same.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lw4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Compounds such as SF₆ (six S-F bonds) and PCl₅ (five P-Cl bonds) place more than 8 electrons around the central atom. Octet expansion requires accessible empty d-orbitals on the central atom, available only for period 3 and beyond. Which of the following CAN expand its octet?","opts":["Fluorine","Oxygen","Nitrogen","Sulfur","Carbon"],"correct":3,"why":"Octet expansion requires available d-orbitals, which appear starting in period 3. Sulfur (period 3) can use its 3d orbitals to host &gt;8 valence electrons (e.g., SF₆ with 12). Period 2 elements C, N, O, F have no 2d subshell and cannot expand.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A reaction at 1 atm has both ΔH &gt; 0 (endothermic) and ΔS &gt; 0 (positive entropy change). Using the equation ΔG = ΔH − T·ΔS, the spontaneity depends on temperature. Under what temperature regime is the reaction spontaneous (ΔG &lt; 0)?","opts":["Never spontaneous at any T","Always spontaneous at any T","Spontaneous only at T = ΔH/ΔS","Spontaneous only at high T","Spontaneous only at low T"],"correct":3,"why":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. With ΔH &gt; 0 and ΔS &gt; 0, raising T makes the −TΔS term dominate negatively. Spontaneity (ΔG &lt; 0) requires T &gt; ΔH/ΔS.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:nr-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"For Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, [Cu²⁺] = 0.001 M, n = 2, E° = +0.34 V. Find E.","opts":["0.34 V","0.16 V","0.25 V","0.43 V","0.31 V"],"correct":2,"why":"Q = 1/[Cu²⁺] = 1000. E = 0.34 − (0.0296)(3) = 0.34 − 0.0888 ≈ 0.25 V.","traps":["0.34 V is the standard-state value, which only holds when Cu²⁺ sits at 1 M.","0.16 V uses the full 0.0592 instead of 0.0592 divided by the 2 electrons, so the correction is twice as large as it should be.",null,"0.43 V flips the sign of the log term: Q is 1/[Cu²⁺] = 1000, so log Q is +3 and the correction comes off E°.","0.31 V subtracts the 0.0296 term once and leaves out the log Q = 3 multiplier."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#wl7b3c","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 1.5 m aqueous solution of glucose? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["2.79 °C","-2.79 °C","-4.19 °C","-0.768 °C","-5.58 °C"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (1)(1.86)(1.5) = 2.79 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 2.79 = -2.79 °C.","traps":["Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","glucose is a molecular solute. It does not split into ions, so i = 1."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:col-q8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A student dissolves 5.85 g NaCl (MW = 58.5) in 100. g water and measures ΔTf = 3.5 °C. The expected ΔTf assuming complete dissociation (i = 2) is 3.72 °C. The most likely explanation:","opts":["some of the NaCl never dissolved, so the true molality was lower","ion pairing in solution makes effective i slightly less than 2","water has Kf &lt; 1.86 in concentrated solutions","NaCl decomposed during dissolution","the experiment was run above the boiling point"],"correct":1,"why":"Real ionic solutions show ion pairing - Na⁺ and Cl⁻ briefly associate, behaving as one particle. So effective i drops from 2 to ~1.88. This is exactly why measured ΔTf is slightly LESS than the i = 2 prediction.","traps":["NaCl dissolves to about 36 g per 100 g of water, so 5.85 g goes fully into solution; the shortfall comes from ion pairing instead.",null,"Kf is a property of the SOLVENT (water), not the solute concentration. Kf is constant at 1.86.","NaCl is stable in water - it does not decompose. The discrepancy is a solution-phase effect, not a chemical reaction.","Freezing-point measurement is done near 0 °C, well below boiling. Temperature is not the issue."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_rl-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"A reaction A -&gt; products at 298 K is studied with several different starting concentrations [A]_0, and the measured half-life is found to be CONSTANT (independent of [A]_0) for all trials. Using the integrated rate-law dependence of t_(1/2) on [A]_0, what is the order of the reaction with respect to A?","opts":["Zero order","Third order","Second order","Half order","First order"],"correct":4,"why":"Only first-order reactions have a half-life t_(1/2) = ln(2)/k that is independent of [A]_0. Zero-order t_(1/2) is proportional to [A]_0; second-order t_(1/2) is inversely proportional to [A]_0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ca5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"A 0.500 g sample of benzoic acid is combusted in a sealed bomb calorimeter (constant volume) with heat capacity C_cal = 5.00 kJ/°C. The combustion releases 2.50 kJ of heat, all absorbed by the calorimeter assembly. What temperature change ΔT does the calorimeter experience?","opts":["5.0°C","12.5°C","0.50°C","25°C","2.5°C"],"correct":2,"why":"For a bomb calorimeter, q_rxn = -C_cal·ΔT. Solving for ΔT: ΔT = q_absorbed/C_cal = 2.50 kJ / 5.00 kJ/°C = 0.500°C. Note that constant-volume calorimetry uses the calorimeter constant C_cal directly, not m·c, since C_cal already lumps in all components.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:ar-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Catalyst lowers Eₐ from 75 to 50 kJ at 300 K. Rate ratio (k_cat/k)?","opts":["~10⁶×","~100,000×","~10,000×","~10×","~100×"],"correct":2,"why":"k_cat/k = exp((75−50)·1000 / (8.314·300)) = exp(25000/2494) = exp(10.02) ≈ 22,400 ≈ 10⁴.","traps":["A factor of 10⁶ would need a drop near 34 kJ/mol, more than this catalyst takes off.","100,000 comes from rounding 25 divided by 5.7 up to five powers of ten, and that ratio is about four and a half.",null,"A factor of 10 would fit a barrier drop near 6 kJ/mol, and this catalyst removes 25.","A factor of 100 would fit a drop near 11 kJ/mol, and this catalyst removes 25."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_if-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Vapor pressure varies INVERSELY with intermolecular force strength: weaker IMFs allow molecules to escape into the gas phase more easily. Among water (BP 100°C, H-bond), ethanol (BP 78°C, H-bond), pentane (BP 36°C, London only), and octane (BP 126°C, London only), which has the HIGHEST vapor pressure at 25°C?","opts":["Pentane","All four are equal","Water","Octane","Ethanol"],"correct":0,"why":"Lowest BP correlates with highest vapor pressure at room T. Pentane (BP 36°C, only London forces, small molecule) is closest to its boiling point at 25°C and therefore has the highest vapor pressure (~520 mmHg). Octane is heavier, water and ethanol have H-bonding.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#12tb68e","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["3","3.3","10","10.7","11"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M. pOH = 3, pH = 14 − 3 = 11.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ks-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Silver chromate (Ag₂CrO₄) dissolves slightly in pure water according to Ag₂CrO₄(s) ⇌ 2 Ag⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq). Given K_sp = 1.1 × 10⁻¹² at 25°C, calculate the molar solubility s (mol/L) of Ag₂CrO₄ in pure water.","opts":["3.3 × 10⁻⁴","1.1 × 10⁻¹²","2.2 × 10⁻¹²","1.0 × 10⁻⁶","6.5 × 10⁻⁵"],"correct":4,"why":"If s = solubility, then [Ag⁺] = 2s and [CrO₄²⁻] = s, so K_sp = (2s)²(s) = 4s³. Solving: s³ = K_sp/4 = 2.75×10⁻¹³, s = (2.75×10⁻¹³)^(1/3) ≈ 6.5×10⁻⁵ M. Forgetting the (2s)² stoichiometric factor gives the much smaller 1.0×10⁻⁶ trap.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-combined-gas#1hwah0q","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A gas occupies 6 L at 3 atm and 127 °C. What is its volume at 2 atm and 227 °C?","opts":["5 L","11.3 L","16.1 L","7.2 L","9 L"],"correct":1,"why":"P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 in Kelvin. V2 = 6 × (3/2) × (500/400) = 11.3 L.","traps":["Pressure ratio is upside down. More pressure squeezes the gas, so volume moves opposite to pressure.",null,"You used Celsius in the ratio. Convert both temperatures to Kelvin first.","Temperature ratio is upside down. Hotter gas expands, so volume moves with temperature.","You ignored the temperature change. Both P and T changed here."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bl3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Consider HCl ionizing in water (Bronsted) and BF3 accepting NH3's lone pair to form H3N-BF3 (Lewis). Comparing the two definitions: a Bronsted acid donates a proton (which has an empty 1s orbital), while a Lewis acid accepts an electron pair. Which statement correctly describes the relationship between Bronsted and Lewis acid categories?","opts":["Every Bronsted acid is also a Lewis acid","Every Lewis acid is also a Bronsted acid","Only Lewis theory applies to organic solvents","The two categories are mutually exclusive","Only Bronsted theory applies in water"],"correct":0,"why":"Donating an H+ is equivalent to having a proton accept an electron pair from the base, so all Bronsted acids meet the Lewis criterion. The reverse fails: Lewis acids like BF3 or AlCl3 contain no proton to donate. Lewis is the broader category and includes Bronsted as a subset.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_imf6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Four solids at 1 atm have the following melting points to be matched: NaCl (801°C), H2O (0°C), CH4 (-182°C), KBr (734°C). Their dominant intermolecular/lattice forces are ionic for the salts, H-bonding for water, and London dispersion for methane. Which solid has the LOWEST melting point?","opts":["KBr","All approximately equal","NaCl","H2O","CH4"],"correct":4,"why":"Methane is nonpolar with only weak London forces (~1 kJ/mol), making it the easiest to melt. Ionic lattices (NaCl 787 kJ/mol lattice energy) and H-bonded water are dramatically harder to disrupt, so CH4 melts at only -182°C.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-18963gw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Methyl benzoate is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product derived from the acyl portion?","opts":["O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the ester fully to a primary alcohol; the acyl carbon of methyl benzoate becomes benzyl alcohol (methanol is released). option A (benzyl alcohol, C7H8O) correct; distractors are aldehyde (does not stop there), acid, toluene over-reduction, and unchanged ester.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-nymgh3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is treated with excess acetaldehyde and NaOH, then heated. Acetaldehyde is in excess so it provides the enolate. Which structure is the major crossed product, and what governs its alkene geometry?","opts":["O=C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC=O","O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=CCCc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Benzaldehyde (no alpha H) is the electrophile; the acetaldehyde enolate is the nucleophile. option B minor Z, option C the beta-hydroxy addition product, option D (phenylacetaldehyde)/option E (3-phenylpropanal) wrong connectivity. Geometry rationale is qualitative (conjugation/sterics), not FMO. Keyed answer correct.","traps":null,"qs":"O=Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-m3kxko","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"The 1H NMR of the molecule shown (1,2-dichloroethane) is a single sharp singlet. Which statement best explains this?","opts":["Rapid rotation about the central C-C bond averages two otherwise non-equivalent doublets into a single peak","The two CH2 groups are chemically different, but their chemical shifts coincide so the two signals overlap exactly","Chlorine quenches all proton coupling, collapsing the peaks to one singlet","The protons are equivalent but should still split each other into a triplet","All four protons are equivalent by symmetry, so there is no observable coupling and one signal appears"],"correct":4,"why":"Both CH2 groups bear one Cl and are related by the molecule's symmetry, so all four protons are chemically equivalent. Equivalent protons do not split one another, so a single singlet is observed (and the 13C shows one signal).","traps":null,"qs":"ClCCCl","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-5xjdvy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"1-Hexyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr (no peroxides). What is the major product?","opts":["C=C(Br)CCCC","CCCC=C(C)Br","CCCCC(Br)C","CCCCC=CBr","CCC=C(Br)CC"],"correct":0,"why":"Ionic HBr (no ROOR) is Markovnikov: Br to C2 -&gt; 2-bromo-1-hexene (C=C(Br)CCCC, option A). Verified valid/distinct. option B = anti-Markovnikov (peroxide regiochem, not operative here), option C/option D = wrong-skeleton internal bromides, option E = saturated. Keeping; flagging difficulty.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CCCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-46zrsw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n3-hexyne + H2, Lindlar catalyst","opts":["CC#CCCC","CC/C=C\\CC","C=CCCCC","CCCCCC","CC/C=C/CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Lindlar does syn addition of one H2 across the internal alkyne, giving cis-3-hexene. Not over-reduced to hexane; skeleton unchanged (not 2-hexyne).","traps":null,"qs":"CCC#CCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-jwcdpe","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Acetic acid (drawn) is heated with excess ethanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (Fischer conditions). The product is then identified by IR. Which single answer correctly pairs the product with its diagnostic IR change relative to the starting acid?","opts":["Product is ethyl acetate; a new broad O-H stretch near 3300 cm-1 appears while the C=O absorption near 1740 cm-1 disappears entirely","Product is ethanol only; all carbonyl absorption is lost","Product is acetamide; a sharp N-H stretch (~3300) replaces the carbonyl","Product is acetaldehyde; the C=O moves to about 1715 cm-1 and a pair of diagnostic aldehyde C-H stretches near 2720 and 2820 cm-1 appears","Product is ethyl acetate; the broad O-H stretch (~2500-3300 cm-1) of the acid disappears while a strong C=O (~1740 cm-1) remains"],"correct":4,"why":"Fischer esterification of acetic acid with ethanol (H2SO4 cat.) gives ethyl acetate (CCOC(C)=O). Diagnostic IR: the very broad carboxylic acid O-H (2500-3300 cm-1) is lost while a strong ester C=O remains near 1740 cm-1. No new O-H, aldehyde C-H, or N-H forms.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-havopu","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"gamma-Butyrolactone (the cyclic ester drawn below) is in equilibrium with its open-chain hydroxy acid form, 4-hydroxybutanoic acid. Which single set of conditions would open the lactone ring to give the carboxylate salt of that hydroxy acid in an essentially irreversible step?","opts":["Pyridine, no water","Excess ethanol with a Dean-Stark trap, catalytic H2SO4","Catalytic H2SO4 in excess methanol, heat","Aqueous NaOH, heat (saponification of the lactone)","Catalytic H2SO4 in water, heat"],"correct":3,"why":"A lactone is an intramolecular ester, so it saponifies. NaOH/heat opens the ring and deprotonates the acid to sodium 4-hydroxybutanoate (irreversible). Acidic water gives a reversible equilibrium favoring the 5-membered lactone; methanol/H2SO4 transesterifies; Dean-Stark/excess alcohol drives ring closure; pyridine does nothing.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CCCO1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fquwbw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane (shown) is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["C[C@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](O)CC","C[C@H](Br)CC","CCC(C)C#N"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary substrate + strong CN- in polar aprotic DMSO = SN2 with backside inversion. 3D inversion of (R)-2-bromobutane gives canonical CC[C@H](C)C#N = the (S)-nitrile (option A). The descriptor flips R to S because CN outranks ethyl; option B is retention, option C is achiral/flat, option D is unreacted, option E is wrong nucleophile.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](Br)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1mc2sbd","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Propanal (shown) is stirred with excess methanol and a trace of acid (HCl). Treating the aldehyde with two equivalents of alcohol under acid gives which product class?","opts":["CCC(=O)OC","CCC(O)OC","CCCO","CCC(OC)OC","CCC(O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"An aldehyde + 2 eq alcohol under acid forms an acetal with two OR groups on the original carbonyl carbon: CCC(OC)OC. CCC(O)OC is the hemiacetal (one equivalent, intermediate); CCC(=O)OC is an ester (wrong connectivity); CCC(O)O is the gem-diol hydrate; CCCO is a reduction product.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-bf43c0807e","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"The CH2 between the two carbonyls of 2,4-pentanedione has a pKa near 9, while the alpha hydrogens of acetone have a pKa near 20. Why is the dione so much more acidic?","opts":["It has a higher molar mass, and the extra mass spreads the negative charge over a larger volume","Its alpha carbon is sp hybridized","Its conjugate base is stabilized by resonance with two carbonyl groups instead of one","The two carbonyl groups donate electron density to the central carbon, making the resulting carbanion easier to form","It forms stronger hydrogen bonds with water"],"correct":2,"why":"Remove a hydrogen from the carbon between two carbonyls and the negative charge can be pushed onto either oxygen. Oxygen is electronegative, so it holds a negative charge well, and two oxygen sinks beat one. That is why the pKa falls by about ten units. Keep in mind that where the charge lands matters more than how many resonance structures you can draw.","traps":["Molar mass has nothing to do with acidity.","The carbon is sp3 before the proton is removed. Hybridization is not the explanation.",null,"Carbonyl groups WITHDRAW electron density. That withdrawal is what stabilizes the anion.","Hydrogen bonding to solvent does not account for a pKa drop of this size."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-72vdn0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"3-Bromopentane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), promoting E2 elimination. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC/C=C/C","CCC(C)=C","CC/C=C\\C","CCC(O)CC","CCCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"The symmetric substrate can only form 2-pentene, so the decision is stereochemical: the anti-periplanar E2 transition state that minimizes steric strain gives the more stable (E)/trans-2-pentene as the major product. Choice B is the minor cis isomer, C is substitution, D is the reduced alkane, and E is a branched wrong skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC(Br)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1x3lymn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"5,5-Dimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn) is treated with the anion of nitromethane (-CH2NO2), a stabilized carbon nucleophile, then mild acid. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["O=C1C(C[N+](=O)[O-])C(C)(C)CC=C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(C[N+](=O)[O-])C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(ON=O)C1","OC1(C[N+](=O)[O-])CC(C)(C)CC=C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CCC1C[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":1,"why":"The nitronate is a soft, stabilized carbon nucleophile and adds 1,4: its carbon bonds to the beta carbon (C3), and the enolate is protonated to restore the saturated ketone = option 0 (verified: nitromethyl carbon sits at the beta position, 2 bonds from the carbonyl carbon). Option 1 is the 1,2-adduct (allylic alcohol, alkene retained). Option 2 attaches nitromethyl at the alpha carbon and keeps the alkene. Option 3 shows nitrite O-N=O (O-attachment, no C-C bond). Option 4 places the new C-C bond at the alpha carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CC(C)(C)CC=C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1jkseoj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"1,2-Dimethylcyclohexene (both alkene carbons are fully substituted, each bearing a methyl) is heated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. Predict the cleavage product.","opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","O=CC(C)CCCC(C)C=O","CC1=C(C)CCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Both alkene carbons are tetrasubstituted (no H), so cleavage gives only ketones even under harsh KMnO4. The endocyclic bond opens the ring to 2,7-octanedione (C8H14O2, mass conserved from C8H14). The C7 diketone is one carbon short, the dialdehyde is impossible (no H on the sp2 carbons), and a keto-acid would require an H-bearing alkene carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1e26x8u","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Using the 2^n rule, how many distinct stereoisomers actually exist for 2,3-dichlorobutane (shown)?","opts":["1","2","3","4","6"],"correct":2,"why":"There are n = 2 stereocenters, so 2^n predicts a maximum of 4. But the two ends are identical (both CH(Cl)CH3), giving an internal mirror plane in the (R,S) configuration, which is a single achiral meso compound. This is the classic symmetry case where the real count is less than 2^n.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1kvy78p","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"1,3-Butadiene (drawn) reacts with one equivalent of HBr. Protonation gives an intermediate cation, and the products are a mix of 1,2- and 1,4-addition. What feature of the intermediate explains the formation of BOTH products, and which product dominates under thermodynamic control?","opts":["A localized secondary cation; only the 1,2-product forms","A resonance-stabilized allylic cation with charge on two carbons; the 1,4-product (more substituted, conjugated-friendly internal alkene) dominates under thermodynamic control","Two separate cations form with no resonance between them, and since neither one is stabilized the two addition products appear in a fixed 1:1 ratio at any temperature or reaction time","A primary cation forms at the terminal carbon, and the 1,2-product dominates because that cation is the more stable of the two","An aromatic cation; the 1,4-product dominates because it is aromatic"],"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of butadiene gives an allylic cation with charge delocalized over C2 and C4. Bromide attacks either carbon (1,2 vs 1,4). Under thermodynamic control the 1,4-product dominates because it has the more substituted, more stable internal double bond. Classic resonance-intermediate / conjugate-addition item.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC=C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-15myqzk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Benzamide (C6H5CONH2) is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","opts":["Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a methylene while retaining the C-N bond, converting C(=O)NH2 into CH2NH2 to give benzylamine (NCc1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (C-N cleavage), aniline (wrong skeleton), benzaldehyde (incomplete), and toluene (full deoxygenation). Exactly one correct.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(N)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1raxpuc","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,3-dibromopropane (BrCH2CH2CH2Br), then heated in aqueous acid. The dibromide drawn is the alkylating agent. Which structure is the major final product?","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Double intramolecular alkylation of the acetoacetate carbon by the 1,3-dibromide closes a ring: the central carbon plus the three-carbon tether forms a four-membered (cyclobutane) ring still bearing the acetyl and ester. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation leave the methyl ketone, giving 1-cyclobutylethanone/acetylcyclobutane (ring size 4 confirmed). The cyclopropyl ketone (option B, 2-C tether) and cyclopentyl ketone (option C, 4-C tether) are the wrong ring size; the open diketone (option D) skips cyclization; cyclobutanecarboxylic acid (option E) is the acid class (malonic route). Verified correct=0.","traps":null,"qs":"BrCCCBr","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1amdlkn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"1,3-Butadiene reacts with the dienophile shown below (acrolein, an alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehyde) under thermal conditions. Which statement best describes both the reaction type and the role of the CHO group?","opts":["An electrophilic addition of HBr across the diene; the CHO group directs the incoming proton so that the Markovnikov product forms","A Diels-Alder [4+2] cycloaddition; the CHO group donates electron density into the alkene, which raises its LUMO and slows the reaction down","An aldol addition; the CHO acts as the nucleophile","A Diels-Alder [4+2] cycloaddition; the CHO is an electron-withdrawing group that activates the dienophile and speeds the reaction","A Wittig olefination; the CHO is converted to a new alkene"],"correct":3,"why":"Butadiene (diene) + acrolein (dienophile) gives a cyclohexene-carbaldehyde via [4+2] Diels-Alder; the CHO is an EWG that lowers the dienophile reactivity barrier and accelerates the reaction. Options 2/3/4/5 are each chemically wrong (no aldol/HBr/Wittig; CHO is not donating).","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-hem77e","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"4-Oxocyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde (drawn; it has both an aldehyde and a ketone) is treated with one equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C, then H3O+. NaBH4 selectively reduces the more reactive carbonyl. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["OCC1CCC(=O)CC1","O=CC1CCC(O)CC1","O=CC1CCC(=O)CC1","O=C(O)C1CCC(=O)CC1","OCC1CCC(O)CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Aldehydes are more electrophilic and less hindered than ketones, so 1 equiv NaBH4 at 0 C reduces the CHO to CH2OH while the ring ketone survives (option A). Reducing only the ketone is backwards; the diol = excess reagent; carboxylic acid = oxidation; unchanged dialdehyde/ketone ignores that NaBH4 reduces aldehydes.","traps":null,"qs":"O=CC1CCC(=O)CC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-akqv0e","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexene (shown) is treated with HBr in the presence of an organic peroxide (ROOR). Classify this reaction and predict where Br ends up relative to the simple HBr (no-peroxide) case.","opts":["Oxidative cleavage of the double bond to give a carboxylic acid","Radical (peroxide-initiated) anti-Markovnikov addition; Br adds to the terminal carbon, opposite the Markovnikov product","Radical addition that still follows Markovnikov selectivity; the bromine atom adds to the internal carbon just as the ionic pathway does","Ionic Markovnikov addition; the peroxide only speeds initiation, so Br still adds to the internal carbon exactly as it does without peroxide","Anti addition of Br2 across the double bond to give a dibromide"],"correct":1,"why":"Peroxides switch HBr addition to a radical chain. The bromine radical adds to form the more stable secondary carbon radical, so Br lands on the terminal carbon, giving 1-bromohexane: the anti-Markovnikov product, opposite the ionic outcome. This peroxide effect is unique to HBr.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCC=C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-fe4b8d","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)=C"],"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide + bulky strong base (tert-butoxide) gives E2 with Hofmann selectivity (less-substituted, more accessible beta-H), yielding 2-methyl-1-butene. The Zaitsev alkene 2-methyl-2-butene is disfavored by the bulky base; bulky ether/tertiary alcohol are substitution products; last is wrong skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC(C)(C)Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-rse4t3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Imidazole, drawn below, contains TWO nitrogen atoms in a five-membered ring: one is an N-H nitrogen and the other is a doubly-bonded (=N-) nitrogen. Determine the pi electron count and classify the ring.","opts":["6 pi electrons, aromatic: both nitrogen lone pairs are donated into the ring and one C=C bond is left out of the cycle, so the sextet comes from the two nitrogens","6 pi electrons, aromatic: the N-H nitrogen donates its lone pair (2 e-) to the ring, while the =N- nitrogen keeps its lone pair in an in-plane sp2 orbital; both nitrogens plus the ring double bonds give the sextet","6 pi electrons, but nonaromatic because the two nitrogens cannot both be planar","8 pi electrons, antiaromatic: BOTH nitrogen lone pairs sit in p orbitals and are donated to the ring, so the two C=C bonds plus the two donated pairs push the count to eight and wreck the Huckel sextet rather than completing it","4 pi electrons, antiaromatic: neither nitrogen lone pair is counted"],"correct":1,"why":"Imidazole: pyrrole-type N-H donates its lone pair (2 e-), pyridine-type =N- contributes via its C=N pi bond and keeps its remaining lone pair in-plane (uncounted, source of basicity). Two ring pi bonds (4 e-) + N-H lone pair (2 e-) = 6 pi e- (4n+2), aromatic. correct=0.","traps":null,"qs":"c1cnc[nH]1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ix6yyh","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"The reaction below is run with HBr and a peroxide initiator. Which species is the chain-carrying radical that adds to the alkene's pi bond in the first propagation step?","opts":["H (hydrogen radical)","The carbocation","Br (bromine radical)","HBr radical cation","RO (alkoxy radical, the initiator)"],"correct":2,"why":"The alkoxy radical from peroxide abstracts H from HBr to generate a bromine radical (Br). The Br radical adds to the alkene first, placing Br on the less substituted carbon and forming the more stable carbon radical. No carbocation forms.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCCCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1mnmhwn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"2-Pentyne (drawn), an UNsymmetrical internal alkyne, is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which statement best describes the outcome?","opts":["A carboxylic acid forms by oxidative cleavage of the triple bond","A single aldehyde forms, because Markovnikov addition of water to an internal alkyne always delivers the oxygen to a chain-end carbon of the molecule","Only 2-pentanone forms, because Markovnikov addition always places the carbonyl oxygen on C2, the alkyne carbon carrying the shorter alkyl chain","No reaction occurs, because internal alkynes are unreactive toward Hg(II)/acid","A roughly equal mixture of two regioisomeric ketones (2-pentanone and 3-pentanone) forms, because neither alkyne carbon is strongly favored"],"correct":4,"why":"For an unsymmetrical INTERNAL alkyne, the two carbons of the triple bond are similar in substitution, so Markovnikov selectivity is poor and acid-catalyzed hydration gives a mixture of both possible ketones. Internal alkynes give ketones, never aldehydes; the reaction does occur; and no C-C cleavage happens under these conditions.","traps":null,"qs":"CC#CCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1sm5f0m","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"3-Phenyl-1,5-hexadiene (drawn) is heated, allowing a Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement to reach the more stable isomer. Which structure is that product?","opts":["C1=CC=CC(c2ccccc2)C1","C=CC(c1ccccc1)C=CC","C=Cc1ccccc1CCC=C","C=CCCC=Cc1ccccc1","C=CCC(C=C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"The Cope [3,3] shift of the 1,5-diene breaks the C3-C4 sigma bond and forms a new C1-C6 bond, relocating the double bonds. The phenyl ends up conjugated with a newly formed alkene (styrene-type), giving 1-phenylhexa-1,5-diene (opt 0), which is thermodynamically favored. Option 1 is the unchanged starting material; the others have incorrect connectivity or form a ring.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(c1ccccc1)CC=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-59l9ke","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"The alkene shown is treated with HBr in the presence of peroxides (ROOR). Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CC(C)CCCBr","CC(C)CC(C)Br","CCC(Br)C(C)C","CCCCC(C)Br","CC(C)CCCO"],"correct":0,"why":"Peroxides give radical anti-Markovnikov HBr addition: Br adds to the less substituted terminal CH2, giving 1-bromo-4-methylpentane (CC(C)CCCBr). Markovnikov (Br on C2) is the trap; others scramble the carbon bearing Br, rearrange the skeleton, or give an alcohol. Verified: option A matches the anti-Markovnikov product; index 0 correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)CC=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fe9pdg","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"The intermediate shown forms when benzaldehyde reacts with methylamine. What kind of species is it, and what reagent converts it to N-methyl-1-phenylmethanamine (PhCH2NHCH3)?","opts":["A hemiaminal (carbinolamine); oxidize it with PCC in dichloromethane","A nitrile; reduce with LiAlH4","An imine (Schiff base); reduce with NaBH4 (or NaBH3CN)","An amide formed by loss of water; reduce it with LiAlH4, then work up","An enamine; hydrolyze with H3O+"],"correct":2,"why":"Aldehyde plus primary amine gives an imine (C=N, Schiff base); reducing the C=N with NaBH4/NaBH3CN gives the secondary amine PhCH2NHCH3. It has no N-C=C (not an enamine), the N is doubly bonded (not hemiaminal/amide), and it is not a nitrile.","traps":null,"qs":"CN=Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1r7kzfa","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"para-Nitrotoluene (drawn below) is brominated with Br2/FeBr3. The ring carries two substituents whose directing effects must be weighed: the methyl (activating, o/p director) and the nitro (deactivating, meta director). Which statement correctly predicts where bromine adds?","opts":["Bromine adds ortho to the nitro group; the nitro group is the stronger of the two directors here because its formally positive nitrogen dominates the whole ring","Bromine adds at the carbon bearing the methyl group via ipso substitution","Bromine adds meta to the methyl group; the deactivating nitro group overrides the weaker methyl group entirely and sets the regiochemistry on the ring by itself","No substitution occurs because the two directors cancel each other completely","Bromine adds ortho to the methyl group; the stronger activating director (CH3) controls the regiochemistry, and that position is also meta to the nitro group"],"correct":4,"why":"When directors compete, the stronger activator wins. The methyl group (o/p director) dominates over the deactivating nitro group, so bromine enters ortho to the methyl. Conveniently, that same position is meta to the nitro group, so the two effects reinforce, giving 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17mk9ys","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then with dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). What is the major product?","opts":["CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","O=CCCCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC(C)=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C, replacing each carbon with a carbonyl and opening the ring to a chain; reductive Me2S workup gives carbonyls (not acids). The methyl-bearing (disubstituted) carbon becomes a ketone, the H-bearing carbon becomes an aldehyde: 6-oxoheptanal (option A, C7H12O2 verified). option B (heptane-2,6-dione, same formula) wrongly makes both ends ketones; option C wrongly makes both aldehydes (ignores the methyl); option D is the over-oxidized acid (oxidative workup); option E is a non-cleavage alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-sr8lt8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with NaOEt and allyl bromide (CH2=CHCH2Br), then with dilute acid and heat. Predict the major product.","opts":["C=CCC(=O)CC(C)=O","C=CCCCC(C)=O","OC(=O)CCC=C","C=CCCC(C)=O","C=CCC(C)=O"],"correct":3,"why":"SN2 allylation at the active-methylene carbon (alkene untouched), then hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH2CH2CH=CH2 = hex-5-en-2-one. opt[0] matches. Distractors: carbon-short enone-ketone, carbon-long ketone, non-decarboxylated diketone, and a carboxylic acid (wrong class).","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"qr-a:sk4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-14","topic":"Test-day execution","nights":[14],"d":1,"q":"After skipping, when do you return?","opts":["Immediately after the next question","Don't return - they're lost","End of section, with remaining time","Halfway through the section","Every five questions, in a quick sweep"],"correct":2,"why":"Returning at the end protects pace. Mid-section returns scramble timing; never returning forfeits guess-able points.","traps":["Mid-section returns recreate the original time-burn.","Eliminate-and-guess on returns adds points for free.",null,"Halfway is no better than mid-section returns.","A fixed sweep every five questions breaks rhythm the same way an immediate return does."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#1la5g4q","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 7 students, in how many ways can a committee of 2 be chosen?","opts":["2","14","21","42","49"],"correct":2,"why":"Order does not matter: 7C2 = 42 / 2! = 21.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 2 people. You still have to choose them from 7.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.",null,"You used a permutation. A committee has no positions, so order does NOT matter: divide out the orderings.","You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-successive-percent#wl6yj0","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $250 item is marked UP by 10% and then the new price is marked DOWN by 20%. What is the final price?","opts":["$200.00","$220.00","$221.00","$225.00","$275.00"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply the factors: 250 × 1.1 × 0.8 = $220.00. Percents stack by multiplying, never by adding.","traps":["You skipped the mark-up and only applied the discount.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You added and subtracted the percents. The second percent is taken of the NEW price, so multiply the two factors.","You stopped after the mark-up. Apply the mark-down to that new price."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1654","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Three steel beams measure 11, 15, and 4 meters. Can they be joined into a triangular frame?","opts":["Cannot determine","Only if it is obtuse","No","Only if right triangle","Yes"],"correct":2,"why":"|11−15| = 4 and 11+15 = 26. 4 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["The three lengths settle it: 11 plus 4 equals 15, and the sum has to be greater than the longest side, not equal to it.","There is no angle to open: 11 and 4 lie flat along the 15 rather than lifting off it.",null,"There is no angle to open up: the two shorter sides lie flat along the 15, tying it rather than beating it.","11 and 4 add to exactly 15, so they lie flat along the longest side instead of rising above it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1210","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A car odometer reads 1,906 miles at the start of a trip that covers 44 miles. What does it read at the end?","opts":["1862","1890","1940","1950","1990"],"correct":3,"why":"Work it out: 1906 + 44 = 1950. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 1906 minus 44, so the plus got read as a minus.","That is below 1906, and adding 44 has to move the total up.","That is 1900 plus 40, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 1,950.",null,"That adds about 85, nearly double the 44 on the page."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1263","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What value of x satisfies 11x + 9 = 3x + 3?","opts":["-1.75","-0.75","0.25","0.75","1.5"],"correct":1,"why":"Move x: (11-3)x = 3-9 → 8x = -6, so x = -0.75.","traps":["Substituting -1.75 gives -10.25 on the left and -2.25 on the right.",null,"Substituting 0.25 gives 11.75 on the left and 3.75 on the right.","Dividing -6 by 8 gives -0.75, so this answer drops the minus sign.","The 9 was added to the 3 instead of subtracted, leaving 8x = 12."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1054","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is 5/8 multiplied by 2/8?","opts":["10/16","5/64","7/64","40/16","5/32"],"correct":4,"why":"Multiply across: (5·2)/(8·8) = 10/64 = 5/32.","traps":["You multiplied the tops but added the bottoms (8 + 8) instead of multiplying them.","You halved the top of 10/64 while leaving the bottom alone; reducing has to act on both.","You added the tops (5 + 2) over the multiplied bottoms instead of multiplying across.","You flipped the second fraction and divided: 5/8 divided by 2/8 is 40/16.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#139t9q","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 60 and standard deviation 5. About what percent of scores fall above 65?","opts":["16%","32%","34%","68%","84%"],"correct":0,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 16%.","traps":[null,"That is BOTH tails together. Only the upper tail is asked for: halve it.","That is the slice between the mean and one SD. The question asks for the TAIL beyond it.","That is the middle band within one SD. The tail is what is left over, split in two.","That is everything BELOW the cutoff. The question asks for above."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp002","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"If log₂(x) + log₂(x − 6) = 4, what is the value of x?","opts":["x = 8","x = −2","x = 12","x = 4","x = 16"],"correct":0,"why":"log₂(x(x−6)) = 4, so x(x−6) = 16, so x² − 6x − 16 = 0 → (x − 8)(x + 2) = 0. x = 8 or x = −2. Reject negative (log undefined). x = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"generated:qr-successive-percent#x565wy","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $50 item is marked UP by 25% and then the new price is marked DOWN by 50%. What is the final price?","opts":["$25.00","$31.25","$37.50","$46.88","$62.50"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply the factors: 50 × 1.25 × 0.5 = $31.25. Percents stack by multiplying, never by adding.","traps":["You skipped the mark-up and only applied the discount.",null,"You added and subtracted the percents. The second percent is taken of the NEW price, so multiply the two factors.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You stopped after the mark-up. Apply the mark-down to that new price."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1377","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A freight truck averages 60 miles per hour for 4.5 hours. How many miles does it cover?","opts":["13.33","210","240","270","330"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 60 × 4.5 = 270 mi.","traps":["Dividing 60 by 4.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 3.5 hours instead of 4.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 4 hours flat.",null,"Using 5.5 hours instead of the 4.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:bn4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A square storage room measures 19 feet on each side. What is its floor area in square feet?","opts":["341","361","379","381","441"],"correct":1,"why":"(20−1)² = 400 − 40 + 1 = 361.","traps":["You made the middle term 60 rather than 40, which lands 20 below 361.",null,"You subtracted 20 and then 1 as well, instead of subtracting 40 and adding 1.","You used 20 for the middle term instead of 2 times 20 times 1, which is 40.","You added the middle term instead of subtracting it, which squares 21, not 19."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1333","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"For which values of x is -5x &lt; 3 true?","opts":["x &lt; -0.6","x &gt; 0.6","x = -0.6","x &lt; 0.6","x &gt; -0.6"],"correct":4,"why":"Divide both sides by -5 (negative) and FLIP: x &gt; -0.6.","traps":["You kept the inequality pointing the same way after dividing by a negative.","You flipped the direction but dropped the negative on 3/-5.","You solved the boundary equation -5x = 3 instead of the range of values.","You divided by 5 rather than -5, so neither the sign nor the direction changed.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1474","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"How many centimeters are in 2.4 meters?","opts":["0.02","24","102.4","240","2400"],"correct":3,"why":"1 m = 100 cm, so 2.4 m × 100 = 240 cm.","traps":["You divided by 100 rather than multiplying, which sends the count the wrong way.","You multiplied by 10 instead of 100; meters to centimeters moves two places.","You added 100 to the 2.4 instead of multiplying the 2.4 by it.",null,"You multiplied by 1000, the kilometer-to-meter step, when meters to centimeters needs 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lem3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies (2x + 3)/5 = 3?","opts":["x = 6","x = 9","x = 4.5","x = 12","x = 7.5"],"correct":0,"why":"Multiply by 5: 2x + 3 = 15. Subtract 3: 2x = 12. Divide: x = 6.","traps":[null,"Substituting 9 gives (18 + 3)/5 = 4.2, past the 3 on the right.","The 3 came off after the divide by 2, but it has to come off first: 15 over 2 is 7.5, and 7.5 − 3 is 4.5.","That is the 12 from 2x = 12, so the final divide by 2 never happened.","The 3 never came off: 2x = 15 gives 7.5, but the 3 has to be subtracted first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-combined-work#feqvm6","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One painter paints a fence in 4 hours. A second painter does the same job in 12 hours. Working together, how long do they take?","opts":["8 hours","16 hours","4 hours","0.333 hours","3 hours"],"correct":4,"why":"Rates add: 1/4 + 1/12 = 1/3 of the job per hour. Time = 3 = 3 hours. Sanity check: it must be less than 4 hours.","traps":["You averaged the times. Two working together must be FASTER than the faster one alone.","You added the times. Adding helpers cannot make the job take longer. Add the RATES: 1/a + 1/b.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","That is the combined RATE (jobs per hour). Flip it to get the time.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1056","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A machine fills 2/8 of a crate in 6/9 of a minute. What fraction of a crate does it fill in one minute?","opts":["12/72","12/17","8/3","8/72","3/8"],"correct":4,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 2/8 times 9/6 gives 18/48, which reduces by 6 to 3/8. Because 6/9 is less than 1, dividing by it has to push 2/8 upward, and 3/8 is larger, so the size is right. Flip, multiply, reduce, in that order.","traps":["You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first.","You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (8 + 9).","That flips the first fraction instead of the second, which inverts the whole answer.","You added the tops (2 + 6) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-mixture#1ytvgkf","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"3 gallons of a 20% acid solution is mixed with 2 gallons of a 80% acid solution. What is the concentration of the mixture?","opts":["36.7%","44%","50%","56%","220%"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid: 20% of 3 plus 80% of 2 = 2.2 gallons. Total volume = 5 gallons. Concentration = 44%.","traps":["You divided by the product of the volumes. Divide by the SUM: total volume.",null,"You averaged the two percents. The mixture leans toward whichever solution you used MORE of, so weight by volume.","You swapped the volumes. Pair each concentration with its own volume.","You found the total acid but never divided by the total volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_pfl1517","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A quality check flips a fair coin for each of eight cartons. What is the probability that all eight flips land heads?","opts":["0.004","0.01","0.03","0.125","1"],"correct":0,"why":"A half to the eighth power is one over two hundred and fifty six, which is about 0.004. Every flip is independent, so the chances multiply rather than add. Watch for the answer that rounds the run away to zero: rare is not the same as impossible, and on this exam the difference is usually the point.","traps":[null,"0.01 is (1/2) to the seventh rounded off, one flip short of eight.","A probability never falls below 0, so this one can go before any arithmetic starts.","0.125 reads eight flips as a 1 in 8 chance, and each flip halves the run, so eight of them give 1/256.","1 would make the run certain, and that sits much closer to the chance of at least one head across eight flips."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:wdm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"If x is twice y and the two numbers add to 27, what is the value of x?","opts":["9","13.5","18","27","54"],"correct":2,"why":"x = 2y, x+y = 27 → 3y = 27, so y = 9, so x = 18.","traps":["That is y, the smaller of the two, and x is double it.","That splits 27 into two equal halves, but the two parts here run 2 to 1.",null,"That is the total of both numbers rather than x on its own.","That doubles the total, but only y gets doubled to make x, not the sum."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pf3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Bag has 4 red, 6 blue. Draw 3 with replacement. P(at least one red)?","opts":["12/10","1 − (4/10)³","(6/10)³","(4/10)³","1 − (6/10)³"],"correct":4,"why":"1 − P(all blue) = 1 − (6/10)³.","traps":["12/10 adds 4/10 three times, and a probability above 1 is the signal to switch to the flip-to-none move.","1 minus (4/10) cubed removes the all red run, when the run to remove is all blue.","(6/10) cubed is the chance of three blues in a row, the piece you subtract from 1.","(4/10) cubed is the chance that all three draws come up red, not that at least one does.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:vs3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":1,"q":"A sphere has a radius of 3, and its volume is (4/3)πr³. Approximating π as 3, what is its volume?","opts":["108","36","9","27","108π"],"correct":0,"why":"(4/3)(3)(27) = 4 × 27 = 108. (Or with π: 36π.)","traps":[null,"Left π out and stopped at 4/3 times 27.","Squared the radius and stopped there.","Cubed the radius and stopped, without the 4/3 or the π.","Used 3 for π and then kept a π on the end as well, counting it twice."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-missing-value-mean#5w0oqf","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A student scored 83, 70, 80, 79 on 4 quizzes. What score is needed on the next quiz to bring the average to exactly 80?","opts":["8","80","82","88","93"],"correct":3,"why":"Needed total = 80 × 5 = 400. Current total = 312. Next score = 400 − 312 = 88.","traps":["You used 4 quizzes. After the next one there are 5.","Scoring the target only works if you were already AT the target. You have to make up the gap on every earlier quiz too.","You averaged the old average with the new score. The old average counts for several quizzes, not one.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Required total = target × number of quizzes; subtract what you already have."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1734","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"Which values of x satisfy |x − 5| = 5?","opts":["0 or 0","10","10 or 0","5 or -5","0 or -10"],"correct":2,"why":"|x − 5| = 5 means x − 5 = ±5, so x = 10 or 0.","traps":["That keeps the branch x - 5 = -5 twice and drops the branch that gives 10.","You solved x - 5 = 5 and stopped, and the branch x - 5 = -5 gives 0 as well.",null,"You set x equal to plus or minus 5 and never undid the minus 5 inside the bars.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1478","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"How many seconds are in 0.6 minutes?","opts":["0.01","3.6","6","36","60.6"],"correct":3,"why":"1 min = 60 sec, so 0.6 min × 60 = 36 sec.","traps":["You divided the 0.6 by 60 instead of multiplying; seconds are smaller, so the count has to grow.","You multiplied by 6 instead of 60, dropping a zero.","You read the 0.6 as six seconds; six tenths of a minute is 36 seconds.",null,"You added 60 to the 0.6 instead of multiplying the 0.6 by it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wd5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"\"The sum of two consecutive integers is 47.\" Smaller integer:","opts":["21","22","23","23.5","24"],"correct":2,"why":"Let smaller = n. Larger = n+1. n + (n+1) = 47 → 2n+1 = 47 → 2n = 46, so n = 23.","traps":["21 and 22 add to 43, which is four short of 47.","22 and 23 add to 45, so the pair needs to sit one higher.",null,"Dividing 47 by 2 gives 23.5, and consecutive integers sit one either side of that.","You found the larger."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1473","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A hallway measures 9.4 meters. What is that length in centimeters?","opts":["0.09","94","109.4","940","9400"],"correct":3,"why":"1 m = 100 cm, so 9.4 m × 100 = 940 cm.","traps":["You divided by 100 rather than multiplying, so the centimeter count came out below the meter count.","You multiplied by 10 instead of 100; meters to centimeters moves two places.","You added 100 to the 9.4 instead of multiplying the 9.4 by it.",null,"You multiplied by 1000, the kilometer-to-meter step, when meters to centimeters needs 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:int1438","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A lab equipment fund of $5000 earns 8.75% interest compounded annually for 3 years. To the nearest cent, what is the total amount in dollars at the end?","opts":["1312.5","5000.26","5437.5","6312.5","6430.69"],"correct":4,"why":"A = P(1+r)^t = 5000·(1.0875)^3 = 6430.69.","traps":["That is the simple interest for three years, and the question asks for the ending amount.","Added rt as about twenty-six cents instead of applying the rate to the balance each year.","Grew the balance for one year instead of three.","Used simple interest, so years two and three never earned interest on the earlier interest.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pco1556","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Disease prevalence 1%, test sensitivity 99%, specificity 95%. P(disease | positive test)?","opts":["0.01","0.17","0.83","0.95","0.99"],"correct":1,"why":"True positives are 0.99 times 0.01, or 0.0099, and false positives are 0.05 times 0.99, or 0.0495, five times as many. Positives total 0.0594, so the true share is 0.0099 over 0.0594, about 17 percent. The ratio between those two branches is the whole story, so build both of them before you divide.","traps":["0.01 is the prevalence, the chance of disease before any test result.",null,"0.83 is the share of positives that are false alarms, 0.0495 over 0.0594, the complement of the answer.","0.95 is the specificity, a fact about people who do not have the disease.","0.99 is the sensitivity, the chance of a positive when the disease is present, which is this conditional backwards."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:psm2","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A textbook's price is raised 30 percent and then cut 10 percent from the raised price. What is the net percent change?","opts":["+43%","+40%","+17%","+20%","−40%"],"correct":2,"why":"×1.30 × 0.90 = ×1.17. Net +17%.","traps":["That raises the second step by 10 percent instead of cutting by it.","You added the 10 percent drop on as another rise instead of taking it off.",null,"You added the 30 up and the 10 down, and sequential percents multiply instead.","You added the sizes of the two changes and pointed the result downward, and the rise is the bigger move here."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:stm3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"30 students take Math, 25 take History, 12 take both. How many take Math but not History?","opts":["7","12","13","18","30"],"correct":3,"why":"|M only| = |M| − |M ∩ H| = 30 − 12 = 18.","traps":["You subtracted from the wrong total; the 12 come out of the 30 Math students.","That's the both-only count.","You took the 12 out of the History total, which gives History without Math.",null,"That is every Math student, including the 12 who also take History."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1028","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A pump moves 4/7 of a tank in the first hour and 4/5 of a tank in the second. How many tanks were moved over the two hours?","opts":["48/35","16/35","11/7","8/35","8/12"],"correct":0,"why":"LCD = 35. 4/7 = 20/35, 4/5 = 28/35. Combine: 48/35 = 48/35.","traps":[null,"You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You rounded 4/5 up to a whole; four fifths is 28/35, so the total is 48/35.","You put both 4s over 35 without rescaling; 4/7 is 20/35 and 4/5 is 28/35.","You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 8 over 12."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1185","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An 80 gram sample loses 25% of its mass in a first drying step and 10% of what is left in a second. What is its mass in grams at the end?","opts":["52","54","60","66","72"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply factors: 80 × 0.75 × 0.9 = 54. Naïvely adding gives 52, which is wrong.","traps":["You added the two cuts into a single 35 percent, and sequential percents multiply instead.",null,"That is the price after the 25 percent cut, one step short of the answer.","That raises the second step by 10 percent instead of cutting by it.","That applies only the 10 percent cut and leaves the 25 percent out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1459","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"In the coordinate plane, how far apart are the points (3, 0) and (9, 5), to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["3.32","6","7.81","11","61"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (5)²) = √61 = 7.81.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1638","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Daily visitors to a library average 100 with a standard deviation of 20. What is the z score of a day with 77 visitors?","opts":["-23","-1.15","-0.23","1.15","3.85"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (77 − 100)/20 = -1.15.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, stopping before the division by 20.",null,"Divided the 23-point gap by the mean of 100 instead of by the standard deviation of 20.","Right size, wrong side: 77 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative.","Divided 77 by 20 without first subtracting the mean."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1024","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A commute takes 2/5 of an hour by bus and 1/4 of an hour on foot. How many hours is the whole commute?","opts":["3/9","12/20","2/20","9/10","13/20"],"correct":4,"why":"LCD = 20. 2/5 = 8/20, 1/4 = 5/20. Combine: 13/20 = 13/20.","traps":["You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 3 over 9.","You scaled 1/4 by 4 instead of by 5; a quarter is 5/20.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You rescaled 1/4 as 10/20, which is a half; a quarter is 5/20.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-rn4znq","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"If cos θ = -0.28 and θ lies in the third quadrant, what is sin θ?","opts":["-0.96","-0.9216","-0.72","0.72","0.96"],"correct":0,"why":"Square and subtract: sin²θ is 1 minus 0.0784, which is 0.9216, and the square root of that is 0.96. The third quadrant has a negative y coordinate, so the sine is negative. The pair 0.28 and 0.96 is the 7-24-25 triangle scaled down by 25, which is a handy tell that the arithmetic came out clean.","traps":[null,"This is sin²θ, so the square root step has not happened yet.","This subtracts 0.28 from 1 instead of subtracting its square.","This subtracts the raw value and drops the sign as well.","This is the right size but the wrong sign, since sine is negative in the third quadrant."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:fam4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A roll holds 4 1/2 meters of tubing, and 1 3/4 meters are cut from it. How many meters are left?","opts":["3 1/4","6 1/4","2 1/4","3 3/4","2 3/4"],"correct":4,"why":"4 1/2 = 9/2 = 18/4. 1 3/4 = 7/4. 18/4 − 7/4 = 11/4 = 2 3/4.","traps":["You subtracted the whole numbers, then took the fraction gap as a positive 1/4 instead of borrowing.","You added instead of subtracting; 4 1/2 plus 1 3/4 is 6 1/4.","You took 4 minus 1 to get 3 and then subtracted the full 3/4, without adding back the 1/2 you were holding.","You subtracted only the whole numbers and carried the 3/4 down untouched.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lg6","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A bacteria population doubles every hour. If it starts at 100, how many hours to reach 1600?","opts":["4","5","8","12","16"],"correct":0,"why":"1600/100 = 16 = 2⁴. So 4 doublings → 4 hours. log₂(16) = 4.","traps":[null,"You counted the starting population as one of the doublings, and 100 doubles four times to reach 1600.","You got 8 doublings - that would be 100 × 256 = 25,600.","Twelve doublings would carry 100 up to 409,600, far past 1600.","You answered with the ratio, 16, when the time is how many doublings that ratio takes."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1374","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A tour bus averages 60 miles per hour for 3.5 hours. How many miles does it cover?","opts":["17.14","150","180","210","270"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 60 × 3.5 = 210 mi.","traps":["Dividing 60 by 3.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 2.5 hours instead of 3.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 3 hours flat.",null,"Using 4.5 hours instead of the 3.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261003"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s13"}},{"id":"FULL_4","name":"Full-Length Test 4","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"written:bio-4-15zgkb4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"While the electron transport chain is running hard, how do the two sides of the inner mitochondrial membrane compare?","opts":["The matrix becomes positively charged relative to the intermembrane space","Both compartments hold the same proton concentration, and only the charge differs","The matrix has a higher proton concentration and therefore a lower pH than the intermembrane space","The intermembrane space has a higher proton concentration and a lower pH than the matrix","The intermembrane space has a higher pH, because protons are consumed there"],"correct":3,"why":"Follow the pumps and the rest falls out. Complexes I, III, and IV move protons from the matrix out into the intermembrane space, so that space ends up crowded with protons, acidic, and positive relative to the matrix. ATP synthase then lets those protons fall back into the matrix, and that downhill flow is what powers ATP synthesis.","traps":["Pumping positive charge out of the matrix leaves the matrix relatively negative, not positive.","If the concentrations matched there would be no gradient at all and nothing to drive ATP synthase.","This has the gradient backwards, since protons are pumped out of the matrix, leaving the matrix proton-poor.",null,"More protons means a lower pH, not higher, and protons are being added to that space rather than used up."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1hymbd2","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"At which point in the Calvin cycle does NADPH hand over its electrons?","opts":["When 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate is reduced to G3P","When protons cross ATP synthase into the stroma","When two G3P are joined to form glucose","When five G3P are rearranged into three RuBP","When RuBisCO joins carbon dioxide to RuBP"],"correct":0,"why":"NADPH is a reducing agent, so hunt for the step where a carbon actually gains electrons and hydrogens. That is the conversion of the phosphorylated acid into the aldehyde G3P, the one place in the cycle where carbon becomes more reduced. Name the three moves in order: fixation adds carbon, reduction adds electrons, regeneration rebuilds the acceptor.","traps":[null,"That is the light reaction step that makes ATP, on the other side of the membrane from the Calvin cycle's chemistry.","Joining G3P into glucose happens after the cycle and is not where NADPH is spent.","Regeneration costs ATP, but no electrons change hands during it.","Fixation needs no energy input from either carrier, since RuBisCO just joins the two molecules together."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p049","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Acetylcholine released by parasympathetic postganglionic neurons acts on which receptors at the target organ?","opts":["GABA receptors","NMDA receptors","Muscarinic receptors","Nicotinic receptors","Adrenergic receptors"],"correct":2,"why":"Parasympathetic traffic runs on acetylcholine at both of its synapses, so the receptor name is what tells the two apart. At the target organ, meaning heart, gut, and glands, the receptor is muscarinic, a G protein-coupled receptor that slows the heart and steps up digestion. Tie each word to its source: muscarine comes from a mushroom and acts on organs, while nicotine acts at the ganglia and at skeletal muscle.","traps":["GABA receptors handle inhibition inside the central nervous system and have no role in autonomic output to organs.","NMDA receptors are glutamate receptors involved in central excitation and learning rather than in parasympathetic organ control.",null,"Nicotinic receptors do bind acetylcholine, but they sit at the autonomic ganglia and at the neuromuscular junction, one synapse earlier than the target organ.","Adrenergic receptors bind norepinephrine and epinephrine, which is the sympathetic side of the autonomic system."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p110","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"An adult whose thyroid gland is underactive would most likely show which set of findings?","opts":["Cold intolerance and fatigue along with steady weight loss","Heat intolerance","Tachycardia","Hyperactivity","Cold intolerance, weight gain, fatigue, slow HR"],"correct":4,"why":"Same dial, turned the other way. With too little thyroid hormone the body makes less heat, so cold is hard to tolerate, burns fewer calories, so weight creeps up, and runs everything slower, including heart rate, gut motility and thinking. Build the list from the furnace idea and you do not have to store two separate memorized sets.","traps":["The cold and the fatigue fit, but a slower metabolism burns fewer calories, so weight tends to rise rather than fall.","Feeling too hot belongs to the overactive thyroid, where extra heat is being produced.","Thyroid hormone drives heart rate up, so a shortage slows the heart rather than speeding it.","Low thyroid hormone leaves people tired and slowed down, while restlessness points toward excess.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1ewpgtm","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"What do carotenoids do for a leaf?","opts":["They absorb wavelengths chlorophyll misses and drain off excess energy","They split water once chlorophyll a is saturated with light","They reflect blue light, which is what gives autumn leaves their orange color","They carry protons across the thylakoid membrane into the lumen","They sit in the reaction center and pass electrons to plastoquinone"],"correct":0,"why":"Accessory pigments hold down two jobs, and most students remember only the first. They widen the band of wavelengths the antenna can harvest, and they also soak up energy that would otherwise damage the photosystem on a blazing day. Think of them as a wider net and a safety valve at the same time.","traps":[null,"Water splitting happens at the manganese containing complex of photosystem II, never at an accessory pigment.","Carotenoids absorb blue light and reflect yellow and orange, which is why those colors appear once chlorophyll breaks down in autumn.","Proton pumping is the job of the electron carriers in the membrane, not of a pigment.","The reaction center is chlorophyll a, and carotenoids feed energy into the antenna system instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p046","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The central chemoreceptors in the medulla oblongata adjust breathing rate primarily in response to changes in which of the following?","opts":["Arterial blood pressure","O₂","Temperature","CO₂ / pH","Glucose"],"correct":3,"why":"This asks what sets your breathing rate minute to minute. Carbon dioxide diffuses from blood into cerebrospinal fluid, forms carbonic acid, and releases hydrogen ions, and the central chemoreceptors read that pH drop and speed breathing up until the excess is blown off. Oxygen is the backup signal, watched by the carotid and aortic bodies, so the choice naming carbon dioxide and pH is the primary driver.","traps":["Pressure is read by baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch, which feed the cardiovascular centers rather than setting respiratory rate.","Oxygen is monitored by the peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies, and only once it falls quite low.","The hypothalamus handles core temperature; the medullary respiratory centers are tuned to the chemistry of the fluid around them.",null,"Glucose sensing belongs to the pancreatic islet cells and the hypothalamus, and it drives insulin and glucagon rather than breathing."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e043","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In speciation, what does the process called reinforcement describe?","opts":["Reduced fitness in hybrids, which strengthens prezygotic isolation","Random drift","Selection favoring the hybrids until the two populations merge back into one","Identical mating behavior","Hybrid sterility decreasing"],"correct":0,"why":"When two diverging populations meet again and their hybrids do poorly, any individual that avoids mating across the line leaves more surviving offspring than one that does not. Selection therefore builds up traits that prevent the cross from happening in the first place, such as different songs, different flowering times or different courtship displays. Reinforcement is prezygotic isolation being strengthened precisely because postzygotic isolation is already costly.","traps":[null,"Drift is random change in allele frequencies, while reinforcement is selection responding to a real cost paid by hybrids.","If hybrids did well, the two populations could blend back into one, which is the alternative outcome when reinforcement does not take hold.","Identical courtship behavior would make interbreeding more likely, which is the opposite of what reinforcement builds up.","Reinforcement does not repair the hybrids, it reduces how often they are produced, so hybrid sterility is not what changes."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev001","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Immediately after the first sperm fuses with the egg membrane, a fast block to polyspermy keeps a second sperm from entering. What produces this fast block?","opts":["Acrosomal enzyme release","A rise in intracellular calcium released from the egg's internal stores","Membrane depolarization (Na+ influx)","Cortical granule fusion","Fertilization envelope hardening"],"correct":2,"why":"The exam wants you to separate the two blocks by speed and by mechanism. The fast block is electrical: sodium rushes in, the egg membrane depolarizes within seconds, and a depolarized membrane will not fuse with another sperm. The slow block is chemical and structural: calcium triggers the cortical granules to empty, and their contents harden the coat into a fertilization envelope. Fast means electrical and seconds, slow means granules and minutes.","traps":["Acrosomal enzymes help a sperm bore through the egg's outer coat, so they assist fertilization rather than prevent a second sperm.","The calcium wave is real, but it is the signal that triggers the cortical reaction and the slow block, and it also restarts the egg's cell cycle, rather than being the instantaneous electrical block.",null,"Cortical granule release starts the slow block, which takes minutes rather than seconds and works by chemically altering the coat outside the egg.","The hardened envelope is the finished product of the slow block, a physical barrier built after the cortical granules have emptied their contents."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e014","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Kin selection raises an individual's inclusive fitness primarily through:","opts":["Predator deterrence","Helping relatives who share genes","Helping unrelated reciprocally","Behaving for the good of the species as a whole","Random help"],"correct":1,"why":"Inclusive fitness counts copies of your alleles that reach the next generation, whether they travel through your own offspring or through a relative's. That is why helping kin can pay even when the helper never reproduces: a full sibling carries about half your alleles, a niece or nephew about a quarter. Hamilton's rule puts numbers on it, favoring the help when relatedness times the benefit to the recipient beats the cost to the helper. When a question describes self-sacrifice among relatives, run that comparison.","traps":["Group living can deter predators, but that does not explain why helping a relative raises the helper's inclusive fitness.",null,"This is reciprocal altruism, which pays off through a returned favor rather than through shared genes.","This is the good-of-the-species idea, and selection acts on genes carried by individuals rather than on a species' interests.","Kin selection is anything but indiscriminate; the payoff depends entirely on helper and recipient sharing alleles."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p059","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Once angiotensin II is formed in the circulation it acts at several targets. Which of the following describes its effects?","opts":["Dilation of the efferent arteriole, which lowers the pressure inside the glomerulus","Vasodilation","Excretes Na+","Lowers BP","Vasoconstriction, with aldosterone and ADH both rising"],"correct":4,"why":"Hold one idea and the whole hormone follows: angiotensin II exists to defend blood pressure. It constricts systemic arterioles directly, tells the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone so the nephron holds onto sodium and water, prompts antidiuretic hormone release from the posterior pituitary, and stimulates thirst. The choice stacking vasoconstriction with aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone is the one built around that single purpose.","traps":["Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole instead, which holds pressure up inside the glomerulus and protects filtration when perfusion is low.","Angiotensin II is one of the strongest vasoconstrictors in the body, so dilation is the reverse of what it does.","Sodium excretion is what atrial natriuretic peptide promotes; angiotensin II does the opposite by driving aldosterone and sodium retention.","The whole renin angiotensin aldosterone pathway is switched on to raise pressure that has already fallen.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e046","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"An innate behavior is best described as a behavior that is:","opts":["Environmental only","Random","Identical across individuals because they share the same environment","Genetically programmed, present without learning","From experience"],"correct":3,"why":"The clean test for an innate behavior is the isolation experiment: raise an animal with no chance to learn or copy the behavior, and if it still performs it correctly on the first try, the behavior is inherited. Innate behaviors are stereotyped, meaning they run nearly the same way in every individual of the species, and many appear as fixed action patterns released by a specific stimulus. Learned behavior, by contrast, changes with experience. Most real behavior sits somewhere between the two, and exam stems give you a clue about whether experience was even possible.","traps":["The environment can trigger an innate behavior, but the program being run is inherited.","Innate behaviors are highly stereotyped, running almost identically in every individual of the species.","A shared environment can make behaviors look alike, while innate means the program is inherited and shows up even in an animal raised in isolation.",null,"Behavior that comes from experience is learned behavior, which is precisely what innate behavior is defined against."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p081","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The antibody class IgE is most closely associated with which of the following?","opts":["Allergic responses + parasitic infections","Bacterial defense","The first antibody class secreted during a primary response","Mucosal immunity","B cell receptor"],"correct":0,"why":"IgE is defined by where it sits rather than by how much of it there is. It binds Fc receptors on mast cells and basophils, and when an allergen cross links two bound IgE molecules those cells degranulate and release histamine, which is the immediate allergic response. The same mechanism evolved against parasitic worms, so the choice pairing allergy with parasite defense is the IgE profile.","traps":[null,"IgG and IgM carry most of the antibacterial work through opsonization and complement, while IgE is a tiny fraction of circulating antibody.","The first antibody secreted in a primary response is IgM, while IgE appears only after class switching in an allergic or parasitic response.","Mucosal surfaces are guarded by IgA, secreted as a dimer into saliva, tears, breast milk, and gut fluid.","The membrane bound receptor on a naive B cell is IgM together with IgD."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1bz3wg9","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"In a resting neuron the inside of the membrane is negative relative to the outside, and sodium is far more concentrated outside than in. For sodium, the concentration gradient and the electrical gradient:","opts":["Cancel exactly, so sodium already sits at equilibrium","Oppose each other, with the electrical gradient winning","Both favor movement of sodium into the cell","Oppose each other, with the concentration gradient winning","Both favor movement of sodium out of the cell"],"correct":2,"why":"Two forces act on every ion, and they have to be read separately before they are combined. Sodium is more concentrated outside, so the concentration force pushes it in, and sodium is positive while the inside is negative, so the electrical force pulls it in as well. Both point the same way, which is why sodium floods in the instant a channel opens.","traps":["Sodium sits far from equilibrium at rest, which is exactly what makes an action potential possible.","This treats the negative interior as repelling sodium, but a negative interior attracts a positive ion.",null,"This assumes the two forces conflict, and for sodium at rest they both point inward.","Sodium is high outside rather than inside, so neither force sends it outward."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g022","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Two genes on the same chromosome lie 20 map units apart. In a testcross, what percentage of the offspring is expected to show a recombinant phenotype?","opts":["10%","20%","40%","50%","80%"],"correct":1,"why":"One map unit, also called a centimorgan, is defined as one percent recombinant offspring, so the conversion is one to one with no arithmetic needed. Twenty map units means about 20 percent of the testcross offspring are recombinant and the remaining 80 percent are parental. Keep the ceiling in mind as a sanity check: recombination frequency tops out at 50 percent, which is what genes that assort independently give.","traps":["This halves the map distance, but map units are already expressed as percent recombinants and need no adjustment.",null,"This doubles the distance, and doubling is not part of the conversion between map units and recombination frequency.","Fifty percent is what you see when two genes are so far apart, or on different chromosomes, that they assort independently.","Eighty percent is the parental fraction, the offspring in which no crossover occurred between the two genes."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p085","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In which structure of the male reproductive tract does spermatogenesis take place?","opts":["Vas deferens","Seminiferous tubules","Epididymis","Prostate","Interstitial (Leydig) cells between the tubules"],"correct":1,"why":"This is testing the map of the male tract: where sperm are made versus where they mature, are stored, or simply travel. Sperm are produced inside the coiled seminiferous tubules of the testis, with Sertoli cells nursing them through meiosis. Everything downstream is plumbing: the epididymis matures and stores them, the vas deferens carries them, and the accessory glands add fluid. Follow the sperm's path from the beginning and the first stop is the answer.","traps":["This is the tube that carries already mature sperm toward the urethra, a transport route rather than a production site.",null,"Sperm finish maturing and gain motility here and are stored here, but they arrive already made.","This gland contributes fluid to semen and never houses developing sperm.","Leydig cells sit between the tubules and make testosterone; the dividing sperm cells are inside the tubules, not in that interstitial tissue."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g083","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-9","topic":"Mendelian genetics","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"In a family of three children, what is the probability that at least one is female? Assume each birth is independent, with an equal chance of either sex.","opts":["3/4","1/2","7/8","1/8","3/8, the chance that exactly one of the three is female"],"correct":2,"why":"At least one is the standard signal to work with the complement. The only way to fail is for all three to be male, which is one half cubed, or one eighth, so the probability of at least one female is one minus one eighth, which is seven eighths. Whenever a stem says at least one, compute the none case and subtract.","traps":["Three quarters is the at least one answer for a family of two children, so it stops one child short.","One half is the chance for a single child, and with three chances the probability of at least one climbs well above that.",null,"One eighth is the probability that none of the three are female, which is the case you subtract rather than the answer itself.","Three eighths counts only the families with exactly one female, leaving out the ones with two or three."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-16zwvy5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Helicase has just pried the two parental strands apart at the fork. Left alone, those exposed single strands would snap back together or fold back on themselves before polymerase could use them. What keeps them available?","opts":["Single-strand binding proteins coat the separated strands and hold them open","Histones wrap each single strand into a nucleosome until polymerase arrives there","Primase stays bound along the entire length of each exposed template strand","Ligase coats the exposed strands and seals them against re-pairing with each other","Topoisomerase clamps the two strands apart by cutting and holding the backbone open"],"correct":0,"why":"This is about keeping the template accessible, which is a job of holding rather than cutting. Single-strand binding protein coats the separated strands, blocks them from re-annealing or folding into hairpins, and keeps them straight enough for polymerase to read. Sort the fork proteins by verb and they stop blurring together: helicase opens, single-strand binding protein holds, topoisomerase relieves twist, primase starts, polymerase builds, ligase seals.","traps":[null,"Nucleosomes package double-stranded DNA and would hide the template rather than expose it.","Primase touches down only where a primer is needed and then leaves, so it cannot hold a whole strand open.","Ligase seals nicks in a backbone, has no role in holding template apart, and does not coat DNA.","Topoisomerase does cut and reseal DNA, but it works ahead of the fork on supercoiling rather than on the separated strands behind it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-n1b43c","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"During anaphase two motions happen at the same time: the chromatids travel toward the poles, and the poles themselves move farther apart. Which microtubules drive each of those motions?","opts":["Astral microtubules drag the chromatids inward, and kinetochore microtubules shove the two poles apart","Kinetochore microtubules shorten to draw chromatids poleward, and polar microtubules slide to push the poles apart","Polar microtubules pull the chromatids, and kinetochore microtubules anchor each pole to the cell cortex","Kinetochore microtubules lengthen to shove the chromatids poleward, while astral microtubules shorten and draw the poles together","Actin filaments pull the chromatids poleward, and microtubules push the two poles apart from the middle"],"correct":1,"why":"Name each fiber by what it touches. Kinetochore microtubules run from a pole to a chromosome and shorten, reeling the chromatids in. Polar microtubules from opposite poles overlap in the middle and slide past one another, which lengthens the spindle and drives the poles apart, with astral fibers pulling from the cortex to help. Two separate motions, two separate sets of fibers, and the names tell you which is which.","traps":["Astral microtubules reach outward from each pole toward the cortex and never touch a chromosome, since only kinetochore microtubules attach to chromosomes.",null,"This swaps the two jobs, because the microtubules that touch chromosomes are the ones named for the kinetochore and the polar ones overlap each other in the middle.","The chromatids are pulled rather than pushed, so those fibers shorten, and drawing the poles together would undo the separation the cell just achieved.","Actin drives the cleavage furrow after the chromosomes have been sorted, and it plays no part in moving them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In a population at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, 16 percent of individuals express a recessive disorder. What percentage of the population are heterozygous carriers?","opts":["16%","24%","36%","40%","48%"],"correct":4,"why":"Start from what you are handed: the affected group is the homozygous recessive genotype, so q squared equals 0.16 and q equals 0.4. Then p equals 1 minus q, which is 0.6, and the carriers are the heterozygotes, 2pq, or 2 times 0.6 times 0.4, which comes to 0.48. The order never changes: take the square root of the affected fraction first, subtract from one to get the other allele, then double the product of the two.","traps":["16 percent is the affected group you were given, the homozygous recessive fraction, and the question asks for the carriers instead.","24 percent is the product of p and q without doubling it, and since heterozygotes can form two ways, that product has to be multiplied by two.","36 percent is p squared, which is the homozygous dominant group rather than the carriers.","0.4 is the recessive allele frequency, the square root of the affected fraction, which stops one step short of the carrier calculation.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g073","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Which feature distinguishes a stem cell from a fully differentiated somatic cell?","opts":["Self-renewal + multipotency/pluripotency","Smaller","It carries a different set of genes from the specialized cells it gives rise to","Have nucleus","No mitochondria"],"correct":0,"why":"Two properties together define a stem cell: it can divide to produce more cells like itself, which is self renewal, and it can give rise to more than one specialized cell type, which is potency. Multipotent cells such as those in bone marrow make a limited family of cell types, while pluripotent cells can make any cell of the body. When a question offers structural features alongside division and developmental options, the defining answer is always the one about what the cell can become.","traps":[null,"Cell size varies enormously in both groups, and nothing about being a stem cell sets a particular size.","A stem cell and the cell it becomes carry the same DNA, and what changes during differentiation is which genes are switched on.","Almost every human cell carries a nucleus whether it is specialized or not, so that cannot be the dividing line between the two groups.","Stem cells do contain mitochondria; many of them lean more heavily on glycolysis for energy, but the organelle is still present."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p017","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"During muscle contraction, which region of the sarcomere stays the same length?","opts":["H-zone","I-band","Sarcomere","A-band","The zone where thick and thin filaments overlap"],"correct":3,"why":"Sliding filament means the filaments themselves never shorten, they slide past each other. The A band is defined by the length of the thick myosin filaments, and since myosin does not change length, the A band does not either. Everything that shrinks is a gap: the I band is thin filament with no myosin over it and the H zone is myosin with no actin over it, so both narrow as the actin slides inward.","traps":["The H zone is the middle stretch of myosin with no actin over it, and it shrinks as actin is pulled inward.","The I band is the stretch of thin filament not yet overlapped by myosin, and it narrows as the filaments slide together.","The whole sarcomere shortening is what contraction is, since the Z discs are pulled toward each other.",null,"The overlap region grows during contraction as more thin filament slides in alongside the myosin."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-yn3j9l","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Oxygen never appears as a reactant in any Krebs cycle reaction, yet the cycle stops almost immediately when a cell runs out of oxygen. Why?","opts":["Without oxygen pyruvate can no longer cross the inner mitochondrial membrane","Without oxygen acetyl-CoA is redirected into fatty acid synthesis by a direct oxygen sensor","Without oxygen the cycle runs backward and consumes the ATP it previously made","Without oxygen the matrix pH drops far enough to denature the cycle's dehydrogenase enzymes","Without oxygen the chain stops, so NAD+ is never regenerated for the cycle"],"correct":4,"why":"The link is the supply of oxidized carriers. Three cycle steps need NAD+ and one needs FAD, and the only way a cell restocks them in bulk is by dumping electrons onto oxygen at the end of the chain. No oxygen means no reoxidation, every carrier sits in its loaded form, and each dehydrogenase step has nothing left to hand electrons to.","traps":["The pyruvate transporter does not depend on oxygen; what runs short is oxidized carriers, not substrate.","Acetyl-CoA can feed other pathways, but no oxygen sensor redirects it, and that would not explain the cycle halting.","The cycle's reactions are not simply reversed when oxygen disappears; the pathway stalls instead.","The matrix does not acidify enough to denature enzymes, and the block is a missing reactant rather than protein damage.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_cm156","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Linear eukaryotic chromosomes end in repetitive sequences called telomeres. What do telomeres protect those chromosome ends from?","opts":["Loss of sequence at the ends, because DNA polymerase can copy the very end of the lagging strand","Being recognized as breaks, leading to fusion or degradation","DNA replication","UV damage","Methylation"],"correct":1,"why":"This is testing what a chromosome end looks like to the cell's repair machinery. A free double-stranded end normally signals a break, and the repair response would either glue chromosomes together or chew the end back. Telomeres are repeated sequences bound by a protein cap that tucks the end away, so the cell reads it as a proper end and leaves it alone. The separate job people mix in is the buffer of expendable sequence they provide against the shortening that happens with each round of replication.","traps":["The end of a lagging strand still cannot be finished by DNA polymerase, which is exactly why telomeres shorten with division and why telomerase exists in some cells.",null,"Telomeres are replicated along with the rest of the chromosome, so they are not there to keep the polymerase away.","Ultraviolet damage forms pyrimidine dimers anywhere along the DNA and is handled by excision repair, not by a cap at the ends.","Methylation is a gene-silencing mark rather than a threat that a chromosome end needs shielding from."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d030","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Which pair of traits defines the class Mammalia?","opts":["Endothermy alone","Just lungs","Live birth alone","Hair + mammary glands","A four-chambered heart together with a muscular diaphragm"],"correct":3,"why":"Defining means unique to the group and present in every member, so test each option both ways. Hair and mammary glands pass on both counts, since every mammal has hair at some stage of life and mammals are the only animals with milk-producing glands. Other mammal features are real and worth holding, a diaphragm, three middle ear bones, a single lower jaw bone, teeth of several shapes, but the pair the exam wants is hair plus mammary glands.","traps":["Birds are endothermic as well, so producing your own body heat does not separate mammals from them.","Lungs are shared with reptiles, birds and adult amphibians, so they cannot define the class.","Live birth is not universal among mammals, since monotremes such as the platypus lay eggs, and some fish and snakes bear live young.",null,"A diaphragm is indeed a mammal feature, but birds have a four-chambered heart too, so this pair does not pick out mammals."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p087","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"One primary oocyte completes both meiotic divisions. How many functional gametes result, and what becomes of the other products?","opts":["4","1 ovum + 3 polar bodies (3 die)","2 ova and 2 polar bodies, one of each per division","8","2"],"correct":1,"why":"This is testing why female meiosis is deliberately lopsided. Each division splits the cytoplasm unequally, so one cell keeps nearly all of the stored material and the other is cast off as a small polar body, leaving one usable egg plus three cells that degenerate. The egg needs that cytoplasm to run the first divisions after fertilization, before the embryo can make its own supplies. Male meiosis has no such requirement, which is why it yields four usable cells.","traps":["Four is the count for sperm, where the cytoplasm is divided evenly; in the female three of the products are stripped down and discarded.",null,"Only one product keeps the cytoplasm, because both divisions are unequal, so a pair of usable eggs is never produced.","Meiosis is two divisions starting from one cell, so four products is the ceiling.","Two would mean only one of the divisions was unequal, but a polar body is thrown off at each of them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p015","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and oxytocin are synthesized in which structure?","opts":["Thyroid","Anterior pituitary","Hypothalamus (released by posterior pit)","The posterior pituitary itself, which manufactures both hormones before releasing them","Adrenal cortex"],"correct":2,"why":"This rewards knowing where a hormone is made as opposed to where it comes out. Both ADH and oxytocin are produced by neurons whose cell bodies sit in the hypothalamus, and those neurons run their axons down the stalk into the posterior pituitary, where the hormones are stored until a signal releases them into the blood. Picture the posterior pituitary as a loading dock rather than a factory.","traps":["The thyroid produces thyroid hormone and calcitonin, neither of which governs water retention or labor.","The anterior pituitary is true glandular tissue that makes its own hormones, but these two are not among them.",null,"The posterior pituitary holds axon terminals rather than hormone-producing gland cells, so it stores and releases these two without synthesizing them.","The adrenal cortex makes aldosterone, which also conserves water and sodium, but that is a different hormone from a different gland."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1ex8fre","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A herbicide blocks electron transfer from photosystem II to plastoquinone. What happens inside a treated chloroplast?","opts":["Oxygen release and NADPH production both stop, while cyclic flow can still make some ATP","Oxygen release continues at a normal rate, while NADPH production stops entirely","Every light reaction stops at once, including all ATP synthesis, since the chain has one entry point","NADPH production continues, but oxygen release stops","The Calvin cycle keeps running normally on carbon dioxide stored in the stroma"],"correct":0,"why":"Treat the chain as a line of people passing buckets. Block one handoff and the person upstream cannot let go, so photosystem II stops pulling replacement electrons out of water and the oxygen stops with it. Downstream of the block, photosystem I can still loop electrons in a circle, which is why a trickle of ATP survives.","traps":[null,"Electrons have nowhere to go, so photosystem II cannot pull replacements out of water and oxygen release stops too.","Photosystem I can still cycle electrons back through the cytochrome complex and pump protons, so some ATP survives the block.","NADPH needs electrons arriving from photosystem II during linear flow, so it stops as well.","A normal leaf stores no pool of carbon dioxide, and without ATP and NADPH the Calvin cycle stalls within seconds."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p076","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"After digestion, the majority of nutrients are absorbed into the bloodstream across the wall of which structure?","opts":["Esophagus","Stomach","The mouth, where chemical digestion begins","Large intestine","Small intestine (especially jejunum)"],"correct":4,"why":"Absorption follows surface area. The small intestine multiplies its surface with circular folds, villi, and microvilli, creating an enormous area served by capillaries and lacteals, and the jejunum handles the largest share of sugars, amino acids, and fats. When a question asks where nutrients actually enter the body, the choice naming the small intestine is the one, with the ileum reserved for bile salts and vitamin B12.","traps":["The esophagus is a muscular tube that moves food along by peristalsis and has no absorptive surface.","The stomach churns food and starts protein digestion, and apart from alcohol and a few drugs it absorbs very little.","Digestion does start in the mouth with amylase, but food is there for seconds and that lining is not built to absorb nutrients.","The colon reclaims water, some electrolytes, and vitamins made by bacteria, all after the nutrients themselves are gone.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p004","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which structure normally sets the rate of the heartbeat?","opts":["Purkinje fibers","The vagus nerve","SA node","Bundle of His","AV node"],"correct":2,"why":"The heart generates its own beat, and the sinoatrial node in the upper wall of the right atrium fires fastest, so it sets the pace and everything downstream follows. The signal spreads across the atria, pauses at the atrioventricular node long enough for the atria to finish filling the ventricles, then runs down the bundle of His and out through the Purkinje fibers. The other parts can fire on their own, just more slowly, which is why they serve as backups rather than the pacemaker.","traps":["Purkinje fibers spread the signal quickly through the ventricular walls, the last leg of the path rather than its origin.","The vagus nerve slows a rhythm the heart is already generating, so it adjusts the pace instead of setting it.",null,"The bundle of His conducts the signal from the atrioventricular node into the ventricles rather than starting it.","The atrioventricular node delays and relays the signal, and it only takes over as a slower backup if the sinoatrial node fails."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g072","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing, the protospacer adjacent motif, or PAM, is best described as which of the following?","opts":["Stop codon","Promoter","The roughly twenty nucleotide stretch of the guide RNA that base pairs with the target","Repressor binding","Short DNA sequence (e.g., NGG) adjacent to target - required for Cas9 cleavage"],"correct":4,"why":"Cas9 only cuts where two conditions line up at once: the guide RNA has to base pair with about twenty bases of the target, and a short PAM has to sit immediately next to that match in the DNA itself. For the widely used Cas9 from Streptococcus pyogenes that motif is NGG, meaning any base followed by two guanines. Remember that the PAM lives in the target DNA and not in the guide, which is also how a bacterium avoids cutting its own stored CRISPR sequences.","traps":["Stop codons are read in messenger RNA during translation, while the PAM is recognized in double stranded DNA by a protein before any transcription is involved.","A promoter is where transcription starts, and the PAM has nothing to do with switching a gene on; it is what licenses the nuclease to cut.","The guide RNA does carry a spacer that pairs with the target, but the PAM is a separate short stretch of the target DNA and is not part of the guide at all.","Operator sequences bind repressors to shut a gene down, whereas Cas9 reads the PAM to decide where to cut rather than how much to express.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d004","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Flowering plants carry out double fertilization inside the ovule. What is the ploidy of the endosperm that results?","opts":["Haploid","Purely maternal, with no contribution from the pollen","Tetraploid","Triploid (3n)","Diploid"],"correct":3,"why":"This is a counting question, so track how many nuclei fuse in each of the two fertilizations. One sperm plus the egg gives the diploid embryo. The other sperm fuses with a central cell that already holds two haploid polar nuclei, so one plus two gives three sets, the triploid endosperm that feeds the embryo. Say it as one and one make the embryo, one and two make the food, and it stays put.","traps":["Haploid describes the nutritive gametophyte tissue in a gymnosperm seed, not the endosperm of a flowering plant.","The second sperm nucleus does fertilize the central cell, so the endosperm carries a paternal set alongside the two maternal ones.","Four sets would require two sperm nuclei joining two polar nuclei, and only one sperm goes to the central cell.",null,"Diploid is the embryo, formed by the other sperm fusing with the egg; the endosperm carries one more set than that."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e010","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In what important way does the phosphorus cycle differ from the nitrogen cycle?","opts":["Has fixation step","Phosphorus is not a limiting nutrient in freshwater","Faster turnover","Has NO atmospheric phase","Done by bacteria"],"correct":3,"why":"The fastest way to keep these two cycles apart is to ask where the element is stored. Nitrogen's main reservoir is the atmosphere, so the cycle needs fixation to pull it down and denitrification to send it back up. Phosphorus has no stable gas phase, so its reservoir is rock and sediment: it enters ecosystems through weathering and leaves by settling back into sediment. That one difference explains why the phosphorus cycle turns over slowly and why phosphorus so often limits growth.","traps":["Fixation belongs to nitrogen, which has a gas to capture; phosphorus enters ecosystems by the weathering of rock instead.","Phosphorus is very often the limiting nutrient in freshwater, which is exactly why phosphate runoff sets off algal blooms.","The phosphorus cycle is the slower of the two, because its main reservoir is rock that releases phosphate only as it weathers.",null,"Bacteria drive most of the nitrogen conversions, while phosphorus largely moves as phosphate without needing its chemical form changed."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g021","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Which description best fits an individual with Turner syndrome (45,X)?","opts":["Phenotypically male","Phenotypically female, with one Barr body in each cell","Phenotypically male; tall","Phenotypically female; tall","Phenotypically female; sterile, short stature"],"correct":4,"why":"Turner syndrome is a single X with no second sex chromosome, and without a Y there is no signal to build male structures, so the individual is phenotypically female. The exam features worth carrying are short stature, non-functional ovaries with infertility, and a webbed neck. The rule underneath is that the Y, not the number of X chromosomes, decides male development.","traps":["Male development requires the Y chromosome, and a 45,X karyotype has none.","A 45,X cell has only one X and that single X stays active, so there is no inactivated X to form a Barr body.","This combines two features that do not apply here, since there is no Y to direct male development and stature is reduced rather than increased.","Tall stature points toward the extra X of triple X or the extra chromosome of Klinefelter, while Turner syndrome is characteristically short.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1v1yzon","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Which molecule of ATP or GTP in cellular respiration is made with no help at all from the proton gradient?","opts":["The ATP made when NADH is oxidized at complex I","The ATP made as protons spin the rotor of ATP synthase","The GTP made as succinyl-CoA becomes succinate","The ATP made as oxygen is reduced to water at the end of the chain","The ATP made when cytochrome c passes electrons onward"],"correct":2,"why":"This is the split between substrate-level phosphorylation and oxidative phosphorylation. In substrate-level phosphorylation a phosphate moves straight from a high-energy substrate onto ADP or GDP, with no membrane required, and the Krebs cycle does it once per turn at the succinyl-CoA step. Glycolysis uses the same trick twice in its payoff phase, which is why glycolysis still works when the mitochondria are shut down.","traps":["Complex I oxidizing NADH pumps protons, so any ATP that follows still rides on the gradient.","This is the definition of chemiosmosis, where the gradient is the entire energy source.",null,"Reducing oxygen is the last electron handoff, and the energy released there goes into the gradient.","Cytochrome c moving electrons between complexes drives proton pumping rather than making ATP directly."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g070","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Real-time quantitative PCR (qPCR) differs from a standard PCR run in what it reports. What does real-time qPCR measure?","opts":["The size of the amplified fragment, read from a gel once the reaction has finished","Quantitative levels of DNA/RNA in real time during amplification","DNA sequence","Methylation","Protein"],"correct":1,"why":"A qPCR reaction is an ordinary PCR with a fluorescent reporter added, so the machine reads a signal at the end of every cycle instead of only at the end of the run. The cycle at which fluorescence rises above background tells you how much target was there to begin with, and RNA can be measured the same way by first copying it into complementary DNA. The word to anchor on is quantitative: this method answers how much, while the other nucleic acid methods answer what sequence.","traps":["Fragment size read off a gel after the run is the endpoint readout of standard PCR, and the whole point of the real-time version is that it collects data during the run rather than after it.",null,"Reading the order of bases is what sequencing does; qPCR copies a target and counts how fast the signal grows, and never reports the sequence itself.","Seeing methylation requires bisulfite treatment or a methylation specific assay, because the copies PCR makes do not carry the methyl groups of the original.","Protein is detected with antibody based methods such as a Western blot or an ELISA, and no form of PCR touches protein."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-fiwnsa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Epinephrine binds a receptor on a liver cell and the cell starts breaking down glycogen. After the receptor changes shape, put the middle of the pathway in order:","opts":["Protein kinase A is activated, cAMP rises, a G protein binds GTP, adenylyl cyclase is activated","A G protein binds GTP, adenylyl cyclase is activated, cAMP rises, protein kinase A is activated","A G protein binds GTP, cAMP rises, adenylyl cyclase is activated, and protein kinase A is activated","Adenylyl cyclase is activated, a G protein binds GTP, cAMP rises, protein kinase A is activated","cAMP rises, a G protein binds GTP, protein kinase A is activated, adenylyl cyclase is activated"],"correct":1,"why":"Order is the entire question, so walk the relay one handoff at a time. The activated receptor loads the G protein with GTP, the G protein switches on adenylyl cyclase, that enzyme makes cAMP from ATP, and cAMP frees protein kinase A to phosphorylate its targets. Receptor, G protein, enzyme, second messenger, kinase is the shape of every version of this pathway.","traps":["The kinase is the last step rather than the first, since cAMP is what releases it.",null,"cAMP is made by adenylyl cyclase, so the enzyme has to be active before the messenger rises.","Adenylyl cyclase cannot switch on before the G protein that activates it.","cAMP is a product of this pathway, so it cannot appear before the enzyme that makes it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g019","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Down syndrome results from which chromosomal abnormality?","opts":["Monosomy X","XXY","Trisomy 18","Trisomy 21","Monosomy 21"],"correct":3,"why":"This is straight recall on the autosomal trisomies, and the one to lock down first is that Down syndrome is three copies of chromosome 21. A clean way to hold the set is by number: 21 is Down, 18 is Edwards, 13 is Patau. The choice naming three copies of chromosome 21 is the one.","traps":["Monosomy X is Turner syndrome, a sex chromosome condition rather than an autosomal trisomy.","The 47,XXY karyotype is Klinefelter syndrome, again a sex chromosome count rather than anything involving chromosome 21.","Trisomy 18 is Edwards syndrome, a different and far more severe autosomal trisomy.",null,"This has the right chromosome but the wrong direction, since Down syndrome is an extra copy and the loss of a whole autosome is not survivable."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g055","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-6","topic":"The central dogma","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"In a female mammal whose cells carry two X chromosomes, what happens during X-inactivation in each somatic cell?","opts":["Loss of X","Y-linked silencing","Random inactivation of one X, producing a Barr body","Selective methylation of all X's","Both X chromosomes are partly inactivated so that each one gives half the normal output"],"correct":2,"why":"This is testing how mammals balance X-linked gene dosage between the sexes. Early in development each cell in a female independently shuts down one of its two X chromosomes, and that condensed chromosome shows up as a Barr body at the edge of the nucleus. The choice is random in each cell but inherited by all of its descendants, which is why a calico cat is patchy: neighboring patches are clones that silenced different X chromosomes. The count worth keeping is that the number of Barr bodies is one fewer than the number of X chromosomes.","traps":["The chromosome is still present in the nucleus; it is condensed and silenced rather than discarded.","The Y is not involved, and a female cell carrying out this process has no Y chromosome to silence.",null,"If every X were silenced the cell would make no X-linked gene products at all, and one X has to stay active in each cell.","The cell does not split the output between its two copies; one whole chromosome is silenced and the other works at full strength."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-19awm96","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"At a synapse, calcium entering the axon terminal triggers vesicles of neurotransmitter to release their contents into the cleft. During that release, the vesicle membrane:","opts":["Pinches off and floats away into the synaptic cleft as a free bubble","Is digested by lysosomal enzymes","Passes intact through the plasma membrane","Is pumped out by the sodium potassium pump","Fuses with the plasma membrane and becomes part of it"],"correct":4,"why":"Exocytosis is fusion, not ejection. The vesicle membrane joins the plasma membrane, the inside of the vesicle opens to the outside world, and the cell surface grows by exactly that patch, which is later recovered by endocytosis. Contents go out, membrane stays.","traps":["Membrane does not survive as a free bubble in the cleft, it merges into the cell surface.","Lysosomal digestion is how a cell breaks down internalized material, not how it secretes.","A whole vesicle cannot pass through an intact bilayer.","That pump moves ions only and has nothing to do with vesicle traffic.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d033","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Lampreys are placed in which group of vertebrates?","opts":["Agnatha (jawless fish)","Cephalochordata, the lancelets","Chondrichthyes","Reptilia","Osteichthyes"],"correct":0,"why":"The single feature being tested here is the jaw. Lampreys and hagfish have a round sucking mouth with rasping teeth and no hinged jaw, which is exactly what agnathan means, without jaws. They also lack paired fins and keep a notochord throughout adult life. Sharks share the cartilage but have hinged jaws and paired fins, so jaws are the line that separates the two.","traps":[null,"Lancelets are small invertebrate chordates with no skull and no vertebrae, while a lamprey is a vertebrate with a cartilaginous braincase.","A lamprey's skeleton is cartilage, which is the tempting part, but cartilaginous fish have hinged jaws and paired fins while a lamprey has neither.","Reptiles are amniotes with lungs and scaly skin, nothing like a jawless aquatic vertebrate.","Bony fish have jaws and a skeleton of true bone, and a lamprey has neither."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-e9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Naturally occurring chlorine consists of two isotopes: ³⁵Cl with mass 34.97 amu at 75.0% abundance and ³⁷Cl with mass 36.97 amu at 25.0% abundance. Using the weighted-average formula AW = Σ(fᵢ · mᵢ), what is the average atomic mass of Cl reported on the periodic table?","opts":["35.5 amu","36.0 amu","35.0 amu","36.5 amu","37.0 amu"],"correct":0,"why":"Average atomic mass = (0.750 × 35) + (0.250 × 37) = 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.5 amu. The 75/25 abundance ratio places Cl's average mass closer to 35 (the more abundant) but not exactly at 35.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#1xvu5bk","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻² M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["1","1.3","12","12.7","13"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻² = 1.0 × 10⁻¹ M. pOH = 1, pH = 14 − 1 = 13.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_p-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"First ionization energy (IE₁) generally decreases down a group because the outermost electron sits in a larger, higher-n orbital that is farther from the nucleus and more shielded by inner shells. Among the alkali metals Li (520 kJ/mol), Na (496), K (419), and Rb (403), which has the LOWEST IE₁?","opts":["Na","K","Rb","All four are equal","Li"],"correct":2,"why":"Going down group 1 (Li → Na → K → Rb), the valence ns electron occupies a progressively larger orbital and is shielded by more inner-shell electrons. The valence electron in Rb (5s¹) is the easiest to remove, giving Rb the lowest IE₁ of the four.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Henry: solubility of O₂ doubles when P doubles. Why?","opts":["Total pressure sets solubility, not partial pressure","Higher T","Higher P drives more gas in","Le Châtelier with no gas mole change","Lower P"],"correct":2,"why":"Henry's law: gas solubility ∝ partial pressure. More P, so more gas dissolves.","traps":["Henry's law uses the partial pressure of the gas in question, so pumping in an inert gas above the liquid dissolves no extra oxygen.","Heating drives dissolved gas back out of solution, so temperature works the other way.",null,"Le Chatelier does fit here, but only because dissolving does change the number of gas-phase particles.","Lowering the pressure pulls gas out of solution, which is why a soda fizzes the moment it is opened."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:x10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Mix 50 mL of 0.20 M AgNO₃ with 50 mL of 0.10 M NaCl. Limiting?","opts":["NaCl","AgNO₃","Need more info","AgCl","Same"],"correct":0,"why":"mol AgNO₃ = 0.20×0.050 = 0.010. mol NaCl = 0.10×0.050 = 0.0050. Ag⁺+Cl⁻ → AgCl (1:1). Less NaCl, so limiting.","traps":[null,"AgNO₃ brings 0.010 mol to the flask, twice what the chloride can pair with, so it ends up in excess.","Everything needed is on the page: multiply each molarity by its volume in liters, then compare the moles against the 1 to 1 ratio.","AgCl is the product here, and a limiting reagent is one of the starting materials, the one that runs out first.","Equal volumes do not mean equal moles, since the silver nitrate is twice as concentrated as the sodium chloride."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-e18","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, MW = 180.0 g/mol) is the primary blood sugar. A 36.0 g sample contains 12 H atoms per glucose molecule. Use n_H = (m / MW) × 12 to compute the moles of hydrogen.","opts":["0.40","1.2","2.4","4.8","12"],"correct":2,"why":"n_glucose = 36/180 = 0.200 mol; n_H = 0.200 × 12 = 2.40 mol. Forgetting the ×12 stoichiometry per molecule gives the 0.20 trap; mistakenly multiplying by 6 (for C₆) gives 1.2.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_el-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Molten Al2O3 (cryolite-dissolved) is electrolyzed in a Hall-Heroult cell. The cathode reaction is Al^3+ + 3 e- -&gt; Al(s), with M(Al) = 27 g/mol and F = 96485 C/mol. A total charge of 9650 C passes through the cell. Calculate the mass of aluminum deposited.","opts":["0.90 g","0.50 g","1.0 g","0.30 g","2.7 g"],"correct":0,"why":"Moles e- = 9650/96485 = 0.100 mol. Moles Al = 0.100/3 = 0.0333 mol. Mass = 0.0333 x 27 = 0.90 g. Forgetting to divide by n = 3 gives the trap value 2.7 g.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A reaction at 1 atm has both ΔH &lt; 0 (exothermic) and ΔS &lt; 0 (decrease in disorder), placing it in the (−,−) quadrant of the spontaneity table. Using ΔG = ΔH − T·ΔS, the sign of ΔG depends on whether |ΔH| or |T·ΔS| dominates. Under what temperature regime is the reaction spontaneous?","opts":["Always spontaneous","Spontaneous only at T = 0 K","Never spontaneous","Spontaneous at high T","Spontaneous at low T"],"correct":4,"why":"With ΔH &lt; 0 and ΔS &lt; 0, the −TΔS term is positive. At low T, |ΔH| &gt; |TΔS|, so ΔG &lt; 0 (spontaneous). At high T, the TΔS penalty dominates and ΔG &gt; 0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Four ground-state species are examined for paramagnetism (presence of unpaired electrons): Ar ([Ne]3s^2 3p^6), Mg^2+ ([Ne]), Fe^2+ ([Ar]3d^6), and Zn^2+ ([Ar]3d^10). Hund's rule applied to the d^6 configuration of Fe^2+ leaves four unpaired electrons. Which species is paramagnetic?","opts":["None of these","Ar","Fe^2+","Zn^2+","Mg^2+"],"correct":2,"why":"Ar (3p^6) and Mg^2+ ([Ne]) are closed-shell. Zn^2+ has 3d^10, fully filled and diamagnetic. Fe^2+ has 3d^6: filling t2g/eg (or just five degenerate d orbitals in a free ion) by Hund's rule leaves 4 unpaired e- and so is paramagnetic.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A pharmacist measures out 4.50 g of pure water (H₂O, MW = 18.0 g/mol). Avogadro's number is 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹. How many individual hydrogen atoms are present in this sample?","opts":["1.5 × 10²⁴","3.0 × 10²³","6.0 × 10²³","1.5 × 10²³","9.0 × 10²³"],"correct":1,"why":"Moles H₂O = 4.50/18.0 = 0.250 mol. Each H₂O contains 2 H atoms, so mol H = 0.500. Atoms H = 0.500 × 6.022×10²³ ≈ 3.0 × 10²³. The factor-of-2 stoichiometric step (H per H₂O) is the most common error point.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:bl4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which species is a Lewis acid in aqueous solution AND lowers pH (acidic)?","opts":["Fe³⁺","Na⁺","K⁺","Ca²⁺","Mg²⁺ (slightly)"],"correct":0,"why":"Fe³⁺ is a strong Lewis acid - it polarizes coordinated water enough to release H⁺: Fe(H₂O)₆³⁺ ⇌ Fe(H₂O)₅(OH)²⁺ + H⁺. Na⁺/K⁺ have negligible polarizing power. Mg²⁺ is weak.","traps":[null,"Sodium is large and singly charged, so it cannot polarize its water shell enough to release a proton.","Potassium polarizes even less than sodium, leaving the pH of the solution untouched.","Calcium holds its water shell too loosely to pull protons off it, so the solution stays essentially neutral.","Magnesium is mildly acidic in water, and the triply charged iron ion is far stronger."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nr-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"At 25 °C, a galvanic cell has E°_cell = 0.50 V and n = 2 electrons transferred per cell reaction. Using the relation between E° and K: log K = nE°/0.0592, calculate the equilibrium constant K for the cell reaction.","opts":["10^(-17)","10^17","10^5","10^10","10"],"correct":1,"why":"log K = nE°/0.0592 = (2)(0.50)/0.0592 = 16.9 ≈ 17. Therefore K ≈ 10^17. Forgetting n (using just E°/0.0592) gives ~10^8.5; sign errors give 10^(-17).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-limiting#543faz","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"2 H₂ + O₂ → 2 H₂O\n\nIf 3 mol of H₂ reacts with 2 mol of O₂, how many moles of H₂O can form?","opts":["4 mol","5 mol","1.5 mol","3 mol","2 mol"],"correct":3,"why":"H₂: 3 / 2 = 1.5. O₂: 2 / 1 = 2. The smaller one limits. Product = 1.5 × 2 = 3 mol.","traps":["You built the answer from the reagent in EXCESS. The smaller moles-per-coefficient runs out first and sets the yield.","You added the reactants. Moles are not conserved; use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","You found the limiting amount but did not multiply by the product coefficient.",null,"You compared raw moles. Divide each by its coefficient first, then pick the smaller."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-combined-gas#1g8cuuw","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A gas occupies 4 L at 3 atm and 27 °C. What is its volume at 4 atm and 327 °C?","opts":["3 L","6 L","36.3 L","1.5 L","10.7 L"],"correct":1,"why":"P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 in Kelvin. V2 = 4 × (3/4) × (600/300) = 6 L.","traps":["You ignored the temperature change. Both P and T changed here.",null,"You used Celsius in the ratio. Convert both temperatures to Kelvin first.","Temperature ratio is upside down. Hotter gas expands, so volume moves with temperature.","Pressure ratio is upside down. More pressure squeezes the gas, so volume moves opposite to pressure."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#pxho1a","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.693 min⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["0.48 min","1.44 min","2.89 min","1 min","2 min"],"correct":3,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.693 = 1 min. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":["You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath.","That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.","You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_pd5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"On the CO2 phase diagram, the triple point lies at 5.11 atm and 216.6 K, while the critical point is at 73 atm and 304 K. A sample of CO2 is held at 1.00 atm and 25°C (298 K), conditions that fall well below the triple-point pressure but above its temperature. What phase is the CO2 in?","opts":["Gas","Liquid","Supercritical fluid","Solid","Mixture of solid and liquid"],"correct":0,"why":"At 1 atm (below 5.11 atm triple-point pressure) liquid CO2 is unstable, so the diagram shows only solid below the sublimation curve and gas above. At 298 K and 1 atm we are above the sublimation curve, so CO2 exists as a gas.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#1ex3jih","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 0.5 m aqueous solution of Na₂SO₄? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-0.768 °C","-0.93 °C","-3.72 °C","-2.79 °C","2.79 °C"],"correct":3,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (3)(1.86)(0.5) = 2.79 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 2.79 = -2.79 °C.","traps":["You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","You forgot the van't Hoff factor. Na₂SO₄ splits into 3 ions, so i = 3.","You counted one ion too many. Write the dissociation out and count the particles.",null,"Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-dilution#1siwg7v","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"How many mL of water must be ADDED to 100 mL of 6 M HCl to make a 1.5 M solution?","opts":["300 mL","25 mL","400 mL","500 mL","301 mL"],"correct":0,"why":"M1V1 = M2V2, so V2 = (6 × 100) / 1.5 = 400 mL total. Water added = 400 − 100 = 300 mL.","traps":[null,"You divided the volume. Diluting makes the volume bigger, not smaller.","That is the FINAL volume. The question asks how much water you add, so subtract the volume you started with.","You added the starting volume on top of the final volume. M1V1 = M2V2 already gives the total.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lc2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), there are 4 mol of gas on the left and 2 mol on the right. Le Chatelier: raising pressure shifts equilibrium toward the side with FEWER gas moles. If pressure is increased from 1 atm to 100 atm at 700 K, which way does equilibrium shift?","opts":["Shifts left (toward N₂ + H₂, 4 mol gas)","Equilibrium constant decreases","Depends on temperature","Shifts right (toward NH₃, 2 mol gas)","No change"],"correct":3,"why":"Higher P favors fewer gas moles. The right side has 2 mol vs 4 mol on the left, so equilibrium shifts right toward NH₃. K_p stays constant (only T changes K), but the position shifts. This is why industrial Haber runs at high pressure.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ca3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A 200.0 g block of iron at 100.0°C (c_Fe = 0.45 J/g·°C) is dropped into 100.0 g of water at 20.0°C (c_H₂O = 4.18 J/g·°C) in a perfectly insulated calorimeter. Assuming no heat loss to the container, what is the final equilibrium temperature?","opts":["40°C","60°C","25°C","34°C","30°C"],"correct":3,"why":"Set heat lost by Fe = heat gained by H₂O: m_Fe·c_Fe·(T_f - 100) + m_w·c_w·(T_f - 20) = 0. → 90(T_f-100) + 418(T_f-20) = 0 → 508 T_f = 17,360 → T_f ≈ 34.2°C. Water's much larger m·c product pulls the final temperature toward water's initial value.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"originals:ab-77b0d210b1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"0.10 mol of a weak acid HA (pKa 5.00) is mixed with 0.050 mol of NaOH in 1.0 L of water. What is the pH of the resulting solution?","opts":["1.30","4.70","5.00","5.30","7.00"],"correct":2,"why":"Do the reaction first, then the equilibrium. NaOH converts 0.050 mol of HA into A-, leaving 0.050 mol HA and 0.050 mol A-. Equal amounts means pH equals pKa, so 5.00. Half neutralizing a weak acid is a classic way to make a buffer.","traps":["You treated 0.050 M as strong acid. The NaOH is consumed and what remains is a mix of HA and A-.","You used a base to acid ratio of 0.5. After the reaction there is 0.050 mol of HA and 0.050 mol of A-, a ratio of 1.",null,"You used a base to acid ratio of 2. Half of the HA remains, so the two are equal.","Partial neutralization of a weak acid does not give a neutral solution. It gives a buffer."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"gchem-a:sw6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which has the strongest conjugate base: HCl, HF, HClO₄, HBr?","opts":["All four are equally weak bases","HClO₄","HCl","HBr","HF"],"correct":4,"why":"Strong acid, so weak conjugate base. HF is the only weak acid in this list, so its conjugate (F⁻) is the strongest base. The other 3 are strong acids, so conjugate bases (Cl⁻, ClO₄⁻, Br⁻) are negligibly basic.","traps":["Three of these come from strong acids and do have negligible conjugate bases, but HF is weak, so fluoride stands apart.","Perchloric acid is the strongest of the four, which makes perchlorate the weakest base of the four.","Hydrochloric acid is strong, so chloride is left with no appetite for protons.","Hydrobromic acid is strong, so bromide behaves as a spectator in water.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_rl-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"A second-order reaction A, so products has rate law: rate = k[A]² with k = 0.500 M⁻¹·s⁻¹. The initial concentration is [A]₀ = 0.200 M. Using the half-life expression t₁/₂ = 1/(k[A]₀) for second-order kinetics, calculate the time required for [A] to fall to 0.100 M.","opts":["5 s","10 s","20 s","40 s","1.4 s"],"correct":1,"why":"For a second-order reaction, t₁/₂ = 1/(k·[A]₀) = 1/(0.500 × 0.200) = 1/0.100 = 10 s. Note that unlike first-order, the second-order half-life DEPENDS on [A]₀, so subsequent half-lives get progressively longer.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"The catalytic Ostwald oxidation of ammonia is 4 NH3 + 5 O2 -&gt; 4 NO + 6 H2O(g). Standard enthalpies of formation (kJ/mol): NH3 = -46, NO = +90, H2O(g) = -242, O2 = 0. Use ΔH_rxn = sum(n·ΔH_f,products) - sum(n·ΔH_f,reactants) to compute ΔH_rxn for one mole of equation.","opts":["+908 kJ","-184 kJ","-724 kJ","-1092 kJ","-908 kJ"],"correct":4,"why":"Products: 4(+90) + 6(-242) = 360 - 1452 = -1092. Reactants: 4(-46) + 5(0) = -184. ΔH = -1092 - (-184) = -908 kJ. The reaction is highly exothermic.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_b-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Single-bond dissociation energies (kJ/mol) at 25°C: C-C ≈ 348, C-N ≈ 308, C-O ≈ 360, C-F ≈ 488, C-Cl ≈ 328. Bond strength generally increases with bond polarity (greater ΔEN) and shorter bond length. Which single bond is the STRONGEST?","opts":["C-N","C-C","C-Cl","C-O","C-F"],"correct":4,"why":"C-F has both high polarity (ΔEN = 1.5) and a short bond length (~138 pm) due to F's small radius, giving the highest single-bond dissociation energy (~488 kJ/mol). The bond strength order C-F &gt; C-O &gt; C-C &gt; C-Cl &gt; C-N reflects competing electronegativity, atomic size, and bond length effects.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:gb6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a reaction at 298 K with ΔH=+60 kJ/mol and ΔS=+150 J/(mol·K), is it spontaneous? ΔG?","opts":["No, ΔG=+60 kJ","No, ΔG=+15 kJ","Yes, ΔG=−15 kJ","Yes, ΔG=−45 kJ","No, ΔG=+105 kJ"],"correct":1,"why":"TΔS = 298 × 150 = 44,700 J = 44.7 kJ. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = 60 − 44.7 = +15.3 kJ. POSITIVE → NOT spontaneous at 298 K. Crossover T = ΔH/ΔS = 60,000/150 = 400 K - needs higher T to flip.","traps":["+60 kJ is ΔH on its own, with the 44.7 kJ that TΔS supplies never taken off.",null,"The magnitude is close and the sign is not, since 60 minus 44.7 leaves a positive 15 kJ.","−45 kJ is the TΔS term itself wearing a minus sign, and ΔG is what is left after taking TΔS off ΔH.","+105 kJ comes from adding TΔS to ΔH, and the free energy equation subtracts it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_imf-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Surface tensions at 25°C (mN/m): hexane 17.9, acetone 23.7, water 72.0, pentane 16.0. Surface tension scales with the strength of cohesive intermolecular forces. Hydrogen bonding produces unusually large cohesion. Which liquid has the HIGHEST surface tension?","opts":["Methanol","Pentane","Hexane","Water","Acetone"],"correct":3,"why":"Water's extensive H-bond network (~20 kJ/mol per bond, 2 H-bonds per molecule) produces strong cohesive forces, giving the largest surface tension (72 mN/m). Hexane and pentane have only London forces (~17 mN/m); acetone is dipolar but cannot H-bond donate strongly.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:nc4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Approximate molar solubility of MX with Ksp = 4 × 10⁻⁹ (no calculator).","opts":["~2 × 10⁻⁵ M","~2 × 10⁻⁴ M","~4 × 10⁻⁹ M","~10⁻³ M","~6 × 10⁻⁵ M"],"correct":4,"why":"For a 1:1 salt, Ksp = s², so s = √Ksp. √(4 × 10⁻⁹) = 2 × √(10⁻⁹). Note √(10⁻⁹) ≈ √(10⁻¹⁰) × √10 ≈ 10⁻⁵ × 3.16 ≈ 3 × 10⁻⁵. So s ≈ 2 × 3 × 10⁻⁵ = 6 × 10⁻⁵ M. Estimate within a factor of 2 of the exact answer.","traps":["2 × 10⁻⁵ M comes from rounding 10⁻⁴·⁵ down to 10⁻⁵, and that half power is closer to 3 × 10⁻⁵.","2 × 10⁻⁴ M would give Ksp = 4 × 10⁻⁸ - one order of magnitude too high.","Ksp is NOT solubility. Ksp = s², so you must take the square root to get s.","10⁻³ M would mean Ksp = 10⁻⁶ - three orders of magnitude too high.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ks4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Two sparingly soluble salts at 25°C are compared: AgCl (Ksp = 1.8 x 10^-10, dissolves as Ag+ + Cl-) and PbI2 (Ksp = 9.8 x 10^-9, dissolves as Pb^2+ + 2 I-). Note the different stoichiometries: s for AgCl satisfies Ksp = s^2, while s for PbI2 satisfies Ksp = 4s^3. Which salt is MORE soluble in pure water?","opts":["Equal because Ksp values are similar","PbI2","Both are completely insoluble","Cannot determine without comparing s values","AgCl"],"correct":1,"why":"AgCl: s = sqrt(1.8e-10) = 1.3 x 10^-5 M. PbI2: s = (9.8e-9/4)^(1/3) = 1.34 x 10^-3 M. PbI2 is ~100x more soluble, even though Ksp values look 'close' on a log scale. Ksp alone cannot rank solubility when stoichiometries differ.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"In water (H₂O), the central oxygen has 2 O-H σ bonds and 2 lone pairs of electrons. VSEPR predicts a tetrahedral electron geometry with bond angles compressed to ~104.5°. What hybridization at oxygen accommodates 4 electron domains?","opts":["sp³d²","sp³d","sp³","sp²","sp"],"correct":2,"why":"Steric number on O = 2 σ bonds + 2 lone pairs = 4. This requires sp³ hybridization (one 2s + three 2p = four sp³ orbitals). Two sp³ orbitals form O-H bonds, the other two hold lone pairs.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-23bj23","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Predict the major product. 3-Hexyne (CH3CH2-C#C-CH2CH3) is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation: (1) BH3.THF, (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the single major product and note why an aldehyde is not an option here.","opts":["CCC=C(O)CC","CCCCCC=O","CCCCC(C)=O","CCC(=O)C(=O)CC","CCCC(=O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"3-Hexyne is internal and symmetrical, so hydration (by either method) can only give a ketone, and both orientations converge on 3-hexanone. Hydroboration's anti-Markovnikov selectivity is irrelevant for a symmetrical internal alkyne. An aldehyde (hexanal) is impossible without a terminal C-H; 2-hexanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the intermediate; the 1,2-diketone is over-oxidation.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC#CCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1juifa8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"The molecule below is 2-bromo-3-chlorobutane drawn with both stereocenters defined. Assign the (C2, C3) configuration. (Number from the bromine-bearing carbon as C2.)","opts":["(2S, 3R)","(2R, 3R) - and the molecule is meso","(2S, 3S) - and the molecule is meso","(2R, 3S)","(2R, 3R)"],"correct":4,"why":"(2R,3R). It is not meso because the two ends carry different halogens (Br vs Cl), so no internal mirror plane. Keyed correct=0 is right; the meso distractors are good traps.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Cl)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8k4jix","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"1-Phenyl-1-propyne (an internal alkyne) is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst. Which alkene is formed?","opts":["CCCc1ccccc1","C/C=C/c1ccccc1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","CC=CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar syn-reduces the internal alkyne to the cis (Z) alkene: cis-1-phenylpropene (option A, STEREOZ). option B = trans (Na/NH3, trap); option C propylbenzene = over-reduction; option D styrene wrong skeleton; option E saturates the ring.","traps":null,"qs":"CC#Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1x7pi97","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"An unknown gives a 1H NMR with three signals: a 3H singlet near 2.5 ppm and two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (~7.4 and ~8.1 ppm). Of the two candidate structures, the one drawn is p-nitrotoluene; the contrast candidate is tert-butanol (CC(C)(C)O). Which structure is consistent with the spectrum?","opts":["The drawn structure (p-nitrotoluene), except that its methyl signal must integrate for 9H rather than 3H against the two aromatic doublets","tert-Butanol, because its 9H singlet matches the 3H methyl peak","The drawn structure (p-nitrotoluene): CH3 singlet plus two aromatic doublets from the para ring","Neither structure fits, because p-nitrotoluene is para-disubstituted and would give four separate aromatic signals rather than two doublets","tert-Butanol, because it produces two aromatic doublets"],"correct":2,"why":"p-Nitrotoluene has a benzylic CH3 (3H singlet, ~2.5 ppm) and a para-disubstituted ring giving an AA'BB' pattern that reads as two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (the H ortho to NO2 shifted downfield ~8.1 ppm). tert-Butanol has no aromatic H and would show only a 9H singlet plus OH, so it cannot match.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1kvubba","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"2-Methylcyclohexanone is condensed with pyrrolidine (acid catalyst, water removed) to form an enamine. Which regiochemical product predominates?","opts":["CC1CCCCC1N1CCCC1","CN=C1CCCCC1C","OC1(C)CCCCC1N1CCCC1","CC1=C(N2CCCC2)CCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1N1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"With an unsymmetrical ketone, enamine formation favors the LESS-substituted double bond: forming the alkene toward the methylated carbon (option B) places the bulky pyrrolidine and the alpha-methyl in destabilizing A-1,3 strain, so it is disfavored. The C=C therefore goes away from the methyl group (option A). Option C is a saturated amine, D is a nonsense carbinolamine, and E is an N-methyl imine (wrong amine, wrong class).","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CCCCC1=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-veki4n","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Identify the MOST acidic proton in ethyl acetoacetate (ethyl 3-oxobutanoate, drawn). Each choice shows the conjugate base from removing a specific proton; choose the most stable one.","opts":["CCOC(=O)CC(=O)[CH2-]","C[CH-]OC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O","CCOC(=O)C=C(C)[O-]","[CH2-]COC(=O)CC(C)=O"],"correct":2,"why":"Central CH2 between the ester and ketone carbonyls is most acidic (pKa ~11), doubly stabilized enolate. Option a (CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O) is that central carbanion (verified: carbanion C bridges ester C=O and ketone C=O). b is alpha to only the ketone; c/d are unstabilized ethyl-group carbanions; e is an O-centered resonance form, not the most-acidic-C-H removal. correct=0 verified.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1pnqwxk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"3-Methyl-2-butanol is treated with concentrated HCl. Under these SN1 conditions, what is the major organic product?","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)C","ClCC(C)CC","CC(Cl)(C)CC","ClCCC(C)C","CC=C(C)C"],"correct":2,"why":"Protonation/loss of water gives a secondary C2 cation; a 1,2-hydride shift from C3 gives the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation, which captures chloride to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane (CC(Cl)(C)CC = CCC(C)(C)Cl). Option 0 is the rearranged tertiary chloride and is the sole correct major product. Distractors are the un-rearranged secondary (o1) and two primary chlorides (o2,o3). Single reactive center, two layered concepts (Markovnikov ionization + hydride shift).","traps":null,"qs":"CC(O)C(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-rcvby2","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"1-Phenyl-2-propanone (phenylacetone, PhCH2COCH3) is treated with LDA, then CH3I. Deprotonation favors the more resonance-stabilized (benzylic) enolate. What is the major product?","opts":["CC(=O)C(CC)c1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(C)(C(C)=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccc(C)cc1","CC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The benzylic alpha carbon (PhCH2) gives the more resonance-stabilized enolate because charge delocalizes into the ring, and the stem specifies that enolate, so methylation occurs there to give PhCH(CH3)COCH3 (option A). Distractors methylate the methyl-side alpha carbon, alkylate the ring, add the wrong group, or dialkylate the benzylic carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-349b912165","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Phenol is far more acidic than cyclohexanol. What is the main reason?","opts":["The ring carbons of phenol are more electronegative than oxygen, so they pull the proton off the hydroxyl group","The negative charge of the phenoxide ion is delocalized into the ring by resonance","Cyclohexanol cannot form hydrogen bonds","Phenol has a higher molar mass, and larger molecules ionize more completely in aqueous solution","The O-H bond in phenol is longer"],"correct":1,"why":"Always look at the conjugate base. In phenoxide the lone pair on oxygen sits next to a pi system, so the negative charge is shared with the ring carbons. In cyclohexanol the charge is stuck on one oxygen. A more stable conjugate base means a stronger acid.","traps":["Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon. The key is that the charge can spread out over the ring.",null,"Both compounds form hydrogen bonds. That does not explain the difference in acidity.","Molar mass has nothing to do with acidity.","The decisive factor is the stability of the conjugate base, not the bond length."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-9446ti","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"(E)-2-Butene is treated with HBr. A new stereocenter forms at the carbon that gains Br. Which option best represents the stereochemical outcome of the major product?","opts":["CCC(Br)C","CC(Br)(C)C","CCCCBr","CC[C@H](Br)C","CC[C@@H](Br)C"],"correct":0,"why":"Protonation gives a planar achiral 2-butyl cation; Br- attacks both faces equally, giving racemic 2-bromobutane, best shown by the structure drawn without defined stereochemistry. The single-enantiomer drawings are each wrong alone; the others are wrong regiochemistry/skeleton. Defensible single answer.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1kibsuj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Toluene (drawn) can react with Cl2/hv or with Br2/hv at the benzylic position. Compared with chlorination, why is radical benzylic BROMINATION far more selective (cleaner) for the benzylic C-H?","opts":["The benzylic C-Br bond is weaker, which speeds initiation.","Bromine abstracts hydrogen faster, leaving no time for side reactions.","Bromine atoms are smaller than chlorine atoms, so they fit neatly into the crowded benzylic pocket and are physically unable to reach any of the ring hydrogens.","The bromine atom is less reactive, so its transition state is later and more product-like, magnifying the preference for the most stable (benzylic) radical.","Bromination proceeds through an ionic pathway while chlorination proceeds through a radical chain, so only the ionic route can tell one C-H bond from another."],"correct":3,"why":"Br is less reactive than Cl, so its H-abstraction transition state is later/more product-like (Hammond postulate), strongly reflecting radical-stability differences and favoring the most stable benzylic radical. Reactive Cl has an early TS and is much less selective.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-gjch5u","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"N,N-diethylaniline (shown), a neutral organic base, is dissolved in dichloromethane and extracted with aqueous 1 M HCl. Which species is now found in the AQUEOUS acidic layer?","opts":["[NH3+]c1ccccc1","CC[N+](CC)([O-])c1ccccc1","CC[NH+](CC)c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","CCN(CC)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Aqueous HCl protonates the amine N to the water-soluble diethylanilinium salt (A), which moves into the aqueous layer. B is the neutral amine (stays in DCM), C requires oxidation, D has lost both ethyl groups, E is a different molecule (phenol).","traps":null,"qs":"CCN(CC)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-p5414p","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n(E)-3-hexene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","opts":["CC[C@H]1O[C@@H]1CC","CCC1OC1CC","CCC(O)C(O)CC","CC[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1CC","CC[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CC"],"correct":0,"why":"Epoxidation is syn/stereospecific, so E (trans) alkene gives trans-3,4-epoxyhexane, the (R,R)/(S,S) chiral pair. Two-concept (stereospecific syn + trans recognition).","traps":null,"qs":"CC/C=C/CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1k5glo1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"In a crossed aldol reaction, ethyl acetoacetate (drawn) is often chosen as the nucleophilic partner instead of a simple ketone. Which statement best explains its behavior under mild base?","opts":["It undergoes dehydration before any C-C bond forms","Its central CH2 is flanked by two carbonyls, so it is readily and selectively deprotonated to a stabilized enolate that acts as the nucleophile","Mild base removes a proton from the terminal ester oxygen to give an alkoxide, and that oxygen, not the central carbon, is what attacks the partner carbonyl","Mild base reduces its ketone to an alcohol before the aldol step","It has no alpha hydrogens between its two carbonyl groups, so it can serve only as the electrophilic partner in the crossed aldol and never as the nucleophile"],"correct":1,"why":"The central CH2 sits between a ketone and an ester carbonyl, so it is selectively deprotonated to a doubly stabilized enolate (the active-methylene nucleophile). option B is false (it clearly has acidic alpha H), option C misplaces deprotonation, option D/option E invent reduction/pre-dehydration.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1mupulc","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Pent-4-en-1-ol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","opts":["O=CCCCC=O","C=CCCC=O","C1CCCO1","C=CCCCO","C=CCCC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"PCC is chemoselective: it oxidizes the 1deg alcohol to the aldehyde (pent-4-enal) while leaving the isolated alkene untouched and not over-oxidizing to acid. option B is the over-oxidized acid (Jones outcome), option C wrongly oxidizes both ends, option D SM, option E THF (unrelated, loses a carbon).","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCCCO","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-10kdmdd","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"Aniline is to be made from the aromatic compound shown. Which reagent set reduces the nitro group to a primary amine?","opts":["KMnO4, hot","LiAlH4 only (no other change possible)","NaBH4 in ethanol","Sn (or Fe) with HCl, then NaOH workup","O3, then Zn/H2O"],"correct":3,"why":"Aromatic nitro groups reduce to anilines under dissolving-metal conditions (Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl, then base). NaBH4 does not reduce nitroarenes, KMnO4 and O3 are oxidative/cleaving, and LiAlH4 on nitroarenes is unreliable (azo stage), so the metal/acid route is the standard answer.","traps":null,"qs":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-cyxcl1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"3-Methyl-2-butanol (shown), a secondary alcohol, is heated with concentrated H2SO4. The major product is 2-methyl-2-butene rather than 3-methyl-1-butene. Which explanation best accounts for this?","opts":["A 1,2-hydride shift converts the secondary carbocation to a more stable tertiary carbocation, which then loses a beta H to give the Zaitsev (more substituted) alkene","An anti-periplanar requirement in the transition state lets only one beta hydrogen line up with the leaving group in this substrate, which forces formation of the terminal alkene","The reaction goes by SN2, so no rearrangement is possible","The branching methyl group sterically blocks elimination toward the more substituted side of the molecule, so the proton must instead be removed from the far end of the chain","Zaitsev's rule does not apply to secondary alcohols"],"correct":0,"why":"E1 dehydration gives a secondary carbocation at C2; a 1,2-hydride shift from the adjacent CH yields a more stable tertiary cation, and loss of a beta hydrogen gives the most substituted (Zaitsev) alkene, 2-methyl-2-butene. This is the hallmark of carbocation rearrangement.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)C(C)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-18a3sra","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"1-Hexyne is treated with 2 equivalents of HCl. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CCCCC(Cl)CCl","CCCCC(Cl)(Cl)C","ClCCCCCCCl","CCCCC(Cl)(Cl)CCl","CCCC(Cl)(Cl)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Both HCl add Markovnikov; both Cl go to C2, giving the geminal dichloride 2,2-dichlorohexane (option A). Distractors are the vicinal 1,2-dichloride, a trichloride, 1,6-dichlorohexane, and the 3,3-isomer. One answer.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCC#C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-b7gq59","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated sequentially: (1) NaOEt then CH3I; (2) NaOEt then CH3CH2I; then (3) dilute H2SO4 and heat. What is the major product?","opts":["CCC(C)C(C)=O","CCCCC(C)=O","CCC(CC)C(C)=O","CCC(C)C(=O)CC(C)=O","CC(=O)CC(C)C"],"correct":0,"why":"Sequential methylation then ethylation puts a methyl and an ethyl on the central carbon; hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH(CH3)-CH2CH3 = 3-methyl-2-pentanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: 2-hexanone (one alkylation), non-decarboxylated diketone, 3-ethyl-2-pentanone (wrong group, two ethyls), and 4-methyl-2-pentanone (wrong substitution position).","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1bog4vm","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is treated with sodium ethoxide (E2). Considering the anti-periplanar requirement, which is the major alkene?","opts":["CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCC=CC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1"],"correct":4,"why":"Classic anti-periplanar override. In the trans isomer, when C-Br is axial the C2 methyl is also axial, so the only beta-H is equatorial (not anti-periplanar) and cannot eliminate; the tempting Zaitsev 1-methylcyclohexene cannot form. The only anti-periplanar (axial) beta-H is on C6, giving the less-substituted 3-methylcyclohexene (option A).","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8ori72","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"N-Methylacetamide (shown) is the least reactive of the common acyl derivatives. Which single statement BEST explains why amides resist nucleophilic acyl substitution and require harsh (strong acid or base, heat) hydrolysis conditions?","opts":["The methyl group on nitrogen sterically shields the carbonyl completely","The C-N bond is a full carbon-nitrogen double bond in the ground state, so nitrogen cannot be displaced by any nucleophile unless that bond is first reduced to a single bond","Amides are protonated at carbon, blocking nucleophilic attack","The nitrogen lone pair is strongly donated into the carbonyl (amide resonance), lowering carbonyl electrophilicity, and amide (NR2-) is the poorest leaving group","Nitrogen is more electronegative than oxygen, so it withdraws electron density from the carbonyl and stabilizes the tetrahedral intermediate so well that it never collapses"],"correct":3,"why":"Amide stability = strong n(N)-&gt;pi*(C=O) resonance donation (less electrophilic carbonyl, partial C-N double bond) plus amide being the worst leaving group. Index 0 correct; nitrogen is LESS electronegative than oxygen, ruling out distractor 1.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)NC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-109jgd2","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Diethyl malonate is alkylated with 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide), then hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. What is the final carboxylic acid?","opts":["OC(=O)C(C)C","OC(=O)CCCC","OC(=O)C(C)CC","OC(=O)CC(C)C","OC(=O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Enolate displaces bromide; (CH3)2CH- attaches to alpha-C. After decarboxylation: (CH3)2CH-CH2-COOH = 3-methylbutanoic (isovaleric) acid, option A (CC(C)CC(=O)O). Isobutyric acid (E) is the trap omitting the malonate CH2 spacer. All options valid/unique.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fp66n0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A benzenediazonium salt (C6H5N2+, from aniline + NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C) is added to a cold basic solution of phenol (drawn). Predict the major azo-coupling product.","opts":["Oc1ccc(Nc2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1O","Oc1ccccc1/N=N/c1ccccc1","O=C1C=CC(=Nc2ccccc2)C=C1"],"correct":1,"why":"Diazonium salts are weak electrophiles that couple at the para position of the strongly activated phenol via EAS, giving 4-(phenylazo)phenol (Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1). The ortho isomer, the reduced N-H (diarylamine) bridge, an extra-OH catechol, and a quinone-imine are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"Oc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-akxqn7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Toluene is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. What is the major product?","opts":["Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=[N+]([O-])Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Cc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":3,"why":"Methyl is a weak activating ortho/para director; para nitration predominates because it avoids ortho steric crowding. Keyed para product is correct; ortho [1] and meta [2] are disfavored, and COOH [3] and benzylic-nitro [4] are wrong skeletons.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1nqrfbl","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"The alkene shown (1-ethylcyclohexene) is treated with BH3-THF followed by H2O2/NaOH. Considering BOTH regiochemistry (anti-Markovnikov) and stereochemistry (syn addition), which description matches the major product?","opts":["OH adds to the less substituted ring carbon (the CH), and H and OH add to the same face (syn), giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol","Two OH groups add cis across the double bond","OH adds to the less substituted ring carbon, but H and OH add to opposite faces of the ring (anti), giving cis-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol","OH adds to the more substituted ring carbon that bears the ethyl group (Markovnikov), with H and OH delivered to the same face of the ring","The ethyl group is oxidized to a carboxylic acid"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov (OH to the less substituted ring CH, not the carbon bearing the ethyl) and syn (H and OH add to the same face). On the ring, syn addition of H and OH places the new OH and the boron-derived H on the same face, which makes the OH trans to the ethyl substituent, giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC1=CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-185lgi6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"1-Methoxy-1,3-butadiene reacts with acrolein (prop-2-enal) thermally. Predict the major product (apply the 'ortho' regiochemistry rule).","opts":["COC=CC=CCCC=O","COC1C=CC1C=O","COC1C=CCCC1C=O","COC1C=CC(C=O)CC1","COC1C=CCC(C=O)C1"],"correct":2,"why":"A 1-substituted (terminal) diene with a mono-substituted dienophile follows the 'ortho' rule: the methoxy and the CHO end up on adjacent (1,2) carbons of the cyclohexene, with the new ring double bond between the former diene C2-C3. option A is the 1,2 product. option B is the 1,3 ('meta') and option C the 1,4 ('para') isomer (wrong regiochemistry), option D is a 4-membered ring (wrong skeleton), option E never cyclized.","traps":null,"qs":"COC=CC=C.C=CC=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-7d9eh6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"(Bromomethyl)cyclopentane (shown) is solvolyzed in water, giving a single rearranged alcohol as the major product. The primary cation undergoes ring expansion. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CC1=CCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCC1","OCC1CCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"Ionization gives a primary exocyclic cation; a ring C-C bond migrates (ring expansion) to give a secondary cyclohexyl cation, trapped by water to give cyclohexanol (OC1CCCCC1). Cyclopentylmethanol (OCC1CCCC1) is the unrearranged product; methylenecyclopentane, 1-methylcyclopentene, and cyclohexene are elimination products, not the alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"BrCC1CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1odc4g1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is heated with excess KMnO4, then treated with acid. What is the major product?","opts":["CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"The isopropyl group has one benzylic C-H, so KMnO4 degrades the whole side chain to a single COOH carbon on the ring, giving benzoic acid (the two methyls are lost as CO2/carbonate). Benzylic alcohol, acetophenone, and 2-phenylpropanoic acid wrongly retain side-chain carbons; KMnO4 does not stop at those stages.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1ywpsoa","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"cis-2-Butene (drawn) reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2. Bromine adds anti across the double bond. Which structure correctly represents the product of this single alkene?","opts":["C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)CCBr","CCC(C)(Br)Br","C/C(Br)=C(\\Br)C","C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Option B is verified MESO (the 2R,3S diastereomer = trans-alkene/syn-addition outcome, the classic trap). C is geminal, D wrong skeleton/regiochem, E unreacted.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C\\C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17l03ao","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"The molecular ion of this compound appears at m/z 112 with an M+2 peak at m/z 114 that is about one-third (3:1) as intense. The presence of which element best accounts for this M:M+2 ratio?","opts":["One chlorine atom","One nitrogen atom","One bromine atom","One sulfur atom","Two oxygen atoms"],"correct":0,"why":"Chlorine's isotopes 35Cl and 37Cl occur in roughly a 3:1 ratio, giving M:M+2 of about 3:1. Chlorobenzene (C6H5Cl) shows M+ at 112 (35Cl) and M+2 at 114 (37Cl). Bromine would give a ~1:1 M:M+2 pattern.","traps":null,"qs":"Clc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"qr-a:fra1075","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A shift log records 5/6 of an hour of machine downtime. To three decimal places, how many hours is that?","opts":["0.167","0.25","0.333","0.5","0.833"],"correct":4,"why":"5/6 = 0.833. Long-divide if you don't have it memorized.","traps":["That is the 1/6 left over, not the 5/6 the question asks for.","That is 1/4, well under five sixths of a whole.","That is 1/3, which is two sixths.","That is 1/2, which is three sixths.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:men1234","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A commute is 23 kilometers each way. How many kilometers is the round trip?","opts":["36","40","46","56","146"],"correct":2,"why":"(20 + 3) × 2 = 40 + 6","traps":["That is ten light, since 40 plus 6 is 46.","That doubles the 20 and leaves the 3 times 2 out.",null,"That is ten over, since 40 plus 6 is 46.","An extra hundred appeared, since 20 times 2 is 40 and 3 times 2 is 6."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1456","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A delivery map is laid out on a unit grid, with a depot at (0, 2) and a drop point at (3, 6). How many units apart are they in a straight line?","opts":["2.65","4","5","7","25"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((3)² + (4)²) = √25 = 5.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1133","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"What is 35 percent of 225?","opts":["7.88","78.75","146.25","260","7875"],"correct":1,"why":"35% of 225 = 35/100 × 225 = 78.75.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.",null,"You found the 65 percent left over rather than the 35 percent the question asks for.","You added the 35 to the 225 instead of taking 35 percent of it.","You multiplied 35 by 225 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#1xwh1p2","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 60 and standard deviation 10. About what percent of scores fall between 60 and 70?","opts":["16%","34%","47.5%","50%","68%"],"correct":1,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 34%.","traps":["That is the tail beyond one SD, not the slice inside it.",null,"That is the slice out to TWO SD.","That is everything above the mean. The slice stops at one SD.","That is one SD on BOTH sides. Only the upper side is asked for."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:wdm5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"\"A 50-lb bag of mix is 4 parts wheat to 1 part corn.\" Pounds of wheat?","opts":["10 lb","20 lb","25 lb","12.5 lb","40 lb"],"correct":4,"why":"5 parts total. 50/5 = 10/part. Wheat = 4 × 10 = 40.","traps":["That is the corn, the single part, rather than the four parts of wheat.","That reads 4 to 1 as 4 out of 10, but the parts add to 5, so wheat is four fifths.","That is half the bag, which would be a 1 to 1 mix.","That splits the bag into 4 parts instead of the 5 the ratio calls for.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#1yxamim","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (4 × 10⁰)(9 × 10⁶) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["3.6 × 10⁰","3.6 × 10⁶","1.3 × 10⁷","3.6 × 10⁵","3.6 × 10⁷"],"correct":4,"why":"4 × 9 = 36 and 10⁰ × 10⁶ = 10⁶. 36 × 10⁶ is not proper form, so write 3.6 × 10⁷. Answer: 3.6 × 10⁷.","traps":["You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1718","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"What is the value of 2^1 ÷ 2^1?","opts":["0","1/2","1","4","2"],"correct":2,"why":"Same base ÷ subtracts: 2^(1-1) = 2^0 = 1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fm5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is the product of 2/3, 3/4 and 5/6?","opts":["15/48","30/12","1/2","10/72","5/12"],"correct":4,"why":"Multiply across: 30/72 = 5/12 reduced.","traps":["You multiplied 3/4 by 5/6 to get 15/24 and then divided by 2 instead of multiplying by 2/3.","That multiplies all three tops but only the first two bottoms. Every denominator has to go in.","You stopped after the first two fractions; the 5/6 still has to come in.","You left the 3 out of the top row; 2 times 3 times 5 is 30.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ut1","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-14","topic":"Test-day execution","nights":[14],"d":1,"q":"You're solving a problem cold (no timer). What's the right move?","opts":["Rush to mimic test conditions","Skip if hard","Set a 90-second timer for each one","Guess","Take your time, get it right"],"correct":4,"why":"Untimed means master the move first. Speed comes after correctness. Always.","traps":["Rushing an untimed problem trades away the one thing untimed work is for, which is getting the move right.","Skipping in untimed mode hides the exact step that needs building.","Putting the clock on a learning set brings the pressure back before the move is steady.","A guess in untimed mode teaches nothing, because nothing gets worked out.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1417","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A kitchen mixes 200 mL of a 10% vinegar dressing with 250 mL of a 5% vinegar dressing. What is the approximate percent concentration of the mixture?","opts":["7.22","7.5","7.78","32.5","33.75"],"correct":0,"why":"Solute total = 20 + 12.5 = 32.5 mL in 450 mL → 7.22%.","traps":[null,"Averaged the two percents without weighting them by how much of each solution went in.","Paired each percent with the other solution's volume, putting the 10 percent on the 250 mL pour.","That is the total milliliters of solute, not the percent of the mixture.","Took 7.5 percent, the plain average of the two strengths, of the 450 mL total, which gives a volume rather than a concentration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1107","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A race route is 37.8 kilometers long, and a runner has covered 15.6 kilometers of it. How many kilometers are left?","opts":["2.22","22.2","22.3","53.4","222"],"correct":1,"why":"Stack the numbers with the decimal points in line and subtract: 8 tenths minus 6 tenths is 2 tenths, then 37 minus 15 is 22, giving 22.2. Rounding to 38 minus 16 gives about 22, so the size checks out.","traps":["You placed the point one spot too far left; 37.8 and 15.6 differ by more than 22, not by about 2.",null,"You slipped in the tenths column; 8 tenths minus 6 tenths is 2 tenths.","You added the two numbers instead of subtracting them.","You dropped the decimal point entirely, which scales the answer up by ten."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-q46gn4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"The terminal side of an angle θ in standard position passes through the point (-3, 4). What is cos θ?","opts":["-3/5","-4/3","-4/5","3/5","4/5"],"correct":0,"why":"Treat the point as a right triangle hanging off the origin: the horizontal leg is -3, the vertical leg is 4, and the distance from the origin is the square root of 9 plus 16, which is 5. Cosine is the x coordinate over that distance, so it is -3/5. The distance is always positive, so the sign of the answer comes entirely from the coordinate on top.","traps":[null,"This is tan θ, the y coordinate over the x coordinate.","This uses the y coordinate and attaches the sign that belongs to the x coordinate.","This drops the negative sign, but the point sits to the left of the y axis.","This is sin θ, using the y coordinate where the x coordinate belongs."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#3tuzdz","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 3 red and 3 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["1/6","1/4","1/2","1/5","2/5"],"correct":3,"why":"First red: 3/6. Second red: 2/5. Multiply: 1/5.","traps":["You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble.","That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.","That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.",null,"That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1640","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A data set has a mean of 75 and a standard deviation of 5. What is the z score of a value of 70?","opts":["-5","-1","-0.07","1","14"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (70 − 75)/5 = -1.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, stopping before the division by 5.",null,"Divided the 5-point gap by the mean of 75 instead of by the standard deviation of 5.","Right size, wrong side: 70 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative.","Divided 70 by 5 without first subtracting the mean."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1660","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Three dowels measure 15, 7, and 7 centimeters. Can the three dowels form a triangle?","opts":["Yes","Only if right triangle","Only if it is obtuse","Cannot determine","No"],"correct":4,"why":"|15−7| = 8 and 15+7 = 22. 7 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["The two shorter sides, 7 and 7, reach only 14, which cannot span the 15, so the ends never meet.","No angle rescues these lengths: 7 and 7 still cover only 14, short of 15.","Angles come after the sides close, and 7 plus 7 never reaches 15, so no obtuse version exists either.","Three side lengths are all the rule needs: 7 plus 7 is 14, and that has to beat 15.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1182","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $250 curing light goes up 30% in price, and that higher price is then discounted 25%. What is the final price in dollars?","opts":["187.5","243.75","262.5","325","406.25"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply factors: 250 × 1.3 × 0.75 = 243.75. Naïvely adding gives 262.5, which is wrong.","traps":["That applies only the 25 percent cut and leaves the rise out.",null,"You added the two changes into a net 5 percent rise, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That is the price after the 30 percent rise, one step short of the answer.","That raises the second step by 25 percent instead of cutting by it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#y5q2s1","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 180 miles at 50 mph and returns the same 180 miles at 75 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["60 mph","68.8 mph","62.5 mph","61.2 mph","30 mph"],"correct":0,"why":"Time out = 180/50 = 3.6 h, time back = 180/75 = 2.4 h. Average = 360 / 6 = 60 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":[null,"Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.","That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1310","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A dental office books 5 cleanings and 2 fillings each day. Over 3 days, how many appointments is that?","opts":["10","11","13","21","30"],"correct":3,"why":"Bracket first: 5 + 2 = 7, then 7 times 3 = 21. Without the bracket this line would be 5 + 6 = 11, which is exactly the trap sitting in the choices. The bracket is the whole question here.","traps":["You added everything, so the times sign never did any work.","You ignored the bracket and multiplied 2 by 3 before adding the 5.","You multiplied the two numbers inside the bracket and then added the 3.",null,"You multiplied all three numbers, treating the plus sign as another times sign."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1376","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A sales representative drives at 75 miles per hour for 3.5 hours. How many miles does the trip cover?","opts":["21.43","187.5","225","262.5","337.5"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 75 × 3.5 = 262.5 mi.","traps":["Dividing 75 by 3.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 2.5 hours instead of 3.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 3 hours flat.",null,"Using 4.5 hours instead of the 3.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#2zw6b2","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 4 red and 2 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["4/9","3/5","2/5","2/3","1/3"],"correct":2,"why":"First red: 4/6. Second red: 3/5. Multiply: 2/5.","traps":["That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.","That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too.",null,"That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1181","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A machine that turns out 480 parts a month drops 30% in output during a repair month, then rises 25% the month after. How many parts does it make in the month after the repair?","opts":["252","336","420","456","600"],"correct":2,"why":"Multiply factors: 480 × 0.7 × 1.25 = 420. Naïvely adding gives 456, which is wrong.","traps":["That cuts the second step by 25 percent instead of raising by it.","That is the price after the 30 percent cut, one step short of the answer.",null,"You added the two changes into a net 5 percent drop, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That applies only the 25 percent rise and leaves the cut out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1280","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A single tray is set with 1 handpiece and 5 burs. How many items are on 6 identical trays?","opts":["6","11","30","36","42"],"correct":3,"why":"Distribute: 6·1 + 6·5 = 6 + 30 = 36.","traps":["That is only the 6 times 1 piece, so the 6 times 5 term never got added.","Distributing the 6 only to the 1 gives 6 + 5 = 11, leaving the 5 unmultiplied.","That is 6 times 5 on its own, so the 6 times 1 piece never got added.",null,"That is 6 times 7, so the sum inside was read as 7 instead of 1 + 5 = 6."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pco1558","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Disease prevalence 1%, test sensitivity 90%, specificity 90%. P(disease | positive test)?","opts":["0.01","0.08","0.108","0.9","0.92"],"correct":1,"why":"Bayes: P(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+) = 0.90·0.01 / 0.108 = 0.083.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:log1753","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A cell culture doubles in number every hour, growing from 1 cell to 512 cells. How many hours does that take?","opts":["2.71","8","9","10","18"],"correct":2,"why":"2^9 = 512, so log_2(512) = 9.","traps":["That is log(512) in base 10; this one asks how many 2s multiply to 512.","Stopping one factor of 2 short, since 2 to the 8th is 256.",null,"Going one factor of 2 too far, since 2 to the 10th is 1,024.","Doubling the exponent; 2 to the 18th is 262,144, far past 512."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1156","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $318 camera is marked down 55 percent. What is the sale price?","opts":["143.1","174.9","263","373","492.9"],"correct":0,"why":"Taking 55 percent off leaves 45 percent behind, so run it in one step: 318 x 0.45 = 143.10. Working with what survives beats finding the 174.90 discount and then subtracting, and it keeps you from stopping on the discount by mistake. Every decreased-by question is that same move, 1 minus the percent written as a decimal.","traps":[null,"You stopped on the discount itself, 174.90, instead of taking it off the 318.","You subtracted 55 dollars instead of 55 percent of the 318.","You added 55 dollars when the price is coming down.","You raised the price by 55% instead of lowering it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1738","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"If x differs from 6 by exactly 1, what are the possible values of x?","opts":["7","-5 or 5","5 or -7","7 or 5","-5 or -7"],"correct":3,"why":"|x − 6| = 1 means x − 6 = ±1, so x = 7 or 5.","traps":["You solved x - 6 = 1 and stopped, and the branch x - 6 = -1 gives 5 as well.","That pairs 5 with its negative, and both branches here land above zero, at 7 and 5.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.",null,"You subtracted the 6 instead of adding it back on both branches."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1262","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"To the nearest hundredth, what value of x satisfies 11x + 10 = -3x - 9?","opts":["-2.36","-1.36","-0.36","0.07","1.36"],"correct":1,"why":"Add 3x to both sides for 14x + 10 = -9, then subtract 10 for 14x = -19. Divide by 14 and x is -19/14, which works out to -1.357 and rounds to -1.36. When every choice carries two decimals, that rounding step is part of the answer, so do the division in full before you trim it.","traps":["Substituting -2.36 leaves about -16 on the left and about -1.9 on the right.",null,"Substituting -0.36 leaves about 6 on the left and about -7.9 on the right.","The 10 was added to the -9 instead of subtracted, leaving 14x = 1.","Dividing 19 by 14 gives 1.36, but 14x = -19 makes x negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#oj4q4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A car travels at 60 miles per hour. What is its speed in feet per second? (1 mile = 5280 ft)","opts":["88 ft/s","0.0167 ft/s","3.17 × 10⁵ ft/s","40.9 ft/s","5280 ft/s"],"correct":0,"why":"60 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi ÷ 3600 s/h = 88 ft/s. Handy anchor: 60 mph is 88 ft/s.","traps":[null,"That is miles per second. Multiply by 5280 to get feet.","That is feet per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so miles and hours cancel.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1593","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"How many real roots does 3x² − 4x + 4 = 0 have?","opts":["no real roots","two imaginary roots","two real roots","one real and one imaginary root","one real root"],"correct":0,"why":"Δ = b² − 4ac = 16 − 4·3·4 = -32. &lt; 0, so none.","traps":[null,"Both roots are complex, and the question is counting how many are real.","You stopped at b² = 16 and never subtracted 4(3)(4) = 48.","Complex roots come in conjugate pairs, so you cannot get exactly one of each.","A repeated real root needs a discriminant of zero, and 16 − 48 is −32."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rm3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"5 painters finish in 12 hr. How long for 8 painters (same pace)?","opts":["19.2 hr","1.5 hr","15 hr","3 hr","7.5 hr"],"correct":4,"why":"Total work = 5 × 12 = 60 painter-hours. With 8: 60/8 = 7.5.","traps":["You scaled the time up with the crew, multiplying 12 by 8/5, when extra painters have to pull the time down.","You divided the original 12 hours by the 8 painters without first finding the 60 painter-hours of work.","You added three hours for the three extra painters instead of splitting the 60 painter-hours among 8.","You cut the time well past the ratio; the crew grows by a factor of 8/5, so 12 hours falls to 7.5, not to 3.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:vol1674","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A spherical tank has a radius of 5 feet. To the nearest tenth, what is its volume in cubic feet?","opts":["104.72","314.16","392.7","523.6","4188.79"],"correct":3,"why":"V = (4/3)πr³ = 523.6.","traps":["Squared the radius instead of cubing it.","That is the sphere's surface area, 4πr², not its volume.","Left out the 4/3 and computed π times the radius cubed.",null,"Used the diameter of 10 in place of the radius before cubing."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#bt630n","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (4 × 10⁻¹)(3 × 10⁻¹) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["1.2 × 10⁻¹","7 × 10⁻¹","1.2 × 10¹","1.2 × 10⁻³","1.2 × 10⁻²"],"correct":0,"why":"4 × 3 = 12 and 10⁻¹ × 10⁻¹ = 10⁻². 12 × 10⁻² is not proper form, so write 1.2 × 10⁻¹. Answer: 1.2 × 10⁻¹.","traps":[null,"You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1325","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"For which values of x is -5x &lt; 2 true?","opts":["x = -0.4","x &gt; 0.4","x &lt; 0.4","x &lt; -0.4","x &gt; -0.4"],"correct":4,"why":"Divide both sides by -5 (negative) and FLIP: x &gt; -0.4.","traps":["You solved the boundary equation -5x = 2 instead of the range of values.","You flipped the direction but dropped the negative on 2/-5.","You divided by 5 rather than -5, so neither the sign nor the direction changed.","You kept the inequality pointing the same way after dividing by a negative.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#183u3rc","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 120 miles at 60 mph and returns the same 120 miles at 90 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["75 mph","73.5 mph","72 mph","82.5 mph","36 mph"],"correct":2,"why":"Time out = 120/60 = 2 h, time back = 120/90 = 1.33 h. Average = 240 / 3.33 = 72 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":["You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.","That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.",null,"Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pfl1518","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A warehouse flips a fair coin to route each of seven packages to one of two docks. What is the probability that exactly one flip lands heads?","opts":["0.01","0.03","0.05","0.11","0.95"],"correct":2,"why":"C(7,1) × (1/2)^7 = 7 × 0.0078 = 0.0547.","traps":["0.01 is the chance of one particular pattern, 1/128, and the single head has seven places it can sit.","0.03 carries one extra factor of 1/2, 7 times (1/2) to the eighth instead of the seventh.",null,"0.11 runs six flips, 7 times (1/2) to the sixth, one flip short.","0.95 is the complement, the chance of anything other than exactly one head."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1b1xn3t","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$2000 is invested at 5% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 2 years?","opts":["$205.00","$2100.00","$2200.00","$2205.00","$2315.25"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 2000 × (1.05)² = $2205.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1356","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A 44-gram sample is separated in a ratio of 2 to 9. How many grams are in the smaller portion?","opts":["4","8","11","22","36"],"correct":1,"why":"Total parts = 11; one part = 44/11 = 4. Smaller = 2·4 = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1206","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A rail line carries 2,460 riders a day. How many riders is that over 51 days?","opts":["123000","124000","125000","125460","126000"],"correct":3,"why":"Work it out: 2460 × 51 = 125460. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 2460 times 50, which leaves one more 2,460 to add.","That sits 1,460 under the product, so part of the multiplication went missing.","That is 2500 times 50, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 125,460.",null,"That rounds both numbers up, landing 540 over the true product."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ds3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Q: What is the area of the rectangle? (1) Length is 4. (2) Perimeter is 14.","opts":["Both together","Either one alone","Neither","(1) alone","(2) alone"],"correct":0,"why":"(1) alone: no width. (2) alone: many shapes. Together: 2(4)+2w=14, so w=3, so area=12.","traps":[null,"Choosing this treats the perimeter as fixing the rectangle, and 4 by 3 and 5 by 2 share that perimeter with areas of 12 and 10.","Together works.","Knowing the length is 4 still leaves the width open, so the area could be anything.","A perimeter of 14 fits a 4 by 3 rectangle and a 5 by 2 rectangle, and those areas differ."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261004"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s12"}},{"id":"FULL_5","name":"Full-Length Test 5","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"written:bio-4-1hr4sgo","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Oligomycin plugs the proton channel of ATP synthase while leaving the electron transport complexes themselves untouched. What happens to the rate of electron flow through the chain?","opts":["It is unchanged, because the chain and ATP synthase work independently of each other","It slows, because the gradient builds and pumping against it gets harder","It slows, because oxygen can no longer accept electrons","It increases, because protons are no longer being used up","It increases briefly and then stops as the matrix becomes acidic"],"correct":1,"why":"This is respiratory control, and the gradient is the link between the two halves. With the synthase plugged, protons have no route back in, the gradient grows steeper, and the pumps eventually cannot push against it, so electron flow and oxygen consumption both fall. Contrast that with an uncoupler, which drains the gradient and makes the chain race.","traps":["The two are coupled through the gradient, which is precisely what the phrase oxidative phosphorylation is telling you.",null,"Oxygen is still present and complex IV is not the target, since the block sits downstream at the synthase.","Protons piling up in the intermembrane space makes the next pump stroke harder, not easier.","Protons are pumped out of the matrix, so the matrix would become less acidic rather than more."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev017","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Bone and cartilage develop primarily from which germ layer?","opts":["Endoderm","Mesoderm","The notochord","Ectoderm","Neural crest"],"correct":1,"why":"Bone, cartilage, blood, muscle and the kidneys are all middle layer structures, so mesoderm is the default answer for connective and supporting tissue. The vertebrae and ribs come from the sclerotome portion of the somites, and the limb skeleton comes from lateral plate mesoderm. The one nuance worth carrying is that neural crest, an ectodermal population, supplies a large part of the facial skeleton, which is why the face often gets carved out as an exception.","traps":["Endoderm lines the gut tube and buds off its glands, and it contributes no structural connective tissue.",null,"The notochord is a midline rod that helps pattern the vertebrae without becoming them, and its only adult remnant is the nucleus pulposus inside each intervertebral disc.","Ectoderm makes the outer covering and the nervous system, while connective and supporting tissues such as bone, cartilage and blood are middle layer products.","Neural crest does build much of the face and parts of the skull, which makes this a fair thought, but the skeleton as a whole, the limbs, ribs and vertebrae included, comes from mesoderm."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g074","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-9","topic":"Mendelian genetics","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"The wild-type allele of a gene is usually described how?","opts":["The allele that arose most recently in the population","Mutant","Sterile","Recessive","Most common in population (often dominant)"],"correct":4,"why":"Wild type means the version that predominates in a natural population, the standard that mutant forms are compared against. It is often dominant, because a working protein frequently masks a non-working one, but that is a tendency rather than a rule. Read wild type as most common, not as best and not as automatically dominant.","traps":["New mutations start out rare by definition, while the wild-type allele is the one already common in the population.","Mutant is the label for the variant forms, which are defined by contrast with wild type.","Fertility is a phenotype and has nothing to do with what the term wild type means.","Some wild-type alleles are recessive, since the term describes prevalence rather than a dominance relationship.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-194ncsd","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"An intestinal cell is treated with a drug that completely blocks the sodium potassium ATPase. Within minutes, glucose uptake across the apical membrane by the sodium glucose cotransporter falls off sharply. What is the best explanation?","opts":["The drug binds the glucose carrier directly and jams its binding site","The apical membrane depolarizes, which closes the carrier","Glucose is no longer phosphorylated once it arrives inside the cell","The sodium gradient that powers the cotransporter runs down","The cotransporter needs ATP, and the drug has cut off its supply"],"correct":3,"why":"The real question is what secondary active transport runs on. The cotransporter never touches ATP: it lets sodium fall downhill and drags glucose uphill with it, and that sodium gradient is built by the pump. Block the pump, sodium piles up inside, the gradient flattens, and the free ride ends. Any time a transporter is labeled secondary active, look upstream for the pump that stocked its gradient.","traps":["The stem says the drug acts on the pump, and the delay of a few minutes points to a gradient draining rather than direct inhibition.","Voltage shifts here are small next to the loss of the sodium gradient itself.","Trapping glucose by phosphorylation keeps the glucose gradient favorable, but it is not the energy source for this carrier.",null,"Secondary active transporters do not hydrolyze ATP themselves, that is the defining difference from primary ones."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d005","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A pseudocoelomate animal has a fluid-filled body cavity. Where does that cavity lie relative to the germ layers?","opts":["Between mesoderm and endoderm","Absent","Fully lined by mesoderm","Between ectoderm and mesoderm","Between the ectoderm and the endoderm, with no mesoderm present at all"],"correct":0,"why":"The word pseudocoelom means a cavity that is real but only half lined. Picture a roundworm in cross section: the outer body wall carries a mesoderm layer facing the cavity, while the gut tube hanging in the middle is endoderm with no mesoderm wrapped around it. So the cavity is caught between mesoderm on the outside and endoderm on the inside, which is the choice to take. A true coelom, by contrast, has mesoderm on both faces.","traps":[null,"No cavity at all describes an acoelomate such as a flatworm, where solid mesoderm fills the space between gut and body wall.","A cavity completely lined by mesoderm is a true coelom, the pattern in annelids, molluscs and chordates.","That flips the layers; the pseudocoelom sits inside the mesodermal body wall and against the gut lining, not out against the skin.","Mesoderm is present in a roundworm, it simply fails to line the cavity all the way around; an animal with only ectoderm and endoderm is a diploblast such as a jellyfish."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d001","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following features is found in both Archaea and Eukarya but NOT in Bacteria?","opts":["Single circular chromosome","A membrane-bound nucleus","Histones organizing DNA","Peptidoglycan walls","70S ribosomes"],"correct":2,"why":"Archaea look like prokaryotes under a microscope, but their gene handling machinery lines up with ours. Many archaea wrap their DNA around histone proteins, and their RNA polymerase, promoters, and translation initiation machinery resemble the eukaryotic versions far more than the bacterial ones. What they share with bacteria is the visible structure: no nucleus, 70S ribosomes, and usually a single circular chromosome. Sort questions like this by splitting traits into structure, which groups archaea with bacteria, and information processing, which groups them with eukaryotes.","traps":["A single circular chromosome is typical of both bacteria and archaea, so it does not separate the two.","Archaea are prokaryotes with no nuclear envelope; it is their information processing machinery, not their compartments, that resembles ours.",null,"Peptidoglycan is the bacterial cell wall polymer; archaeal walls use different materials such as pseudopeptidoglycan, and eukaryotes have none of it.","Archaea and bacteria both carry 70S ribosomes while eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S, so this groups archaea with bacteria instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p064","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Steroid hormones such as cortisol and estrogen produce their effects by binding to what?","opts":["RTK","DNA directly, with no receptor protein involved","Ion channels","Cell surface GPCR","Intracellular receptors acting on DNA"],"correct":4,"why":"Start with solubility and the rest follows. Steroids are lipid-soluble, so they pass straight through the plasma membrane, meet a receptor in the cytoplasm or the nucleus, and the hormone-receptor complex then binds DNA and changes which genes are transcribed. That is why steroid effects take hours to appear and last a long time, while peptide hormones working through surface receptors act within seconds.","traps":["Receptor tyrosine kinases handle insulin and a range of growth factors, again from the outer surface of the cell.","A steroid cannot find a gene on its own; it needs its receptor, which supplies the DNA-binding region that recognizes the right sequence.","Ligand-gated ion channels open for neurotransmitters on a millisecond timescale, which is not how a steroid acts.","G protein-coupled surface receptors are how peptide hormones such as glucagon work, since those hormones cannot cross the membrane.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d016","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"An adult marine animal is radially symmetric, but its larva is bilaterally symmetric. The animal most likely belongs to which group?","opts":["A flatworm such as a planarian","Mollusc","Sponge","Cnidarian","Echinoderm"],"correct":4,"why":"Symmetry is worth checking at both ends of the life cycle, because one group changes partway through. Sea stars, urchins and sand dollars hatch as bilateral swimming larvae and only take on five-part radial symmetry as adults, which tells you the radial look is secondary, acquired later. That is also why echinoderms group with chordates as deuterostomes despite how the adult looks. When a stem hands you a bilateral larva with a radial adult, it is pointing at the echinoderm choice.","traps":["Flatworms are bilateral as larvae and as adults, with a definite head end, and never become radial.","Molluscs stay bilateral as adults; even a snail is a bilateral animal whose shell coils, rather than a radial one.","Sponges are mostly irregular in shape and have no true tissues, so neither half of the description fits.","Cnidarians are radial animals and are not bilaterians at all, so a bilateral larval stage is not part of their story.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A right shift of the oxygen hemoglobin dissociation curve indicates which change in hemoglobin's behavior?","opts":["A rise in blood pH together with a fall in carbon dioxide","Decreased affinity (unloads at tissue)","No change","Increased Hb affinity","Higher pO₂ in blood"],"correct":1,"why":"The curve plots how saturated hemoglobin is at each oxygen level, and sliding it to the right means hemoglobin holds oxygen less tightly, so it gives oxygen up more readily at the tissues. Working tissue creates exactly the conditions that push it right: more carbon dioxide, more acid, higher temperature, more 2,3 BPG. Picture hard working muscle calling for oxygen and hemoglobin letting go, so right shift means release.","traps":["Rising pH and falling carbon dioxide shift the curve the other way, toward tighter binding.",null,"A shifted curve is by definition a change in how tightly hemoglobin binds oxygen at a given oxygen level.","Higher affinity means hemoglobin holds on more tightly, and that is a shift to the left.","The curve describes hemoglobin's binding behavior at each oxygen level, so shifting the curve is not the same as changing the oxygen level itself."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-x1d1j6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Two cardiac muscle cells are joined so that ions and small molecules pass directly from the cytoplasm of one into the cytoplasm of the other. The structure responsible is:","opts":["A tight junction","A gap junction","A desmosome","A plasmodesma","A hemidesmosome"],"correct":1,"why":"The question turns on the word directly: no ligand, no receptor, just a pore joining two cytoplasms. Gap junctions are those pores in animal tissue, and they are why cardiac muscle contracts as one unit. Tight junctions seal the space between cells and desmosomes rivet cells together, so neither one passes a signal.","traps":["Tight junctions seal the space between cells to stop leakage, they do not pass molecules between cytoplasms.",null,"Desmosomes are mechanical rivets that hold cells together against stress.","Plasmodesmata do exactly this job, but they are found in plant cells, and cardiac muscle is animal tissue.","Hemidesmosomes anchor a cell to the extracellular matrix below it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-y5m7wf","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Linear chromosomes lose a little sequence each round of replication, because the extreme end of the lagging strand has nowhere to place a final primer. Some cells avoid that loss using telomerase. What does telomerase do?","opts":["It copies the missing end from the homologous chromosome, which serves as a template","It removes the last RNA primer and fills the gap with DNA, the way DNA polymerase I does","It carries its own RNA template and extends the 3' end with repeat sequence","It caps the end with protein so ordinary polymerase can place a primer there","It joins the two chromosome ends together so that no sequence can be lost"],"correct":2,"why":"Telomerase is a reverse transcriptase that carries a short RNA template inside itself and uses it to add telomeric repeats onto the 3' overhang. That lengthened overhang gives the lagging strand machinery room to prime, so the chromosome stops getting shorter. Notice what it does not do: it never restores lost gene sequence, it extends the buffer at the tip. Look for it in germ cells, stem cells, and many cancer cells, and not in most somatic cells.","traps":["Using the homolog as a template happens in some repair pathways, and telomerase needs no second chromosome because it brings its own template.","Removing and replacing a primer works in the middle of a strand, but at the very end there is no upstream 3' hydroxyl to extend from, which is the whole problem.",null,"A protein cap does protect the end, but it cannot create the 3' hydroxyl that priming requires, so the shortening would continue.","Joining chromosome ends is exactly the accident telomeres exist to prevent, and a fused chromosome tears apart at the next anaphase."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1n24d6b","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Trace one electron through noncyclic (linear) electron flow. Which order is correct?","opts":["Water, photosystem I, ferredoxin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, plastocyanin, photosystem II, NADP+","Water, photosystem II, ferredoxin, plastocyanin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, photosystem I, and finally NADP+","Water, photosystem I, plastoquinone, cytochrome complex, plastocyanin, photosystem II, ferredoxin, NADP+","NADP+, ferredoxin, photosystem I, plastocyanin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, photosystem II, water","Water, photosystem II, plastoquinone, cytochrome complex, plastocyanin, photosystem I, ferredoxin, NADP+"],"correct":4,"why":"The trap in this one is the numbering. Photosystem II fires first even though it carries the higher number, because it was discovered second. Hold the shape in your head as the letter Z: water at the bottom left, an uphill jump at photosystem II, a slide down through the carriers, a second uphill jump at photosystem I, then NADP+ waiting at the top right.","traps":["Photosystem I appears first here and ferredoxin appears far too early, since ferredoxin only receives electrons after the second light hit.","Ferredoxin sits on the far side of photosystem I, not between photosystem II and the cytochrome complex.","The photosystems are numbered by discovery order, not working order, so starting at photosystem I flips the chain.","This runs the whole chain backwards, with NADP+ donating electrons instead of accepting them at the end.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-2c5o89","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Insulin binds the outer portion of its receptor. What does the receptor do next?","opts":["Adds phosphate groups to tyrosines on its own cytoplasmic tails","Cleaves PIP2 into IP3 and DAG","Exchanges GDP for GTP on an attached G protein at the inner surface","Carries insulin with it into the nucleus","Opens as an ion channel and lets sodium in"],"correct":0,"why":"The insulin receptor is the classic receptor tyrosine kinase, and the word kinase is the whole mechanism. Once the hormone binds, the two halves of the receptor activate one another and hang phosphate groups on tyrosine side chains of their own cytoplasmic tails. Those phosphorylated tyrosines then act as docking sites for relay proteins that carry the message inward. Hormone binds, receptor phosphorylates itself, relay proteins dock.","traps":[null,"Cleaving PIP2 is the job of phospholipase C, further along a different pathway.","Loading GTP onto a G protein is what a G-protein-coupled receptor does.","Insulin is a peptide and never enters the cell, that route belongs to steroids.","Ion channel receptors respond to neurotransmitters, not to insulin."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g029","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-6","topic":"The central dogma","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"The same deletion on chromosome 15 produces Prader-Willi syndrome in some patients and Angelman syndrome in others. What determines which of the two syndromes develops?","opts":["Age","Random chance","Sex of patient","Parent of origin (imprinting)","Whether the deletion is newly arisen in the patient or inherited from a parent"],"correct":3,"why":"This is testing genomic imprinting, where a gene's activity depends on which parent supplied it. In this region of chromosome 15 the maternal and paternal copies are silenced at different genes, so losing the paternal copy leaves no working version of one set of genes, and losing the maternal copy leaves none of the other set. Same missing DNA, two different syndromes, decided by the parent of origin. Whenever one deletion gives two named diseases, look for imprinting.","traps":["The two syndromes differ in how they present over time, but the patient's age does not decide which one a given deletion produces.","The outcome is predictable once the parent of origin is known, so it is not left to chance.","The patient's own sex plays no part here; what matters is which parent supplied the chromosome that carries the deletion.",null,"A new deletion and an inherited one give the same result as long as they remove the same parent's copy, so that is not the deciding factor."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p103","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The Bohr effect describes how carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions change hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen. What does this accomplish physiologically?","opts":["Glucose transport","Unloading O₂ at metabolically active tissues","CO₂ removal directly","A leftward shift of the oxygen dissociation curve so hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly","Loading O₂ at lungs only"],"correct":1,"why":"This is testing why hemoglobin is smart rather than just sticky. Tissue that is working hard produces carbon dioxide and acid, and those lower hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, shifting the dissociation curve to the right so more oxygen falls off exactly where it is needed. The recognition cue is that anything saying a tissue is busy, more acid, more carbon dioxide, more heat, more 2,3-BPG, pushes the curve right and unloads oxygen.","traps":["Glucose crosses membranes on GLUT carriers and has no connection to hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen.",null,"Carbon dioxide is carried mostly as bicarbonate, with smaller amounts on hemoglobin and dissolved in plasma, so the Bohr effect is about what carbon dioxide does to oxygen binding rather than about hauling the carbon dioxide itself.","A left shift would make hemoglobin grip oxygen more tightly, which is the opposite of what acid and carbon dioxide do and would starve the very tissue asking for oxygen.","Hemoglobin does load oxygen well in the lungs, but that happens because the lungs are low in carbon dioxide and acid, which is the absence of the Bohr effect rather than its purpose."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e023","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In population ecology, the carrying capacity (K) of an environment is best defined as:","opts":["Maximum sustainable population in environment","Growth rate","Birth rate","Death rate","The population size at which growth is fastest"],"correct":0,"why":"K is a size, not a rate, and that single distinction answers most questions on this topic. It is the largest population the environment's resources can sustain over time, so as N approaches K the growth term in the logistic equation shrinks toward zero and the curve flattens. Push a population above K and growth turns negative until it settles back. Picture the S-shaped curve: growth is fastest in the steep middle and zero at the top.","traps":[null,"The growth rate is r; K is the population size at which growth levels off to zero.","Birth rate is one input to growth, but K is a ceiling set by resources rather than a rate.","Death rate does climb as a population nears K, but K itself is a number of individuals, not a rate.","Fastest growth happens near half of K, where the population is large enough to reproduce quickly but resources are not yet tight."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev005","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The cleavage divisions of an early embryo differ from ordinary mitotic divisions in a body tissue. What happens to the cells during cleavage?","opts":["Get smaller (only S + M, no G1/G2)","Get larger","Stay same size (no growth between divisions)","Don't replicate DNA","Undergo meiosis rather than mitosis"],"correct":0,"why":"This question is about which parts of the cell cycle the embryo keeps. Cleavage runs S phase and M phase back to back with the G1 and G2 growth gaps stripped out, so the cells copy their DNA and divide without ever growing back to full size. The result is a zygote carved into many smaller cells while the ball as a whole stays about the size it started, which is how one enormous cell becomes a workable population of normal sized cells.","traps":[null,"Growing between divisions is what an ordinary body cell does, and it is exactly the step cleavage leaves out.","The embryo as a whole stays about the same size, which is where this idea comes from, but each individual cell is halved at every division.","DNA is replicated before every cleavage division, which is how each new cell gets a complete genome, so it is the growth phases that are skipped and not S phase.","Meiosis happened before fertilization, during the making of the gametes, and the zygote and everything after it divides by mitosis."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-867ilc","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Two electron carriers in the chain are mobile rather than locked inside a complex. Which pair?","opts":["Cytochrome c and cytochrome a3","Oxygen and water","Ubiquinone and ATP synthase","Ubiquinone and cytochrome c","NADH and FADH2"],"correct":3,"why":"Four complexes sit fixed in the membrane, so something has to ferry electrons across the gaps between them. Ubiquinone, also called coenzyme Q, is lipid-soluble and drifts within the membrane from complexes I and II over to complex III, while cytochrome c is a small protein that skims the intermembrane space face from complex III to complex IV. Remember the pattern as one carrier inside the membrane and one riding on its surface.","traps":["Cytochrome a3 is built into complex IV rather than being free to move.","Oxygen is the endpoint and water is the product, so neither shuttles electrons between complexes.","ATP synthase spends the gradient the chain builds and carries no electrons at all.",null,"These two deliver electrons to the chain from the matrix, but neither is part of the chain itself."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a family pedigree, every child of an affected mother is affected, both sons and daughters, while no child of an affected father is ever affected. Which inheritance pattern does this describe?","opts":["X-linked dominant","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial","Autosomal dominant","Y-linked"],"correct":2,"why":"This combination is the fingerprint of mitochondrial inheritance. Mitochondria travel in the cytoplasm of the egg, so a mother hands her mitochondrial DNA to every child she has, while sperm contribute essentially none. The pattern to recognise is all children of affected mothers affected, zero transmission from affected fathers, and no sex bias at all.","traps":["An X-linked dominant mother passes the allele to only about half her children, and an affected father would pass it to all of his daughters.","An affected mother would give every son the condition, but her daughters would be unaffected carriers unless the father also carried the allele.",null,"Under autosomal dominance a heterozygous parent of either sex transmits to roughly half the children, so affected fathers would have affected children too.","Y-linked traits move from father to son only, and daughters can never be affected."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g064","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A woman who is red-green colorblind has children with a man who has normal color vision. Red-green color blindness is X-linked recessive. What is expected among their children?","opts":["25% affected each","All daughters affected and all sons unaffected","All sons affected, all daughters carriers","No effect","All sons normal"],"correct":2,"why":"An affected mother carries the colorblind allele on both of her X chromosomes, so every single child receives one copy from her. A son pairs that X with a Y and has nothing to mask it, so all sons are colorblind. A daughter pairs it with her father's normal X, so she sees color normally while carrying the allele. This criss-cross from mother to son is the classic X-linked recessive signature worth recognising on sight.","traps":["Quarter fractions arise when each parent contributes an allele by chance, but here the mother's contribution is certain.","This reverses the pattern: the father gives each daughter a normal X that masks the allele, and he gives his sons only a Y.",null,"The mother is affected and passes an affected X to every child, so this cross certainly has consequences.","Sons take their only X chromosome from the mother, and both of hers carry the colorblind allele."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-169wbe8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A culture of dividing cells is treated with a drug that prevents tubulin subunits from polymerizing into microtubules. A few hours later the cells are examined. Where would most of them be, and why?","opts":["Arrested in G1, because tubulin is needed to pass the restriction point","Arrested in S phase, because the replication fork travels along microtubules","Arrested in telophase, because the nuclear envelope cannot reassemble without tubulin","Dividing on schedule, because actin filaments take over the spindle's job","Arrested in mitosis before anaphase, with the spindle checkpoint still signaling"],"correct":4,"why":"Ask which step becomes impossible first, then ask which checkpoint notices. With no microtubules the kinetochores are never captured, so the spindle checkpoint keeps sending its wait signal and the cell cannot begin anaphase. Cells already past that point finish, and everything behind them piles up in mitosis. Drugs at either end of tubulin dynamics, one blocking assembly and one freezing filaments so they cannot shorten, stall cells at the very same place.","traps":["The restriction point weighs growth factors and cell size, and nothing about it requires a spindle to exist yet.","Replication forks are driven by enzymes acting directly on the DNA, not by anything being hauled along microtubules.","Telophase comes after the chromosomes have already been separated, so a cell that never separated them cannot reach it.","Actin drives the cleavage furrow, but it cannot attach kinetochores or sort chromosomes, so it is no substitute for the spindle.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p018","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which ion binds troponin to start muscle contraction?","opts":["Mg²⁺","Ca²⁺","K+","Na+","Cl-"],"correct":1,"why":"This is the trigger question. Calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin, troponin changes shape and drags tropomyosin off the myosin binding sites on actin, and the heads can then attach and pull. Keep the two ion jobs separate: sodium and potassium carry the action potential along the membrane, while calcium converts that electrical signal into an actual pull.","traps":["Magnesium partners with ATP in the myosin head's chemistry, but the binding site on troponin is built for calcium.",null,"Potassium leaving the cell repolarizes the membrane after the impulse, ending the signal rather than starting the pull.","Sodium entry carries the action potential along the muscle fiber membrane, which is the arriving signal rather than the trigger inside the cell.","Chloride helps stabilize the resting membrane and has no role in uncovering the myosin binding sites."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p094","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the best example of a hinge joint?","opts":["Elbow, knee","Vertebrae","The joint between the atlas and the axis at the top of the neck","Hip","Thumb base"],"correct":0,"why":"Joint questions ask you to match the shape of the joint surfaces to the motion they allow. A hinge joint is a cylinder sitting in a trough, so it opens and closes in a single plane like a door, and the elbow and the knee are the classic pair. When a joint name shows up on the exam, picture the motion first: bending in one plane points to a hinge, motion in every direction points to ball and socket, and spinning around a long axis points to a pivot.","traps":[null,"The small facet joints between vertebrae glide on each other and the discs between the bodies are cartilaginous, so neither one works like a hinge.","The atlas and axis form a pivot joint, which rotates the head side to side rather than bending it open and closed.","The hip is a ball and socket joint, so it swings in every direction rather than being limited to one plane of motion.","The base of the thumb is a saddle joint, which is what lets the thumb swing across the palm and oppose the fingers."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p114","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A mature sperm cell is built in three regions: a head, a mid-piece, and a tail. What does the mid-piece contain?","opts":["Nucleus","Golgi apparatus that keeps rebuilding the acrosome","Acrosome","Mitochondria (energy for tail)","Centrioles"],"correct":3,"why":"This question is testing the sperm as a machine with three parts, each built for one job. The head carries the nucleus capped by the acrosome, a pouch of enzymes for getting through the egg's coats. The mid-piece is packed with mitochondria wound in a tight spiral, and the ATP they make drives the flagellum. The tail is that flagellum. Read any sperm diagram by asking what each region has to accomplish, and the mid-piece is the engine room.","traps":["The nucleus rides in the head, which is where the genetic payload is carried.","The Golgi does build the acrosome while the sperm is developing, but a mature sperm has shed that machinery and packs mitochondria into the mid-piece instead.","The acrosome is the enzyme cap on the head that digests a path through the egg's coats, sitting in front of the nucleus rather than behind it.",null,"Centrioles sit in the narrow neck just ahead of the mid-piece and matter after fertilization; the mid-piece itself is filled with the energy-producing organelles."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p037","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In the circulating blood of a healthy adult, which type of white blood cell is present in the greatest numbers?","opts":["Neutrophil","Basophil","Lymphocyte","Monocyte","Eosinophil"],"correct":0,"why":"This is testing the normal white blood cell differential from most to least common. A useful order to hold is neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils, running roughly from 60 percent down to about 1 percent. Neutrophils lead because they are the first responders to bacterial infection, so the choice naming the neutrophil is the one you want.","traps":[null,"Basophils release histamine and heparin and are the rarest white cell, well under one percent.","Lymphocytes are the second most common in blood and dominate the lymph nodes, but in circulation neutrophils outnumber them about two to one.","Monocytes are the largest white cells and become macrophages once they enter tissue, yet they are only a few percent of the circulating count.","Eosinophils rise with parasites and allergic disease but sit at a small percentage of a normal differential."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g078","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Why does X inactivation matter in a person with Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)?","opts":["Both Xs active","Y inactivated","Doesn't happen","One X is inactivated, leaving one Barr body","The extra X chromosome is physically lost from the cell"],"correct":3,"why":"X inactivation holds the dose of X linked gene products steady no matter how many X chromosomes a cell carries, by silencing all but one. In a 47,XXY cell that means one X is condensed into a single Barr body, which is a large part of why the extra chromosome is tolerated and the phenotype is comparatively mild. The general rule to carry is that Barr bodies equal the number of X chromosomes minus one.","traps":["If both X chromosomes stayed active, the cell would carry double the dose of X linked gene products, which is exactly what inactivation prevents.","The Y chromosome stays active, and it carries the gene that directs male development.","X inactivation is not limited to females, since any cell carrying more than one X will silence the extras.",null,"The extra chromosome stays in the nucleus and is silenced rather than discarded, which is precisely why it can be seen as a Barr body."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g050","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"DNA replication and repair are extremely accurate but not perfect. Which of the following is the best estimate of the spontaneous mutation rate in the absence of mutagens?","opts":["About 10⁻³ per base pair per generation","10⁻¹ per gene","Variable","1 per cell division","~10⁻⁹ per base pair per generation"],"correct":4,"why":"What is worth carrying here is an order of magnitude rather than a precise figure: errors that slip past proofreading and repair occur on the order of one per billion base pairs per generation. That is vanishingly small per base, yet a genome holds billions of bases, so a small handful of new mutations per generation is entirely normal. Scale is the whole question, so look for the option that is stated per base pair and is extremely small.","traps":["This is roughly a million times too high, and a genome of billions of bases would pick up millions of changes every generation.","One gene in ten mutating each generation would make stable inheritance impossible.","Rates do differ somewhat between species and between regions of a genome, but this option gives no magnitude, and the question is asking for one.","Read as a per base pair rate this would mean essentially every base changing at every division, which no cell could survive.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e024","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following best illustrates a Type II survivorship curve?","opts":["Many birds and hydra","Trees","Elephants and other long-lived large mammals","Oysters","Humans"],"correct":0,"why":"A Type II curve is a straight line because mortality does not care how old the organism is: predation, disease, or accident removes roughly the same fraction of survivors in each interval. Many songbirds, small mammals, lizards, and hydra live this way. Set that against the Type I pattern of large mammals, which stays high and then drops late, and the Type III pattern of organisms whose young die in enormous numbers right at the start.","traps":[null,"Most tree seeds and seedlings die early while the few survivors live a long time, which is Type III.","Elephants raise few offspring with heavy parental care and concentrate mortality in old age, which is Type I.","Oysters release vast numbers of larvae and lose nearly all of them young, the Type III pattern.","Humans survive at high rates until old age, which is the Type I pattern."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g012","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Which event generates genetic variation by physically exchanging segments between homologous chromosomes?","opts":["Independent assortment metaphase I","Random fusion of one egg with one sperm at fertilization","Sister chromatid separation","Cytokinesis","Crossing-over prophase I"],"correct":4,"why":"This is testing which source of variation involves an actual physical exchange. In prophase I homologs pair as tetrads and non-sister chromatids trade matching segments at the chiasmata, producing chromosomes that carry a mix of both parents' alleles. Independent assortment and random fertilization also create variety, but they shuffle whole chromosomes rather than rewriting one. When a question says exchange or recombination between homologs, go straight to prophase I.","traps":["This decides which homolog of each pair heads to which pole, a genuine source of variation, but no segments change hands.","Which sperm meets which egg does add variety, but that happens after meiosis is over and involves no exchange between homologous chromosomes.","Sister chromatids are copies of the same chromosome, so pulling them apart in the second division does not mix parental alleles.","Cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm after the chromosomes have already been sorted and adds nothing to genetic variety.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-xm2ae3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"RuBisCO attaches carbon dioxide to the five carbon sugar RuBP. What comes out of that first step?","opts":["One four carbon oxaloacetate","Two molecules of G3P, ready to leave the cycle","Two molecules of the three carbon acid 3-PGA","One stable six carbon sugar that leaves the cycle","One two carbon fragment and one three carbon acid"],"correct":2,"why":"Count carbons and the answer falls out on its own. Five carbons of RuBP plus one carbon from carbon dioxide makes six, and that six carbon intermediate is so unstable it immediately splits into two three carbon acids. Say the sentence every time: five plus one is six, and six breaks into two threes.","traps":["Oxaloacetate is what PEP carboxylase makes in a C4 plant's mesophyll cell, a different enzyme in a different place.","G3P arrives two steps later, after ATP and NADPH have done their work on 3-PGA.",null,"A six carbon intermediate does form, but it lasts only an instant before splitting in half.","That pairing is what comes out when RuBisCO grabs oxygen instead of carbon dioxide, which is photorespiration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-b115107d15","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Epinephrine is classified as which type of hormone, and how does it signal?","opts":["A steroid that binds a receptor inside the nucleus","An amino acid derivative that crosses the membrane like thyroid hormone","A steroid released from the adrenal cortex","An amino acid derivative that binds surface receptors","A peptide made of many amino acids that enters the cell through a channel"],"correct":3,"why":"Both epinephrine and thyroid hormone come from tyrosine, but they behave differently. Epinephrine is water soluble, so it binds a surface receptor and a second messenger such as cAMP carries the signal. That is why fight or flight switches on in seconds.","traps":["Epinephrine is not made from cholesterol. It is water soluble and stays outside the cell.","Thyroid hormone is the lipid soluble exception. Epinephrine behaves like a peptide and uses surface receptors.","Epinephrine comes from the adrenal MEDULLA and is not a steroid.",null,"It is derived from a SINGLE amino acid and it does not enter the cell."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1u7pjce","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"The very first thing a cell does to glucose is attach a phosphate to it. What does that accomplish?","opts":["It supplies the phosphate that is eventually handed to oxygen","It releases the energy later captured as ATP","It converts glucose into fructose","It gives the sugar a charge so it cannot leave the cell","It tags the sugar for transport into the mitochondrion"],"correct":3,"why":"Phosphate groups carry negative charge, and charged molecules do not slide back through a lipid membrane or ride the glucose transporter. Phosphorylating glucose therefore locks it inside the cell and commits it to being used. The same trick shows up all over metabolism: add a phosphate, trap the molecule.","traps":["Oxygen never receives a phosphate. It receives electrons and protons and leaves as water.","That step costs energy rather than releasing it, and the payoff does not arrive until later in the pathway.","An isomerase does that conversion, and it happens in a separate step after the phosphate is already on.",null,"Glucose-6-phosphate is not what crosses into the mitochondrion; pyruvate is, and it uses its own transporter."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p039","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The intrinsic and extrinsic pathways of coagulation converge on a shared final step. Which of the following describes how the clotting cascade ends?","opts":["Platelet aggregation only","Plasmin breaking the clot down into fibrin fragments","Thrombin converts fibrinogen into a fibrin mesh","Factor X","Vitamin K"],"correct":2,"why":"Every clotting pathway funnels into the same finish, so that is what this question is after. Activated factor X builds prothrombinase, prothrombin becomes thrombin, and thrombin snips soluble fibrinogen into fibrin that polymerizes into a mesh and traps the platelet plug. Picture the cascade as a funnel that ends in a net, and the choice naming thrombin acting on fibrinogen to make fibrin is the finish line.","traps":["The platelet plug is primary hemostasis and forms first; without a fibrin mesh woven through it, that plug stays fragile.","Plasmin goes to work after the clot has done its job, dissolving fibrin; that is the takedown crew rather than the end of the building cascade.",null,"Factor X activation is the junction where the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways meet, which puts it in the middle of the story rather than at the end.","Vitamin K is needed to build several clotting factors in the liver, so it is a requirement upstream, not the final product."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_cm154","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"How does cytokinesis begin in a dividing animal cell?","opts":["Spindle disassembly","Cleavage furrow (actin + myosin contractile ring)","Nuclear envelope reformation","A contracting ring of microtubules that pinches the cell in two","Cell plate"],"correct":1,"why":"This is testing how animal and plant cells solve the same problem differently. An animal cell has a flexible plasma membrane, so a ring of actin filaments with myosin tightens like a drawstring just inside the membrane at the old metaphase plate and pinches the cell into two. A plant cell cannot be pinched through a rigid wall, so vesicles deliver wall material to the middle and build a cell plate outward instead. Ask whether there is a cell wall, and the mechanism follows.","traps":["The spindle does come apart around this time, but taking the spindle apart does not divide the cytoplasm.",null,"Rebuilding the nuclear envelope is part of what happens to the nucleus in telophase, separate from splitting the cytoplasm.","Microtubules build the spindle that moved the chromosomes; the ring that pinches the cell closed is made of actin and myosin.","A cell plate is built from vesicles in a plant cell, which cannot be pinched because of its rigid wall."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1d8y656","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"How many turns of the Calvin cycle does a chloroplast need to build one six carbon glucose?","opts":["Six","Three","Two","Twelve","Eighteen"],"correct":0,"why":"Each turn fixes exactly one carbon, and glucose holds six of them. The longer route to the same answer: three turns release one exportable G3P, and two G3P join to form glucose, so double the three. Counting carbons in against carbons out settles every Calvin cycle bookkeeping question.","traps":[null,"Three turns yields a single G3P, which is only half a glucose.","Two is the number of G3P molecules that combine into glucose, not the number of turns.","Twelve is how many G3P molecules are made across those turns, and most of them go straight back into RuBP.","Eighteen is the ATP bill for one glucose, not a count of turns."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p109","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A patient whose thyroid gland is overactive would most likely show which set of findings?","opts":["Cold intolerance, weight gain","Heat intolerance, weight loss, tachycardia","Heat intolerance and weight loss together with a slow resting heart rate","Lethargy","Bradycardia"],"correct":1,"why":"Think of thyroid hormone as the dial on the body's metabolic furnace. Turn it up and everything speeds up: more heat is produced, so warm rooms feel unbearable, calories burn faster than they are eaten, so weight falls, and the heart beats faster. Once you hold the furnace picture, you can build either list on the spot instead of memorizing both.","traps":["That is the underactive thyroid picture, the furnace turned down rather than up.",null,"The metabolic half is right, but the same hormone excess speeds the heart, so a slow resting rate does not fit.","Excess thyroid hormone tends to bring restlessness, tremor and trouble sleeping instead of sluggishness.","Thyroid hormone increases the heart's sensitivity to adrenaline, so the rate climbs rather than slows."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e032","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which process is the main route by which carbon returns to the atmosphere as carbon dioxide?","opts":["Volcanism only","Dissolution of carbon dioxide into the ocean surface","Cellular respiration + combustion","Sedimentation","Photosynthesis"],"correct":2,"why":"Track the carbon cycle as a pair of opposite reactions and it stays manageable. Photosynthesis pulls CO₂ down into organic molecules, and cellular respiration in plants, animals, fungi, and bacteria breaks those molecules back down and releases CO₂. Combustion does chemically the same thing to carbon that was buried long ago, which is why burning fossil fuel adds to the same return flux. Keep the sinks separate in your head: the ocean absorbs CO₂, and sediment and rock store carbon for a very long time.","traps":["Volcanoes do release CO₂, but the amount is small next to respiration and fossil fuel combustion.","The ocean absorbs a large share of atmospheric CO₂, so this also moves carbon out of the air.",null,"Sedimentation buries carbon in rock and ocean sediment, making it a long-term sink rather than a source.","Photosynthesis removes CO₂ from the air and fixes it into sugar, which is the opposite direction."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g049","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A translocation is a chromosomal mutation that involves:","opts":["Single base change","Within same chromosome","Polyploidy","Crossing over between homologous chromosomes during prophase I","Exchange between non-homologous chromosomes"],"correct":4,"why":"The load-bearing word is non-homologous. A piece of one chromosome ends up attached to a chromosome that is not its partner, so genes that belong on one chromosome find themselves sitting on an entirely different one. Compare that with ordinary crossing over, which swaps matching pieces between true homologs and is a routine, scheduled part of meiosis rather than a mutation.","traps":["A single base change is a point mutation, a change at the level of one nucleotide rather than a whole chromosome segment.","Rearrangements confined to a single chromosome are inversions or deletions, not translocations.","Polyploidy means whole extra sets of chromosomes, a change in number rather than in arrangement.","That exchange happens between matching partners and is normal meiotic recombination, not a chromosomal mutation.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g043","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A volcanic eruption kills most of a beetle population at random with respect to genotype, and the few survivors rebuild the population. Which Hardy-Weinberg assumption does this event violate?","opts":["No gene flow into or out of the population","No mutation","Infinite population (no drift)","No selection","Random mating"],"correct":2,"why":"Hardy-Weinberg holds only when chance cannot shove allele frequencies around, and that requires a population large enough for sampling error to average out. A bottleneck leaves a handful of survivors, so the next generation's allele frequencies reflect who happened to live rather than the original pool. That is genetic drift, and the assumption it breaks is the infinitely large population. When a stem tells you the deaths were random with respect to the trait, think drift rather than selection.","traps":["Gene flow moves alleles between populations through migration, while a bottleneck is a crash inside one population.","A bottleneck does not change the mutation rate; it changes how strongly chance decides which alleles get passed on.",null,"Selection requires that genotype affect survival, and this stem states the deaths were random with respect to genotype.","Survivors of a bottleneck can still choose mates at random, so what fails here is population size rather than mate choice."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g010","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The Hardy-Weinberg model applies only when a specific set of conditions is met. Which of the following is NOT one of those conditions?","opts":["No migration into or out of the population","Infinite population","Random mating","Equal sex ratio","No mutation"],"correct":3,"why":"The model asks what allele frequencies do when nothing disturbs them, so its conditions are simply a list of the five things that do disturb them: mutation, non-random mating, gene flow, drift in a small population, and selection. Test each choice by asking whether it would change allele frequencies from one generation to the next. The ratio of males to females is not on that list, because the model tracks alleles rather than counts of each sex.","traps":["No gene flow is one of the required conditions, since migrants arriving with different allele frequencies would shift the population's own.","An effectively infinite population is required so that chance sampling between generations, which is genetic drift, does not shift frequencies.","Non-random mating such as inbreeding or assortative mating changes genotype proportions, so random mating is genuinely required.",null,"Mutation creates new alleles and shifts frequencies, so ruling it out really is one of the required conditions."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-a:p-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Which has greater 1st IE: N or O?","opts":["Cannot tell","Neither, carbon is higher than both","Same","O","N"],"correct":4,"why":"N has half-filled 2p³ (extra stable). Removing an e⁻ from O's 2p⁴ (paired) is easier than from N's half-full.","traps":["This one is settled, and the half filled 2p set is the reason nitrogen comes out on top.","Carbon comes in near 1086 kJ/mol, below both, so reading the period trend from right to left is what puts it on top.","They are not equal: nitrogen comes in near 1402 kJ/mol and oxygen near 1314.","The left to right trend does point here, and nitrogen's half filled 2p³ is unusually hard to disturb.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"The standard Gibbs free-energy change is related to the equilibrium constant by ΔG° = −RT ln K, where R = 8.314 J/(K·mol) and T = 298 K. If a reaction has K = 1.00 at 298 K (products and reactants equally favored), what is ΔG°?","opts":["ΔG° = 0","ΔG° = −RT","ΔG° &lt; 0","ΔG° = +RT","ΔG° &gt; 0"],"correct":0,"why":"ln(1) = 0, so ΔG° = −RT(0) = 0. K = 1 means standard products and reactants are equally favored; no net driving force at standard conditions. ΔG &gt; 0 corresponds to K &lt; 1; ΔG &lt; 0 corresponds to K &gt; 1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-e13","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Catalyst lowers Eₐ by 15 kJ/mol at 300 K. Approx rate ratio (k_new/k_old)?","opts":["~5","~1","~50","~6000","~400"],"correct":4,"why":"k_cat/k_old = exp(15000/(8.314×300)) = exp(6.01) ≈ 408.","traps":["That is far too small: an exponent near 6 multiplies the rate constant by hundreds, not by a handful.","That comes from putting 15 into the exponent instead of 15000, leaving the barrier in kilojoules while R is in joules.","That is roughly e⁴, which would correspond to a barrier cut near 10 kJ/mol instead of 15.","That is closer to e⁸·⁷, which would need a cut of about 22 kJ/mol.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"Given ΔHf° (kJ/mol): CO2(g) = −394, H2O(g) = −242, CH3OH(l) = −239, and O2(g) = 0, calculate the standard enthalpy of combustion for CH3OH(l) + 1.5 O2(g) -&gt; CO2(g) + 2 H2O(g) at 25 °C.","opts":["−639 kJ","+639 kJ","+1117 kJ","−717 kJ","−400 kJ"],"correct":0,"why":"ΔH_rxn = sum(ΔHf° products) − sum(ΔHf° reactants) = [−394 + 2(−242)] − [−239 + 1.5(0)] = −878 − (−239) = −639 kJ. Sign errors on reactants give −1117 or +639.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_m7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Combustion analysis of an unknown carbohydrate yields mass percentages of 40.0% C, 6.7% H, and 53.3% O. Assuming a 100 g sample (40.0 g C, 6.7 g H, 53.3 g O) and dividing each by atomic mass to find moles, what is the empirical formula?","opts":["CHO","CH₂O","C₂H₄O₂","C₆H₁₂O₆","C₃H₆O₃"],"correct":1,"why":"Moles: C 40.0/12 = 3.33; H 6.7/1 = 6.7; O 53.3/16 = 3.33. Dividing by smallest (3.33) gives 1:2:1 → CH₂O. C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose) is the molecular formula but not the empirical formula; the empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lw5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"The sulfate ion SO₄²⁻ has S as the central atom bonded to four oxygen atoms. Sulfur contributes 6 valence electrons, each oxygen contributes 6, and the 2− charge adds 2 more. What is the total number of valence electrons in the Lewis structure of SO₄²⁻?","opts":["36 electrons","30 electrons","28 electrons","34 electrons","32 electrons"],"correct":4,"why":"Total valence e⁻ = 6 (S) + 4(6) (O) + 2 (extra electrons from 2− charge) = 32. These distribute over four S-O bonds and lone pairs on each oxygen, with formal charges minimized in resonance structures featuring two S=O double bonds.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#vh8wbg","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 1.5 m aqueous solution of sucrose? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-5.58 °C","-2.79 °C","-0.768 °C","2.79 °C","-4.19 °C"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (1)(1.86)(1.5) = 2.79 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 2.79 = -2.79 °C.","traps":["sucrose is a molecular solute. It does not split into ions, so i = 1.",null,"You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ic-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Phosphorus pentachloride decomposes per PCl5(g) ⇌ PCl3(g) + Cl2(g) with K_c = 1.0 at 525 K. A 1.00 L flask is charged with [PCl5]0 = 1.00 M (no PCl3 or Cl2 present). Set up the ICE table with x = [PCl3]_eq, then solve x^2/(1 - x) = 1 for [PCl3] at equilibrium.","opts":["0.71 M","1.0 M","0.50 M","0.62 M","0.40 M"],"correct":3,"why":"x^2 = 1·(1 - x) gives x^2 + x - 1 = 0, so x = (-1 + sqrt(5))/2 ~ 0.618 M. The small-x approximation fails here (K is comparable to [PCl5]0), so the quadratic is necessary.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A microbalance reading reports a sample mass of 0.030 g, which the analyst records exactly as displayed. Using the convention that the last significant digit conveys the precision (uncertainty in the last decimal place), how many sig figs does this measurement carry, and what is the implied uncertainty?","opts":["2 sig figs; ±0.001 g","2 sig figs; ±0.01 g","1 sig fig; ±0.1 g","3 sig figs; ±0.0001 g","5 sig figs; ±0.00001 g"],"correct":0,"why":"Leading zeros are placeholders, but the trailing zero after the decimal is significant. The digits 3 and 0 give 2 sig figs, with implied uncertainty in the last decimal place of ±0.001 g. Writing 3 x 10^-2 would imply only 1 sig fig and a larger uncertainty.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gl-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A 1.00 mol sample of an ideal gas is held at 0.50 atm and 300. K inside a flexible balloon. Using R = 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K) and assuming ideal behavior throughout, what is the volume of the gas under these conditions?","opts":["60.0 L","12.3 L","30.0 L","49.3 L","24.6 L"],"correct":2,"why":"V = nRT/P = (1.00)(0.0821)(300)/0.50 = 49.3 L. Forgetting to divide by P (using P=1) gives 24.6 L; doubling instead of dividing gives 12.3 L.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_el-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"An electroplating bath of CuSO4 deposits Cu via Cu^2+ + 2 e- -&gt; Cu(s) at a constant current of 0.50 A. To deposit exactly 1.0 g of Cu (M = 63.5 g/mol) using F = 96485 C/mol, compute the time required from t = nFm/(IM) and convert to minutes.","opts":["50 min","200 min","400 min","100 min","25 min"],"correct":3,"why":"Moles Cu = 1.0/63.5 = 0.01575; moles e- = 0.0315; charge Q = 0.0315 x 96485 = 3039 C; t = Q/I = 3039/0.50 = 6078 s ~ 101 min ~ 100 min.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ti-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 25.0 mL aliquot of 0.100 M acetic acid (CH₃COOH, K_a = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵) is titrated with 0.100 M NaOH. At the half-equivalence point, exactly half the acid has been converted to its conjugate base, so [HA] = [A⁻]. What is the pH of the solution at this point?","opts":["2.87","4.74","7.00","8.72","9.26"],"correct":1,"why":"At half-equivalence, the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation pH = pK_a + log([A⁻]/[HA]) reduces to pH = pK_a since [A⁻] = [HA] makes log(1) = 0. pK_a = -log(1.8×10⁻⁵) = 4.74, so pH = 4.74.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:el4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"How many moles of e⁻ needed to plate 1 mol of Al from Al³⁺?","opts":["0.33","1","2","3","6"],"correct":3,"why":"Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al(s). One mole of Al requires 3 moles of electrons.","traps":["Dividing one mole by the three plus charge inverts the relationship; each atom needs three electrons, so the electron count is the larger number.","One electron per ion is the silver case, Ag⁺; aluminum carries a three plus charge.","Two electrons per ion fits Cu²⁺, and aluminum needs one more than that.",null,"Six electrons would plate two moles of aluminum, and the question asks about one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A 100. mL buffer at 25 °C contains 0.010 mol HA and 0.010 mol A- (pKa = 4.74). 0.001 mol NaOH is added with no volume change. NaOH converts HA -&gt; A-: new mol HA = 0.009, new mol A- = 0.011. Using pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]), what is the new pH?","opts":["4.65","4.74","4.83","5.00","5.74"],"correct":2,"why":"log(0.011/0.009) = log(1.222) ≈ 0.087. pH = 4.74 + 0.09 = 4.83. Forgetting the small adjustment leaves 4.74; flipping the ratio gives 4.65.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:imf5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Which is most soluble in water?","opts":["CCl₄ (carbon tetrachloride)","CH₃-O-CH₃ (dimethyl ether)","C₂H₆ (ethane)","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ (butane)","CH₃CH₂OH (ethanol)"],"correct":4,"why":"Like dissolves like. Ethanol's O - H can H-bond directly with water - most soluble. Dimethyl ether ACCEPTS H-bonds but doesn't donate. Butane/ethane only have London, so poor solubility.","traps":["Each C-Cl bond is polar, and the four point to the corners of a tetrahedron and cancel, leaving a nonpolar molecule.","Ether can accept a hydrogen bond from water, and with no O-H of its own to donate it dissolves less readily than ethanol.","Ethane is nonpolar as well, with only dispersion forces to offer water.","Butane is a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and water molecules would rather hydrogen bond to each other than open up for it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A chemist prepares an NH4+/NH3 buffer at 25 °C with pKa(NH4+) = 9.25 and target pH = 10.00. Using the Henderson - Hasselbalch equation pH = pKa + log([NH3]/[NH4+]), what ratio [NH3]/[NH4+] is required to reach the target pH?","opts":["0.18","1.0","5.6","10","100"],"correct":2,"why":"log([NH3]/[NH4+]) = pH − pKa = 10.00 − 9.25 = 0.75. [NH3]/[NH4+] = 10^0.75 ≈ 5.6. Inverting gives 0.18; using base-e instead of base-10 gives ~2.1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-molecular-formula#161hg1z","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A compound has the empirical formula CH₂ and a molar mass of 28 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?","opts":["C₃H₆","C₂H₄","CH₂","C₄H₈","C₅H₁₀"],"correct":1,"why":"Empirical mass = 14. 28 / 14 = 2, so multiply every subscript by 2: C₂H₄.","traps":["Multiplier is one too high. Molar mass divided by empirical mass must come out to a whole number.",null,"That is the empirical formula again. Divide the molar mass by the empirical mass to get the multiplier.","You doubled the multiplier. Check the empirical formula mass.","Wrong multiplier. Molar mass divided by empirical mass gives it exactly."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nu-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A 1.00 g sample of radium-226 (Z = 88), used historically in luminous paints, undergoes alpha decay by emitting a 4He nucleus from each parent atom. Writing the balanced nuclear equation 226-88 Ra -&gt; X + 4-2 He, identify the daughter nuclide X produced in this single decay step.","opts":["222-88 Ra","222-86 Rn","224-86 Rn","226-89 Ac","226-86 Rn"],"correct":1,"why":"Alpha emission reduces mass number A by 4 and atomic number Z by 2. From 226-88 Ra: A = 226 - 4 = 222 and Z = 88 - 2 = 86, giving radon-222. Conservation of A and Z drives every nuclear decay equation.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-molecular-formula#157imz2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A compound has the empirical formula C₂H₅ and a molar mass of 58 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?","opts":["C₂H₅","C₁₀H₂₅","C₆H₁₅","C₄H₁₀","C₈H₂₀"],"correct":3,"why":"Empirical mass = 29. 58 / 29 = 2, so multiply every subscript by 2: C₄H₁₀.","traps":["That is the empirical formula again. Divide the molar mass by the empirical mass to get the multiplier.","Wrong multiplier. Molar mass divided by empirical mass gives it exactly.","Multiplier is one too high. Molar mass divided by empirical mass must come out to a whole number.",null,"You doubled the multiplier. Check the empirical formula mass."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nu4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Three forms of ionizing radiation differ greatly in penetration: α particles (helium nuclei, stopped by paper), β particles (high-speed electrons/positrons, stopped by aluminum foil), and γ rays (high-energy photons, requiring lead/concrete shielding). Which is the MOST penetrating?","opts":["Neutrino","Alpha (α)","Gamma (γ)","Positron (β⁺)","Beta (β)"],"correct":2,"why":"γ rays are massless, chargeless, high-energy photons that interact only weakly with matter, requiring thick lead or concrete to attenuate them. α particles are stopped by paper; β by a few mm of aluminum. Order of penetration: γ &gt;&gt; β &gt;&gt; α.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#xf6469","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 1 m aqueous solution of sucrose? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-1.86 °C","-2.79 °C","-3.72 °C","-0.512 °C","1.86 °C"],"correct":0,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (1)(1.86)(1) = 1.86 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 1.86 = -1.86 °C.","traps":[null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","sucrose is a molecular solute. It does not split into ions, so i = 1.","You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:x8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Rate doubles when temperature rises from 300 K to 310 K. Qualitatively, what does this tell you about the activation energy?","opts":["Eₐ is moderate (around 50-60 kJ/mol)","Eₐ is large (&gt;200 kJ/mol)","Eₐ is very small (a few kJ/mol)","Eₐ cannot be inferred from rate vs T data","The 10 K doubling reports ΔH, not Eₐ"],"correct":0,"why":"The \"rate doubles per 10 K rise\" rule of thumb corresponds to a moderate Eₐ around 50-60 kJ/mol for most organic reactions near room temperature. Plugging into Arrhenius (ln 2 = Eₐ/R · ΔT/(T₁T₂)) gives Eₐ ≈ 54 kJ/mol, but the qualitative DAT version is \"rate doubles per 10 K, so moderate Eₐ.\" Very small Eₐ would give a much weaker T sensitivity; very large Eₐ would multiply rate by far more than 2.","traps":[null,"&gt;200 kJ/mol would make rate jump by orders of magnitude per 10 K, not just double.","A few kJ/mol gives almost no temperature dependence - rate would barely change.","Eₐ can absolutely be inferred from rate vs T via Arrhenius. That is the whole point of the equation.","ΔH is the energy gap between reactants and products, while the temperature sensitivity of the rate reports on the barrier Eₐ."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"Pure water at 25°C has vapor pressure P° = 23.8 torr. A NaCl solution is prepared so the mole fraction of solvent water is chi_solvent = 0.90 (treating NaCl as a non-volatile, non-dissociating solute for this calculation). Apply Raoult's law P_solution = chi_solvent · P° to find the new vapor pressure.","opts":["23.8 torr","26.4 torr","19.0 torr","21.4 torr","2.4 torr"],"correct":3,"why":"P_solution = 0.90 x 23.8 = 21.42 torr ~ 21.4 torr. The 2.4 torr drop is the colligative vapor-pressure lowering. (A real NaCl solution would dissociate into 2 ions per formula unit, doubling the apparent solute fraction.)","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Two related reactions are tabulated at 298 K: (1) A -&gt; B with ΔH1 = -50 kJ; (2) 2 B -&gt; C with ΔH2 = +30 kJ. Apply Hess's law (multiply step 1 by 2 to match B stoichiometry, then add to step 2) to compute ΔH for the combined reaction 2 A -&gt; C.","opts":["+70 kJ","+30 kJ","-130 kJ","-70 kJ","-20 kJ"],"correct":3,"why":"Multiply (1) by 2: 2 A -&gt; 2 B, ΔH = 2(-50) = -100 kJ. Add (2): 2 B -&gt; C, ΔH = +30 kJ. Sum: 2 A -&gt; C, ΔH = -100 + 30 = -70 kJ.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#z35bkv","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 17 g of NH₃? (molar mass 17 g/mol)","opts":["6.02 × 10²³","2.01 × 10²³","1.02 × 10²⁵","3","1.81 × 10²⁴"],"correct":4,"why":"17 g / 17 g/mol = 1 mol NH₃. Times 3 hydrogen per molecule = 3 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.81 × 10²⁴.","traps":["That is the number of MOLECULES. Each NH₃ holds 3 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 3.","You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1nbu46t","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 3 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 127 °C and 2 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["16.4 L","4990 L","197 L","15.6 L","49.3 L"],"correct":4,"why":"T = 127 + 273 = 400 K. V = nRT / P = (3)(0.0821)(400) / 2 = 49.3 L.","traps":["You dropped the moles. n belongs on top with R and T.","You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.","You multiplied by pressure. V = nRT / P, so pressure goes underneath.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:ks-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Solubility of AgCl in 0.010 M AgNO₃ (common ion). Ksp(AgCl) = 1.8×10⁻¹⁰.","opts":["1.34×10⁻⁵","1.8×10⁻¹²","1.8×10⁻⁸","1.34×10⁻⁴","1.8×10⁻¹⁰"],"correct":2,"why":"[Ag⁺] ≈ 0.010 (from AgNO₃, dominant). Ksp = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = (0.010)(s), so s = 1.8×10⁻⁸ M. Common ion crushes solubility ~750×.","traps":["That is the solubility in pure water, which ignores the silver already in solution.","That divides Ksp by 100 instead of by 0.010, two decimal places the wrong way.",null,"That takes a square root at the end out of habit; with silver fixed at 0.010 M, s comes straight from Ksp divided by it.","That is Ksp itself, but it still has to be divided by the 0.010 M silver present."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:hb-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"How many σ and π bonds in HCN (H−C≡N)?","opts":["1σ + 3π","2σ + 2π","3σ + 2π","3σ + 1π","4σ + 0π"],"correct":1,"why":"H−C: 1σ. C≡N: 1σ + 2π. Total: 2σ + 2π.","traps":["This counts all three lines of the triple bond as pi bonds; the first bond between any two atoms is always a sigma.",null,"Three sigma bonds would need three atom-to-atom connections, and HCN has two, H to C and C to N.","This treats the triple bond as three sigma plus one pi; a triple bond is one sigma and two pi.","Four sigma counts every line in H-C≡N as a sigma bond; only the first bond of each pair is sigma and the other two lines are pi."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-gibbs#13ywdc2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a reaction, ΔH = -120 kJ and ΔS = 100 J/K. What is ΔG at 300 K?","opts":["-150 kJ","-90 kJ","-30100 kJ","-120 kJ","-30 kJ"],"correct":0,"why":"TΔS = 300 × 100 J/K = 30 kJ. ΔG = -120 − (30) = -150 kJ, so the reaction is spontaneous at this temperature.","traps":[null,"Sign slip. ΔG = ΔH MINUS TΔS.","Unit trap. ΔS is in J/K but ΔH is in kJ. Divide TΔS by 1000 before subtracting.","You forgot to multiply ΔS by the temperature.","You dropped ΔH. Both terms matter."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ph-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 0.010 M Ba(OH)2 solution is prepared in water at 25 °C, where Ba(OH)2 dissociates completely to give 1 Ba(2+) and 2 OH- per formula unit. Therefore [OH-] = 0.020 M. Using pOH = -log[OH-] and pH + pOH = 14, what is the pH?","opts":["11.0","12.0","12.3","12.7","13.0"],"correct":2,"why":"[OH-] = 2 x 0.010 = 0.020 M; pOH = -log(0.020) = 1.70. Therefore pH = 14 - 1.70 = 12.30. Forgetting the factor of 2 (using 0.010 M) gives pH = 12.0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-1iyt40m","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Optically pure (R)-3-phenylbutan-2-one (shown) is stirred in aqueous NaOH and then re-isolated. The alpha carbon bearing the phenyl group is the stereocenter. What is the stereochemical outcome and why?","opts":["The compound is destroyed by a haloform-type C-C cleavage at the stereocenter","A single diastereomer forms because a new stereocenter is created at the carbonyl carbon","The product is racemic (R and S in equal amounts) because base-promoted enolization at that alpha carbon makes it sp2 and planar, and reprotonation occurs equally on both faces","The (R) configuration is fully retained, because enolization occurs only toward the methyl group on the far side of the carbonyl, so the stereocenter is never flattened and never reprotonated","It is converted entirely to the (S) enantiomer, because hydroxide attacks the stereocenter from the face opposite the proton and displaces it with clean backside inversion of configuration"],"correct":2,"why":"The stereocenter is the alpha carbon and bears an alpha H. Aqueous NaOH (no halogen present) enolizes it, making it sp2/planar and erasing chirality; reprotonation on either face gives a 50:50 (R)/(S) racemate. option A correct. Not an SN2 inversion, no new stereocenter at the carbonyl, and haloform cleavage is impossible here because there is no halogen and the stereocenter is not a methyl-ketone CH3.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(C)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-e8tuz7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"The alkene shown is treated with HBr (no peroxides). The only major product isolated is tert-butyl bromide (2-bromo-2-methylpropane). Which statement best explains this outcome?","opts":["H+ adds to the more-substituted alkene carbon, which places the positive charge on the terminal carbon and gives a primary carbocation that bromide then traps at that same carbon","Br adds to the more-substituted carbon because Br is the larger, more nucleophilic atom","The reaction proceeds through a free-radical chain in which a bromine atom adds first, so Br ends up on the less-substituted carbon rather than on the more-substituted tertiary carbon","The product forms by a concerted syn addition with no carbocation intermediate","H+ adds to the less-substituted carbon, placing the positive charge on the more-substituted carbon to give the more stable (tertiary) carbocation (Markovnikov addition)"],"correct":4,"why":"Ionic HX addition is Markovnikov: the proton adds to the carbon with more hydrogens so the carbocation forms on the more-substituted carbon (here tertiary). Bromide then traps that cation, putting Br on the more-substituted carbon (tert-butyl bromide). Radical pathways need peroxides, which are absent.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=C)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1s6ocho","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Aniline (drawn below) has its nitrogen attached directly to a benzene ring. Compared with the nitrogen in a simple sp3 alkylamine, the aniline nitrogen is best described as:","opts":["sp3, with the lone pair fully delocalized into the aromatic ring the way pyridine's lone pair is","Closer to sp2/planar, because its lone pair partially conjugates with the aromatic ring","Purely sp3 and identical in geometry to a simple alkylamine","Purely sp and linear at nitrogen","sp2, with the ring carbon bearing the nitrogen rehybridizing to sp3"],"correct":1,"why":"The aniline N lone pair conjugates (donates) into the ring, flattening the nitrogen toward planarity and giving it sp2 character relative to an sp3 amine, which weakens its basicity. It is not sp/linear, and the ipso carbon stays sp2. Distractor 4 confuses aniline (exocyclic donor N) with pyridine (ring N).","traps":null,"qs":"Nc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-umba16","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"trans-2-butene reacts with Br2 in CCl4 (anti addition through a bromonium ion). Considering both new stereocenters, what is the stereochemical relationship of the 2,3-dibromobutane product?","opts":["A single optically active (2R,3R) product only","A cis-dibromide formed by syn addition","A 1:1 mixture of the meso compound and the racemic (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) pair, because the bromonium ion opens at both carbons","A racemic mixture of the (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) enantiomers, formed in equal amounts by attack at either carbon","A single meso compound (the two enantiomers superimpose due to an internal mirror plane)"],"correct":4,"why":"Anti addition of Br2 to trans-2-butene gives MESO 2,3-dibromobutane (R,S) with an internal mirror plane; cis-2-butene would give the racemic R,R/S,S pair. This is the canonical anti-addition diagnostic. Index 0 correct.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"own-organic:ace-0002","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"3-methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. Which product comes out of the flask in the largest amount?","opts":["CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(Br)C(C)C","CC=C(C)C","BrCCC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O"],"correct":0,"why":"The proton lands on the end carbon and leaves a secondary cation sitting right next to a tertiary carbon. A hydrogen slides over with its pair, the charge moves to the better spot, and the bromide finds it there. The skeleton you started with is not the skeleton you finish with.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)C","os":true,"src":"own-organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1e45crv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Methyl vinyl ketone is treated with KCN followed by aqueous acid workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC(=O)CCC#N","CC(=O)C(C#N)C","C=CC(O)(C)C#N","CC(O)(C#N)C=C","CC(=O)CC#N"],"correct":0,"why":"Cyanide is a soft nucleophile that undergoes 1,4 (conjugate/Michael) addition to the beta carbon of the alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone. After tautomerization the ketone is regenerated, placing the nitrile on the terminal (beta) carbon: 4-oxopentanenitrile (CC(=O)CCC#N). Option B adds at the alpha carbon (wrong regiochemistry). Options C and D are 1,2 cyanohydrin additions (disfavored for a soft stabilized nucleophile). Option E is missing a carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(=O)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1uwohsb","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Predict the major product when 1,2-dimethylcyclohexene reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2.","opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1(C)Br","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@]1(C)Br","C[C@]1(Br)CCCC[C@]1(C)Br","C[C@]1(Br)CCCC[C@@]1(C)Br","CC1(C)C(Br)(Br)CCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Bromonium ion opened by anti backside attack puts the two C-Br bonds trans across the ring: trans-1,2-dibromo-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane. Each alkene carbon becomes a fully substituted sp3 stereocenter. C ignores stereochem, D = gem-dibromide rearranged skeleton, E = bromohydrin (wrong reagent). Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-s4anoj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"cis-4-tert-Butylcyclohexyl acetate (drawn) is treated with aqueous NaOH, then mild acidic workup (saponification). Which alcohol is produced?","opts":["CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](OC)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(=O)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"correct":3,"why":"3D geometry analysis confirms SM acetate is cis and keyed option A alcohol is cis (option B is the trans diastereomer). Saponification cleaves the acyl-oxygen bond (C=O carbon attacked), never the alkyl C-O bond at the ring stereocenter, so the ring carbon keeps its configuration and the cis relationship to tert-butyl is preserved.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)O[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-38z2ou","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"(S)-Mandelic acid (drawn) is heated with methanol and catalytic HCl. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1","COC(C(=O)O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)OC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Only the -COOH esterifies to the methyl ester; the benzylic secondary -OH is unreactive and its stereocenter is untouched, so S is retained. option B is the inverted (R) epimer, option C methylates the OH (free acid), option D is an anhydride, option E is the ethyl ester.","traps":null,"qs":"O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ivsaxp","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Cyclopent-2-en-1-one (shown) can react with carbon nucleophiles to give either 1,2- or 1,4-addition products. Which reagent is MOST likely to give predominantly the 1,4 (conjugate) addition product?","opts":["Sodium borohydride is irrelevant; all four add 1,2","Lithium diphenylcuprate (Ph2CuLi)","Phenylmagnesium bromide (PhMgBr) at low temperature","Phenyllithium (PhLi)","Lithium aluminum hydride (LiAlH4)"],"correct":1,"why":"Organocuprates (Gilman reagents) are the classic reagents for 1,4 (conjugate) addition to enones because the soft, less basic organocopper species adds to the beta carbon. Organolithiums (PhLi) and largely Grignards add 1,2 (hard nucleophiles), and LiAlH4 is a hydride that reduces the carbonyl 1,2 rather than acting as a carbon nucleophile. Option E is a false statement. Exactly one correct answer (index 0).","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CCC=C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-f05ba3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"The allyl aryl ether shown is heated to 200 C with no catalyst or solvent. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this Claisen rearrangement?","opts":["C=CCc1ccc(O)cc1","C=CCc1ccccc1O","C=CCOc1ccccc1","CC=Cc1ccccc1O","Oc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Aromatic Claisen [3,3]-sigmatropic shift: allyl migrates to the ortho ring carbon, then tautomerization restores aromaticity to give 2-allylphenol (C9H10O conserved). With an unsubstituted symmetric allyl, the terminal vinyl is regenerated. Distractors are unchanged ether, para isomer (only when both ortho blocked), internal-alkene isomer, and plain phenol.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-163l4so","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"The keto-aldehyde 4-oxopentanal (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CHO, drawn) is treated with exactly 1 equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C. Which carbonyl is reduced preferentially, and what is the reason?","opts":["Both are reduced equally; NaBH4 shows no selectivity","The aldehyde is reduced first because its carbonyl carbon is the more sterically hindered of the two and binds borohydride more tightly","The ketone is reduced first, because the two alkyl groups on a ketone make its carbonyl carbon more electrophilic than an aldehyde carbon","The aldehyde is reduced first because aldehydes are more electrophilic and less sterically hindered than ketones","Neither is reduced; NaBH4 only reduces esters"],"correct":3,"why":"Aldehydes are both more electrophilic (one electron-donating alkyl group vs. two for the ketone) and less sterically hindered, so with a limited amount of NaBH4 the aldehyde is reduced selectively to give the hydroxy-ketone. Ketones are less, not more, electrophilic. The reasoning in the steric distractor is reversed.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-qld0d2","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Aniline is treated with acetic anhydride. The IR of the product shows a band near 3300 cm-1 (one sharp N-H) together with a strong carbonyl band near 1660 cm-1. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["Nc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCNc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Acetic anhydride acylates the more nucleophilic nitrogen, giving the secondary amide acetanilide. IR demands both N-H (~3300, one band) and amide C=O (~1660). N-ethylaniline has N-H but no C=O; phenyl ester and acetophenone have C=O but no N-H; aniline has two N-H bands but no C=O.","traps":null,"qs":"Nc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1djfonx","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Cyclohexene (drawn) is treated with OsO4, then aqueous NaHSO3 workup. OsO4 adds both oxygens to the same face of the double bond through a cyclic osmate ester. Which structure is the product?","opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","C1CCC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCCC1=O"],"correct":2,"why":"OsO4 is syn-dihydroxylation: both OH on the same face = cis-1,2-cyclohexanediol, the meso compound. NaHSO3 is the reductive workup that cleaves the osmate to the diol. Epoxide, mono-ol, and 1,2-diketone come from different reagents.","traps":null,"qs":"C1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-uqdgqy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"1-Phenyl-1,2-epoxypropane (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. Which is the major product?","opts":["COC(c1ccccc1)C(C)O","COC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","OC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)Cc1ccccc1","COC(C)C(OC)c1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Both epoxide carbons are secondary. Under basic conditions the SN2-like attack goes to the less-hindered, non-benzylic CH(CH3); methoxide adds there and OH stays on the benzylic carbon: PhCH(OH)-CH(OCH3)CH3 = COC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1 (option A, connectivity verified). B is the acidic regiochemistry (attack at the benzylic/positive-stabilized carbon, verified), C is the diol, D double-substitutes, E has lost an oxygen.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1OC1c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ty07ll","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The molecule drawn is imidazole, a 5-membered ring with two nitrogens (one =N- pyridine-type and one N-H pyrrole-type). Which statement correctly accounts for its 6 aromatic pi electrons?","opts":["Both nitrogen lone pairs enter the pi system, giving 8 pi electrons, so imidazole is antiaromatic and unusually reactive","Imidazole is non-aromatic because it has two different nitrogens","The two ring double bonds give 4 pi e- and the N-H nitrogen donates its lone pair (2 pi e-) for 6 total; the =N- lone pair stays in-plane and is basic","The =N- nitrogen donates its lone pair into the p system to complete the sextet, while the N-H nitrogen keeps its lone pair in an in-plane orbital and supplies the basicity of the ring","Neither nitrogen contributes a lone pair; imidazole has only 4 pi electrons"],"correct":2,"why":"Imidazole C3H4N2 (valid, aromatic). Two ring double bonds = 4 pi electrons; the pyrrole-type N-H nitrogen donates its lone pair via a p orbital (+2) for 6 total (4n+2). The pyridine-type =N- keeps its lone pair in an in-plane sp2 orbital, which is basic/nucleophilic. Option 3 swaps which nitrogen donates (the common trap) and is wrong. Only option 0 is correct.","traps":null,"qs":"c1cnc[nH]1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-gto0y9","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"A student recrystallizes impure benzoic acid (shown) from hot water. Which choice of recovered material and rationale reflects correct recrystallization technique?","opts":["Collect the hot filtrate immediately and set it aside; the purified product is whatever passes through the fluted filter paper","Choose a solvent in which benzoic acid stays highly soluble even at room temperature, so none of it is lost to the filtrate","Collect the crystals that form on slow cooling; soluble impurities stay dissolved in the cold mother liquor","Evaporate all the solvent to dryness to maximize crystal yield","Cool the solution as fast as possible in ice to trap the most impurities in the crystals"],"correct":2,"why":"Proper recrystallization dissolves the solid in minimal hot solvent, then slow cooling grows pure crystals while soluble impurities remain in the cold mother liquor and are filtered off. Fast cooling traps impurities, an always-soluble solvent prevents crystallization, and evaporating to dryness defeats purification.","traps":null,"qs":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-anc6po","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"(E)-4-Phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone) is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi), then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)(C)/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)/C=C(C)/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Option B adds methyl at the alpha carbon, option C is the 1,2 allylic alcohol (organolithium behavior, alkene retained), option D shows extra carbonyl reduction, and option E retains the alkene (incorrect). Index 0 is correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-xwdwpy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"1-Octyne (drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","opts":["CCCCCCCC=O","CCCCCCC(C)=O","CCCCCCCCO","CCCCCC(=O)CC","C=C(O)CCCCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal carbon, and after oxidation the enol tautomerizes to an aldehyde (octanal). 2-Octanone is the Markovnikov (Hg-catalyzed) product; 3-octanone is wrong regiochemistry; 1-octanol comes from over-reduction/alkene thinking; the enol is the intermediate before tautomerization.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCCCC#C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gntirq","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"(2S,3S)-2,3-dimethyloxirane (a single enantiomer of trans-2,3-epoxybutane) is treated with dilute aqueous acid (H3O+). What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","opts":["CC(O)C(C)O","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](C)O","C[C@H](O)[C@H](C)O","C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","CCC(O)CO"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed anti (backside) opening inverts one stereocenter. From (2S,3S) trans-epoxide, inverting one center gives (2R,3S) = meso-2,3-butanediol. Options 1/2 are the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) diols.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H]1O[C@H]1C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-u5zahn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"(2E,4E)-2,4-hexadiene undergoes a THERMAL 4-pi-electron electrocyclic ring closure (conrotatory). Which structure correctly shows the stereochemistry of the 3,4-dimethylcyclobutene product?","opts":["C[C@H]1[C@H](C)C=C1","C/C=C/C=C/C","CC1CCC1C","CC1=CC1C","C[C@H]1[C@@H](C)C=C1"],"correct":0,"why":"A thermal 4-pi (4n-electron) electrocyclic reaction is CONROTATORY by the Woodward-Hoffmann rules. With both terminal methyls in (2E,4E) geometry, conrotatory closure places the methyls on OPPOSITE faces, giving trans-3,4-dimethylcyclobutene (the chiral, non-meso diastereomer). Distractors: cis (meso) diastereomer (the disrotatory/photochemical outcome), two wrong skeletons, and the unreacted open triene.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/C=C/C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1g5ezpj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"A chemist runs a competitive hydrogenation comparing the relative rates of H2/Pd uptake for the three alkenes below. Based on alkene substitution, which ranks them CORRECTLY from FASTEST to SLOWEST hydrogenation? I = monosubstituted (1-hexene), II = disubstituted (cyclohexene), III = tetrasubstituted (2,3-dimethyl-2-butene).","opts":["I &gt; III &gt; II","II &gt; I &gt; III","III &gt; I &gt; II","I &gt; II &gt; III","III &gt; II &gt; I"],"correct":3,"why":"Heterogeneous hydrogenation rate decreases with increasing alkene substitution (more steric hindrance to flat adsorption on the catalyst; also slightly greater thermodynamic stability). Least-substituted reacts fastest: monosubstituted (I) &gt; disubstituted (II) &gt; tetrasubstituted (III).","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCC=C.C1CCC=CC1.CC(C)=C(C)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-vk6oa3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"The IR spectrum of the molecule shown has a single strong absorption near 1715 cm-1 and shows NO significant broad absorption in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region. Which class of compound is most consistent with these data?","opts":["Ketone","Terminal alkyne","Primary amide","Carboxylic acid","Alcohol"],"correct":0,"why":"Cyclohexanone: strong C=O ~1715 and no O-H or N-H bonds, so nothing broad in 2500-3500. Carboxylic acids/alcohols show broad O-H and amides show N-H bands, so their absence points to a ketone.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1230rhz","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Two structures are relevant: benzene (drawn) and cyclobutadiene. Both are fully conjugated carbocyclic rings, yet they could not be more different in stability. Which classification is correct?","opts":["Benzene is aromatic, and cyclobutadiene is simply non-aromatic, with no special instability beyond that of an ordinary conjugated diene","Benzene is antiaromatic and cyclobutadiene is aromatic","Both are antiaromatic, because an even pi-electron count always produces a 4n ring","Both are aromatic because both are cyclic and conjugated","Benzene (6 pi e-) is aromatic and stable; cyclobutadiene (4 pi e-) is antiaromatic and highly reactive"],"correct":4,"why":"Benzene: planar, cyclic, conjugated, 6 pi electrons (4n+2, n=1) -&gt; aromatic, very stable. Cyclobutadiene: 4 pi electrons (4n, n=1) -&gt; antiaromatic, strongly destabilized and so reactive it dimerizes on formation. Aromatic stabilization vs antiaromatic destabilization is the key contrast. Option 0 only correct.","traps":null,"qs":"c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ic1oiw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A neutral compound has a molecular ion at M+ = 96 and contains only C and H, with the formula C7H12. Which drawn structure is consistent with BOTH its degrees of unsaturation AND the C7H12 formula? (Reminder: C7H12 requires DoU = 2.)","opts":["Cc1ccccc1","C1CC2CCCC2C1","C=C1CCCCC1C","C1CCCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"DoU for C7H12 = (14 + 2 - 12)/2 = 2. 1-Methylcyclohexene (CC1=CCCCC1) is C7H12 with one ring + one C=C = 2 DoU. Toluene is C7H8 (DoU 4). Cycloheptane is C7H14 (DoU 1). The methylene option C=C1CCCCC1C is C8H14 and the fused bicyclic C1CC2CCCC2C1 is C8H14 (bicyclo[3.3.0]octane); both are off-formula. Only the methylcyclohexene matches C7H12 with 2 DoU.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-y8rsnv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanamide is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. The stereocenter is at the carbon alpha to the carbonyl. What is the major organic product, including correct configuration?","opts":["NC[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","O=C[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1C","NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","OC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide to a primary amine (C=O becomes CH2, nitrogen retained), giving 2-phenylpropan-1-amine. Distractor 1 inverts the center (R), the alcohol comes from wrongly treating the amide like an acid, the aldehyde is an incorrect reduction, and the last option adds a ring methyl.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(N)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1nni7r4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (shown) is converted to a product whose IR shows a strong carbonyl absorption near 1715 cm-1 and whose 1H NMR shows a 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm but NO aldehydic proton near 9-10 ppm. Which reagents produce this compound?","opts":["H2, Lindlar catalyst","1. O3, 2. (CH3)2S","1. BH3, 2. H2O2/NaOH","H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","1. Disiamylborane, 2. H2O2/NaOH"],"correct":3,"why":"A 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm plus a carbonyl band and no aldehyde proton identifies a methyl ketone (CH3-C=O), i.e. 1-cyclohexylethanone, which is the Markovnikov hydration product made with Hg2+/H3O+. Hydroboration (options 2,3) would give an aldehyde with a 9-10 ppm CHO; ozonolysis cleaves; Lindlar only gives an alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CC1CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-b28so6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane, shown) reacts extremely slowly in SN2 reactions despite being a primary halide. Why?","opts":["The primary carbon forms a very stable carbocation, diverting the substrate into a slow SN1 pathway instead of SN2","The adjacent quaternary carbon's bulky tert-butyl group sterically blocks backside attack on the primary carbon","Bromide is a poor leaving group on primary carbons","Backside attack is electronically forbidden for primary halides because the carbon has no orbital available to accept the nucleophile","The C-Br bond is unusually strong in neopentyl systems"],"correct":1,"why":"Neopentyl halides are primary but bear a bulky adjacent quaternary carbon that crowds the backside trajectory, making SN2 very slow; a primary cation rules out SN1 too.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)CBr","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-15mt4a5","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"The molecule shown is (R)-1-phenylethanol. Which structure is its ENANTIOMER?","opts":["O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C","OCc1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC","O[C@H](c1ccccc1)C","O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"option B is identical to the stem (R). option C and option D are 1-phenyl-1-propanol (C9, extra CH2, constitutional). option E = 2-phenylethanol (no stereocenter).","traps":null,"qs":"O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17oj8iy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"3-Methylpent-1-ene reacts with HCl. Account for Markovnikov addition and any hydride shift. Which is the major product, and what stereochemistry is expected at the new C-Cl center?","opts":["CCC(C)CCCl","CCC(Cl)CC","CCC(C)C(C)Cl","CCCC(C)(Cl)C","CCC(Cl)(C)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov gives a secondary cation at C2; a 1,2-hydride shift from C3 (tertiary C-H) makes a tertiary cation at C3. Chloride adds to give 3-chloro-3-methylpentane (option A), whose central carbon bears two ethyls, a methyl, and Cl, so it is not a stereocenter (the stereochem part of the prompt resolves to no stereocenter). option B is unrearranged, option C is anti-Markovnikov, option D is a wrong-skeleton C6 isomer; option E is C5H11Cl (missing a carbon). Single correct answer.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"generated:qr-successive-percent#1ff0sqj","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $50 item is marked UP by 20% and then the new price is marked DOWN by 25%. What is the final price?","opts":["$37.50","$45.00","$46.00","$47.50","$60.00"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply the factors: 50 × 1.2 × 0.75 = $45.00. Percents stack by multiplying, never by adding.","traps":["You skipped the mark-up and only applied the discount.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You added and subtracted the percents. The second percent is taken of the NEW price, so multiply the two factors.","You stopped after the mark-up. Apply the mark-down to that new price."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1694","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A line passes through (-8,8) and (-5,5). What is its slope?","opts":["-3","-1","0","1","3"],"correct":1,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = -3/3 = -1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#111f4pw","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 9 runners, in how many ways can 1st through 2nd place be awarded?","opts":["2","18","36","72","81"],"correct":3,"why":"Order matters: 9P2 = 9 × 8 = 72.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 2 people. You still have to choose them from 9.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.","You used a combination. Places are different prizes, so ORDER matters: use a permutation.",null,"You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1178","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A courier made 80 deliveries the first week on a route, 10% more the next week, and 25% more than that the week after. How many deliveries were made in the third week?","opts":["88","90","100","108","110"],"correct":4,"why":"Multiply factors: 80 × 1.1 × 1.25 = 110. Naïvely adding gives 108, which is wrong.","traps":["That is the price after the 10 percent rise, one step short of the answer.","That cuts the first step by 10 percent instead of raising by it.","That applies only the 25 percent rise and leaves the 10 percent out.","You added the two rises into a single 35 percent, and sequential percents multiply instead.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1108","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A crate weighs 24.9 kilograms, and 1.1 kilograms of packing material is taken out. What does the crate weigh now in kilograms?","opts":["2.38","23.8","23.9","26.0","238"],"correct":1,"why":"Line the decimal points up and subtract: 9 tenths minus 1 tenth is 8 tenths, and 24 minus 1 is 23, so the answer is 23.8. Taking a little over 1 away from nearly 25 should land just under 24, which it does.","traps":["You placed the point one spot too far left; the gap between 24.9 and 1.1 is close to 24.",null,"You subtracted 1.0 rather than 1.1, leaving one tenth too much.","You added the two numbers instead of subtracting them.","You dropped the decimal point, which makes the answer ten times too big."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ski1764","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-14","topic":"Test-day execution","nights":[14],"d":1,"q":"You've spent 95 seconds on a probability problem. Best move?","opts":["Mark and skip; return after pass 1","Leave it blank and keep moving forward","Restart from question 1","Guess and move on","Keep working until you solve it"],"correct":0,"why":"Skip after ~90s. Time saved goes toward problems you can solve.","traps":[null,"Leaving it blank gives up a free guess, since wrong answers carry no penalty.","Going back to question 1 spends time on questions that already have answers.","Guessing right now closes a question the return pass could still win.","Past 90 seconds with no progress, one problem usually costs two easier ones."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1255","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies -3x - 2 = x + 10?","opts":["-6","-4","-3","-2","3"],"correct":2,"why":"Move x: (-3-1)x = 10+2 → -4x = 12, so x = -3.","traps":["The x terms were combined as -3 - 1 = -2 instead of -4, leaving -2x = 12.","Substituting -4 gives 10 on the left and 6 on the right.",null,"The -2 was subtracted from the 10 instead of added, giving -4x = 8.","Dividing 12 by -4 gives -3, so this answer drops the minus sign."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1254","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies -3x + 1 = -2x + 7?","opts":["-8","-7","-6","-5","6"],"correct":2,"why":"Move x: (-3+2)x = 7-1 → -x = 6, so x = -6.","traps":["The 1 was added to the 7 instead of subtracted, giving -x = 8.","Substituting -7 gives 22 on the left and 21 on the right.",null,"Substituting -5 gives 16 on the left and 17 on the right.","The last line reads -x = 6, so the sign has to flip when x is freed."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-combined-work#fuu075","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One printer finishes a print run in 5 hours. A second printer does the same job in 20 hours. Working together, how long do they take?","opts":["0.25 hours","25 hours","15 hours","4 hours","12.5 hours"],"correct":3,"why":"Rates add: 1/5 + 1/20 = 1/4 of the job per hour. Time = 4 = 4 hours. Sanity check: it must be less than 5 hours.","traps":["That is the combined RATE (jobs per hour). Flip it to get the time.","You added the times. Adding helpers cannot make the job take longer. Add the RATES: 1/a + 1/b.","You subtracted the times. Rates add when both are working.",null,"You averaged the times. Two working together must be FASTER than the faster one alone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:vol1676","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A steel ball has a radius of 4 millimeters. To the nearest hundredth, what is its volume in cubic millimeters?","opts":["50.27","67.02","201.06","268.08","804.25"],"correct":3,"why":"V = (4/3)πr³ = 268.08.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1657","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Three cardboard strips measure 4, 3, and 1 centimeters. Can they be arranged into a triangle?","opts":["Only if right triangle","Cannot determine","Only if it is obtuse","Yes","No"],"correct":4,"why":"|4−3| = 1 and 4+3 = 7. 1 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["There is no angle to open up: the two shorter sides lie flat along the 4, tying it rather than beating it.","The three lengths settle it: 3 plus 1 equals 4, and the sum has to be greater than the longest side, not equal to it.","Nothing bends into an obtuse corner: 3 and 1 lie flat along the 4.","3 and 1 add to exactly 4, so they lie flat along the longest side instead of rising above it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1500","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"The sum of a number and 3 times the number is 40. Find the number.","opts":["9","10","11","13","30"],"correct":1,"why":"The number itself counts as one piece, so a number plus 3 times the number is 4 of them, not 3. That gives 4x = 40 and x = 10. Any time a sum holds the number and a multiple of it, add the 1 for the number before you divide.","traps":["Four 9s make 36, four short of the 40 given.",null,"Four 11s make 44, four past the 40 in the stem.","Divided 40 by 3 and rounded, which leaves out the number itself as one of the pieces.","That is 3 times the number, the larger piece of the sum, not the number itself."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:st4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"At a college, 25 students take biology and 20 take chemistry, and 35 students take at least one of the two. How many take both?","opts":["5","10","15","20","45"],"correct":1,"why":"Rearrange inclusion-exclusion: |A ∩ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∪ B| = 25 + 20 − 35 = 10.","traps":["You took the difference between the two set sizes instead of working back from the union.",null,"You subtracted |B| from the union, which gives the part of A outside the overlap.","That is |B| on its own, which would only be the overlap if all of B sat inside A.","That's the sum of |A| + |B|."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-missing-value-mean#1y7ihef","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A student scored 82, 88, 86 on 3 quizzes. What score is needed on the next quiz to bring the average to exactly 88?","opts":["8","88","91","96","100"],"correct":3,"why":"Needed total = 88 × 4 = 352. Current total = 256. Next score = 352 − 256 = 96.","traps":["You used 3 quizzes. After the next one there are 4.","Scoring the target only works if you were already AT the target. You have to make up the gap on every earlier quiz too.","You averaged the old average with the new score. The old average counts for several quizzes, not one.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Required total = target × number of quizzes; subtract what you already have."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1027","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"What is the sum of 9/15 and 10/14?","opts":["46/35","50/35","19/29","97/70","90/210"],"correct":0,"why":"LCD = 210. 9/15 = 126/210, 10/14 = 150/210. Combine: 276/210 = 46/35.","traps":[null,"You multiplied each top by its own bottom instead of by the other fraction's bottom.","You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 19 over 29.","One of the rescalings slipped; in seventieths the pieces are 42 and 50, which total 92/70.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1418","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A photo lab combines 200 mL of a 10% developer solution with 150 mL of a 20% developer solution. What is the approximate percent concentration of the combined solution?","opts":["14.29","15","15.71","50","52.5"],"correct":0,"why":"Solute total = 20 + 30 = 50 mL in 350 mL → 14.29%.","traps":[null,"Averaged the two percents without weighting them by how much of each solution went in.","Paired each percent with the other solution's volume, putting the 20 percent on the 200 mL pour.","That is the total milliliters of solute, not the percent of the mixture.","Took 15 percent, the plain average of the two strengths, of the 350 mL total, which gives a volume rather than a concentration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1600","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"How many real roots does x² − 4x + 1 = 0 have?","opts":["no real roots","one real and one imaginary root","one real root","two real roots","two imaginary roots"],"correct":3,"why":"Δ = b² − 4ac = 16 − 4·1·1 = 12. &gt; 0, so two.","traps":["A discriminant of 12 is positive, so both roots are real, at 2 + √3 and 2 − √3.","Complex roots come in conjugate pairs, so one real and one imaginary is not possible.","One root needs a discriminant of zero, and 16 − 4 is 12.",null,"Imaginary roots require a negative discriminant, and this one is positive."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-successive-percent#1gsyt7e","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $200 item is marked UP by 10% and then the new price is marked DOWN by 25%. What is the final price?","opts":["$150.00","$165.00","$170.00","$220.00","$247.50"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply the factors: 200 × 1.1 × 0.75 = $165.00. Percents stack by multiplying, never by adding.","traps":["You skipped the mark-up and only applied the discount.",null,"You added and subtracted the percents. The second percent is taken of the NEW price, so multiply the two factors.","You stopped after the mark-up. Apply the mark-down to that new price.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#2mgckx","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A car travels at 30 miles per hour. What is its speed in feet per second? (1 mile = 5280 ft)","opts":["20.5 ft/s","0.00833 ft/s","1.58 × 10⁵ ft/s","2640 ft/s","44 ft/s"],"correct":4,"why":"30 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi ÷ 3600 s/h = 44 ft/s. Handy anchor: 60 mph is 88 ft/s.","traps":["Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so miles and hours cancel.","That is miles per second. Multiply by 5280 to get feet.","That is feet per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp005","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A right triangle has legs of length 3 and 4 (and hypotenuse 5). It is inscribed in a CIRCLE such that the hypotenuse is a diameter. What is the area of the region INSIDE the circle but OUTSIDE the triangle?","opts":["25π − 6","6 − (25π/4)","5π − 12","(25π/2) − 6","(25π/4) − 6"],"correct":4,"why":"Hypotenuse = diameter = 5, so radius = 2.5. Circle area = π(2.5)² = 25π/4. Triangle area = (1/2)(3)(4) = 6. Region = 25π/4 − 6.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:pan1541","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"events: P(A) = 0.4, P(B) = 0.4, P(A and B) = 0.11. P(A or B)?","opts":["0","0.16","0.69","0.8","0.91"],"correct":2,"why":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) = 0.4 + 0.4 − 0.11 = 0.69.","traps":["0 subtracts one probability from the other, and the or rule only removes the overlap.","0.16 multiplies 0.4 by 0.4, and the problem gives the overlap as 0.11.",null,"0.8 adds the two probabilities and leaves the 0.11 overlap in twice.","0.91 adds the 0.11 overlap instead of subtracting it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:int1432","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the final amount when $2000 earns 4.5% interest compounded annually for 2 years?","opts":["180","2000.09","2090","2180","2184.05"],"correct":4,"why":"A = P(1+r)^t = 2000·(1.045)^2 = 2184.05.","traps":["That is the simple interest for two years, not the ending amount.","Added rt as nine cents instead of as 9 percent of the 2000.","Grew the balance for one year instead of two.","Used simple interest, so year two never earned interest on year one's interest.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1639","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A delivery fleet averages 50 drop-offs a day with a standard deviation of 10. What is the z score of a day with 35 drop-offs?","opts":["-15","-1.5","-0.67","-0.3","1.5"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (35 − 50)/10 = -1.5.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, one step short of dividing by the standard deviation.",null,"Flipped the ratio and divided 10 by the 15-point gap.","Divided the 15-point gap by the mean of 50 instead of by the standard deviation of 10.","Right size, wrong side: 35 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#y9j3s6","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 7 runners, in how many ways can 1st through 3rd place be awarded?","opts":["6","21","35","210","343"],"correct":3,"why":"Order matters: 7P3 = 7 × 6 × 5 = 210.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 3 people. You still have to choose them from 7.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.","You used a combination. Places are different prizes, so ORDER matters: use a permutation.",null,"You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-combined-work#faust7","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One printer finishes a print run in 2 hours. A second printer does the same job in 6 hours. Working together, how long do they take?","opts":["1.5 hours","0.667 hours","4 hours","8 hours","2.25 hours"],"correct":0,"why":"Rates add: 1/2 + 1/6 = 2/3 of the job per hour. Time = 3/2 = 1.5 hours. Sanity check: it must be less than 2 hours.","traps":[null,"That is the combined RATE (jobs per hour). Flip it to get the time.","You averaged the times. Two working together must be FASTER than the faster one alone.","You added the times. Adding helpers cannot make the job take longer. Add the RATES: 1/a + 1/b.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1136","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"What is 42 percent of 476?","opts":["19.99","199.92","276.08","518","19992"],"correct":1,"why":"42% of 476 = 42/100 × 476 = 199.92.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.",null,"You found the 58 percent left over rather than the 42 percent the question asks for.","You added the 42 to the 476 instead of taking 42 percent of it.","You multiplied 42 by 476 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1739","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"A thermostat is set to 10 degrees, and the room temperature of x degrees is exactly 4 degrees away from that setting. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["14 or 6","4 or -4","14","6 or -14","-6 or 6"],"correct":0,"why":"|x − 10| = 4 means x − 10 = ±4, so x = 14 or 6.","traps":[null,"You set x equal to plus or minus 4 and never undid the minus 10 inside the bars.","You solved x - 10 = 4 and stopped, and the branch x - 10 = -4 gives 6 as well.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","That pairs 6 with its negative, and the other branch is 10 plus 4, which is 14."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1idv8g5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"Given that sin 25° = 0.423, what is cos 65°?","opts":["-0.423","0.179","0.423","0.577","0.906"],"correct":2,"why":"The two angles add to 90 degrees, and the cosine of an angle equals the sine of its complement, so cos 65 is exactly sin 25. That makes the value 0.423 with no calculation at all. Any time a problem pairs an angle with its complement and switches between sine and cosine, the number carries straight across.","traps":["This attaches a negative sign, but 65 degrees is acute so every basic ratio there is positive.","This squares 0.423, which gives sin squared of 25 rather than any cosine.",null,"This subtracts the sine from 1, but the Pythagorean identity works on squares, not on the raw values.","This is sin 65, which pairs the wrong two values, and it is also what you get by running the Pythagorean identity on 25 degrees instead of using the complement."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#qcdpvt","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the PRODUCT of the solutions of x² + x − 2 = 0?","opts":["3","2","1","−2","−1"],"correct":3,"why":"It factors as (x − 1)(x + 2), so the roots are 1 and −2. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = −1, product = c/a = −2.","traps":["That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their product.","Sign slip. The product of the roots is c/a with its sign kept.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.",null,"That is the SUM of the roots (−b/a). The product is c/a."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1735","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A target reading is 10, and an actual reading of x differs from the target by exactly 7. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["17","-3 or -17","17 or -3","-3 or 3","17 or 3"],"correct":4,"why":"|x − 10| = 7 means x − 10 = ±7, so x = 17 or 3.","traps":["You solved x - 10 = 7 and stopped, and the branch x - 10 = -7 gives 3 as well.","You moved the 10 to the wrong side and subtracted it instead of adding it back.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","That pairs 3 with its negative, and the other branch is 10 plus 7, which is 17.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1497","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Half of a number, increased by 5, equals 13.5. What is the number?","opts":["8.5","16","17","18","21"],"correct":2,"why":"Set it up as x/2 plus 5 = 13.5. Subtract the 5 for x/2 = 8.5, then double for 17. A half in the total is a signal that the answer will be odd, so resist rounding before the doubling.","traps":["Stopped at the half: 13.5 minus 5 leaves 8.5, which is half the number.","Rounded the 8.5 down to 8 before doubling, which loses the odd half.",null,"Rounded the 8.5 up to 9 before doubling, which pushes the number one past.","Half of 21 is 10.5, and 10.5 plus 5 is 15.5, above the 13.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1495","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Five trays each hold one full set of instruments, but 5 instruments are missing overall, leaving 70 in total. How many instruments are in a full set?","opts":["13","14","15","16","20"],"correct":2,"why":"'Five less than 5 times a number' becomes 5x minus 5 = 70. Add the 5 to both sides for 5x = 75, then divide by 5 for 15. Undo the addition or subtraction first and the multiplication second.","traps":["Moved the 5 the wrong way: 70 minus 5 is 65, and 65 split five ways gives this.","Divided 70 by 5 and stopped there, leaving the subtracted 5 unaccounted for.",null,"Added the 5 back twice: 70 plus 5 plus 5 is 80, and 80 split five ways gives this.","Five 20s make 100, well past the 75 that 5x has to equal."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#87b900","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (6 × 10⁴)(5 × 10¹) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["30 × 10⁵","3 × 10⁵","3 × 10⁴","1.1 × 10⁶","3 × 10⁶"],"correct":4,"why":"6 × 5 = 30 and 10⁴ × 10¹ = 10⁵. 30 × 10⁵ is not proper form, so write 3 × 10⁶. Answer: 3 × 10⁶.","traps":["Right size, wrong form. In proper scientific notation the front number sits between 1 and 10.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:rm5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Concrete mix is 1:2:4 (cement:sand:gravel). For 14 cu ft total, how much sand?","opts":["4 cu ft","2 cu ft","3.5 cu ft","7 cu ft","8 cu ft"],"correct":0,"why":"Total parts = 7. 14/7 = 2/part. Sand = 2 × 2 = 4.","traps":[null,"That is the cement share, one part of the mix at 2 cu ft per part, while sand is two parts.","You divided the 14 by 4 rather than by the 7 total parts, so each part came out too large.","You split the 14 in half instead of dividing it into the 7 parts that 1:2:4 calls for.","That is the gravel share, four parts at 2 cu ft each; sand is the middle number in 1:2:4."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1022","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"An auditor finishes 3/12 of a file on the first day and 6/8 of it on the second. What fraction of the file is done?","opts":["9/20","1/2","1","18/96","9/8"],"correct":2,"why":"Reduce first: 3/12 is 1/4 and 6/8 is 3/4. A quarter plus three quarters is exactly one whole. When both fractions simplify, do that before reaching for a common denominator.","traps":["You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 9 over 20.","You subtracted instead of adding; three quarters minus a quarter is a half.",null,"You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You left the 3 on top when moving 3/12 into eighths; three twelfths is a quarter, which is 2/8."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#xrv1ml","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A car travels at 15 miles per hour. What is its speed in feet per second? (1 mile = 5280 ft)","opts":["22 ft/s","79200 ft/s","0.00417 ft/s","10.2 ft/s","1320 ft/s"],"correct":0,"why":"15 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi ÷ 3600 s/h = 22 ft/s. Handy anchor: 60 mph is 88 ft/s.","traps":[null,"That is feet per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","That is miles per second. Multiply by 5280 to get feet.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so miles and hours cancel.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:fa8","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A tank holds 3/4 of a quart of coolant, and a technician drains 1/3 of a quart then adds 1/6 of a quart. What fraction of a quart is in the tank?","opts":["7/12","11/12","5/12","15/12","3/12"],"correct":0,"why":"LCD = 12. 9/12 − 4/12 + 2/12 = 7/12.","traps":[null,"You flipped both signs after the first term, adding the 1/3 and subtracting the 1/6.","You stopped after 3/4 minus 1/3 and never added the last sixth.","You added all three terms instead of subtracting the middle one.","You subtracted the last term instead of adding it, 9/12 minus 4/12 minus 2/12."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1595","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"How many real roots does 2x² + 4 = 0 have?","opts":["two imaginary roots","two real roots","no real roots","one real and one imaginary root","one real root"],"correct":2,"why":"Δ = b² − 4ac = 0 − 4·2·4 = -32. &lt; 0, so none.","traps":["That pair is complex, and the count the question wants is of real roots.","With b = 0 the discriminant is just −4ac, which is −32.",null,"Complex roots come in pairs for real coefficients, so one of each is not possible.","One real root needs a discriminant of zero, and here it is −32."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#hzr2b","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 6 red and 4 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["3/10","9/25","3/5","5/9","1/3"],"correct":4,"why":"First red: 6/10. Second red: 5/9. Multiply: 1/3.","traps":["You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble.","That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.","That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#1a1rbm5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 60 miles at 50 mph and returns the same 60 miles at 75 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["68.8 mph","61.2 mph","62.5 mph","60 mph","30 mph"],"correct":3,"why":"Time out = 60/50 = 1.2 h, time back = 60/75 = 0.8 h. Average = 120 / 2 = 60 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":["Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.","You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.",null,"You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261005"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s11"}},{"id":"FULL_6","name":"Full-Length Test 6","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"written:bio-3-pw61ua","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Cholesterol sits wedged between phospholipids in an animal cell membrane. At body temperature it reduces fluidity, and at low temperature it keeps the membrane from stiffening. The best description of its role is:","opts":["It buffers fluidity against temperature change","It makes the membrane permanently more fluid","It makes the membrane permanently more rigid","It anchors the membrane to the cytoskeleton beneath it","It seals the membrane so that water cannot cross"],"correct":0,"why":"Cholesterol does not have one direction of effect, it has one job: holding fluidity steady. A warm membrane gets too loose and cholesterol restrains the tails, a cold membrane gets too ordered and cholesterol wedges in to stop tight packing. If a choice says cholesterol only increases or only decreases fluidity, half the temperature story is missing.","traps":[null,"This captures only the cold-temperature half of the effect.","This captures only the warm-temperature half of the effect.","Anchoring is the job of membrane proteins that bind cytoskeletal filaments, not of cholesterol.","Water still crosses the bilayer and moves through aquaporins, cholesterol does not seal it out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-p9zot1","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cytotoxic T cell presses Fas ligand against the Fas receptor on the surface of a target cell, and the target cell dies by apoptosis. Which pathway did that use, and which caspase is switched on first?","opts":["The intrinsic pathway, working through a death receptor, activating caspase-3 first","The extrinsic pathway, working through cytochrome c release, activating caspase-9","Neither pathway, since Fas binding produces necrosis rather than apoptosis","The intrinsic pathway, working through the mitochondrion, activating caspase-9","The extrinsic pathway, working through a death receptor, activating caspase-8"],"correct":4,"why":"Sort these by where the signal comes from. A ligand binding a death receptor on the outside of the cell is the extrinsic pathway, and it activates caspase-8 right at the membrane. Trouble arising inside the cell, such as DNA damage that cannot be repaired, is the intrinsic pathway, working through the mitochondrion and caspase-9. Both funnel into the same executioner caspases, so the only thing you have to hold separate is the trigger and the initiator.","traps":["Death receptors do sit at the start, but that is what makes it extrinsic, and caspase-3 is an executioner activated further down either route.","The pathway name is right and cytochrome c belongs to the other route, because the extrinsic pathway starts at the membrane rather than the mitochondrion.","Fas signaling is a controlled program with every hallmark of apoptosis, not the uncontrolled rupture of necrosis.","The intrinsic pathway begins with a problem inside the cell, and this signal arrived from outside through a surface receptor.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g077","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Early spontaneous abortion is most often associated with which kind of genetic abnormality?","opts":["Translocations","Trisomies","Triploidy, from two sperm fertilizing one egg","Point mutations","Inversions"],"correct":1,"why":"Most early pregnancy losses with an identified genetic cause are chromosomal, and among those the autosomal trisomies are the largest group. Think about why: an extra whole chromosome unbalances the dosage of hundreds of genes at once, which is far more disruptive than a single base change. When a stem asks what is most often behind early loss, reach for whole chromosome errors first.","traps":["Translocations do cause loss, particularly when a balanced carrier makes unbalanced gametes, but they account for far fewer cases than trisomies.",null,"Triploidy does cause pregnancy loss, but it accounts for far fewer cases than the autosomal trisomies as a group.","A single base change usually affects one protein, which is rarely enough on its own to end a pregnancy.","An inversion keeps all of the genetic material present, so carriers are often completely unaffected."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g059","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A newborn is diagnosed with Edwards syndrome. Which chromosomal abnormality accounts for it? In the choices below, T followed by a number means trisomy of that chromosome.","opts":["A deletion on chromosome 18","XXY","T13","T18","T21"],"correct":3,"why":"Edwards syndrome is three copies of chromosome 18. The fastest way to keep this set straight is to memorize the three numbers as a block, 21 for Down, 18 for Edwards, 13 for Patau, and then read the number in each choice rather than the name. The choice naming trisomy 18 is the one.","traps":["This has the right chromosome but the wrong mechanism, since Edwards syndrome is an extra whole copy of 18 rather than a missing segment of it.","The 47,XXY karyotype is Klinefelter syndrome, a sex chromosome condition rather than an autosomal trisomy.","Trisomy 13 is Patau syndrome, the most severe of the three.",null,"Trisomy 21 is Down syndrome."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-jwpv4p","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Bread dough rises because the yeast in it is fermenting. Which products of that pathway explain both the rise and the alcohol that bakes off?","opts":["Lactate and water","Ethanol and carbon dioxide","Ethanol and oxygen","Pyruvate and hydrogen gas","Acetyl-CoA and carbon dioxide"],"correct":1,"why":"Alcoholic fermentation takes two steps. Pyruvate loses a carbon as carbon dioxide to become acetaldehyde, and then acetaldehyde accepts the electrons from NADH to become ethanol, which regenerates NAD+. The gas is what lifts the dough, and it is also the clean point of contrast with lactate fermentation, which releases no gas at all.","traps":["Lactate fermentation releases no gas, so nothing would inflate the dough.",null,"Fermentation consumes no oxygen and produces none, since oxygen is exactly what the cell has run out of.","Pyruvate is the starting material for fermentation rather than its end product, and yeast release no hydrogen gas.","Acetyl-CoA is an aerobic intermediate that stays inside mitochondria and never piles up as a product."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1wnmho1","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"DNA polymerase cannot begin a strand from bare template. Something else must lay down a short starter that polymerase can then extend. What is that starter made of, and what happens to it later?","opts":["A short DNA piece made by ligase, which is then left permanently in place","A short protein tag made by helicase, later stripped off by the polymerase","A short RNA piece made by primase, later removed and replaced with DNA","A short DNA piece made by telomerase, later extended into the whole strand","A short RNA piece made by RNA polymerase II, later spliced out as an intron"],"correct":2,"why":"The point here is that the starter is RNA and not DNA, and that is not an accident. Primase can begin a chain from nothing, polymerase cannot, so the RNA primer hands polymerase the free 3' hydroxyl it needs. The RNA is later excised, the gap filled with DNA, and the nick sealed. If a question mentions something that is put down during replication and then removed and replaced, it is the primer.","traps":["Ligase joins pieces of DNA that already exist and cannot build a new one, and a DNA starter would not need removing in the first place.","Helicase unwinds DNA and makes no starter of any kind, and polymerase extends nucleic acid rather than protein.",null,"Telomerase does extend DNA, but only at chromosome ends and only from its own RNA template, so it is not what starts an ordinary fragment.","RNA polymerase II transcribes messenger RNA, and splicing removes introns from a transcript, which is a separate process from replication."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g092","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following best defines recombinant DNA?","opts":["DNA combined from different sources (e.g., human gene in plasmid)","DNA from same species","DNA produced by crossing over between homologous chromosomes during meiosis","Natural DNA","Mutated DNA"],"correct":0,"why":"Recombinant DNA is a molecule built in the laboratory out of pieces that were not joined before, classically a gene cut out with a restriction enzyme and sealed into a plasmid vector with DNA ligase. The test is whether separate pieces were deliberately joined, not whether the sequence was altered. Human insulin produced by bacteria carrying a human gene is the standard example.","traps":[null,"Pieces from a single species can be joined too, so naming the species misses the point, which is that separate pieces were joined artificially.","Crossing over is natural genetic recombination between chromosomes in meiosis, while recombinant DNA refers to pieces joined from separate sources in the laboratory.","Recombinant molecules are constructed by hand, which is the opposite of DNA left exactly as it is found.","A mutation changes bases inside one molecule, and nothing new has been joined in, so no recombinant molecule has been made."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-idaha0","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A yeast culture is kept strictly anaerobic and fed 5 molecules of glucose. Every glucose runs through glycolysis and then alcoholic fermentation. How much ATP does the culture net?","opts":["150 ATP","5 ATP","20 ATP","10 ATP","35 ATP"],"correct":3,"why":"Fermentation contributes no ATP of its own, so the entire yield is the net 2 ATP per glucose from glycolysis. Five glucose at 2 ATP each is 10. When a question puts a cell in anaerobic conditions, reset your count to glycolysis alone and leave the electron carriers out of it.","traps":["This is roughly the aerobic yield of about 30 per glucose, which requires oxygen and the full chain.","This counts one ATP per glucose, but the net from glycolysis is 2 per glucose.","This uses the gross figure of 4 ATP per glucose and forgets the 2 invested up front.",null,"This cashes in the 2 NADH per glucose at 2.5 ATP each, but with no oxygen there is no chain to cash them in on."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p082","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Vaccination protects a person against a later infection primarily through which mechanism?","opts":["Strengthening innate barriers such as skin, mucus, and stomach acid","Direct antibody injection","Killing pathogens directly","Inducing memory B/T cells","Antibiotics"],"correct":3,"why":"A vaccine is a rehearsal. It presents antigen without causing real disease, so the B and T cells that recognize that antigen expand and leave behind long lived memory cells, and a later real exposure then meets a secondary response that is faster, larger, and mostly IgG. Active immunity like this takes a week or two to build but it lasts, so the choice about inducing memory cells is what a vaccine actually does.","traps":["Innate barriers are already in place and are not antigen specific, so strengthening them could not explain protection against one particular pathogen.","Injecting antibodies is passive immunity, which works within hours but fades in weeks and leaves no memory behind.","A vaccine contains nothing that kills a pathogen; it presents antigen so that your own cells learn the target.",null,"Antibiotics treat a bacterial infection already underway and do nothing for viruses or for a future exposure."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e001","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In regions where malaria is common, the sickle cell allele remains at a far higher frequency than its severe effect in homozygotes would predict. Which type of selection maintains it?","opts":["Disruptive","Stabilizing selection, which narrows variation around an intermediate value","Balancing (heterozygote advantage)","Directional selection","Drift"],"correct":2,"why":"Work through the three genotypes. Two sickle alleles cause severe disease, two normal alleles leave a person vulnerable to malaria, and the heterozygote resists malaria while staying healthy enough to reproduce. When the heterozygote is the fittest genotype, selection cannot push either allele out of the population, so both are held at a stable frequency. That is the signature of balancing selection through heterozygote advantage.","traps":["Disruptive selection favors both extremes over the middle, whereas here the middle genotype, the heterozygote, is the favored one.","Stabilizing selection trims a trait toward an intermediate value and reduces variation, while here two alleles are both being preserved in the population.",null,"Directional selection drives a population toward one extreme and would carry one allele to fixation, yet both alleles persist here.","Drift is random change and is strongest in small populations, but this pattern maps closely onto where malaria occurs, which points to selection rather than chance."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e013","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Dogs that repeatedly heard a bell just before receiving food eventually began to salivate at the sound of the bell alone. This result illustrates:","opts":["Classical conditioning","Insight","An unlearned reflex present from birth","Habituation","Operant conditioning"],"correct":0,"why":"Classical conditioning takes a reflex the animal already has and attaches it to a new trigger. Food causes salivation with no learning at all, so food is the unconditioned stimulus and that salivation is the unconditioned response. Pairing the bell with food often enough turns the bell into a conditioned stimulus that draws out the same salivation, now a conditioned response. The test is that the animal is passive: nothing it chooses to do changes the outcome, and that is what separates this from operant conditioning.","traps":[null,"Insight is sudden problem solving without trial and error, and a reflex response to a tone is not problem solving.","Salivating to food is the unlearned reflex, but salivating to a bell appears only after the bell has been paired with food, which makes it learned.","Habituation is a response fading away to a repeated harmless stimulus, the opposite of a new response being built.","Operant conditioning requires the animal's own action to produce a consequence, but the dogs were not doing anything to earn the food."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-iupkei","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Researchers grow algae in water whose oxygen atoms carry a heavy isotope label, and feed them ordinary unlabeled carbon dioxide. Where does the label first show up?","opts":["In the chlorophyll of the algae's thylakoid membranes","In the oxygen gas bubbling off the algae","In the carbon dioxide the algae take in from the air","In the glucose the algae build","In the phosphate groups of newly made ATP"],"correct":1,"why":"The real question is which reactant donates the oxygen atoms in the gas. Water is the molecule torn apart at photosystem II, so the oxygen released came from water, while carbon dioxide's oxygen ends up in sugar. When a labeling experiment confuses you, follow the atom rather than the molecule.","traps":["Chlorophyll was built long before the experiment began and is not remade out of the labeled water.",null,"Carbon dioxide is a starting material taken from the air, not a product that could collect the label.","The oxygen atoms in glucose trace back to carbon dioxide, which was deliberately left unlabeled here.","ATP is assembled from ADP and phosphate already sitting in the stroma, not from the atoms of the split water."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e025","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Gause's competitive exclusion principle states that:","opts":["Competition leads to extinction","Two species with identical niches cannot coexist long-term","Two species can always coexist","Mutualism wins","Two species whose niches overlap at all cannot coexist in the same habitat"],"correct":1,"why":"This principle is about a limiting resource, not about species disliking each other. If two species need exactly the same thing and one is even slightly better at obtaining it, that small edge compounds generation after generation until the other is pushed out of the habitat. What you usually observe in nature instead is resource partitioning, where the two shift to slightly different niches, different perch heights, feeding times, or prey sizes, and both persist. So when a stem shows two species coexisting on an overlapping resource, look for how they divide it.","traps":["The loser is excluded from that habitat, which usually means local displacement rather than the species going extinct everywhere.",null,"This ignores what happens when two species depend on the same limiting resource and nothing separates them.","Mutualism is a separate kind of interaction and does not determine the outcome of competition for one resource.","Partial overlap is common and stable; the principle only bites when the niches are effectively identical on the limiting resource, and partitioning handles the rest."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p048","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Once an action potential has been triggered, how does it propagate along an unmyelinated axon?","opts":["Membrane shifting","By saltatory conduction, with the signal jumping from one node of Ranvier to the next","Sequential opening of voltage-gated channels along axon","Bulk flow","Diffusion through axon"],"correct":2,"why":"An action potential is not an object traveling down the axon, it is a wave that gets rebuilt over and over. Sodium entering at one patch of membrane depolarizes the neighboring patch past threshold, that patch opens its own voltage-gated channels, and the sequence repeats to the end. Because every patch regenerates the full spike, the signal arrives at the terminal at exactly the strength it started with.","traps":["The membrane itself stays put; what moves is the wave of channel opening across it.","Saltatory conduction requires a myelin sheath with gaps between segments, so it cannot describe an axon that carries no myelin.",null,"Bulk flow moves fluid through vessels in circulatory and plant transport systems, not charge along a membrane.","Ions diffusing along the inside of the axon would spread out and fade with distance, and the signal survives only because channels regenerate it at each step."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p090","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Sweat glands are distributed across most of the body surface, but they are most densely concentrated in which region?","opts":["Back","Eyelids","Lips","Spread at the same density over the entire body surface","Palms, soles, axillae"],"correct":4,"why":"Where sweat glands sit follows what each patch of skin is for. They are densest on the palms, soles, and axillae, which is why nervous sweat shows up in the hands first, and the palms and soles pair that with thick skin and friction ridges for grip. The choice naming palms, soles, and axillae is the concentrated set.","traps":["The back does carry sweat glands, but at a much lower density than the palms and soles.","Eyelid skin is the thinnest on the body and carries few sweat glands, with its glands mostly oil producing along the lid margin.","The lip margin has no sweat glands and no hair follicles, which is part of why lips dry out so easily.","Density varies widely by region, from several hundred glands per square centimeter on the palm down to far fewer on the trunk and limbs.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-voh2c9","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"In that same pathway, what becomes of diacylglycerol after phospholipase C cleaves PIP2?","opts":["It stays in the plasma membrane and helps activate protein kinase C","It is converted into cAMP","It opens calcium channels on the endoplasmic reticulum","It diffuses into the cytoplasm and binds a receptor that enters the nucleus","It is exported from the cell as a paracrine signal"],"correct":0,"why":"DAG keeps its fatty acid tails, so it cannot go anywhere: it stays in the bilayer. Protein kinase C is recruited to the membrane, where DAG together with calcium switches it on. The C in phospholipase C and the C in protein kinase C travel together, which is a handy way to remember the handoff.","traps":[null,"cAMP comes from ATP by way of adenylyl cyclase, which is a separate pathway.","Opening those calcium channels is the job of IP3, the water-soluble half.","Its tails keep it anchored in the membrane, and receptors that shuttle to the nucleus belong to the steroid story.","DAG acts inside the cell that made it rather than being secreted."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d012","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A lichen growing on bare rock is best described as which of the following?","opts":["Mutualism between fungus + alga/cyanobacterium","Slime molds","Parasitic plants","A fungus that has evolved the ability to photosynthesize on its own","A single kingdom"],"correct":0,"why":"Think of a lichen as a house and a tenant. The fungus builds the structure, holds water and grips the rock, and the photosynthetic partner living inside, an alga or a cyanobacterium, pays rent in sugar. Both partners gain, which makes this mutualism rather than parasitism. It is also why lichens colonize bare stone, where neither partner could survive alone.","traps":[null,"Slime molds are protists that creep and engulf food as a mass of cytoplasm, with no fungal partner and no photosynthetic partner.","Nothing in a lichen is a plant, and the fungus is not drawing nutrients from a host plant the way mistletoe does.","The photosynthesis in a lichen is done by the algal or cyanobacterial partner housed inside the fungal body; no fungus makes its own sugar.","A lichen is not a kingdom; it is two organisms from different kingdoms living together as one working body."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p070","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Intercalated discs join neighboring cardiac muscle cells. Which structures do these discs contain?","opts":["Synapses","Z-lines only","Gap junctions + desmosomes","Tight junctions only","Motor end plates that let a nerve stimulate each cell individually"],"correct":2,"why":"This is asking how the heart manages to contract as one unit. Intercalated discs do two jobs at once: gap junctions form pores that let ions and the action potential flow straight from one cell into the next, and desmosomes together with fascia adherens glue the cells so they do not tear apart while pulling. The choice naming both gap junctions and desmosomes covers the electrical half and the mechanical half, which is what makes cardiac muscle a functional syncytium.","traps":["No synapse sits between heart cells; current passes cell to cell directly, which is why the heart does not need a nerve reaching every fiber.","Z lines anchor the thin filaments at the ends of each sarcomere inside a cell, while intercalated discs join one whole cell to the next.",null,"Tight junctions seal sheets of epithelium against leaks, while heart cells need electrical coupling and mechanical anchoring instead.","Cardiac muscle is not driven by an end plate on each cell; the sinoatrial node fires and the signal spreads through the coupled network on its own."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p031","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Cardiac output equals stroke volume multiplied by which quantity?","opts":["Blood pressure","Ejection fraction","Heart rate","Hematocrit","Vessel diameter"],"correct":2,"why":"Cardiac output is how much blood the heart delivers each minute, so it has to combine how much leaves per beat with how many beats happen per minute. Stroke volume times heart rate gives volume per minute, and a typical resting value is about 70 milliliters times 70 beats per minute, close to 5 liters per minute. When a cardiac formula gets confusing, check the units, since the pairing that yields volume per minute is the right one.","traps":["Blood pressure is what results when output meets resistance in the vessels, not a factor inside the output formula.","Ejection fraction is the share of the filled ventricle that gets ejected, so it helps determine stroke volume rather than multiplying it.",null,"Hematocrit describes what fraction of blood is red cells, which affects viscosity and oxygen carrying rather than volume pumped.","Vessel diameter sets resistance downstream and says nothing about how much blood the heart ejects per minute."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d042","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Phloem is responsible for transporting which substance, and in which direction?","opts":["O₂","Sucrose in one direction only, always downward from the leaves to the roots","Hormones only","Water + minerals","Sugars (sucrose) from source to sink"],"correct":4,"why":"This separates the two plumbing systems of a plant. Phloem carries dissolved sugar, mainly sucrose, from wherever it is made or stored, the source, to wherever it is used or stored, the sink, driven by pressure flow. Because source and sink change with the season and with the stage of growth, phloem can run either way, and that is the detail that separates it from the one-way upward pull of xylem.","traps":["Oxygen moves by diffusion through air spaces and stomata rather than through a transport tissue.","Direction follows wherever the source and sink sit, so sugar can move up to a growing fruit or shoot just as readily as down to a root.","Hormones can travel in phloem sap, but sugar is the bulk cargo, so limiting the tissue to hormones misses its main job.","Water and dissolved minerals travel in xylem, pulled upward from the roots by transpiration.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g030","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-9","topic":"Mendelian genetics","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"In genetics, pleiotropy refers to which situation?","opts":["Sex linkage","Both alleles of a gene are fully and separately expressed in a heterozygote","Many genes one trait","Lethal alleles","One gene many traits"],"correct":4,"why":"Pleiotropy is one gene reaching into many traits, which happens whenever a single protein is used in several tissues or pathways. Sickle cell disease is the standard illustration, where one change in beta globin produces anemia, pain crises, spleen damage, and more. Read the word as one cause with many effects.","traps":["Sex linkage describes where a gene sits, on the X or the Y, rather than how many traits it influences.","That describes codominance, as in the AB blood type, where both alleles show up side by side in the phenotype.","Many genes contributing to one trait is polygenic inheritance, which is the mirror image of pleiotropy.","A lethal allele describes a genotype that does not survive, which is a separate phenomenon.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-ih7vz8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Some bacteria living without oxygen still use an electron transport chain, passing electrons to sulfate or nitrate instead of to oxygen. How does that differ from fermentation?","opts":["Fermentation passes electrons to nitrate, while anaerobic respiration passes them to pyruvate","It produces less ATP per glucose than fermentation does","Fermentation uses no electron transport chain and builds no proton gradient","Fermentation uses a chain too, but with oxygen as the final acceptor","It is the same process under a different name"],"correct":2,"why":"Both happen without oxygen, which is why students blur them together, but only one of them uses a membrane. Anaerobic respiration still runs electrons through a chain and still builds a proton gradient, just with a different molecule waiting at the end, so it out-earns fermentation. Fermentation has no chain and no gradient: it hands NADH's electrons to an organic molecule so that glycolysis can carry on.","traps":["This has the two acceptors swapped, since fermentation uses pyruvate or a molecule made from it, and nitrate or sulfate belongs to anaerobic respiration.","Running a chain and a gradient yields more ATP per glucose than fermentation, not less.",null,"If oxygen were the final acceptor it would be aerobic respiration, and fermentation has no chain at all.","The two are distinct: one uses a membrane chain and a gradient while the other does not."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g056","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-6","topic":"The central dogma","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"What does the term genomic imprinting describe?","opts":["Gene memorization","Parent-of-origin specific gene expression","A permanent change in the DNA sequence that is passed on to offspring","Random inactivation","X-linked"],"correct":1,"why":"This is testing the idea that a gene can carry a memory of which parent it came from. During gamete formation certain genes receive methylation marks that silence them, and the pattern differs between eggs and sperm, so an offspring expresses only the maternal copy of some genes and only the paternal copy of others. Nothing in the sequence changes, only the marks laid on top of it, and those marks are erased and reset in each generation's gametes. When a disorder depends on whether the mother or the father passed the mutation, imprinting is what you are looking at.","traps":["This reads the word literally; the term names a chemical mark placed during gamete formation, not anything the organism learns.",null,"The marks involved sit on top of the DNA and change no bases at all, and they are wiped and re-set when the next generation's gametes form.","Random silencing describes what happens to one X chromosome in a female cell, and this process is the opposite of random: the parent of origin decides.","Imprinted genes sit on autosomes as well, so this confuses a pattern of inheritance with a parent-of-origin mark."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g023","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Red-green color blindness is X-linked recessive. A colorblind man has children with a woman who has normal color vision and is not a carrier. What percentage of their sons is expected to be colorblind?","opts":["0%","25%","50%","75%","100%"],"correct":0,"why":"Track the X chromosomes and the question answers itself. A son receives his single X from his mother and a Y from his father, so the father's affected X never reaches a son at all. This mother carries two normal alleles, so every son gets a normal X and normal color vision. The daughters are the interesting ones: each gets the father's affected X plus a normal X from the mother, making them unaffected carriers.","traps":[null,"One box out of four looks like a tidy Punnett square answer, but the affected allele is not present in the mother at all.","Half the sons being affected would require the mother to be a carrier so that she could pass an affected X to about half of them.","Three quarters of the sons could only be affected if the mother carried the affected allele, and she carries two normal ones.","This assumes the father hands his X chromosome to his sons, when in fact sons receive his Y."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p066","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Type 1 diabetes mellitus develops through which underlying mechanism?","opts":["Autoimmune destruction of the pancreatic alpha cells","Autoimmune destruction of β cells","Glucagon excess","Lifestyle","Insulin resistance"],"correct":1,"why":"This question is really asking you to separate the two diabetes stories. Type 1 is an immune problem: the body's own T cells attack the insulin producing beta cells of the pancreatic islets, so almost no insulin is made, which is why it usually appears young and needs injected insulin. Type 2 is a receptor problem, where insulin is present but muscle, fat and liver stop responding to it. If the stem tells you the cells that make insulin are gone, you are in Type 1.","traps":["Alpha cells secrete glucagon, so losing them would blunt the response to low blood sugar instead of producing insulin deficiency.",null,"Glucagon does run high in poorly controlled diabetes, but that is a downstream consequence of losing insulin rather than the cause of Type 1.","Body weight and activity strongly shape Type 2 risk, while Type 1 is an autoimmune event that lifestyle does not bring on.","Insulin resistance is the Type 2 story, where insulin is still being produced but the tissues respond to it poorly."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1d483j","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Glucose taken into an intestinal epithelial cell across the apical membrane then leaves across the basolateral membrane into the blood, moving down its concentration gradient through a GLUT carrier. That exit step is:","opts":["Secondary active transport","Exocytosis","Facilitated diffusion","Simple diffusion","Primary active transport"],"correct":2,"why":"The same sugar uses two different mechanisms on two faces of one cell, and that contrast is the point. Entry at the apical side is uphill and borrows the sodium gradient, while exit at the basolateral side is downhill through a carrier with no energy spent, which is facilitated diffusion. At every step ask two things: which way does the gradient run, and is a protein involved.","traps":["That describes the apical entry step, the exit has no gradient to borrow from.","Glucose leaves through a carrier protein, not packaged inside a vesicle.",null,"Glucose is polar and bulky, so it needs a carrier rather than the bare bilayer.","No ATP is hydrolyzed at this step, the pump spending ATP in this cell is working on sodium."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1qws3oz","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"What does a round of photorespiration cost the plant?","opts":["It consumes ATP and gives off carbon dioxide while making no sugar","It destroys RuBisCO, which the leaf then has to rebuild","It converts glucose back into carbon dioxide to generate ATP","It makes one extra G3P at the cost of one NADPH","It shuts down the light reactions until the oxygen level inside the leaf falls"],"correct":0,"why":"Name what actually comes out. RuBP plus oxygen gives one usable three carbon acid and one two carbon piece the cell has to salvage, and that salvage operation spends ATP and loses carbon as carbon dioxide. Photorespiration is the hole in the bucket: same machinery, wrong gas, nothing gained.","traps":[null,"The enzyme is not consumed and goes right back to work on the next molecule it meets.","That description belongs to cellular respiration, a controlled energy yielding pathway, not to this.","Photorespiration pulls carbon out of the cycle rather than adding a usable three carbon sugar to it.","The light reactions keep running, which is part of the problem, because they keep making more oxygen."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Echinoderms and chordates are placed together in which major group of bilaterian animals?","opts":["Lophotrochozoa","Protostomes","Deuterostomes","Radiata, the radially symmetric animals","Ecdysozoa"],"correct":2,"why":"The question is testing the top-level split of bilaterian animals, and the split is made on how the embryo builds its gut. In deuterostomes the first opening of the gastrula becomes the anus and the mouth forms second, and cleavage is radial and indeterminate. Echinoderms and chordates both develop this way, which is why a sea star is a closer relative of yours than a snail is. The name is the memory hook, since deuteros means second, and it is the mouth that comes second.","traps":["Lophotrochozoans are a protostome subgroup, the animals with a lophophore or a trochophore larva, such as molluscs and annelids.","Protostomes are the other branch, the molluscs, annelids and arthropods, where the blastopore becomes the mouth.",null,"Adult sea stars are radially symmetric, but their larvae are bilateral and chordates are never radial, so radial symmetry is not what unites these two.","Ecdysozoans are the molting protostomes, the arthropods and roundworms."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p092","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the basic repeating structural unit of compact bone?","opts":["Lamellae","Osteon (Haversian system)","Lacunae","Trabeculae","Perforating (Volkmann) canals"],"correct":1,"why":"Compact bone is organized around its blood supply. Each osteon is a cylinder of concentric lamellae built around a central Haversian canal carrying a vessel and a nerve, with osteocytes sitting in lacunae between the rings and reaching one another through canaliculi. Ask which structure contains all of the others and the choice naming the osteon is the repeating unit, while spongy bone uses trabeculae instead.","traps":["Lamellae are the concentric rings of matrix inside an osteon, which makes them a component of the unit rather than the unit itself.",null,"Lacunae are the small spaces in the matrix where individual osteocytes sit, linked to each other by canaliculi.","Trabeculae are the strut network of spongy bone, arranged along lines of stress rather than into cylinders.","Perforating canals run crosswise to connect the central canals of neighboring osteons, carrying vessels in from the periosteum."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p063","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Cortisol is released from which structure?","opts":["Adrenal medulla","Anterior pituitary","Pancreas","The zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex","Adrenal cortex (zona fasciculata)"],"correct":4,"why":"Cortisol is a steroid, and steroid hormones come from the adrenal cortex rather than the medulla. The cortex is layered, and its middle layer, the zona fasciculata, makes the glucocorticoids that raise blood glucose and damp inflammation during sustained stress. Split the adrenal gland in two whenever you study it: the cortex makes steroids slowly, the medulla dumps catecholamines fast.","traps":["The adrenal medulla releases epinephrine and norepinephrine for the immediate stress response, not steroid hormones.","The anterior pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone, which tells the adrenal cortex to make cortisol, so it is the signal rather than the source.","The pancreas releases insulin and glucagon, which manage blood glucose but are peptides from a different organ.","The outer zona glomerulosa makes aldosterone, the mineralocorticoid that conserves sodium, while cortisol comes from the layer beneath it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev016","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The pancreas, including both its enzyme secreting acini and its hormone secreting islets, develops from which germ layer?","opts":["Neural crest","Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Multiple"],"correct":3,"why":"Follow the gut tube and this one is settled. The pancreas begins as dorsal and ventral buds from the endodermal lining of the foregut, and those buds grow, branch and fuse, producing both the acini that make digestive enzymes and the islets that make insulin and glucagon. The same rule places the liver, the thyroid, the thymus and the lining of the lungs: if it buds off the gut tube, it is endoderm.","traps":["Islet cells make peptide hormones and were once suspected of a neural crest origin, but tracing their lineage showed they come from the same endodermal buds as the rest of the pancreas.","Ectoderm makes the outer covering and the nervous system, and the pancreas is neither of those.","Mesoderm supplies the connective tissue, vessels and capsule around the pancreas, but the secretory cells themselves come from the lining of the gut.",null,"Both the exocrine acini and the endocrine islets trace back to the same endodermal buds, so the working tissue has a single origin."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p005","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"During a normal quiet inhalation, what does the diaphragm do?","opts":["Stays still","Relaxes and rises","Contracts and domes upward into the chest","Moves laterally","Contracts and flattens (down)"],"correct":4,"why":"Breathing in is an active pull, not a push. The diaphragm contracts, which flattens its dome downward, and the external intercostals lift the ribs up and out, so the chest cavity gets bigger, the pressure inside drops below the air pressure outside, and air flows in to even things out. Quiet exhalation is the reverse and takes no effort, since the diaphragm relaxes back up and the lungs recoil.","traps":["If chest volume never changed, there would be no pressure difference to move air in.","That is quiet exhalation, when the diaphragm relaxes back into its dome and the chest gets smaller.","Contraction is right, but a contracting diaphragm pulls its dome flat and downward, which is what enlarges the chest.","The ribs swing outward to the sides, while the diaphragm's contribution is a downward flattening.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1btzdwa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Three turns of the Calvin cycle produce six G3P, and only one of them leaves. What becomes of the other five?","opts":["They move into the thylakoid lumen to feed the next round of light reactions","They are rearranged, with three more ATP, into three molecules of RuBP","They are exported to the cytosol and stored there as starch","They are oxidized back to carbon dioxide to recover the ATP that was spent","They are reduced further by NADPH into two molecules of glucose"],"correct":1,"why":"The cycle has to pay itself back before it pays anything out. Fifteen carbons sitting in five G3P get reshuffled into three five carbon RuBP, and that reshuffle costs three ATP, which is why the ATP bill runs higher than the NADPH bill. Count carbons on both sides, fifteen and fifteen, and regeneration stops feeling like sleight of hand.","traps":["Nothing from the Calvin cycle feeds back into the lumen; the traffic runs the other way, as ATP and NADPH into the stroma.",null,"Starch is stored in the stroma, and only the single exported G3P ever leaves the cycle.","Burning them would undo the entire point of fixing that carbon in the first place.","Five three carbon molecules cannot become two six carbon sugars, and glucose assembly happens outside the cycle anyway."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The blastopore forms during gastrulation. In which group of animals does the blastopore become the mouth?","opts":["Both equally","Deuterostomes","Humans and other vertebrates","Neither","Protostomes"],"correct":4,"why":"The names carry the answer once you translate them. Protostome means first mouth, so the blastopore, the first opening, becomes the mouth and the anus forms second. Deuterostome means second mouth, so the blastopore becomes the anus instead. Attach the rest of the pattern to each name: protostomes show spiral determinate cleavage and schizocoely, while deuterostomes show radial indeterminate cleavage and enterocoely and include the echinoderms and all chordates.","traps":["The fate of the blastopore is one of the defining splits between these two groups, so it cannot come out the same way in both.","In deuterostomes the blastopore becomes the anus and the mouth breaks through second, which is what the prefix deutero, meaning second, is telling you.","Vertebrates, humans included, are deuterostomes, so the blastopore becomes the anus and the mouth forms later as a second opening.","The blastopore always becomes one of the two openings of the gut, either the mouth or the anus depending on the lineage.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev004","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The liver and the pancreas develop from which germ layer?","opts":["Ectoderm and endoderm together","Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Both meso + endo"],"correct":3,"why":"Follow the gut tube and this one stops being memorization. The liver and the pancreas begin as buds from the endodermal lining of the foregut, which is why each one keeps a duct connecting it back to the duodenum in the adult. The same rule covers the thyroid, the thymus, the lining of the lungs and the lining of the bladder: anything that buds off the tube keeps the tube's endodermal lining.","traps":["There is no ectodermal contribution to either organ, since both bud directly from the endodermal gut tube.","Ectoderm builds skin and nervous tissue, and among the glands only the anterior pituitary has an ectodermal origin, from the roof of the primitive mouth.","Mesoderm supplies the connective tissue, vessels and smooth muscle that surround and support these organs, but not their secretory cells.",null,"The working tissue of both organs is endodermal, and mesoderm contributes supporting stroma to nearly every organ in the body, so counting that would make the question meaningless."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g035","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a dihybrid cross where a homozygous recessive genotype at one gene masks the expression of a second gene, as in the Bombay phenotype, what phenotypic ratio is expected in the F2 generation?","opts":["9:3:3:1","13:3","12:3:1","9:3:4","9:6:1"],"correct":3,"why":"Start from the ordinary dihybrid 9:3:3:1 and ask which classes end up looking identical. In recessive epistasis, being homozygous recessive at the masking gene hides the second gene completely, so one of the 3 classes merges with the 1 class and becomes 4. That leaves 9:3:4, and the appearance of a 4 in any modified ratio is your signal that a recessive genotype is doing the masking.","traps":["That is the unmodified dihybrid ratio, seen when two genes act independently with no interaction between them.","This comes from dominant suppression, where the 9, a 3 and the 1 all end up sharing one phenotype.","A 12 appears when a dominant allele at one gene does the masking, merging the 9 and one 3 class together.",null,"This is duplicate gene action with an additive effect, where the two single-dominant classes look alike and combine into 6."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-17676nh","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"Bacteria are grown for many generations in medium whose only nitrogen source is the heavy isotope 15N, so all of their DNA is heavy. They are then moved into medium containing only light 14N and allowed to divide exactly twice. What fraction of the DNA molecules now contain one heavy strand and one light strand?","opts":["One half of them","Three quarters of them","All of them","One quarter of them","None of them, because every molecule is now fully light"],"correct":0,"why":"This is semiconservative replication counted out rather than recited: you start with one molecule holding two heavy strands, and those two strands survive every round intact. After one round there are two molecules and both are hybrid. After two rounds there are four molecules, and the two heavy strands still sit in two of them, so half are hybrid. The shortcut worth keeping is that the number of hybrid molecules stops changing after the first round while the total keeps doubling, so the hybrid fraction halves each generation.","traps":[null,"Three quarters is the fraction of individual strands that are light after two rounds, again a strand count rather than a molecule count.","All hybrid is the picture after one round, and the second round dilutes it, because each hybrid molecule gives one hybrid daughter and one fully light daughter.","One quarter is the fraction of individual strands that are heavy after two rounds, which is not the same as the fraction of molecules carrying one.","This assumes the old strands are discarded, but replication keeps and reuses every parental strand, so the two original heavy strands are still somewhere."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e003","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A horse and a donkey mate and produce a mule, which grows into a healthy adult but cannot reproduce. This illustrates which type of reproductive isolation?","opts":["Prezygotic - behavioral","Prezygotic - gametic","Postzygotic - inviability","Postzygotic - hybrid sterility","Temporal isolation, in which the two species breed at different times of year"],"correct":3,"why":"Split the timeline at fertilization. Anything that stops a zygote from forming, such as different habitats, different breeding seasons, incompatible courtship, or gametes that cannot fuse, is prezygotic. Here a zygote clearly formed and grew into an adult, so the barrier acted afterward, and the specific failure is in meiosis: a horse has 64 chromosomes, a donkey has 62, and the mule's 63 cannot pair up evenly to make working gametes.","traps":["Behavioral isolation means the two species never court each other successfully, and these two mated.","Gametic isolation means sperm and egg never fuse, yet fertilization plainly succeeded in this cross.","Hybrid inviability means the hybrid dies as an embryo or before reaching maturity, while a mule is healthy and long lived.",null,"Temporal isolation would keep the two species from ever breeding together, but this cross produced offspring."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g091","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a polymerase chain reaction to amplify one specific stretch of DNA, what must be true of the two primers?","opts":["Methylated","They must be made of RNA, as the primers laid down during DNA replication inside a cell are","Random sequence","Same as target","Complementary to flanking regions of target"],"correct":4,"why":"DNA polymerase cannot start a strand from nothing, it can only add to an existing three prime end, so PCR supplies two short single stranded pieces of DNA that anneal just outside each end of the region you want. Because each primer is complementary to the template strand it lands on and the two point inward toward each other, only the stretch between them gets copied. That is also why you need to know some sequence on both sides of a gene before you can amplify it.","traps":["Methylation is a regulatory mark on genomic DNA, and a synthetic primer needs none of it for the polymerase to extend from it.","Inside a cell primase does lay down short RNA primers, but PCR uses synthetic DNA oligonucleotides, which is why no primer removal step is needed.","Random sequences would anneal in countless places across the genome and give a smear of products rather than one specific piece.","A primer has to base pair with the template it lands on, so a piece identical to that strand would have nothing to anneal to.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e034","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is an example of habituation?","opts":["Imprinting","A rat that presses a lever less and less often once the lever no longer produces food","Insight","Aplysia (sea slug) reduces gill withdrawal to repeated harmless touch","Pavlov's dogs"],"correct":3,"why":"Habituation is learning to ignore. A stimulus repeats, nothing good or bad follows it, and the animal's response fades, which frees up attention and energy for things that matter. The sea slug Aplysia is the classic laboratory case: repeated harmless touches to the siphon shrink the gill withdrawal reflex. The test is that nothing is paired with the stimulus and nothing is earned by responding, which is what separates habituation from both classical and operant conditioning.","traps":["Imprinting is a lasting attachment formed during a critical period, not a response that weakens with repetition.","This is extinction of an operant behavior, since the consequence changed, while habituation requires no reward or punishment at all.","Insight is solving a novel problem in one stroke, with no repeated stimulus involved.",null,"The dogs built a new response to the bell, which is classical conditioning, the opposite of a response fading away."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"}],"gc":[{"id":"generated:gc-cell-potential#h3cpe","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Standard reduction potentials:\n\nAl³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al E° = −1.66 V\nFe²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Fe E° = −0.44 V\n\nWhat is E°cell for the galvanic cell built from these two half-cells?","opts":["−2.10 V","+1.83 V","−1.22 V","+1.22 V","+2.00 V"],"correct":3,"why":"Cathode = higher reduction potential (Fe). Anode = Al. E°cell = (-0.44) − (-1.66) = +1.22 V. Red cat, an ox: reduction at the cathode, oxidation at the anode.","traps":["You added the two table values. E°cell = E°cathode MINUS E°anode, both read as reductions.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","A galvanic cell runs by itself, so E°cell must be POSITIVE. The higher reduction potential is the cathode.",null,"You multiplied E° by the electron coefficients. Potential is intensive: never scale it when you balance."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:col-q11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A real 0.10 m MgSO₄ solution shows ΔTf = 0.24 °C in water (Kf = 1.86). The \"ideal\" ΔTf for MgSO₄ assuming complete dissociation would be 0.372 °C. The effective van't Hoff factor i_eff is:","opts":["1.00","1.29","1.55","1.86","2.00"],"correct":1,"why":"i_eff = ΔTf_measured / (Kf · m) = 0.24 / (1.86 × 0.10) = 0.24 / 0.186 = 1.29. MgSO₄ is famously underdissociated - extensive ion pairing of Mg²⁺ with SO₄²⁻ in water means effective i is much less than the predicted 2.","traps":["i = 1 would mean MgSO₄ does not dissociate at all - that is wrong, it does dissociate, just incompletely.",null,"That is the ratio taken upside down, ideal over measured; i_eff is the measured depression divided by Kf times m.","1.86 is Kf, not i. You confused two different constants in the formula.","2.00 is the IDEAL i (full dissociation). The question asks for the actual measured i_eff, which is reduced by ion pairing."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ic-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A weak acid HA dissociates as HA(aq) ⇌ H+(aq) + A-(aq) at 25 °C, with Ka = 1.0x10^(-4) and [HA]_0 = 0.05 M, [H+]_0 ≈ 0, [A-]_0 = 0. Using the small-x approximation in an ICE table, calculate the equilibrium [H+].","opts":["5.0x10^(-3) M","1.4x10^(-3) M","7.1x10^(-4) M","2.2x10^(-3) M","1.0x10^(-2) M"],"correct":3,"why":"Ka = x^2 / (0.05 − x) ≈ x^2 / 0.05. So x^2 = (1.0x10^(-4))(0.05) = 5.0x10^(-6); x = sqrt(5.0x10^(-6)) ≈ 2.24x10^(-3) M. Forgetting to multiply by [HA]_0 gives 1x10^(-2).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"originals:ab-ec59a41c94","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Blood is buffered by carbonic acid and bicarbonate. If a person hyperventilates and blows off a large amount of CO2, what happens to blood pH?","opts":["It falls, because less bicarbonate is made","It rises, because hyperventilating adds extra bicarbonate to the blood","It rises, because removing CO2 lowers carbonic acid","It falls, because CO2 is a base","It does not change, because blood is buffered"],"correct":2,"why":"CO2 + H2O is in equilibrium with H2CO3, which is in equilibrium with H+ and HCO3-. Blow off CO2 and the chain shifts toward CO2, which uses up H+. Fewer H+ means a higher pH. That is respiratory alkalosis.","traps":["Losing CO2 pulls the equilibrium away from H+, which makes the blood less acidic, not more.","Breathing faster does not add bicarbonate. Bicarbonate actually dips a little, because H+ and HCO3- combine to replace the CO2 that was lost. The pH rises because that shift uses up H+.",null,"CO2 forms carbonic ACID in water. Removing it removes acid.","Buffers limit changes. A large loss of CO2 still shifts the pH."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A stockroom 12.0 M concentrated HCl is to be diluted into 500.0 mL of 0.100 M HCl for a titration prep. Using the dilution relation M1V1 = M2V2 (assuming volumes are additive at low concentration), what volume of stock HCl is required?","opts":["6.0 mL","4.2 mL","12 mL","0.42 mL","50 mL"],"correct":1,"why":"V1 = M2V2/M1 = (0.100)(500)/12.0 = 50/12 = 4.17 mL ~ 4.2 mL. Pipet 4.2 mL of stock into a 500 mL flask, then dilute (always add acid to water).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_poly1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A 25.0 mL aliquot of 0.100 M phosphoric acid (H3PO4; pKa1 = 2.15, pKa2 = 7.20, pKa3 = 12.35) is titrated with 0.100 M NaOH while monitoring pH with a calibrated electrode. Considering the three sequential acid dissociations, how many distinct equivalence-point inflections appear in the resulting titration curve (the third is often poorly resolved but theoretically present)?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":3,"why":"A polyprotic acid with n acidic protons gives n equivalence points, one per stepwise neutralization. H3PO4 has three acidic protons, so three equivalence points exist at moles NaOH = 1, 2, and 3 times moles H3PO4. The third is faint because pKa3 is near pKw, but it is still a true equivalence point.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Mix 25 mL 0.20 M HCl + 50 mL 0.10 M NaOH. Final pH?","opts":["0.7","1","7","11","13"],"correct":2,"why":"Count moles, not molarity: 0.025 L times 0.20 M gives 0.0050 mol of HCl, and 0.050 L times 0.10 M gives 0.0050 mol of NaOH. Equal moles of a strong acid and a strong base cancel completely, so nothing is left but NaCl, and neither of its ions tugs on water. That is why the answer is neutral, and it is also why a smaller volume of a stronger acid can still be an exact match for a larger volume of a weaker one.","traps":["0.7 is the pH of the 0.20 M HCl on its own, before the base arrived and before the volume tripled.","A pH near 1 assumes the acid survives the mixing, and every mole of HCl here meets a mole of NaOH.",null,"A pH of 11 is mildly basic, which would take a small excess of NaOH, and the two are matched mole for mole.","A pH of 13 assumes leftover hydroxide, and the base does not outnumber the acid in this flask."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1ldwwc0","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 1 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 127 °C and 4 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["8.21 L","831 L","2.61 L","131 L","12.3 L"],"correct":0,"why":"T = 127 + 273 = 400 K. V = nRT / P = (1)(0.0821)(400) / 4 = 8.21 L.","traps":[null,"You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.","You multiplied by pressure. V = nRT / P, so pressure goes underneath.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#y94x96","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 2 m aqueous solution of Na₂SO₄? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-3.07 °C","-3.72 °C","-14.9 °C","11.2 °C","-11.2 °C"],"correct":4,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (3)(1.86)(2) = 11.2 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 11.2 = -11.2 °C.","traps":["You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","You forgot the van't Hoff factor. Na₂SO₄ splits into 3 ions, so i = 3.","You counted one ion too many. Write the dissociation out and count the particles.","Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#1vmxu2f","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"What is the pH of a 1.0 × 10⁻³ M solution of NaOH at 25 °C?","opts":["3","7","10","11","17"],"correct":3,"why":"Strong base: [OH⁻] = 10⁻³ M, pOH = 3, pH = 14 − 3 = 11.","traps":["That is the pOH. NaOH gives you [OH⁻], so finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","You assumed neutral. A strong base is well above 7.","Exponent slip. For 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ the pOH is exactly n.",null,"You added instead of subtracting from 14. A pH that high is a signal to recheck."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_p-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Four main-group elements are compared for atomic radius using x-ray covalent radii: Si (118 pm), P (110 pm), Ge (122 pm), Sn (140 pm). Atomic radius increases down a group (more shells) and decreases across a period (higher Z_eff). Which element is the LARGEST?","opts":["Sn","Si","P","Ge","All approximately equal"],"correct":0,"why":"Sn lies lowest in group 14, so it has the most occupied principal shells (5p valence) and the largest radius. Going down trumps going across: Sn &gt; Ge &gt; Si and Si &gt; P (same row).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:gb-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"(−,−) sign quadrant - spontaneous when?","opts":["Only above T = ΔH/ΔS","Low T","Always","High T","Never"],"correct":1,"why":"With ΔH negative and ΔS negative, enthalpy helps you and entropy fights you. The entropy term enters as TΔS, so its penalty grows with temperature, and keeping T small keeps the penalty small. Below the crossover at ΔH/ΔS the enthalpy still wins, and above it the reaction stalls.","traps":["Above that temperature the entropy penalty wins and ΔG turns positive, so the crossover is a ceiling here rather than a floor.",null,"Always spontaneous belongs to the corner with negative ΔH and positive ΔS, where nothing opposes.","High T belongs to the corner with positive ΔH and positive ΔS, where entropy has to do the work.","Never spontaneous belongs to the corner with positive ΔH and negative ΔS, where both terms oppose."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1mruwsv","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 1 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 27 °C and 1 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["24.6 L","36.9 L","2.22 L","2490 L","12.3 L"],"correct":0,"why":"T = 27 + 273 = 300 K. V = nRT / P = (1)(0.0821)(300) / 1 = 24.6 L.","traps":[null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.","You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_v-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Among the molecules CO₂ (linear), BF₃ (trigonal planar), CH₂Cl₂ (tetrahedral), and CCl₄ (tetrahedral), each contains polar bonds. Yet only some are net polar molecules. Which of these molecules has individual polar bonds AND a non-zero net dipole moment?","opts":["CCl₄","BF₃","CO₂","All are nonpolar","CH₂Cl₂"],"correct":4,"why":"Symmetric arrangements (CO₂ linear, BF₃ trig planar, CCl₄ tetrahedral with identical substituents) cause bond dipoles to cancel - net nonpolar. CH₂Cl₂ has two different substituents (2 H and 2 Cl) on a tetrahedral C, so the dipoles do NOT cancel - net polar.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:nu-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A 100 mg sample decays to 12.5 mg. How many half-lives elapsed?","opts":["2","3","4","5","8"],"correct":1,"why":"12.5/100 = 1/8 = (½)³ → 3 half-lives.","traps":["Two half-lives would leave 25 mg, a quarter of the sample.",null,"Four half-lives would leave 6.25 mg, one step past where this sample stopped.","Five half-lives would leave about 3.1 mg, far below the 12.5 mg measured.","8 is the denominator of the 1/8 that remains, and the number of half-lives is the power sitting above the 2 in that 8."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#pdignc","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.0116 s⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["0.0167 s","0.00804 s","59.7 s","86.2 s","172 s"],"correct":2,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.0116 = 59.7 s. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":["Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.","You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath.",null,"That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.","You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:b-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Which N-O bond is the SHORTEST?","opts":["NO₂⁻ (avg 1.5)","NH₂OH (single bond)","NO₃⁻ (avg 1.33)","NO (bond order 2.5)","NO₂⁻ and NO₃⁻ are equal"],"correct":3,"why":"Higher bond order = shorter bond. NO has BO 2.5 (~1.15 Å); NO₃⁻ avg ~1.26 Å; NH₂OH single ~1.45 Å.","traps":["A bond order of 1.5 is real spread, and it is a full point below NO's 2.5, so nitrite's bonds are longer.","A plain N−O single bond near 1.45 Å is the longest of the set.","Nitrate spreads one double bond over three oxygens, the lowest bond order here, so its bonds are the longer for it.",null,"Their averages are close but not equal, 1.5 against 1.33, and both sit well below NO's 2.5."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#y96ihy","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 36 g of H₂O? (molar mass 18 g/mol)","opts":["4","1.2 × 10²⁴","2.17 × 10²⁵","6.02 × 10²³","2.41 × 10²⁴"],"correct":4,"why":"36 g / 18 g/mol = 2 mol H₂O. Times 2 hydrogen per molecule = 4 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 2.41 × 10²⁴.","traps":["That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.","That is the number of MOLECULES. Each H₂O holds 2 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 2.","You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.","You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-e4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Neutral iron (Fe, Z = 26) has a ground-state electron configuration of [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶. The six 3d electrons distribute across five degenerate 3d orbitals according to Hund's rule (maximize parallel spins before pairing). How many unpaired electrons does Fe have?","opts":["0","2","4","5","6"],"correct":2,"why":"Distribute 6 d-electrons in 5 orbitals: ↑↓ ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑. After all five are singly occupied (5 unpaired), the 6th electron pairs with one of them, leaving 4 unpaired electrons. The 4s² electrons are all paired and contribute 0 unpaired.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-gibbs#14sv6ez","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a reaction, ΔH = 80 kJ and ΔS = -50 J/K. What is ΔG at 300 K?","opts":["15100 kJ","80 kJ","15 kJ","65 kJ","95 kJ"],"correct":4,"why":"TΔS = 300 × -50 J/K = -15 kJ. ΔG = 80 − (-15) = 95 kJ, so the reaction is not spontaneous at this temperature.","traps":["Unit trap. ΔS is in J/K but ΔH is in kJ. Divide TΔS by 1000 before subtracting.","You forgot to multiply ΔS by the temperature.","You dropped ΔH. Both terms matter.","Sign slip. ΔG = ΔH MINUS TΔS.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:cal7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Why use Styrofoam in a coffee-cup calorimeter rather than glass or metal?","opts":["Insulator - minimizes heat lost to surroundings","Reacts less with hot solutions","Easier to seal","It keeps the solution from evaporating during the run","Cheaper"],"correct":0,"why":"Calorimetry assumes the system is isolated (q_lost = q_gained inside). Styrofoam is a poor thermal conductor, so minimal heat exchange with the surroundings, so measured ΔT reflects only the chemistry, not heat sink to the cup.","traps":[null,"Foam is fairly inert, but so is glass, and inertness is not what keeps the heat in.","A coffee-cup calorimeter sits open to the air, so sealing is not the point.","An open cup does not stop evaporation; what foam does is refuse to conduct heat out to the room.","Foam cups are cheap, but cost is not what makes the measurement trustworthy."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#rbfoi5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.0462 min⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["21.6 min","15 min","43.3 min","0.0667 min","0.032 min"],"correct":1,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.0462 = 15 min. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":["That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.",null,"You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693.","Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.","You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sw-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Four 0.10 M aqueous acid solutions at 25 °C are tested: HCl (strong), HF (Ka = 6.6x10^(-4)), HCN (Ka = 4.9x10^(-10)), and CH3COOH (Ka = 1.8x10^(-5)). Considering that pH depends on the extent of dissociation, which solution has the LOWEST pH?","opts":["CH3COOH","HF","HCN","HCl","All equal"],"correct":3,"why":"HCl is the only strong acid here; it fully dissociates to give [H+] = 0.10 M and pH = 1.0. The weak acids dissociate only partially, giving higher pH (HF ~ 2.1, CH3COOH ~ 3.4, HCN ~ 5.2).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#truyc6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 18 g of H₂O? (molar mass 18 g/mol)","opts":["3.01 × 10²³","1.08 × 10²⁵","1.2 × 10²⁴","6.02 × 10²³","2"],"correct":2,"why":"18 g / 18 g/mol = 1 mol H₂O. Times 2 hydrogen per molecule = 2 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.2 × 10²⁴.","traps":["You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.",null,"That is the number of MOLECULES. Each H₂O holds 2 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 2.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:if5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Which liquid has the LOWEST vapor pressure at 25°C?","opts":["Ethylene glycol (HOCH₂CH₂OH)","Pentane (C₅H₁₂)","Isopropyl alcohol ((CH₃)₂CHOH)","Diethyl ether ((C₂H₅)₂O)","Acetone ((CH₃)₂CO)"],"correct":0,"why":"Two -OH groups, so strongest H-bonding, so hardest to vaporize, so lowest VP.","traps":[null,"Pentane has only dispersion forces, so it is the easiest of these to push into the vapor.","This one does hydrogen bond, and a single O-H per molecule holds far less than the two on ethylene glycol.","Ether has an oxygen but no O-H, so it can accept hydrogen bonds without donating any, and it is famously volatile.","Acetone is polar through its C=O, and with no O-H it cannot donate a hydrogen bond, so it evaporates readily."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A 0.10 mol methane sample combusts in a bomb calorimeter (C_cal = 5.0 kJ/°C). T rises 18°C. ΔH per mol?","opts":["−900 kJ/mol","−90 kJ/mol","−1800 kJ/mol","−180 kJ/mol","−450 kJ/mol"],"correct":0,"why":"q = C_cal × ΔT = 5.0 × 18 = 90 kJ released. Per mol: 90/0.10 = 900 kJ. ΔH = −900 kJ/mol (matches the actual ~890 kJ/mol for CH₄ combustion).","traps":[null,"That is the total heat released in the bomb, stopping before it is spread over the 0.10 mol.","That divides by 0.050 mol, half the sample; 0.10 mol burned.","That divides by 0.50 mol instead of 0.10 mol; only a tenth of a mole burned.","That uses half the heat, or 0.20 mol; the calorimeter took in 90 kJ and 0.10 mol reacted."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lc-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"An exothermic gas-phase equilibrium A(g) ⇌ B(g) + heat is held at 300 K, and the temperature is then raised to 400 K while pressure is constant. Treating heat as a product for exothermic reactions and applying Le Chatelier's principle, how does the equilibrium shift?","opts":["Stays the same forever","Reverse (toward A)","Cannot determine","No shift","Forward (toward B)"],"correct":1,"why":"Exothermic forward reaction has heat as a product. Adding heat (raising T) shifts the system reverse to absorb the added heat. K decreases for exothermic reactions as T rises.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_m8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A 0.500 mol sample of liquid water at 25°C contains both H and O atoms. Each H₂O molecule has 3 atoms (2 H + 1 O). Using Nₐ = 6.02×10²³ molecules/mol, how many TOTAL atoms (H plus O) are in the sample?","opts":["1.5 × 10²³ atoms","3.01 × 10²³ atoms","6.02 × 10²³ atoms","1.8 × 10²⁴ atoms","9.03 × 10²³ atoms"],"correct":4,"why":"Molecules = 0.500 × 6.02×10²³ = 3.01×10²³. Each molecule has 3 atoms, so total atoms = 3 × 3.01×10²³ = 9.03×10²³. Forgetting the ×3 for atoms-per-molecule gives 3.01×10²³ (just molecules).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nr-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"A concentration cell at 25°C uses Cu electrodes in two Cu^2+ solutions: [Cu^2+] = 0.10 M (left) and 1.0 M (right). Both half-cells share E° = 0 (same reaction) so E_cell is determined entirely by the concentration difference via E = -(0.0592/n) log([dilute]/[concentrated]) with n = 2. Find E_cell.","opts":["0.059 V","0.25 V","0.118 V","0 V","0.030 V"],"correct":4,"why":"E_cell = (0.0592/2) log(1.0/0.10) = 0.0296 x 1 = 0.0296 V ~ 0.030 V. Concentration cells extract free energy from a concentration gradient until the two cells equalize.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:sf6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"(2.36 + 0.011) × 1.20 = ? Apply both rules.","opts":["2.37","2.8","2.84","2.844","2.85"],"correct":2,"why":"Step 1 (addition, so fewest decimal places): 2.36 + 0.011 = 2.371, so round to 2 decimals (2.36 limits) → 2.37. Step 2 (multiplication, so fewest sig figs): 2.37 × 1.20 = 2.844, so round to 3 sf (both factors have 3) → 2.84.","traps":["2.37 is the rounded sum from the first step, with the multiplication by 1.20 still to go.","2.8 keeps only two sig figs, and both factors going into the product carry three.",null,"2.844 is the raw product with no sig fig rounding applied at the end.","2.85 comes from carrying the unrounded 2.371 into the multiplication, and the addition rule caps that sum at 2.37 first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-n1kur6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"What is the major product when 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","opts":["C=CC(C)(C)C","CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Non-rearranged Hofmann alkene, substitution, and unreacted alcohol are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1reuicz","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"A compound C3H7Br shows TWO 1H NMR signals. The most downfield signal is a 1H septet, and the upfield signal is a 6H doublet. Which structure fits this pattern?","opts":["CCCBr","CC(C)Br","CCBr","CCCCBr","CC(C)(C)Br"],"correct":1,"why":"2-Bromopropane: the CH couples to 6 equivalent neighbor H (septet, 1H); the two CH3 couple to one CH (doublet, 6H). 1-bromopropane gives 3 signals; the other distractors are wrong formulas (EtBr, n-BuBr, t-BuBr). Index 0 uniquely fits.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-5lgtfp","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Styrene is treated with HBr in the presence of benzoyl peroxide (ROOR). What is the major product?","opts":["BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Peroxides trigger the radical anti-Markovnikov pathway: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to give the stabilized benzylic radical, so Br lands on the terminal carbon. option B is the Markovnikov (no-peroxide) product, option C wrong nucleophile, option D Br2 chemistry, option E needs Br2/FeBr3.","traps":null,"qs":"C=Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-15ou09x","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with NaOEt, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br), then warm aqueous acid. Which structure is the product?","opts":["CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Methyl-ketone product CH3-CO-CH2-R, R=benzyl = 4-phenyl-2-butanone. B drops a CH2 (phenylacetone), C is an ethyl ketone (wrong pattern), D adds a CH2, E is a carboxylic acid (malonic confusion).","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1itm6jg","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Acetaldehyde (drawn) is treated with dilute NaOH and then warmed. Which structure is the major product of this self-aldol condensation?","opts":["C/C=C\\C=O","C=CC=O","CC(O)CC=O","C/C=C/C=O","CCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Self-aldol of acetaldehyde gives 3-hydroxybutanal, which dehydrates on warming to conjugated (E)-but-2-enal (crotonaldehyde). option B is minor Z, option C is the un-dehydrated aldol, option D (acrolein) loses a carbon, option E (propanal) is no reaction. Keyed answer correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gcn5rx","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"2-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. Choose the major product.","opts":["CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)C(C)Br","CCCC(C)Br","CCC(C)(Br)CBr","CCC(C)CBr"],"correct":4,"why":"Radical (peroxide) conditions give anti-Markovnikov addition: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to form the more stable tertiary radical at C2, then H caps C2, so Br ends up on the primary carbon (1-bromo-2-methylbutane). The tertiary-bromide option is the Markovnikov product; the rest are wrong-regiochemistry, rearranged, or dibromide structures.","traps":null,"qs":"C=C(C)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ykycfl","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A student wants to make a six-membered cyclic beta-ketoester by a Dieckmann condensation. Of the diesters below, which gives a six-membered ring (cyclohexanone) product, while diethyl succinate (shown) fails to give a useful Dieckmann product?","opts":["Diethyl succinate (1,4-diester)","Diethyl malonate (1,3-diester)","Diethyl oxalate (1,2-diester)","Diethyl pimelate (1,7-diester)","Diethyl adipate (1,6-diester)"],"correct":3,"why":"Ring size tracks chain length: a 1,7-diester (pimelate) closes to a favorable six-membered ring. Succinate (drawn, 1,4) would form a strained three-membered ring and fails, adipate (1,6) gives a five-membered ring, and malonate/oxalate are too short to cyclize. Only pimelate gives the six-membered cyclohexanone beta-ketoester.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-yraq7h","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Three protonated nitrogen acids are compared: methylammonium (CH3NH3+, shown), anilinium (C6H5NH3+), and pyridinium (C5H5NH+). Which is the strongest acid (lowest pKa), reflecting the WEAKEST/most-stabilized conjugate base?","opts":["Methylammonium; a primary amine cation is always the most acidic","Pyridinium; the aromatic ring feeds electron density to nitrogen and makes pyridine a stronger base than methylamine","Anilinium; its conjugate base aniline has the N lone pair delocalized into the benzene ring, making aniline a weak base and anilinium the strongest acid","All three have identical pKa because each loses an N-H proton","Methylammonium; the alkyl group's electron donation makes methylamine the weakest base of the three, which leaves its cation as the strongest acid of the set by a wide margin"],"correct":2,"why":"Aniline is the weakest base (N lone pair delocalized into the ring), so its conjugate acid anilinium (pKa ~4.6) is the strongest acid, below pyridinium (~5.2) and methylammonium (~10.6).","traps":null,"qs":"C[NH3+]","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1eq5a2f","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl 2-methyladipate (shown) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acid. Of the two possible Dieckmann ring closures, which beta-ketoester is the major product?","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC(C)C1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CC(C)CC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1(C)CCCC1=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Dieckmann is reversible and is driven by deprotonation of the product's carbon flanked by both carbonyls. If the methyl-bearing alpha carbon attacks the far carbonyl, that carbon becomes quaternary (option B) with no acidic proton, so that path reverses. The favored product comes from the unsubstituted CH2 enolate attacking, leaving an acidic alpha proton on the ester-bearing ring carbon: option A (C9H14O3), verified to equal the path-B closure.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)C(C)CCCC(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fqqcym","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexene (drawn) is treated with NBS under radical allylic conditions. The allylic radical formed is delocalized, and the product that results from bromination at the terminal carbon (with double-bond transposition) predominates as the conjugated, more substituted alkene. Which structure is that product?","opts":["BrC/C=C\\CCC","C=CC(Br)CCC","BrCCCCCC=C","BrC/C=C/CCC","C=CCC(Br)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"NBS abstracts the C3 allylic H, giving an allyl radical delocalized between C1 and C3. Trapping at C1 transposes the double bond to C2=C3, giving the more substituted internal alkene (E)-1-bromo-2-hexene (option A); the E isomer is favored over Z. The non-transposed 3-bromo-1-hexene keeps a terminal alkene; the Z isomer and homoallylic placements are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-157suh4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"p-Toluidine (4-methylaniline) is converted to its diazonium salt with NaNO2 / HCl (0-5 C), then warmed in H2O. What is the major product?","opts":["Cc1ccc(O)cc1","Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","Cc1ccc(N=O)cc1","Cc1ccccc1O","Oc1ccc(O)cc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Warm aqueous hydrolysis of an aryl diazonium salt replaces -N2+ by -OH at the same position, giving p-cresol (Cc1ccc(O)cc1) with methyl still para. The ortho phenol (position migration), aryl bromide (no Cu halide), nitroso compound, and hydroquinone are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccc(N)cc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1ml3r51","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"Predict the major product. Phenylacetylene (Ph-C#CH) is treated with (1) disiamylborane (a hindered borane), then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the structure of the major product.","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC=Cc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov, placing the carbonyl on the terminal carbon to give phenylacetaldehyde (PhCH2CHO). Acetophenone is the Markovnikov methyl ketone (Hg/acid); benzaldehyde loses a carbon; the enol is the intermediate; benzoic acid is over-oxidation.","traps":null,"qs":"C#Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-f00uzq","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"1,3-Butadiene (drawn) reacts with 1 equivalent of HBr. Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized allylic cation, and under thermodynamic control the 1,4-addition product dominates. Which structure is that major 1,4-product (note its alkene geometry)?","opts":["C/C=C/CBr","CCC(Br)C","C=CC(C)Br","C/C=C\\CBr","C=CCCBr"],"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives allylic cation CH3-CH(+)...CH=CH2; bromide capture at the 1,4-terminus gives 1-bromo-2-butene, with the more stable (E) alkene under thermodynamic control: C/C=C/CBr. (Z) is wrong geometry; C=CC(C)Br is the kinetic 1,2-product; CCC(C)Br (2-bromobutane) is wrong skeleton; C=CCCBr (4-bromo-1-butene) is anti-Markovnikov.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1g31xwm","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt). Remembering that E2 on a cyclohexane requires the leaving group and the beta-hydrogen to be trans-diaxial (anti-periplanar), which is the MAJOR alkene product?","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1C=CCCC1","CCOC1CCCCC1C","CC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"E2 needs Br axial with an anti-periplanar axial beta-H. In the trans-1,2 isomer, when Br is axial the methyl is also axial, so C2 has no axial H. Elimination must go toward C6, giving 3-methylcyclohexene (option A). The Zaitsev product 1-methylcyclohexene (option B) cannot form because the required C2-H is equatorial.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-19ftnpq","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"You want to convert 1-methylcyclohexene (drawn) into a vicinal cis-diol in a single operation. Which reagent set should you choose?","opts":["OsO4, then NaHSO3 (or cold dilute KMnO4, OH-)","mCPBA in CH2Cl2","O3 in CH2Cl2 at -78 C, then (CH3)2S (reductive workup)","HBr with peroxides","Hot, concentrated KMnO4, then acidic workup at reflux"],"correct":0,"why":"OsO4 with reductive workup (or cold dilute basic KMnO4) gives the syn cis-diol directly in one step. mCPBA stops at the epoxide; hot conc. KMnO4 and ozonolysis cleave the C=C; HBr/peroxides gives anti-Markovnikov hydrobromination.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1i1rddo","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"tert-Butyl bromide (drawn) is dissolved in pure ethanol and warmed gently with no added base or nucleophile. Which pair of mechanisms operates, and which is favored?","opts":["Only E2 occurs because ethanol is a strong base","SN2 and E2 compete; E2 dominates","SN1 and E1 compete; SN1 (substitution) dominates because solvolysis with a weak nucleophile favors substitution","SN1 and E1 compete; the two give exactly equal amounts of product at every temperature because they share the same rate-determining step","Only SN2 occurs, because the three methyl groups of a tertiary substrate leave the backside wide open for ethanol to attack"],"correct":2,"why":"Tertiary halide in polar protic ethanol, weak nucleophile, no strong base = solvolysis: SN1 and E1 compete, SN1 dominates under gentle heat. Distractors (forced bimolecular, ethanol as strong base) are plausible-but-wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)Br","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1v0wmr4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this E2 reaction?","opts":["C=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)(C)C=C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CCOC(C)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"E2 is concerted with no carbocation, so no rearrangement. The only beta-H is on the CH3 of the C-Br carbon (the other neighbor is the quaternary tert-butyl carbon, no H), giving 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (option A). option B (2,3-dimethyl-2-butene) is the rearranged Zaitsev trap requiring a hydride/methyl shift, which E2 cannot do.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)C(C)Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1jzd8nw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Menthyl chloride (shown) undergoes E2 with sodium ethoxide. The leaving Cl is equatorial in the stable chair, so the ring must flip to an all-axial chair before elimination. Which alkene is the MAJOR (essentially only) product?","opts":["CC1CC=C(C(C)C)CC1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(O)C1","C=C1CCC(C(C)C)CC1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)CC1","CC1C=CC(C(C)C)CC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Verified: classic menthyl chloride E2. With Cl axial only the C6 H is anti-periplanar (the isopropyl-bearing C2 H is equatorial), so the less-substituted 2-menthene (option A, double bond between unsubstituted ring carbons) is the sole product, not the Zaitsev menthene at the iPr carbon (option B).","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CC[C@@H](C(C)C)[C@@H](Cl)C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-2ct5ye","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Styrene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute, basic KMnO4. Which observation and product correctly describe this reaction?","opts":["The double bond is cleaved at the C=C to give benzaldehyde and formaldehyde as the two carbonyl fragments","Benzoic acid forms as the only organic product, with the terminal carbon lost as carbon dioxide","HBr adds across the double bond","Purple color fades and 1-phenyl-1,2-ethanediol (a cis-diol) forms","An epoxide forms with no color change"],"correct":3,"why":"Cold dilute basic KMnO4 is a syn-dihydroxylation reagent (Baeyer test): gives the vicinal cis-diol 1-phenyl-1,2-ethanediol while purple permanganate is reduced (color fades to brown MnO2). Oxidative cleavage to carbonyls/acids requires HOT concentrated KMnO4.","traps":null,"qs":"C=Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1khayk8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A single enantiomer of 2-phenylpropanoic acid (stereocenter shown) is heated with methanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which structure is the major product, accounting for the stereochemistry at the alpha carbon?","opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Fischer esterification cleaves the acyl carbon-oxygen bond of the acid; the bond to the alpha stereocenter is never broken, so its spatial configuration is fully retained. The inversion structure (B) is wrong because no bond to the stereocenter is broken; (C) uses ethanol; (D) loses the stereochemistry; (E) is a chain-extended skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-lbh6f5","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Toluene is treated with HNO3 and H2SO4 (one equivalent). Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","opts":["Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccc(N)cc1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Cc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c([N+](=O)[O-])c1"],"correct":2,"why":"Methyl is a weak activator and o/p director; with one equivalent the para product (p-nitrotoluene) predominates over the more hindered ortho. Meta is minor, aniline is the wrong FG, and the dinitro structure is an overreaction.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-o828tv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (ethynylcyclohexane, drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","opts":["CC(=O)C1CCCCC1","O=CC1CCCCC1","OCCC1CCCCC1","OC=CC1CCCCC1","O=CCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"This is a terminal alkyne (C#C-) bearing a cyclohexyl group; hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov, so the carbonyl ends up on the terminal carbon, giving cyclohexylacetaldehyde (a two-carbon aldehyde side chain). The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov product; cyclohexanecarbaldehyde (ring-CHO) has lost a carbon and the wrong skeleton; the alcohol is over-reduction; the enol is the intermediate.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CC1CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1vndb83","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Two carbonyls are available for reduction by NaBH4 in methanol: acetophenone (an aryl methyl ketone, drawn) and benzaldehyde. Considering electronic and steric effects on hydride addition, which statement about relative rate is correct?","opts":["Acetophenone is reduced faster because the extra methyl group donates electron density that makes the carbonyl a better hydride acceptor","Both are reduced at identical rates because NaBH4 reacts only with esters","Neither reacts because NaBH4 cannot reduce conjugated aryl carbonyls","Acetophenone is reduced faster because ketones are inherently more electrophilic than aldehydes at the carbonyl carbon in every solvent","Benzaldehyde is reduced faster than acetophenone because the aldehyde carbonyl is less hindered and more electrophilic"],"correct":4,"why":"Aldehydes are reduced faster than ketones by hydride: the aldehyde carbonyl carbon is less sterically hindered and more electrophilic (only one alkyl/aryl group donating electron density), so benzaldehyde reacts faster than the more hindered, more electron-rich acetophenone.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-djzol0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Cyclopentadiene (drawn below) is a highly reactive diene. To run a fast Diels-Alder reaction at or near room temperature, you want to pair it with the most electron-poor dienophile available. Which reagent is the best dienophile partner?","opts":["Ethane","1-Hexene, a simple unactivated terminal alkene","Diethyl ether","Maleic anhydride","Cyclohexane, an unstrained saturated ring"],"correct":3,"why":"Only maleic anhydride bears an activated C=C flanked by two carbonyls (electron-poor dienophile), the textbook fast partner. Cyclohexane, ethane, diethyl ether have no reactive C=C; 1-hexene is unactivated and sluggish. Exactly one best answer.","traps":null,"qs":"C1=CCC=C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-duu60b","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"5-Aminopentanal (drawn) is treated with NaBH3CN under mildly acidic conditions, where the molecule undergoes INTRAMOLECULAR reductive amination. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["C1CCNCC1","C1CCCNCC1","NCCCCCO","C1CCNC1","CCCCC=N"],"correct":0,"why":"The -NH2 and aldehyde condense to a cyclic iminium reduced to a saturated cyclic amine. Distractors: pyrrolidine (5-membered) and azepane (7-membered) wrong ring sizes, the acyclic amino alcohol, and an acyclic imine that also lost a carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"O=CCCCCN","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8gdlsf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Benzene is treated with 1-chloropropane (CH3CH2CH2Cl) and AlCl3. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Friedel-Crafts alkylation goes through a carbocation; the primary propyl cation undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the secondary isopropyl cation, so cumene (isopropylbenzene, choice A) is major. B n-propyl (no-rearrangement trap), C tert-butyl and D ethyl (wrong carbon count), E isobutyl (wrong skeleton).","traps":null,"qs":"ClCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-okszib","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n4-methylpent-3-en-2-one (mesityl oxide) + H2 (1 equiv), Pd/C","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)C","CC(O)CC(C)CO","CC(C)=CC(C)O","CC(=O)C=C(C)C","CC(C)CC(C)O"],"correct":0,"why":"H2/Pd selectively reduces the C=C and leaves the ketone C=O intact, giving 4-methyl-2-pentanone (o0). Pd/C does not reduce an isolated ketone to an alcohol, so the alcohol/diol options are wrong, and o4 (alkene retained, carbonyl reduced) is backwards.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)C=C(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-2335q9","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then Zn/AcOH (reductive workup). Which structure is the product?","opts":["CC1CCCCC1=O","CC(=O)CCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC=O","CC(O)CCCCCO","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the endocyclic C=C and OPENS the ring to an acyclic chain. Reductive (Zn/AcOH) workup makes the more-substituted (methyl-bearing) carbon a methyl ketone and the CH carbon an aldehyde: 6-oxoheptanal (CC(=O)CCCCC=O, C7H12O2). Option 2 omits the methyl, option 3 is oxidative over-oxidation to an acid, option 4 is a diol, and option 5 wrongly keeps the ring intact (impossible for an endocyclic alkene). Index 0 correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-7ow4v1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone (drawn; the bulky tert-butyl group locks the chair) is reduced with LiAlH4, then H3O+. The small hydride attacks the unhindered carbonyl predominantly from the axial direction. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(=O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCCC1O"],"correct":1,"why":"Axial hydride delivery places the new OH equatorial. The cis isomer (equatorial attack) is minor; flat structure = undefined stereo; ketone = unreacted; last option = 2-tBu (1,2 regiochemistry).","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)C1CCC(=O)CC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1qi3zde","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"The molecule shown is meso-tartaric acid. Which structure represents the SAME compound (i.e., is identical to it)?","opts":["OC(=O)C(O)C(O)CC(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","OC(=O)C(O)CC(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C(=O)O"],"correct":4,"why":"Meso-tartaric acid has an internal mirror plane, so it is achiral and superimposable on its own mirror image. The two mixed-tag options are the chiral diastereomers of meso. Malic acid (one OH) and the five-carbon diacid have different connectivity, so they are constitutional isomers.","traps":null,"qs":"OC(=O)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"qr-a:lin1253","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies 6x - 3 = -2x - 3?","opts":["-1","-0.75","0","1","8"],"correct":2,"why":"Move x: (6+2)x = -3+3 → 8x = 0, so x = 0.","traps":["Substituting -1 gives -9 on the left and -1 on the right.","Adding 2x gives 8x - 3 = -3, and then the 3 was subtracted instead of added, leaving 8x = -6.",null,"Substituting 1 gives 3 on the left and -5 on the right.","That is the 8 from 8x = 0, and 0 divided by 8 is still 0."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:gue1767","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-14","topic":"Test-day execution","nights":[14],"d":1,"q":"Question asks for distance. Eliminate by units: which can NOT be the answer?","opts":["24 hr","60 mi","24 mi","2.4 km","150 mi"],"correct":0,"why":"Distance is measured in mi (or m, km), never in hours. Drop the time-unit option immediately.","traps":[null,"A 60 here looks like a speed, but written in miles it is still a distance and still in play.","Sharing the number 24 with the time option is not the same as sharing its unit.","Kilometers measure distance too, so the unit check leaves this one standing.","Miles are a distance unit, so the unit check the question asks for cannot rule this one out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1634","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Boxes coming off a line weigh a mean of 50 grams with a standard deviation of 5 grams. What is the z score of a box weighing 48 grams?","opts":["-2.5","-2","-0.4","-0.04","0.4"],"correct":2,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (48 − 50)/5 = -0.4.","traps":["Flipped the ratio and divided 5 by the 2-point gap.","This is the raw gap in points, stopping before the division by 5.",null,"Divided the 2-point gap by the mean of 50 instead of by the standard deviation of 5.","Right size, wrong side: 48 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1330","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"If -3x &lt; 1, which inequality must be true of x?","opts":["x &gt; -0.33","x = -0.33","x &lt; -0.33","x &lt; 0.33","x &gt; 0.33"],"correct":0,"why":"Divide both sides by -3 (negative) and FLIP: x &gt; -0.33.","traps":[null,"You solved the boundary equation -3x = 1 instead of the range of values.","You kept the inequality pointing the same way after dividing by a negative.","You divided by 3 rather than -3, so neither the sign nor the direction changed.","You flipped the direction but dropped the negative on 1/-3."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#11beqev","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 5 runners, in how many ways can 1st through 3rd place be awarded?","opts":["6","10","15","60","125"],"correct":3,"why":"Order matters: 5P3 = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 3 people. You still have to choose them from 5.","You used a combination. Places are different prizes, so ORDER matters: use a permutation.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.",null,"You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:est1211","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A bottling line fills 2,984 vials an hour. How many vials does it fill in 80 hours?","opts":["23872","238720","239000","240000","241000"],"correct":1,"why":"Work it out: 2984 × 80 = 238720. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 2984 times 8, so the zero in the 80 went missing.",null,"That is the product rounded to the nearest thousand, when the exact 238,720 is offered.","That is 3000 times 80, the rounded estimate rather than the exact product.","That is more than 2,000 above the product, so the rounding ran past even 3000 times 80."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#2h7tql","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 500 and standard deviation 10. About what percent of scores fall between 490 and 520?","opts":["47.5%","68%","81.5%","84%","95%"],"correct":2,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 81.5%.","traps":["That is only the upper half of the band. Add the 34% below the mean.","That only covers one SD each side. The upper limit here is TWO SD above.",null,"Close. Add 34% (one SD below) to 47.5% (two SD above).","That covers two SD each side. The lower limit here is only ONE SD below."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:est1205","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A reservoir holds 2,600 cubic meters of water, and rainfall adds 48 cubic meters. How many cubic meters does it hold now?","opts":["2552","2600","2648","2650","2700"],"correct":2,"why":"Work it out: 2600 + 48 = 2648. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 2600 minus 48, so the plus got read as a minus.","That is the starting number, with the 48 never added.",null,"That is 2600 plus 50, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 2,648.","That rounds 48 up to 100, which overshoots by about 50."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1351","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A total of 12 is divided in a ratio of 5 to 7. What is the smaller part?","opts":["1","5","6","7","12"],"correct":1,"why":"Total parts = 12; one part = 12/12 = 1. Smaller = 5·1 = 5.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1a7yu0w","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$10000 is invested at 20% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 3 years?","opts":["$7280.00","$14400.00","$16000.00","$17280.00","$20736.00"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 10000 × (1.2)³ = $17280.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#1ylorxc","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A car travels at 45 miles per hour. What is its speed in feet per second? (1 mile = 5280 ft)","opts":["66 ft/s","30.7 ft/s","2.38 × 10⁵ ft/s","0.0125 ft/s","3960 ft/s"],"correct":0,"why":"45 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi ÷ 3600 s/h = 66 ft/s. Handy anchor: 60 mph is 88 ft/s.","traps":[null,"Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so miles and hours cancel.","That is feet per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","That is miles per second. Multiply by 5280 to get feet.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:est1207","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A vineyard has 1,953 vines, and 73 of them are removed. How many vines remain?","opts":["1880","1920","1930","1980","2026"],"correct":0,"why":"Round to friendly numbers: 1950 - 70 = 1880, and the exact value of 1953 - 73 is also 1880.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1104","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"To the nearest hundredth, what is 21.1 divided by 21.7?","opts":["0.1","0.97","1.07","1.14","9.72"],"correct":1,"why":"Move both decimal points one place and divide: 211 by 217. The top number is slightly smaller than the bottom one, so the answer sits just under 1, at about 0.972. Any time you divide a number by a slightly larger one, expect a result a little below 1.","traps":["You pushed the point too far left, which shrinks the answer by a factor of ten.",null,"You divided the larger number by the smaller, and an answer above 1 means the two swapped places.","You split the division piece by piece, 21 into 21 plus 0.1 into 0.7; division does not break apart across a sum.","You moved the point one place right; two numbers this close divide to something near 1, not near 10."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1332","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"Which inequality is equivalent to -3x &lt; 0?","opts":["x = 0","x ≥ 0","x &lt; 0","x ≤ 0","x &gt; 0"],"correct":4,"why":"Divide both sides by -3 (negative) and FLIP: x &gt; 0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1058","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"One beaker holds 4/12 of a liter and a second holds 15/12 of a liter. The first amount is what fraction of the second?","opts":["60/24","19/144","60/144","12/5","4/15"],"correct":4,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 4/12 times 12/15. The two 12s cancel on sight, which leaves 4/15 without any heavy arithmetic. Cancelling before you multiply is what keeps numbers like 180 off your page.","traps":["You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (12 + 12).","You added the tops (4 + 15) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.","You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first.","That flips the first fraction instead of the second. Check the size: dividing by something bigger than one gives an answer smaller than what you started with.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:psm3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A savings balance of $500 falls 40 percent and then grows 40 percent from that lower balance. What is the balance then?","opts":["$200","$300","$420","$500","$700"],"correct":2,"why":"×0.60 × 1.40 = ×0.84. $420. Big swings, so big asymmetry.","traps":["That is the 40 percent that came off, not the 60 percent that stayed.","That is the price after the 40 percent cut, one step short of the answer.",null,"You added minus 40 and plus 40 into no change, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That applies only the 40 percent rise and leaves the cut out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pan1542","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"events: P(A) = 0.4, P(B) = 0.2, P(A and B) = 0.03. P(A and B)?","opts":["0.03","0.08","0.2","0.57","0.6"],"correct":0,"why":"This stem hands you the answer inside its own setup: the chance of both events together is stated as 0.03. Multiplying 0.4 by 0.2 is the shortcut for independent events, and nothing here says these two are independent, so that product does not apply. When a question gives you a joint probability, read the last line twice and check which piece it is actually asking for.","traps":[null,"0.08 multiplies 0.4 by 0.2, the shortcut for independent events, and this stem gives the actual overlap as 0.03.","0.2 reports the chance of B on its own rather than the chance of both together.","0.57 is the chance at least one happens, 0.4 plus 0.2 minus 0.03, which answers the or version.","0.6 adds 0.4 and 0.2, which starts the or rule rather than answering the and."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:psm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A technician's hourly pay is cut 20 percent and later raised 25 percent from the reduced rate. What is the net change in pay?","opts":["+45%","+5%","−45%","0% (back to original)","−5% (a small net loss)"],"correct":3,"why":"×0.80 × 1.25 = ×1.00. Exact return. (One of the rare cases that nets zero.)","traps":["You added the sizes of the two changes, 20 and 25, into one rise.","You added minus 20 and plus 25 into a 5 percent gain, and sequential percents multiply instead.","You added the sizes of the two changes and pointed the result downward.",null,"A raise on a smaller base usually falls short, and here 0.80 times 1.25 comes to exactly 1.00, so the salary lands right back where it started."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pcm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A share priced at $50 needs to reach $75. What percent gain does that take?","opts":["5%","25%","33%","50%","150%"],"correct":3,"why":"change = 25. Original = 50. 25/50 = 50%.","traps":["You lost a decimal place turning 0.5 into a percent, and one half is 50%.","You reported the raw 25 dollar gain as though it were the percent.","You divided the 25 gain by the new price of 75 instead of by the starting 50.",null,"You reported the multiplier, since 75 is 150% of 50, rather than the gain on top of it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-riknol","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"A triangle has sides a = 7, b = 5, and c = 8. What is cos C?","opts":["-0.143","0.143","0.286","0.5","0.786"],"correct":1,"why":"Rearranged, the Law of Cosines reads cos C equals a squared plus b squared minus c squared, all over 2ab. That is 49 plus 25 minus 64, which is 10, divided by 2 times 7 times 5, which is 70, giving 1/7 or about 0.143. Angle C sits opposite side c, so the side that gets subtracted on top is always the one facing the angle you want.","traps":["This subtracts in the reverse order, using c squared minus a squared minus b squared.",null,"This divides by ab instead of 2ab, leaving out the factor of 2.","This is cos A, which comes from subtracting a squared instead of c squared.","This is cos B, which comes from subtracting b squared instead of c squared."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1099","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A bus carries 21 passengers on its morning run and 25 on its afternoon run. How many passengers is that in total?","opts":["4","4.6","46","46.1","460"],"correct":2,"why":"Both numbers are whole, so there is no decimal work to do: add the tens, 20 + 20 = 40, then the ones, 1 + 5 = 6, for 46. When a question in a decimals set has no decimals in it, the place-value habit still applies, it just finishes faster.","traps":["Subtracted the two instead of adding them.","The digits are right but a decimal point crept in, shrinking the sum by a factor of ten.",null,"Both numbers are whole, so nothing can land in the tenths column.","An extra zero got attached, as if the numbers were 210 and 250."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1637","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A machine part lasts a mean of 150 hours with a standard deviation of 20 hours. What is the z score of a part that lasts 127 hours?","opts":["-23","-1.15","-0.87","-0.15","1.15"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (127 − 150)/20 = -1.15.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, one step short of dividing by the standard deviation.",null,"Flipped the ratio and divided 20 by the 23-point gap.","Divided the 23-point gap by the mean of 150 instead of by the standard deviation of 20.","Right size, wrong side: 127 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1394","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One conveyor can sort a shipment in 3 hours and a second conveyor can sort it in 10 hours. Running together, how many hours does the shipment take?","opts":["0.43","2.31","3","6.5","13"],"correct":1,"why":"1/T = 1/3 + 1/10 = 13/30. T = 30/13 = 2.31 hr.","traps":["Stopping at the combined rate of 13/30 of the job per hour instead of flipping it to get the hours.",null,"Reporting the faster worker's solo time; the help pulls it lower still.","Averaging the two times instead of combining the rates.","Adding the two times; together they finish sooner than either one alone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:g2d1","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":1,"q":"A circle has a radius of 6. What is its area?","opts":["12π","π","6π","36π","144π"],"correct":3,"why":"A = πr² = π(6²) = 36π.","traps":["That's the circumference.","Dropped the radius altogether and kept only the π.","That is π times the radius, which is half the circumference rather than an area.",null,"Used the diameter of 12 in place of the radius before squaring."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1305","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is the value of 3 × 6 − 6 × 3?","opts":["-36","0","12","18","36"],"correct":1,"why":"Do both products first: 3 × 6 = 18 and 6 × 3 = 18, then 18 - 18 = 0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1163","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A $318 tablet is marked down 45 percent. What is the sale price?","opts":["143.1","174.9","273","303.69","363"],"correct":1,"why":"New = 318 × (1 - 45/100) = 318 × 0.55 = 174.9.","traps":["That is 45 percent of 318, the discount itself rather than what is left to pay.",null,"You subtracted 45 dollars instead of 45 percent, and 45 percent of 318 is 143.10.","That takes off 4.5 percent; a 45 percent cut means multiplying by 0.55.","You added 45 dollars, and this price is coming down by 45 percent."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#az79xq","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (3 × 10⁵)(7 × 10⁻⁶) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["1 × 10⁰","2.1 × 10⁻²","2.1 × 10⁻¹","2.1 × 10⁰","2.1 × 10⁻³⁰"],"correct":3,"why":"3 × 7 = 21 and 10⁵ × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻¹. 21 × 10⁻¹ is not proper form, so write 2.1 × 10⁰. Answer: 2.1 × 10⁰.","traps":["You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.",null,"You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1658","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Can a triangle have sides of lengths 8, 7, and 1?","opts":["Cannot determine","Only if it is isosceles","Yes","Only if right triangle","No"],"correct":4,"why":"|8−7| = 1 and 8+7 = 15. 1 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["The three lengths settle it: 7 plus 1 equals 8, and the sum has to be greater than the longest side, not equal to it.","Equal sides are not the condition: 7 plus 1 ties the 8 exactly, and the sum has to be greater.","7 and 1 add to exactly 8, so they lie flat along the longest side instead of rising above it.","There is no angle to open up: the two shorter sides lie flat along the 8, tying it rather than beating it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:cou1577","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A farm will plant 2 of its 6 fields with a new crop. How many different pairs of fields can be chosen?","opts":["8","12","15","30","36"],"correct":2,"why":"nCr = 6!/(2!·4!) = 15.","traps":["Added 6 and 2.","Multiplied 6 by 2.",null,"That counts ordered pairs, 6 times 5; each unordered pair appears twice there.","Treated it as 6 options for each of two slots, which allows picking the same item twice."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#43ul2y","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 5 red and 5 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["2/9","4/9","1/4","1/2","1/5"],"correct":0,"why":"First red: 5/10. Second red: 4/9. Multiply: 2/9.","traps":[null,"That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too.","That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.","That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:lgm3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the value of log(20) + log(5)?","opts":["log(25)","log(15)","3","log(4)","2"],"correct":4,"why":"Product law: log(20) + log(5) = log(100) = 2.","traps":["You added the inputs, 20 and 5, when the product law multiplies them.","That subtracts the numbers inside the logs. Adding two logs multiplies what is inside them.","You counted the digits of 100 rather than its zeros, and log(100) is 2.","You divided 20 by 5, which is the rule for a difference of logs, not a sum.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1719","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"Expressed as a decimal, what is the value of 3^5 ÷ 3^6?","opts":["0.33","3","972","177147","205891132094649"],"correct":0,"why":"Same base, so the exponents subtract: 3^(5-6) = 3^-1. A negative exponent means one over that power, so this is 1/3, which rounds to 0.33. When the bottom exponent is the bigger one, expect an answer below 1.","traps":[null,"You subtracted the exponents the wrong way round, as 6 - 5 rather than 5 - 6.","You added 243 and 729 instead of dividing them.","You added the exponents instead of subtracting, which gives 3^11.","You multiplied the exponents, which gives 3^30."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#18ntb5a","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 180 miles at 30 mph and returns the same 180 miles at 60 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["40 mph","20 mph","42.4 mph","52.5 mph","45 mph"],"correct":0,"why":"Time out = 180/30 = 6 h, time back = 180/60 = 3 h. Average = 360 / 9 = 40 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":[null,"You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d.","That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.","Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1259","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"One tank starts 7 liters below a fill mark and gains 6 liters each hour, while a second starts 7 liters above the mark and loses 2 liters each hour. After how many hours do the two hold the same amount?","opts":["-1.75","0","0.75","1.75","2.75"],"correct":3,"why":"Move x: (6+2)x = 7+7 → 8x = 14, so x = 1.75.","traps":["Dividing 14 by 8 gives a positive 1.75, so the minus sign never comes out of the work.","The 7 was subtracted from the 7 instead of added, leaving 8x = 0.","Substituting 0.75 gives -2.5 on the left and 5.5 on the right.",null,"Substituting 2.75 gives 9.5 on the left and 1.5 on the right."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1653","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Can a triangle have sides of lengths 14, 13, and 1?","opts":["Yes","Only if right triangle","Cannot determine","Only if it is isosceles","No"],"correct":4,"why":"|14−13| = 1 and 14+13 = 27. 1 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["13 and 1 add to exactly 14, so they lie flat along the longest side instead of rising above it.","There is no angle to open up: the two shorter sides lie flat along the 14, tying it rather than beating it.","The three lengths settle it: 13 plus 1 equals 14, and the sum has to be greater than the longest side, not equal to it.","Equal sides are not the test: 13 plus 1 ties the 14, and the sum has to be greater.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:da4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A supplier ships 800 units, and 0.025 of them arrive damaged. How many units are damaged?","opts":["0.2","2","20","200","2000"],"correct":2,"why":"0.025 = 25/1000 = 2.5%. 2.5% of 800 = 20. Or move decimals: 25 × 800 = 20000, divided by 1000 = 20.","traps":["Read 0.025 as 0.00025, a hundred times too small.","Read 0.025 as 0.0025, which is ten times too small.",null,"Read 0.025 as 0.25, which is ten times too large.","Read 0.025 as 2.5 and multiplied from there."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#19hs487","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 60 miles at 30 mph and returns the same 60 miles at 45 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["41.3 mph","18 mph","37.5 mph","36.7 mph","36 mph"],"correct":4,"why":"Time out = 60/30 = 2 h, time back = 60/45 = 1.33 h. Average = 120 / 3.33 = 36 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":["Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d.","You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.","That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#1wt0ylw","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the PRODUCT of the solutions of x² − 11x + 18 = 0?","opts":["7","−11","11","−18","18"],"correct":4,"why":"It factors as (x − 2)(x − 9), so the roots are 2 and 9. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = 11, product = c/a = 18.","traps":["That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their product.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.","That is the SUM of the roots (−b/a). The product is c/a.","Sign slip. The product of the roots is c/a with its sign kept.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#x7vu8n","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 126 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["454 m/s","0.035 m/s","1.26 × 10⁵ m/s","35 m/s","2100 m/s"],"correct":3,"why":"126 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 35 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":["Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.","That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.","That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.",null,"You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:int1434","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A trust holds $2000 at 12% interest compounded annually for 10 years. Which amount in dollars is closest to its value after 10 years?","opts":["2001.2","2240","2400","4400","6211.7"],"correct":4,"why":"A = P(1+r)^t = 2000·(1.12)^10 = 6211.7.","traps":["Added rt as one dollar and twenty cents rather than applying the rate to the balance each year.","Grew the balance for one year instead of ten.","That is the simple interest for ten years, not the ending amount.","Used simple interest, so ten years of interest earning interest went missing.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261006"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s10"}}]};
